</li>
<li>'),
  ('ap-mock-phys1-full-2_q36', 'ap-mock-phys1-full-2', 36, false, 'D', 'Comprehensive AP Physics 1', 'hard', 'A mass $M$ at the end of a string is spun in a circle of radius $R$ and a constant speed $V$. Compare the tension in the line when the circle is in a horizontal plane to that of the tensions at the top and bottom of a vertical plane.</p>
<p><b>Columns:</b> Horizontal &bull; Vertical-top &bull; Vertical-bottom</p>', '["MV 2 / R | MV 2 / R | MV 2 / R","MV 2 / R | MV 2 / R - Mg | MV 2 / R - Mg","MV 2 / R | MV 2 / R + Mg | MV 2 / R - Mg","MV 2 / R | MV 2 / R - Mg | MV 2 / R + Mg"]'::jsonb, 'In a horizontal plane, the tension is the only centripetal force. However, in a vertical plane, the tension and the force due to gravity are both along the radial line. At the top, gravity is working downward along with the tension so $F_{net} = T$ + <i>mg</i> whereas, at the bottom, gravity is still downward while the tension is still directed inward $F_{net} = T$ − <i>mg</i>. In all cases $F_{net}$ = <i>MV</i><sup>2</sup>/$R$.</p></li>
<li>'),
  ('ap-mock-phys1-full-2_q37', 'ap-mock-phys1-full-2', 37, false, 'C', 'Comprehensive AP Physics 1', 'hard', 'An astronaut wakes up inside an accelerating rocket ship in deep space. Using her rubber band of unknown spring constant with attached (unknown) mass, she holds it up against the acceleration and notices the mass stretches the band downward by $X$ meters. She then pulls it down an additional 5 cm and times the oscillation up and down to be $T$ seconds. The acceleration of her rocket ship in terms of her measurements is</p>', '["$X$/$T^{2}$","<i>KX</i>/$m$","$X$(2π/$T$)<sup>2</sup>","0.2/$T^{2}$"]'::jsonb, 'From her first experiment:</p> T = 2\pi\sqrt{\frac{m}{k}} $$
<p class="exp">From her second experiment:</p> k = \frac{4\pi^2 m}{T^2}  </li>
<li><p class="exp">The only two measurement values she has are $T$ and $X$, so eliminating $K$ and $m$ from these two equations and solving for $a$:</p> \Delta x = \frac{mg}{k}  
<p class="exp">and</p> g = \frac{k \Delta x}{m}  
<p class="exp">Combining:</p> g = \frac{4\pi^2 \Delta x}{T^2} $$</li>
<li>'),
  ('ap-mock-phys1-full-2_q38', 'ap-mock-phys1-full-2', 38, false, 'D', 'Comprehensive AP Physics 1', 'hard', 'A woman standing on a scale in an elevator notices that the scale reads her true weight. From this, she may conclude that</p>', '["the elevator must be at rest","the elevator must be accelerating precisely at 9.8 m/$s^{2}$","the elevator must be moving upward","the elevator must not be accelerating"]'::jsonb, 'The elevator must be traveling at constant velocity to insure the normal force (reading on the scale) is the same as her true weight. However, this velocity value can be any number: positive, zero, or negative.</p></li>
<li>'),
  ('ap-mock-phys1-full-2_q39', 'ap-mock-phys1-full-2', 39, false, 'C', 'Comprehensive AP Physics 1', 'hard', 'A planet has half the mass of Earth and half the radius. Compared with the acceleration due to gravity at the surface of Earth, the acceleration due to gravity at the surface of this planet is</p>', '["the same","halved","doubled","quadrupled"]'::jsonb, 'Remember that $g$ = <i>Gm</i>/$r$<sup><i>2</i></sup>. Half the mass means half the $g$. Half the $r$ means 4 × $g$. Collect the changes:</p> P = \frac{F}{A} = \rho g h $$</li>
<li>'),
  ('ap-mock-phys1-full-2_q40', 'ap-mock-phys1-full-2', 40, false, 'C', 'Comprehensive AP Physics 1', 'hard', 'Which of the following materials will exert the highest pressure on its bottom surface?</p>', '["1 liter of water in a puddle on the ground","1 liter of water, frozen into a cube on the ground","1 liter of water in a tall, thin, vertical tube","1 liter of water, frozen into a cube, floating in liquid water"]'::jsonb, 'Pressure equals density times height times $g$. The tall tube of water will have the most height and thus the greatest pressure at its bottom surface.</p></li>
</ol>'),
  ('ap-mock-phys1-full-2_q41', 'ap-mock-phys1-full-2', 41, false, 'C', 'Comprehensive AP Physics 1', 'easy', 'A mass is suspended from the ceiling by one rope at an angle as indicated. The mass is held in place by a second, horizontal rope. Without knowing the exact angle from the ceiling, except that it is greater than 45 degrees, which statement best represents the possible value for tension in the horizontal line?</p>', '["$T$ &gt; <i>mg</i>","$T$ = <i>mg</i>","$T$ &lt; <i>mg</i>","Cannot be determined without knowing the angle"]'::jsonb, '$T$ &lt; <i>mg.</i> Since the mass is stationary, we conclude that the net force is zero. The upward tension of the angled rope must be <i>mg</i> in order to cancel out gravity. The free-body diagram on the hanging mass is:</p>
<p class="center1"><img alt="" class="w35" role="presentation" src="images/f023-01.jpg"/></p>
<p class="exp">Since the angle θ is greater than 45 degrees, the vertical component of tension $T_{2}$ must be bigger than the horizontal component. (To see this quickly, draw an extreme case, such as 85 degrees.) Therefore, the horizontal component of tension must be less than <i>mg</i>. The horizontal rope $T$ must cancel this horizontal component of $T_{2}$.</p>
<p class="center1"><img alt="" class="w23" role="presentation" src="images/f023-02.jpg"/></p></li>
<li>'),
  ('ap-mock-phys1-full-2_q42', 'ap-mock-phys1-full-2', 42, false, 'B', 'Comprehensive AP Physics 1', 'easy', 'Pushing on a mass of 15 kg with a sideways force of 120 N is not enough to get the mass to start sliding across the floor. Which of the following best describes the coefficient of static friction between the floor and the mass?</p>', '["$\\mu$ = 0.8","$\\mu$ &gt; 0.8","$\\mu$ &lt; 0.8","$\\mu$ = 1.25"]'::jsonb, '$\mu$ &gt; 0.8. The normal force between the mass and the floor is 150 N. The needed sideways push to overcome static friction is <b>greater than</b> 120 N. Dividing these two forces would give us:</p>
<p class="center1">$\mu_s = 120/150 = 0.8$</p>
<p class="exp">Since a greater force is needed, the coefficient of static friction must be greater than this value.</p></li>
<li>'),
  ('ap-mock-phys1-full-2_q43', 'ap-mock-phys1-full-2', 43, false, 'B', 'Comprehensive AP Physics 1', 'easy', 'While standing on an elevator and experiencing a downward acceleration of 4.9 m/$s^{2}$, what is the force between the floor and your feet? The variable $m$ is your mass.</p>', '["<i>mg</i>","0.5<i>mg</i>","1.5<i>mg</i>","4.9<i>mg</i>"]'::jsonb, '0.5<i>mg</i>. The free-body diagram consists of only two forces.</p> N - mg = ma $$
<p class="exp">Newton’s second law gives us $+N$ − <i>mg</i> = <i>ma</i>. Let $a$ = −0.5$g$, which is half the value of $g$ and downward:</p> N - mg = m(-0.5g) \implies N = 0.5mg $$</li>
<li>'),
  ('ap-mock-phys1-full-2_q44', 'ap-mock-phys1-full-2', 44, false, 'A', 'Comprehensive AP Physics 1', 'medium', 'After throwing a ball at an upward angle, the reason the ball continues to go horizontally while falling due to gravity is that</p>', '["the ball’s inertia keeps it going","the force of the throw keeps it going","the energy from the gravitational potential keeps replenishing the lost energy","air pressure keeps the ball from falling too quickly"]'::jsonb, 'The ball’s inertia keeps it going. Newton’s first law says that a body’s inertia maintains its current motion once in motion. After the force of the throw, the only force on the ball is the vertical force of gravity. This causes the ball to accelerate in the vertical direction while the ball’s sideways velocity is maintained due to its inertia.</p></li>
<li>'),
  ('ap-mock-phys1-full-2_q45', 'ap-mock-phys1-full-2', 45, false, 'C', 'Comprehensive AP Physics 1', 'medium', 'Consider the situation in which your hand is pushing a book across a rough desktop with lots of friction. Of the many forces involved, consider only these two individual forces: the force on your hand from the book and the force the book is experiencing from your hand. While the book is accelerating from rest to its final velocity, which statement best compares the force experienced by your hand compared with that experienced by the book?</p>', '["Force on book &gt; force on hand","Force on hand &gt; force on book","Force on hand = force on book","The relationship between these two forces depends on the frictional force."]'::jsonb, 'Force on hand = force on book. Newton’s third law of action-reaction states that the force pair of interaction between two objects is always equal and opposite. The acceleration of either object has no role in the third law. The acceleration comes about from the net force on a single object.</p></li>
<li>'),
  ('ap-diag-physc-mech_q1', 'ap-diag-physc-mech', 1, false, 'B', 'Diagnostic Test – Mechanics', 'medium', 'A block lies on an inclined plane with angle of elevation \theta, as shown in the figure. The inclined plane is frictionless, and the plane is accelerated to the left such that the block’s height remains constant. What is the net force on the block?', '["mg cot\\theta","mg tan\\theta","mg cos\\theta","mg sin\\theta","zero"]'::jsonb, '(B) Consider the figure. Because the block accelerates horizontally, we choose horizontal (x) and vertical (y) coordinates rather than the usual tilted coordinates of inclined plane problems. The y-component of the normal force must balance gravity for the block’s height to remain constant: Because the force in the y-direction is zero, the force in the x-direction is equal to the net force. Based on the diagram, this force is For more practice with this type of problem, see Chapter 3.'),
  ('ap-diag-physc-mech_q2', 'ap-diag-physc-mech', 2, false, 'E', 'Diagnostic Test – Mechanics', 'medium', 'Consider an Atwood machine consisting of two unequal masses m1 and m2 (with m1 > m2) connected by a light string that passes over a cylindrical pulley. If the pulley has non-negligible mass M and radius R (rotational inertia I = 1/2 M R^2), which of the following is true of the linear acceleration a of the masses compared to the acceleration a_0 when the pulley is ideal and massless?', '["a = g","a = a_0","a > a_0","a = (m1 + m2)g / (m1 - m2)","a < a_0 because rotational inertia of the pulley reduces linear acceleration"]'::jsonb, 'For a massless pulley, linear acceleration is a_0 = (m1 - m2)g / (m1 + m2). When the pulley has non-negligible mass M and rotational inertia I = 1/2 M R^2, torque equation tau = (T1 - T2)R = I alpha = I (a/R) requires different tensions on either side of the pulley. Summing Newton''s second law for the system yields (m1 - m2)g = (m1 + m2 + I/R^2)a = (m1 + m2 + 1/2 M)a, so a = (m1 - m2)g / (m1 + m2 + 1/2 M) < a_0. Thus, the acceleration is strictly less than in the massless pulley case.'),
  ('ap-diag-physc-mech_q3', 'ap-diag-physc-mech', 3, false, 'D', 'Diagnostic Test – Mechanics', 'medium', 'An object moves in the xy-plane with instantaneous velocity vector v directed along the positive x-axis. Five different constant acceleration vectors a are tested over a short time interval Delta t. In which case does the object experience the greatest increase in speed?', '["a is perpendicular to v (directed along the positive y-axis)","a is directed at an angle of 135 degrees relative to v","a is directed opposite to v (along the negative x-axis)","a is directed parallel to and in the same direction as v","a has zero magnitude"]'::jsonb, 'The rate of change of speed is given by d|v|/dt = a_parallel = a * cos(theta), the component of acceleration parallel to velocity. When acceleration is parallel and in the same direction as velocity (theta = 0 degrees), cos(0) = 1, giving the maximum possible rate of increase in speed. Perpendicular acceleration changes direction only, while antiparallel acceleration decreases speed.'),
  ('ap-diag-physc-mech_q4', 'ap-diag-physc-mech', 4, false, 'D', 'Diagnostic Test – Mechanics', 'medium', 'The moment of inertia (rotational inertia), \int r2 dm, is the rotational analog of', '["displacement","center of mass","velocity","mass","force"]'::jsonb, '(D) Mass is a measure of linear inertia. (For more information, see the definition of rotational inertia and the table comparing linear and angular quantities in Chapter 7.)'),
  ('ap-diag-physc-mech_q5', 'ap-diag-physc-mech', 5, false, 'D', 'Diagnostic Test – Mechanics', 'medium', 'A mass m connected to a spring with spring constant k oscillates at a frequency f. If the mass is increased by a factor of 4 and the spring constant is increased by a factor of 2, the new frequency will be', '["2f","8f","1","2","none of the above Questions 6–8 refer to the figure below, which shows two masses connected by a massless pulley. Assume the incline is frictionless."]'::jsonb, '(D) According to the equation , quadrupling the mass and doubling the spring constant causes the frequency to decrease by a factor of . For more information about oscillating spring systems, see Chapter 8.'),
  ('ap-diag-physc-mech_q6', 'ap-diag-physc-mech', 6, false, 'B', 'Diagnostic Test – Mechanics', 'medium', 'A mass m1 moves horizontally to the right with speed v along the positive x-axis, while a mass m2 moves vertically downward with speed v along the negative y-axis. What is the total momentum vector p_total of the two-mass system?', '["(m1 + m2)v i","m1 v i - m2 v j","m1 v i + m2 v j","(m1 - m2)v j","Zero"]'::jsonb, 'Linear momentum is a vector quantity given by p = m v. The momentum of mass m1 is p1 = m1 v i (directed to the right). The momentum of mass m2 is p2 = -m2 v j (directed downward). The total momentum of the system is the vector sum p_total = p1 + p2 = m1 v i - m2 v j.'),
  ('ap-diag-physc-mech_q7', 'ap-diag-physc-mech', 7, false, 'B', 'Diagnostic Test – Mechanics', 'medium', 'When m2 has fallen a distance d, what is the change in the total kinetic energy of the system?', '["g(m2d + m1d sin \\theta)","g(m2d − m1d sin \\theta)","g(m2d + m1d cos \\theta)","g(m2d − m1d cos \\theta)","Total kinetic energy is conserved."]'::jsonb, '(B) The change in the potential energy is Because of conservation of energy, the change in the kinetic energy must have the opposite sign of this change in the potential energy. More information about kinetic energy can be found in Chapter 4.'),
  ('ap-diag-physc-mech_q8', 'ap-diag-physc-mech', 8, false, 'C', 'Diagnostic Test – Mechanics', 'medium', 'Now consider that the inclined plane, which has mass M and rests on a frictionless surface, can move as well. When both small masses start from rest, which of the following quantities evaluated for the system of the three masses is conserved?', '["gravitational potential energy","kinetic energy","x-momentum","y-momentum","None of the above are conserved for this system."]'::jsonb, '(C) As shown in question 6, the potential and kinetic energy both change, so choices (A) and (B) are not correct. There are no external forces acting in the x-direction, so the momentum in this direction must be conserved. There are external forces acting in the y-direction (gravity and the normal force exerted on the incline by the horizontal surface), so y-momentum is not conserved. Conservation of momentum is discussed in Chapter 5.'),
  ('ap-diag-physc-mech_q9', 'ap-diag-physc-mech', 9, false, 'B', 'Diagnostic Test – Mechanics', 'medium', 'Two objects with equal masses and speeds as shown collide and stick together. What is the final speed of the combined mass?', '["v/2","v/","v","(E) 2v","Zero net field / magnitude"]'::jsonb, '(B) This is an inelastic collision in two dimensions such that momentum (but not energy) is conserved. Thus, in the horizontal (x) direction we have the equation mv + m(0) = (2m)v; similarly, in the vertical (y) direction we have m(0) + mv = (2m)v. Therefore, the final velocity has components in both directions that are equal to v/2, so that the final velocity has a magnitude of Collision problems are discussed in Chapter 5.'),
  ('ap-diag-physc-mech_q10', 'ap-diag-physc-mech', 10, false, 'C', 'Diagnostic Test – Mechanics', 'medium', 'An object moving with constant acceleration can have how many of the following path types? I. a linear path II. a circular path III. a parabolic path IV. an elliptical path V. a spiral path', '["none","one","two","three","four"]'::jsonb, '(C) An object with constant acceleration can have a linear path (e.g., a projectile thrown directly upward) or a parabolic path (e.g., a projectile launched at an angle). Moving in circular, elliptical, or spiral paths all require centripetal forces that change in direction. Motion in multiple dimensions with a constant acceleration is essentially identical to projectile motion, which is discussed in Chapter 2.')
ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.ap_exam_questions (id, test_id, question_order, is_frq, answer_key, topic, difficulty, question_text, options_json, explanation)
VALUES
  ('ap-diag-physc-mech_q11', 'ap-diag-physc-mech', 11, false, 'E', 'Diagnostic Test – Mechanics', 'medium', 'A mass is pulled up a frictionless incline by a force applied parallel to the incline. The change in the object’s kinetic energy is equal to which of the following?', '["the negative of the change in potential energy","the work done by gravity","the work done by the applied force","the work done by the applied force minus the work done by gravity","the work done by the applied force plus the work done by gravity"]'::jsonb, '(E) The kinetic energy is not equal to the negative of the change in the potential energy due to the presence of the external force. (Imagine, for example, that the force F accelerates the mass as it moves up the incline; certainly energy is not being conserved in this situation.) The change in kinetic energy is equal to the net work done on the object, which in turn is equal to the sum of the work done by each of the forces, as stated in the work-energy theorem. (The normal force of the incline does zero work.) Choice (D) is incorrect because although gravity does do negative work, this negative sign is contained within the phrase “the work done by gravity.” The work-energy theorem is derived and discussed in Chapter 4.'),
  ('ap-diag-physc-mech_q12', 'ap-diag-physc-mech', 12, false, 'E', 'Diagnostic Test – Mechanics', 'medium', 'Which of the following changes does not affect the period of a horizontal mass-spring system undergoing simple harmonic motion?', '["doubling the mass","attaching an identical spring in parallel (as in the figure)","attaching an identical spring in series (as in the figure)","the presence of friction between the block and the horizontal surface","any manipulation of the masses and springs that does not affect the ratio of the maximum amplitude to the maximum velocity"]'::jsonb, '(E) The period is given by the equation . Therefore, doubling the mass, adding a spring in parallel (which doubles the effective spring constant), or adding a spring in series (which halves the effective spring constant) all affect the period. Adding friction complicates the motion such that it is no longer simple harmonic motion, and the period changes. Based on conservation of energy, , so that . Therefore, any change in the parameters of the system that does not affect the ratio A/vmax does not affect the ratio m/k and thus does not affect the period. For further information on mass-spring systems, see Chapter 8.'),
  ('ap-diag-physc-mech_q13', 'ap-diag-physc-mech', 13, false, 'E', 'Diagnostic Test – Mechanics', 'medium', 'As a block moves up an incline at constant speed, the frictional force performs −5 J of work on the block. As the block moves down the same incline at constant speed over the same distance, how much work does the frictional force perform on the block?', '["−5 J","0 J","+5 J","It depends on the angle of elevation of the incline.","It depends on whether the applied force is the same in both situations."]'::jsonb, '(E) The fact that the net force is zero does not constrain the frictional force to a single value. Instead, the frictional force depends on the nature of the applied force and the resulting normal force. Consider, for example, the two cases in the figure that show two different ways to push a mass up an incline. In case 1, assume that the applied force exactly cancels the gravitational force; thus, both the normal and frictional forces are zero. In case 2, on the other hand, there are nonzero normal and frictional forces. (The component of the applied force parallel to the direction of motion cancels the frictional force such that the net force is zero.) Note: If the normal force were the same on the way up and on the way down, the frictional force would perform −5 J of work on the way down. The kinetic frictional force always opposes the motion and always performs negative work. The friction force is discussed further in Chapter 3; work is discussed in Chapter 4.'),
  ('ap-diag-physc-mech_q14', 'ap-diag-physc-mech', 14, false, 'E', 'Diagnostic Test – Mechanics', 'medium', 'An object of mass m undergoes simple harmonic motion with position x(t) = A cos(omega t) and velocity v(t) = -A omega sin(omega t). Which of the following correctly describes the potential energy U(t) of the oscillator as a function of time?', '["U(t) is constant and independent of time","U(t) reaches its maximum whenever the speed of the object is maximum","U(t) oscillates with angular frequency omega/2","U(t) is zero at the turning points x = +A and x = -A","U(t) oscillates between 0 and 1/2 k A^2, reaching its maxima whenever the velocity is zero"]'::jsonb, 'Total mechanical energy in simple harmonic motion is conserved: E = K + U = 1/2 k A^2. When velocity is zero (at the turning points x = +/- A), kinetic energy K = 1/2 m v^2 = 0, so potential energy U reaches its maximum value of 1/2 k A^2. When velocity is maximum (at equilibrium x = 0), potential energy is zero. Thus U(t) reaches its maximum whenever velocity is zero.'),
  ('ap-diag-physc-mech_q15', 'ap-diag-physc-mech', 15, false, 'D', 'Diagnostic Test – Mechanics', 'medium', 'Consider the two concentric cylinders in the figure, which are fastened to each other and suspended by a pin through their center, which exerts a force of F1. Two additional forces, F2 and F3, act on the cylinders as shown. Which of the following equations is a necessary condition for the two-cylinder system to remain in static equilibrium?', '["F1 − F3 sin − F2 sin \\theta = 0","F1 − F3 cos + F2 cos \\theta = 0","F2 cos\\theta − F3 cos = 0","F2(cos\\theta)r2 − F3(cos )r1 = 0","F2(cos\\theta)r1 − F3(cos )r2 = 0 Questions 16–18 refer to the figure below, which shows a mass m connected to a spring (of spring constant k) rotating in a circle on a frictionless table at constant speed."]'::jsonb, '(D) The following equations are required for static equilibrium. The net force in the x-direction must be zero: The net force in the y-direction must be zero: The net torque about their centers must be zero: Inspection reveals that the only valid choice is (D), which is the negative of the torque condition above. For further information about the static equilibrium of extended objects (objects that can rotate), see Chapter 7.'),
  ('ap-diag-physc-mech_q16', 'ap-diag-physc-mech', 16, false, 'D', 'Diagnostic Test – Mechanics', 'medium', 'If x is the extension of the spring from its unstretched length, v is the speed of the mass, and r is the radius of the circular path, which of the following equations is valid?', '["kx = mv 2/2","kx 2/2 = mv 2/2","kx 2/2 = mv 2/r","kx = mv 2/r","None of the above equations are valid."]'::jsonb, '(D) The spring force provides the centripetal force, so it must equal the centripetal force. The centripetal force is discussed in the section on uniform circular motion in Chapter 2.'),
  ('ap-diag-physc-mech_q17', 'ap-diag-physc-mech', 17, false, 'E', 'Diagnostic Test – Mechanics', 'medium', 'A block of mass m is attached to one end of a light horizontal spring with spring constant k. The other end of the spring is pivoted at a fixed point on a frictionless horizontal table. The block is set into uniform circular motion of radius r with constant speed v, maintaining a constant spring stretch x. What is the rate at which the spring does work on the block (the instantaneous power delivered by the spring)?', '["kx v","1/2 k x^2 (v / r)","k x^2 v / m","k x r sqrt(k/m)","Zero"]'::jsonb, 'Instantaneous power is defined as P = F * v. For uniform circular motion, the spring force acts as the centripetal force and is directed radially inward toward the pivot, while the instantaneous velocity is directed tangentially along the circle. Because F and v are perpendicular at all times, F * v = |F||v| cos(90 deg) = 0. Therefore, the spring does zero work and delivers zero power to the block.'),
  ('ap-diag-physc-mech_q18', 'ap-diag-physc-mech', 18, false, 'A', 'Diagnostic Test – Mechanics', 'medium', 'Consider the block in the previous problem moving in a circle of radius r with the spring stretched by an extension x. If the spring is suddenly cut, what is the change in the elastic potential energy stored in the spring as it returns to its unstretched equilibrium length?', '["A decrease of 1/2 k x^2","An increase of 1/2 k x^2","A decrease of k x^2","A decrease of 1/2 m v^2","No change in energy"]'::jsonb, 'Before being cut, the spring is stretched by x and stores elastic potential energy U_initial = 1/2 k x^2. After being cut, the spring is free of tension and relaxes to its unstretched equilibrium length where x = 0 and U_final = 0. The change in the spring''s potential energy is Delta U = U_final - U_initial = 0 - 1/2 k x^2 = -1/2 k x^2 (a decrease of 1/2 k x^2).'),
  ('ap-diag-physc-mech_q19', 'ap-diag-physc-mech', 19, false, 'B', 'Diagnostic Test – Mechanics', 'medium', 'Consider a disk that has uniform density and a hole as shown. Where is the center of mass of this disk located?', '["point A","point B","point C","point D","point E"]'::jsonb, '(B) There is more mass to the left of point C than to the right of point C, so the center of mass must lie to the left of point C. Because all the mass lies to the right of point A, point A cannot be the center of mass. Thus, by elimination, the center of mass must be point B. Note that the center of mass must lie on the horizontal line AE because of symmetry. Further information about calculating the center of mass of this object can be found in Example 5.10.'),
  ('ap-diag-physc-mech_q20', 'ap-diag-physc-mech', 20, false, 'D', 'Diagnostic Test – Mechanics', 'medium', 'A mass m is suspended by two ropes as shown. What is the tension in each of the ropes?', '["Directly proportional to the parameter (linear scaling)","Inversely proportional to the square of the separation","Equal in magnitude with opposite orientation","Independent of the mass and charge distribution","Zero net change under static equilibrium conditions"]'::jsonb, '(D) Because of symmetry, the two tension forces will be equal in magnitude. Therefore, to keep the mass in static equilibrium requires . Solving this equation for the tension force gives choice (D). More information about the static equilibrium of point masses can be found in Chapter 3 (particularly Example 3.3).'),
  ('ap-diag-physc-mech_q21', 'ap-diag-physc-mech', 21, false, 'D', 'Diagnostic Test – Mechanics', 'medium', 'What is the maximum speed of a simple pendulum with length l whose maximum angle of displacement from the vertical is \theta?', '["Directly proportional to the parameter (linear scaling)","Inversely proportional to the square of the separation","Equal in magnitude with opposite orientation","Independent of the mass and charge distribution","Not enough information given."]'::jsonb, '(D) At the maximum height of the pendulum, all the energy is gravitational potential energy. At the minimum height of the pendulum, all this potential energy has been converted to kinetic energy. Energy conservation between these extreme points yields (y is set equal to zero at the bottom of the pendulum’s arc; see Example 8.1 for the geometry of the height difference.) Solving for velocity yields choice (D). Further information about the conservation of energy can be found in Chapter 4.'),
  ('ap-diag-physc-mech_q22', 'ap-diag-physc-mech', 22, false, 'D', 'Diagnostic Test – Mechanics', 'medium', 'A ball released from rest at height h rolls down a curved incline without slipping, as shown. What fraction of its final angular speed \omegaf does the ball have at point P?', '["\\omegap = \\omegaf / 4","(C) \\omegap = \\omegaf / 2","1","(E) \\omegap = \\omegaf","4"]'::jsonb, '(D) Because the ball rolls without slipping, the relationship between the velocity of its center of mass and its angular velocity is v = r\omega. Therefore, the total kinetic energy of the ball is equal to so that the kinetic energy is proportional to \omega2. The kinetic energy at the bottom of the incline is twice the kinetic energy at point P (because half of the gravitational potential energy has been converted to kinetic energy at point P). Therefore, the angular velocity is smaller by a factor of at point P. Gravitational potential energy is discussed in Chapter 4, while rotational kinetic energy is covered in Chapter 6.'),
  ('ap-diag-physc-mech_q23', 'ap-diag-physc-mech', 23, false, 'E', 'Diagnostic Test – Mechanics', 'medium', 'Which of the following is true of the potential and kinetic energies in a system undergoing SHM?', '["The total energy is maximized at the equilibrium point.","The total energy is maximized at the turning points.","The sum of the potential and kinetic energies varies sinusoidally.","The kinetic energy is maximized when the acceleration is maximized.","The maximum potential energy equals the maximum kinetic energy."]'::jsonb, '(E) Choices (A), (B), and (C) are false because the total energy is constant. Choice (D) is false because the kinetic energy is maximized at the equilibrium point, where the acceleration is zero. Choice (E) is correct because the maximum potential energy equals the maximum kinetic energy, which equals the total energy.'),
  ('ap-diag-physc-mech_q24', 'ap-diag-physc-mech', 24, false, 'D', 'Diagnostic Test – Mechanics', 'medium', 'Consider a planet in an elliptical orbit around the sun as shown. Which of the following is the correct ratio of the magnitudes of the planet’s acceleration at points A and B?', '["aA/aB = rA/rB","aA/aB = rB/rA","1","2","4"]'::jsonb, '(D) The acceleration is equal to the net force divided by the mass, and the net force at either of the points is equal to the gravitational force due to the sun. Therefore, and . Finding the ratio of these two accelerations yields choice (D). Further information about universal gravitation can be found in Chapter 9.'),
  ('ap-diag-physc-mech_q25', 'ap-diag-physc-mech', 25, false, 'C', 'Diagnostic Test – Mechanics', 'medium', 'If an object of mass m moves along a straight line such that its position as a function of time is given by x(t) = v0 t, where v0 is a constant velocity, what is the object''s kinetic energy?', '["Zero","1/2 m v0 t","1/2 m v0^2 (a constant independent of time)","m v0^2 t^2","1/2 m (v0 t)^2"]'::jsonb, 'The velocity of the object is v(t) = dx/dt = d(v0 t)/dt = v0, which is constant over time. The kinetic energy is K = 1/2 m v^2 = 1/2 m v0^2, which is constant and independent of time.'),
  ('ap-diag-physc-mech_q26', 'ap-diag-physc-mech', 26, false, 'B', 'Diagnostic Test – Mechanics', 'medium', 'In order to swim directly across a stream with water moving with speed vstream with respect to the ground as shown, which of the following is required of the magnitude of the swimmer’s speed with respect to the water, vswimmer?', '["vswimmer = vstream","vswimmer > vstream","Perpendicular into the page","Perpendicular out of the page","vswimmer > 0"]'::jsonb, '(B) The relative velocity vectors are shown in the figure accompanying the question. The velocity of the swimmer with respect to the ground is equal to the velocity of the swimmer with respect to the water (vswimmer) plus the velocity of the water with respect to the ground (vstream). In order for the sum of these velocities to point directly across the stream, the swimmer’s velocity vswimmer must (1) cancel the velocity of the current vstream and (2) provide a perpendicular component across the stream. For vswimmer to have a y-component equal in magnitude to vstream and a nonzero x-component, its magnitude must be greater than vstream. Imagine what would happen if vswimmer = vstream. If the swimmer swam in the direction opposite the current, the swimmer would remain stationary with respect to the shore. If the swimmer tried to cross the stream, the y-component of vswimmer would be less than vstream, and the swimmer would be swept downstream. Although it would be possible to cross the stream, it would be impossible to cross the stream along a path perpendicular to the banks of the stream. Further information about relative velocity can be found in Chapter 2 (in particular, see Example 2.9).'),
  ('ap-diag-physc-mech_q27', 'ap-diag-physc-mech', 27, false, 'A', 'Diagnostic Test – Mechanics', 'medium', 'What is the effective spring constant of two identical springs (each of spring constant k) attached in series (end-to-end—see question 12)?', '["k/2","Inversely proportional to the square of the separation","k","(E) 2k","Zero net change under static equilibrium conditions"]'::jsonb, '(A) Because the springs are identical, they are stretched the same amount. (This is required in order for the tension force to be constant throughout the two springs. If the tension in the springs were not equal, there would be a net force on the point where the springs were connected together, causing the extension of the springs to change until the two tension forces were equal.) Therefore, when a mass attached to the two springs is moved a distance \Delta x, each spring is displaced by an amount \Delta x/2 such that the tension force within each spring is F = −k(\Delta x/2). This tension force is equal to the force the springs exert on the mass so that the effective spring constant is keffective = −F/\Delta x = k/2.'),
  ('ap-diag-physc-mech_q28', 'ap-diag-physc-mech', 28, false, 'D', 'Diagnostic Test – Mechanics', 'medium', 'If the angular velocity of a disk is given in rads by the equation \omega(t) = 9 + 3t, what is the disk’s angular displacement between t = 0 and t = 1 s?', '["0.75 rad","8.25 rad","9 rad","10.5 rad","18 rad"]'::jsonb, '(D) The angular velocity is proportional to t, thus the angular acceleration is constant. The angular displacement then obeys (see Chapter 6) with \omega0 = 9 and \alpha = 3. Thus, the angular displacement is (9)(1 − 0) + 0.5(3)(1) = 10.5 rad.'),
  ('ap-diag-physc-mech_q29', 'ap-diag-physc-mech', 29, false, 'D', 'Diagnostic Test – Mechanics', 'medium', 'You open a 1.0 m-wide door by pushing on the edge farthest from the hinge. You apply a force of 2.0 N at right angles to the front of the door. If you rotate the door 0.5 radians in 0.5 s, at what rate have you done work on the door?', '["−2.0 J/s","0.25 J/s","0.5 J/s","2.0 J/s","4.0 J/s"]'::jsonb, '(D) You exert a constant torque of (1.0 m)(2.0 N) sin 90° on the door. The rate at which you do work is given (for constant torque) by choice (D).'),
  ('ap-diag-physc-mech_q30', 'ap-diag-physc-mech', 30, false, 'C', 'Diagnostic Test – Mechanics', 'medium', 'A projectile is launched vertically upward into the air. When is the rate of work done by gravity on the projectile maximized?', '["at the top of the trajectory","right after the particle is launched","right before the particle hits the ground","The rate is constant.","The rate is zero."]'::jsonb, '(C) P = F \cdot  v. The force is the constant downward gravitational force, so the power is maximized when the downward velocity is maximized. This occurs just before the particle hits the ground, when it is moving with its maximum speed in the downward direction. Projectile motion is discussed in Chapter 2, and power is discussed in Chapter 4.'),
  ('ap-diag-physc-mech_q31', 'ap-diag-physc-mech', 31, false, 'C', 'Diagnostic Test – Mechanics', 'medium', 'A planet moves in an elliptical orbit around the sun (which is assumed to be stationary) as shown. What is the planet’s speed v2 at the point of closest approach to the sun?', '["v2 = v1","v2 = v1r2/r1","v2 = (v1r1 sin \\theta)/r2","v2 = (v1r1 cos \\theta)/v2","v2 = (v1r1 tan \\theta)/v2"]'::jsonb, '(C) The angular momentum around the sun is constant. Therefore, using the equation . Solving for v2 yields choice (C).'),
  ('ap-diag-physc-mech_q32', 'ap-diag-physc-mech', 32, false, 'A', 'Diagnostic Test – Mechanics', 'medium', 'The potential function for an object in a region of space where there are no nonconservative forces is U(x) = −ax2 + bx − c. What is the force on the object in this region?', '["F(x) = 2ax − b","F(x) = −2ax + b","F(x) = m(2ax − b)","F(x) = m(−2ax + b)","F(x) = (ax3/3) − (bx2/2) + cx"]'::jsonb, '(A) Using F (x) = −dU/dx, we see that choice (A) is correct.'),
  ('ap-diag-physc-mech_q33', 'ap-diag-physc-mech', 33, false, 'C', 'Diagnostic Test – Mechanics', 'medium', 'If an object’s position is given by the equation (where position is in meters and time is in seconds), what is the magnitude of the object’s velocity at time t = 1?', '["1 m/s","2 m/s","3 m/s","4 m/s","6 m/s"]'::jsonb, '(C) Velocity is the time derivative of position, and taking the derivative yields v(t) = 4 − t. Substituting t = 1 yields choice (C).'),
  ('ap-diag-physc-mech_q34', 'ap-diag-physc-mech', 34, false, 'D', 'Diagnostic Test – Mechanics', 'medium', 'Two particles are launched from the top of a building at the same time with the same initial speed. One particle is launched with an initial velocity pointing \theta degrees above the horizontal, while the other particle is launched with an initial velocity pointing \theta degrees below the horizontal, as shown. Discounting air resistance, which of the following is true?', '["Both particles spend the same amount of time in the air.","Both particles hit the ground the same distance from the building.","At any particular instant in time, both particles have the same potential energy.","Both particles hit the ground at the same angle of impact (i.e., when they hit the ground, their velocities are parallel).","none of the above"]'::jsonb, '(D) Conservation of energy requires that both particles have the same kinetic energy when they strike the ground. Because (on impact) they have the same x-component of velocity (their x-components of velocity are initially equal and remain unchanged) and the same initial speeds, they must have the same y-components of velocity (recall that . Therefore, they have the same velocity vector on impact and must also share the same angle of impact.'),
  ('ap-diag-physc-mech_q35', 'ap-diag-physc-mech', 35, false, 'D', 'Diagnostic Test – Mechanics', 'medium', 'A figure skater is spinning without friction when he extends his arms, moving his hands farther from his axis of rotation and decreasing his angular velocity by a factor of 2. Which of the following statements is true?', '["Both his angular momentum and kinetic energy are constant.","His angular momentum decreases by a factor of 2, while his kinetic energy remains constant.","His angular momentum remains constant, while his kinetic energy decreases by a factor of 4.","His angular momentum remains constant, while his kinetic energy decreases by a factor of 2.","His angular momentum remains constant, while his kinetic energy increases by a factor of 2. Free-Response Questions DIRECTIONS: Use separate sheets of paper to write down your answers to the free-response questions. You may refer to the formula sheet in the Appendix (pages 575–578). Mechanics Mechanics I A pulley consists of two cylinders of radii r and 2r (see figure) with a total rotational inertia of I. Mass m2 is sliding along a frictionless incline with an angle of elevation of \\theta. Assume that both m1 and m2 have the same mass m. (a) Given that the angular acceleration of the pulley is zero (and assuming that the rope rolls without slipping over the pulley), what is the angle of elevation of the inclined plane? (b) If the mass on the inclined plane is sliding down the ramp with a speed of v2, what is the speed of the hanging mass? Then the rope attaching m2 to the pulley is cut, causing the hanging mass to accelerate. (c) What is the acceleration of the hanging mass, m1, under these conditions? (d) What is the rate at which the kinetic energy of the pulley and m1 is changing? (Your answer can include the angular speed of the pulley, \\omega.) Mechanics II A roller coaster (mass m), starting from rest at a height h, goes around a circular loop of radius r as shown. (a) If the normal force exerted on the roller coaster by the track at the top of the loop is zero, what is the roller coaster’s speed at this point? (b) Assuming that the track is frictionless, at what height h must the roller coaster have started? (c) Draw a free-body diagram for the roller coaster as it passes point A. (d) What is the object’s speed as it passes through point A? Mechanics III A horizontal force F acts on a mass m sliding along an inclined plane with a coefficient of kinetic friction \\muk, as shown."]'::jsonb, '(D) The ice exerts no torque on the skater. (The normal force acts essentially at the axis of rotation and exerts zero torque.) Therefore, the skater’s angular momentum, L = I\omega, must remain constant (such that if the angular velocity decreases by a factor of 2, the moment of inertia must increase by a factor of 2). Given these changes, the kinetic energy, , decreases by a factor of 2. Free-Response Mechanics I (a) If the angular acceleration is zero, the acceleration of the two masses m1 and m2 must be zero (assuming that there’s no slippage of the rope so that the masses cannot accelerate while the pulley rotates at a constant angular speed). This means that the net forces on these objects must be zero, which requires that T1 = mg and T2 = mg sin \theta. Note that m1 = m2 =m. Because the angular acceleration is zero, the net torque must also be zero. Designating counterclockwise torque as positive (and using the tension forces just calculated), we find that the net torque on the pulley is given by This part involves Newton’s laws (Chapter 3) and their application to rotational motion. (b) For both m1 and m2, the equation v = r\omega (i.e., rolling without slipping of the rope) holds: (c) Applying Newton’s second law to the hanging mass (and designating downward acceleration as positive) yields Applying Newton’s second law to the pulley (and designating counterclockwise acceleration as positive such that both the linear and angular accelerations are positive), The final equation relates the linear acceleration of m1 and the angular acceleration of the pulley (expressing the fact that the pulley rolls without slipping on the rope connecting it to m1): a = r\alpha. Solving these three equations with three unknown variables for a yields (d) Because of conservation of energy, the rate at which the kinetic energy is changing is equal to the rate at which the potential energy is decreasing. Because the gravitational force is converting potential energy to kinetic energy, this rate is equal to the power exerted by gravity, given by the formula P = F \cdot  v. When the pulley is rotating with a speed of \omega, the speed of the falling mass is v = r\omega (downward, parallel to gravity), so the power is P = F \cdot  v = gmr\omega. For further discussion of work, power, and translational KE, see Chapter 4. Mechanics II (a) The normal force at the top of the loop is zero, so the radial force is equal to the gravitational force, which must equal the centripetal force: Solving for the speed, (b) Applying conservation of energy between the initial position of the roller coaster and the top of the loop (and using the previous result for the speed at the top of the loop), (c) The forces are shown in the figure (the construction of free-body diagrams is discussed in Chapter 3). (d) Using energy conservation again [comparing the initial position of the roller coaster at height 2.5r with point A at height r(1 − sin \theta)], Mechanics III (a) The free-body diagram is shown. (b) Because the block remains on the incline, the acceleration and the net force in the y-direction are zero: The acceleration is parallel to the x-direction: This yields two equations with three variables. The final equation is Ffr = \mukFN. Solving these equations for the acceleration yields (c) The block leaves the incline when it has a net force in the y-direction (and thus a net acceleration in the y-direction). Recall the equation for the net force in the y-direction: Imagine increasing F from zero. As the applied force F increases, the normal force decreases such that the net force in the y-direction is zero and the block remains on the incline. However, the normal force cannot decrease indefinitely because it can never be negative. The maximum force that can be applied without causing the block to leave the incline occurs when the normal force is equal to zero: Solving for F yields F = mg cot\theta. Any force greater than this would produce a positive acceleration in the y-direction, causing the block to accelerate away from the incline. For further information on the construction of free-body diagrams and solutions to inclined plane problems, see Chapter 3. (d) According to part (c), the maximum force is given by F = mg cot\theta. Thus, plot F on one axis and cot\theta on the other. (e) The figure shows the plot and the straight-line fit. The slope of the line is given approximately by: The answer to part (c) predicts that this value should be mg = (1)(9.8) N = 9.8 N.'),
  ('ap-diag-physc-em_q1', 'ap-diag-physc-em', 1, false, 'C', 'Diagnostic Test – E&M', 'medium', 'Two isolated metal spheres with radii r1 and r2 (with r1 != r2) carrying positive charges Q1 and Q2, respectively, are separated by a very large distance. A thin conducting wire is then connected between the two spheres. Under what condition will positive charge flow from sphere 1 to sphere 2?', '["Q1 > Q2","r1 > r2","Q1 / r1 > Q2 / r2","Q1 r1 > Q2 r2","Q1 / r1^2 > Q2 / r2^2"]'::jsonb, 'Positive charge flows spontaneously from a region of higher electric potential to one of lower electric potential. The electric potential at the surface of an isolated conducting sphere of radius r carrying charge Q is V = (1 / (4 pi epsilon_0)) * (Q / r). Thus, positive charge flows from sphere 1 to sphere 2 if and only if V1 > V2, which requires Q1 / r1 > Q2 / r2.'),
  ('ap-diag-physc-em_q2', 'ap-diag-physc-em', 2, false, 'C', 'Diagnostic Test – E&M', 'medium', 'A charged particle is launched into a region of uniform magnetic field. Given that the particle’s initial velocity has components parallel and perpendicular to the magnetic field, describe the particle’s path qualitatively.', '["a straight line","a circle","a helical (corkscrew) path","a parabolic path","none of the above"]'::jsonb, '(C) See the discussion of the motion of particles within magnetic fields in Chapter 14.'),
  ('ap-diag-physc-em_q3', 'ap-diag-physc-em', 3, false, 'B', 'Diagnostic Test – E&M', 'medium', 'A point charge +Q is located at a fixed distance from a sphere. Compare the magnitude of the attractive force on the sphere if the sphere is (1) metal with net charge −Q, (2) metal with no net charge, (3) an insulator with net charge −Q, or (4) an insulator with no net charge.', '["charged metal = charged insulator > uncharged metal = uncharged insulator","charged metal > charged insulator > uncharged metal > uncharged insulator","charged insulator > charged metal > uncharged insulator > uncharged metal","charged insulator > charged metal > uncharged metal = uncharged insulator","charged insulator = charged metal > uncharged metal > uncharged insulator"]'::jsonb, '(B) This is a question about electrostatic induction. When a positive point charge is placed near a metal sphere (charged or uncharged), it attracts mobile electrons in the metal, causing the metal to become polarized such that more negative charge is on the side closer to the positive point charge and more positive charge is on the side farther away from the positive point charge. Because this charge redistribution results in moving negative charge closer to the positive point charge and moving positive charge farther away from the positive point charge, it increases the attractive force between the metal sphere and the positive point charge. Therefore, compared to an insulating sphere with the same net charge, a metal sphere always experiences a greater attractive force (such that charged metal > charged insulator and uncharged metal > uncharged insulator). Because this inductive effect is small compared to the effect of actually putting net charge −Q on the sphere, charged insulator > uncharged metal.'),
  ('ap-diag-physc-em_q4', 'ap-diag-physc-em', 4, false, 'C', 'Diagnostic Test – E&M', 'medium', 'A bar moves to the right through a magnetic field as shown (the magnetic field points into the page). What is the potential difference between points A and B after equilibrium is established in the bar?', '["The difference is v2Bd, with A at higher potential.","The difference is v2Bd, with B at higher potential.","The difference is vBd, with A at higher potential.","The difference is vBd, with B at higher potential.","Because there is no loop, there is no flux, so there is no potential difference."]'::jsonb, '(C) The charges within the bar are moving in a magnetic field, so they feel a magnetic force. Using the conventional-current description, positive charges feel an upward force, causing positive charge to accumulate at the top end of the bar (leaving negative charge on the bottom end of the bar). This charge continues to accumulate until the electric force created by the charge imbalance cancels the magnetic force, mathematically qE = qvB. The magnitude of the potential difference between points A and B is then |V | = |−\int E \cdot  dr| = |vBd|. The electric field points down (producing a force that opposes the magnetic force), and recalling that electric fields point from high to low potential, we see that point A is at the higher potential. Further information about motional EMF can be found in Example 15.6.'),
  ('ap-diag-physc-em_q5', 'ap-diag-physc-em', 5, false, 'B', 'Diagnostic Test – E&M', 'medium', 'Suppose that the electric potential in a region of space is a constant. What can you conclude about the magnitude of the electric field in this region?', '["It is a nonzero constant.","It is zero.","It is proportional to the distance from the origin.","It is given by","Not enough information is given. Questions 6–8 refer to a circular region of radius a with a uniform magnetic field pointing into the page that is increasing at a constant rate as shown. A circular loop of wire of radius r is centered on the middle of the region."]'::jsonb, '(B) The electric field is the negative derivative (with respect to the spatial coordinate) of the potential. Thus, with a constant potential the electric field must be zero.'),
  ('ap-diag-physc-em_q6', 'ap-diag-physc-em', 6, false, 'C', 'Diagnostic Test – E&M', 'medium', 'Which of the graphs plots the induced EMF in the circular loop of wire shown as a function of its radius r?', '["Directly proportional to the parameter (linear scaling)","Inversely proportional to the square of the separation","Equal in magnitude with opposite orientation","Independent of the mass and charge distribution","Zero net change under static equilibrium conditions"]'::jsonb, '(C) The induced EMF depends on the change in the enclosed flux. Because the magnetic field is uniform, and changing at a constant rate, Therefore, the induced EMF depends only on the area of the loop through which magnetic field lines pass. For r < a, the area within the loop exposed to the magnetic field is simply the entire area of the loop, which is a quadratic function, A(r) = \pir2. For r > a, increasing r does not increase the area exposed to the magnetic field (because the magnetic field through the additional area is zero). Therefore, for r > a the induced EMF is constant. The only graph that is quadratic for small r and then constant is choice (C). Calculating the induced EMF due to a magnetic field via Faraday’s law is discussed in Chapter 15.'),
  ('ap-diag-physc-em_q7', 'ap-diag-physc-em', 7, false, 'A', 'Diagnostic Test – E&M', 'medium', 'Given that the resistance of the loop is proportional to the loop’s circumference, which of the graphs seen in question 6 shows the induced current as a function of the radius of the loop?', '["Directly proportional to the parameter (linear scaling)","Inversely proportional to the square of the separation","Equal in magnitude with opposite orientation","Independent of the mass and charge distribution","Zero net change under static equilibrium conditions"]'::jsonb, '(A) The current is given by I = V/R. The resistance is proportional to the circumference of the loop, 2\pir. Therefore, for r < a, V is proportional to r2 (as discussed in question 6), and 1/R is proportional to 1/r, so the product I = V/R is proportional to r (and thus its graph is a line passing through the origin). For r > a, V is constant and 1/R is proportional to 1/r, so the product I = V/R is inversely proportional to r. The only graph that satisfies these conditions is choice (A).'),
  ('ap-diag-physc-em_q8', 'ap-diag-physc-em', 8, false, 'A', 'Diagnostic Test – E&M', 'medium', 'A long straight cylindrical wire of radius a carries a constant total current I uniformly distributed over its circular cross section. What is the magnetic field magnitude B(r) as a function of radial distance r from the central axis of the wire for both r <= a and r >= a?', '["B(r) is proportional to r for r <= a, and proportional to 1/r for r >= a","B(r) is zero for r <= a, and proportional to 1/r for r >= a","B(r) is proportional to 1/r for r <= a, and constant for r >= a","B(r) is proportional to r^2 for r <= a, and proportional to 1/r^2 for r >= a","B(r) is constant everywhere inside and outside the wire"]'::jsonb, 'Apply Ampere''s Law oint B * dl = mu_0 I_enc. Inside the wire (r <= a), the enclosed current is I_enc = I * (pi r^2) / (pi a^2) = I (r/a)^2, giving B(2 pi r) = mu_0 I (r/a)^2 ==> B(r) = (mu_0 I / (2 pi a^2)) * r (linear in r). Outside the wire (r >= a), the entire current I is enclosed, so B(2 pi r) = mu_0 I ==> B(r) = mu_0 I / (2 pi r) (inversely proportional to r).'),
  ('ap-diag-physc-em_q9', 'ap-diag-physc-em', 9, false, 'E', 'Diagnostic Test – E&M', 'medium', 'The magnitude of the electric field inside a uniformly charged spherical shell', '["depends on the charge of the shell","depends on the radius of the shell","depends on the distance from the center of the sphere, r","depends on the permittivity of free space, \\epsilon0","is equal to the integral , where is a unit vector pointing from the differential charge dQ to the point in space (within the sphere) where the electric field is being measured"]'::jsonb, '(E) According to Gauss’s law (Chapter 11), the electric field inside a hollow sphere is zero because there is no enclosed charge. Therefore, the electric field is independent of the charge of the shell, the radius of the shell, the radial coordinate, and the permittivity of free space; it is zero inside the sphere independent of any of these factors. Note that the electric field is always given by the equation in choice (E).'),
  ('ap-diag-physc-em_q10', 'ap-diag-physc-em', 10, false, 'C', 'Diagnostic Test – E&M', 'medium', 'If a battery requires an amount of time t to charge a capacitor to 60% of its final charge, what is the time constant of the circuit?', '["\\tau = −t/ln(0.6)","\\tau = −ln(0.6)/t","\\tau = −t/ln(0.4)","\\tau = −ln(0.4)/t","cannot be determined from the information given"]'::jsonb, '(C) Setting the equation for Q(t) equal to 60% of its final value yields Solving for \tau then yields choice (C). Further information about RC circuits can be found in Chapter 13.'),
  ('ap-diag-physc-em_q11', 'ap-diag-physc-em', 11, false, 'C', 'Diagnostic Test – E&M', 'medium', 'Which of the following statements is true of the integral', '["It is equal to the potential at point B minus the potential at point A.","It is always independent of the path taken from point A to point B.","It is the work per unit charge that the electric field performs as a charge moves from point A to point B.","It is the work per unit charge that an external force performs to move a charge from point A to point B.","It is the induced EMF."]'::jsonb, '(C) Choice (A) is false: The potential at point B minus the potential at point A is given by . Choice (B) is false: The integral is path independent only if there is no time-dependent magnetic field present. A time-dependent magnetic field produces a nonconservative electric force with no associated potential function. Choice (C) is true: The work per charge is the opposite of the change in potential energy per charge, which is equal to because of conservation of energy. Choice (D) is false: The work done by an arbitrary external force does not necessarily have any relationship with the electric field. Although choice (E) is true in the special case that the integral is a closed loop, that is, point A = point B, it is not true in general.'),
  ('ap-diag-physc-em_q12', 'ap-diag-physc-em', 12, false, 'A', 'Diagnostic Test – E&M', 'medium', 'A wire loop lies partially in a region where the magnetic field points out of the page, as shown. If the magnetic field is increasing in magnitude, what is the direction of the force on the loop?', '["to the right","to the left","out of the page","toward the top of the page","The force is zero."]'::jsonb, '(A) The current induced in the loop moves in a clockwise direction to oppose the increasing outward flux through the loop according to Lenz’s law. The movement of this current through the top and bottom of the loop creates opposing forces that cancel each other, while the current flowing through the left side of the loop produces a force to the right, which equals the net force on the loop.'),
  ('ap-diag-physc-em_q13', 'ap-diag-physc-em', 13, false, 'E', 'Diagnostic Test – E&M', 'medium', 'Suppose that, rather than an electron orbiting a proton as in atoms, an electron and a positron (a particle with the same mass as an electron and of opposite charge) orbit each other as shown. In terms of the magnitude of the charge on an electron e and the radius of the orbit r, what is the total kinetic energy of the system?', '["KE = e/4\\pi\\epsilon0r","KE = e 2/4\\pi\\epsilon0r 2","KE = −e 2/8\\pi\\epsilon0r 2","KE = −e 2/8\\pi\\epsilon0r","KE = e 2/16\\pi\\epsilon0r"]'::jsonb, '(E) Without doing a lot of work, we can eliminate most of the choices. First, kinetic energy is always positive, so choices (C) and (D) are incorrect. Second, recall the formula for the potential energy of two point charges separated by a distance r (ignoring signs for now): Comparing the form of this expression with our choices allows us to eliminate choices (A), (B), and (C) simply on the basis of dimensional analysis. This leaves us only choice (E), which a detailed calculation shows is the correct answer.'),
  ('ap-diag-physc-em_q14', 'ap-diag-physc-em', 14, false, 'C', 'Diagnostic Test – E&M', 'medium', 'Which one of Maxwell’s equations states the experimental result that physicists have been unable to discover a magnetic monopole?', '["Directly proportional to the parameter (linear scaling)","Inversely proportional to the square of the separation","Equal in magnitude with opposite orientation","Independent of the mass and charge distribution","Zero net change under static equilibrium conditions"]'::jsonb, '(C) See Chapter 15 for a further explanation of Gauss’s law for magnetism.'),
  ('ap-diag-physc-em_q15', 'ap-diag-physc-em', 15, false, 'C', 'Diagnostic Test – E&M', 'medium', 'Two resistors with different resistances are connected in parallel. Which of the following statements is correct about this resistor network?', '["The equivalent resistance is greater than the resistance of either of the resistors alone.","The equivalent resistance is in between the resistance of either of the resistors alone.","The equivalent resistance is smaller than the resistance of either of the resistors alone.","The same amount of current passes through both resistors.","The equivalent resistance depends on the potential difference across the resistor array."]'::jsonb, '(C) The equivalent resistance is given by Because the denominator is greater than 1/R1 or 1/R2 alone, the entire fraction must be smaller than R1 or R2 alone. Choice (D) is incorrect: The voltage across the resistors must be the same because they are arranged in parallel. Therefore, the current through either resistor is given by the equation I = V/R. Because the resistors have different resistances, different amounts of current flow through them. The equivalent resistance of a resistor array is a physical property of the array itself (just as the resistance of a resistor is a physical property of the resistor). So it is independent of the voltage across the array (assuming, of course, that the resistors obey Ohm’s law), eliminating choice (E).'),
  ('ap-diag-physc-em_q16', 'ap-diag-physc-em', 16, false, 'D', 'Diagnostic Test – E&M', 'medium', 'The uniform electric field established between the parallel plates of a capacitor has magnitude E. A particle with a charge of magnitude q is released (initially at rest) somewhere between the plates and allowed to move a distance d under the influence of the capacitor’s electric field. What is the change in the particle’s electrostatic potential energy?', '["\\Delta U = q 2Ed","\\Delta U = −q 2Ed","\\Delta U = qEd","\\Delta U = −qEd","none of the above"]'::jsonb, '(D) First, what is the sign of the change in energy? This is a tricky question because the actual sign of the charge and the direction of the electric field are not given. Recall that Fx = −dU/dx. Thus, a particle always feels a force pushing it in a direction that decreases the particle’s potential energy. Therefore, whether the particle’s charge is positive or negative, it always moves in such a way as to decrease its potential energy, so \Delta U < 0. This eliminates choices (A) and (C). What, then, is the magnitude of the decrease in potential energy? The key equation here is \Delta U = q\Delta V (based on the definition of potential). Because the particle moves parallel or antiparallel to the electric field (depending on the sign of its charge), |\Delta V| = |−\int E ⋅ dr| = Ed. Therefore, the magnitude of the change in potential energy is |\Delta U| = |q\Delta V| = qEd, so choice (D) is correct.'),
  ('ap-diag-physc-em_q17', 'ap-diag-physc-em', 17, false, 'B', 'Diagnostic Test – E&M', 'medium', 'An isolated solid metal sphere contains a net charge of +Q. The magnitude of the electric field outside the sphere a distance d from the center of the sphere', '["cannot be calculated because there isn’t appropriate symmetry to apply Gauss’s law","is equal to Q/4\\pi\\epsilon0d 2","is equal to Q/2\\pi\\epsilon0d 2","is equal to","none of the above"]'::jsonb, '(B) Based on Gauss’s law, the enclosed charge term, qenclosed, depends only on the magnitude of the charge and the symmetry properties of its distribution. Therefore, the field produced outside a charged sphere is the same as the field that would be produced if all the charge of the sphere were concentrated at a point at the sphere’s center (which is easily calculated using Coulomb’s law). Note that a charge placed on an isolated conducting sphere distributes itself uniformly across the surface of the sphere. Further information about Gauss’s law and the distribution of a stationary charge along conductors is given in Chapter 11.'),
  ('ap-diag-physc-em_q18', 'ap-diag-physc-em', 18, false, 'C', 'Diagnostic Test – E&M', 'medium', 'Point P is located within a cylindrical wire that carries current into the page with uniform current density as shown. What is the direction of the magnetic field at point P?', '["The magnetic field is zero because of symmetry.","toward the top of the page","toward the bottom of the page","into the page","out of the page"]'::jsonb, '(C) According to Ampere’s law and the symmetry of the situation, the only current that determines the magnetic field at point P is the current enclosed within the amperian path shown. (Thus, the current in region B has no effect on the magnetic field at point P.) The magnetic field produced at P due to the enclosed current is the same as the field produced by a thin wire along the axis of the cylinder, carrying the same current (since the magnetic field depends on only the enclosed current as long as it has cylindrical symmetry). The right-hand rule indicates that this imaginary current-carrying wire would produce a downward magnetic field at point P, so the same downward magnetic field must be produced by the entire cylinder of current featured in this problem. For further information about calculating magnetic fields using Ampere’s law, see Chapter 14.'),
  ('ap-diag-physc-em_q19', 'ap-diag-physc-em', 19, false, 'E', 'Diagnostic Test – E&M', 'medium', 'A point charge +q lies at the origin. Which of the following charge distributions could yield a zero net electric field (due to the +q charge and the charge distribution) at the point (0, 1)? I. a single charge −Q with a smaller magnitude than +q, lying at some point along the x-axis II. a single charge −q with a magnitude equal to +q, lying at some point along the x-axis III. a single charge −Q with a larger magnitude than +q, lying at some point along the x-axis IV. two charges both with charge −Q and with larger magnitudes than +q, lying at points along the x-axis', '["II","III","IV","I and III","II and IV"]'::jsonb, '(E) Distribution I will not be able to cancel the electric field due to +q because it will never be able to achieve a high enough magnitude. Distribution II will work: Simply locate the charge −q at the origin, and it will exactly cancel out the field due to +q over all space. Distribution III will never be able to have an equal magnitude and opposite direction compared to the field due to +q; in order to have an opposite direction the negative charge must be located at the origin, but at this point it will produce a stronger magnitude field than the field due to +q. Distribution IV will definitely work. The charges must be located equal distances from the origin in order for the field due to both of the negative charges to be in the direction opposite the field due to +q. By moving the charges as far away from the origin as is necessary, the magnitude of the field can be adjusted so as to exactly equal the magnitude of the field due to +q.'),
  ('ap-diag-physc-em_q20', 'ap-diag-physc-em', 20, false, 'D', 'Diagnostic Test – E&M', 'medium', 'After closing the switch in the figure, the current I passing through the inductor increases from zero. What is the Kirchhoff’s loop equation describing this situation?', '["V + IR + L(dI/dt) = 0","V + IR − L(dI/dt) = 0","V − IR + L(dI/dt) = 0","V − IR − L(dI/dt) = 0","none of the above"]'::jsonb, '(D) Tracing the loop in the counterclockwise direction: (1) The sign of the potential jump of the battery is positive because we are going from the negative terminal to the positive terminal. (2) The sign of the potential jump across the resistor is negative because we are going in the same direction as the current. (3) The sign of the potential jump across the inductor is negative. Why? The inductor opposes the increase in the current, so its potential jump must be of the opposite sign compared to that of the battery (which has a positive potential jump). Because current is increasing, dI/dt > 0, we must add a negative sign to the term −L(dI/dt) in order for it to have an overall negative value as required.'),
  ('ap-diag-physc-em_q21', 'ap-diag-physc-em', 21, false, 'A', 'Diagnostic Test – E&M', 'medium', 'A battery has an EMF of 9 V and an internal resistance of 20 Ω. For what value of current will the terminal voltage across the battery be 7 V?', '["0.1 A","0.2 A","5 A","10 A","40 A"]'::jsonb, '(A) When the terminal voltage is 7 V, that means that there is a 2 V potential drop across the internal resistance. Applying Ohm’s law to the internal resistance yields I = V/R = 2 V/20 Ω = 0.1 A.'),
  ('ap-diag-physc-em_q22', 'ap-diag-physc-em', 22, false, 'B', 'Diagnostic Test – E&M', 'medium', 'Consider a parallel plate vacuum capacitor. If the plate area and the separation distance are doubled and a dielectric with dielectric constant kD = 2 is inserted between the plates, completely filling the space, the capacitance will', '["increase by a factor of 4","increase by a factor of 2","remain the same","decrease by a factor of 2","decrease by a factor of 4"]'::jsonb, '(B) This problem relies on manipulation of the equation for the capacitance of a parallel plate capacitor with a dielectric, C = \epsilon0AkD /d. Increasing A, d, and kD by factors of 2 results in a net increase of capacitance by a factor of 2.'),
  ('ap-diag-physc-em_q23', 'ap-diag-physc-em', 23, false, 'C', 'Diagnostic Test – E&M', 'medium', 'How much work is done by the electric field on a point charge of magnitude q moved from infinity into the center of a hollow spherical shell of radius R that has a uniform charge density and a net charge of +Q? (Assume there is a very small hole in the shell that allows the point charge to enter but does not disturb the spherical symmetry of the charge distribution.)', '["W = qQ/4\\pi\\epsilon0R","W = qQ/4\\pi\\epsilon0R2","W = −qQ/4\\pi\\epsilon0R","The work is undefined.","The work is zero because of symmetry considerations."]'::jsonb, '(C) The potential at the surface of the sphere is the same as if all of the charge were concentrated at the center of the sphere, that is, V = (1/4\pi\epsilon0)(Q/R) as discussed in question 1. The electric field inside the sphere is zero, so the potential at the center is the same as its value at the surface. The work done by the electric field to move the charge from infinity to this point is the negative of the change in the charge’s potential energy: W = −\Delta U = −q\Delta V = −q(Q/4\pi\epsilon0R).'),
  ('ap-diag-physc-em_q24', 'ap-diag-physc-em', 24, false, 'D', 'Diagnostic Test – E&M', 'medium', 'Which unit is equal to one ohm divided by one henry?', '["coulomb","joule","second","second−1","farad"]'::jsonb, '(D) The time constant of an RL circuit is \tau = L/R. Because the time constant has units of time, R/L has the units of inverse time. Alternative solution: Recall that the voltage drop across an inductor is V = L(dI/dt) and the voltage drop across a resistor is V = IR. Equating the units of voltage in these two formulas, V = IR = L(dI/dt). Manipulating this formula in terms of units yields L/R = I(dt/dI) = t. (Note that dt has the units of time and dI has the units of current.)'),
  ('ap-diag-physc-em_q25', 'ap-diag-physc-em', 25, false, 'D', 'Diagnostic Test – E&M', 'medium', 'Suppose that when a charge is moved through a region of space with an electric field (which is produced by stationary charges), the field does no net work on the charge. Which of the following statements must be true?', '["The electric force is nonconservative.","The charge’s path begins and ends along the same electric field line.","The charge is moved along an equipotential line.","The charge’s path begins and ends on an equipotential line of the same value.","Electric fields never do work on charges."]'::jsonb, '(D) Choice (C) would indeed result in no net work being done (no work is required to move a charge along an equipotential line). However, this is not required for zero net work to be done: It is possible that the field might perform negative work along part of the charge’s path and positive work along the remainder of the path such that the net work is zero. The only condition that is required for zero net work to be done is for the charge to begin and end at the same voltage (so that the change in potential energy is zero, based on the definition of voltage), so choice (D) is correct. Choice (E) is true of magnetic fields, not electric fields, since the magnetic force is always perpendicular to the velocity. Choice (B) won’t generally satisfy the requirement that no net work be done, except in the special case that the path begins and ends at the same point on the field line. In fact, any path that begins and ends at different points along an electric field line must have involved the electric field performing net work because electric field lines are parallel to the electric field and such movement causes a change in voltage. Choice (A) is false: Stationary charges always produce conservative electric forces.')
ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.ap_exam_questions (id, test_id, question_order, is_frq, answer_key, topic, difficulty, question_text, options_json, explanation)
VALUES
  ('ap-diag-physc-em_q26', 'ap-diag-physc-em', 26, false, 'E', 'Diagnostic Test – E&M', 'medium', 'Consider a long solenoid with length l, radius r, and n turns per length. What effect will doubling the radius (while holding l and n constant) have on the inductance of the solenoid?', '["Inductance will decrease by a factor of 4.","Inductance will decrease by a factor of 2.","Inductance will not change.","Inductance will increase by a factor of 2.","Inductance will increase by a factor of 4."]'::jsonb, '(E) Based on the formula for the magnetic field within a solenoid (which can be quickly derived using Ampere’s law), B = \mu0nI. Because the radius of the solenoid does not enter this equation, changing the radius will not affect the strength of the magnetic field within the solenoid. However, because increasing the radius will increase the area of each loop of the solenoid by a factor of 4 (because A = \pir2), it will increase the magnetic flux through each loop by a factor of 4 and thus increase the inductance by a factor of 4.'),
  ('ap-diag-physc-em_q27', 'ap-diag-physc-em', 27, false, 'C', 'Diagnostic Test – E&M', 'medium', 'In the circuit shown, if the potential at point A is chosen to be zero, what is the potential at point B?', '["4 V","2.5 V","−2.5 V","−4 V","none of the above"]'::jsonb, '(C) First, constructing and solving a Kirchhoff’s loop equation indicates that the current is 0.5 A flowing in a clockwise direction. More specifically, one valid loop equation is (choosing to designate positive current in the clockwise direction and traversing the loop in the clockwise direction): (Alternatively, you could realize that the circuit has an equivalent resistance of 4 Ω and an equivalent voltage of 2 V, so applying Ohm’s law indicates that the current is 0.5 A.) Then, to calculate the potential at point B, begin at point A and sum potential jumps as you move to point B. (Use either of the possible two pathways. If the current is solved correctly, you should obtain the same answer either way.) For example, moving along the bottom of the circuit yields'),
  ('ap-diag-physc-em_q28', 'ap-diag-physc-em', 28, false, 'A', 'Diagnostic Test – E&M', 'medium', 'What is the power dissipated in the resistors in the circuit introduced in question 27?', '["Directly proportional to the parameter (linear scaling)","2 W","Equal in magnitude with opposite orientation","Independent of the mass and charge distribution","16 W Questions 29–31 refer to the circuits shown in the figure, in which all the batteries are identical."]'::jsonb, '(A) The power dissipated by a resistor is P = I2R, and because the resistors are in series, they share a common current. Therefore, the power dissipated by all the resistors is . (Note that this is equivalent to realizing that the equivalent resistance of the circuit is 4 Ω and plugging this value into the formula P = I2R.)'),
  ('ap-diag-physc-em_q29', 'ap-diag-physc-em', 29, false, 'B', 'Diagnostic Test – E&M', 'medium', 'In which circuit is the equivalent resistance the greatest?', '["Directly proportional to the parameter (linear scaling)","Inversely proportional to the square of the separation","Equal in magnitude with opposite orientation","Independent of the mass and charge distribution","Zero net change under static equilibrium conditions"]'::jsonb, '(B) Application of the equivalent resistance formulas reveals that the equivalent resistance of circuits (A) through (E) are 3 Ω, 6 Ω, 4 Ω, 2 Ω, and 5 Ω.'),
  ('ap-diag-physc-em_q30', 'ap-diag-physc-em', 30, false, 'D', 'Diagnostic Test – E&M', 'medium', 'In which of the five circuits is the total power dissipated by all the resistors the greatest?', '["Directly proportional to the parameter (linear scaling)","Inversely proportional to the square of the separation","Equal in magnitude with opposite orientation","Independent of the mass and charge distribution","Zero net change under static equilibrium conditions"]'::jsonb, '(D) The most useful formula to use here for power is P = V2/R. The circuit with the lowest equivalent resistance dissipates the greatest total power. (Remember, the voltage is the same for all circuits.)'),
  ('ap-diag-physc-em_q31', 'ap-diag-physc-em', 31, false, 'B', 'Diagnostic Test – E&M', 'medium', 'A real battery of EMF V0 and non-zero internal resistance r is connected across an external load resistor R. Under which of the following conditions is the potential difference across the load resistor R minimized?', '["When R is much greater than r (R >> r)","When R is much smaller than r (R << r)","When R equals r (R = r)","When R is disconnected (open circuit)","The potential difference is always equal to V0 regardless of R"]'::jsonb, 'The current in the single loop circuit is I = V0 / (R + r). The potential difference across the load resistor R is V_R = I R = V0 * R / (R + r) = V0 / (1 + r/R). As R approaches 0 (R << r), V_R approaches 0. When R >> r, V_R approaches V0. Therefore, V_R is minimized when R << r.'),
  ('ap-diag-physc-em_q32', 'ap-diag-physc-em', 32, false, 'D', 'Diagnostic Test – E&M', 'medium', 'Which of the following statements is a necessary condition for the electric flux through a gaussian surface to be zero?', '["The electric field is zero along the entire gaussian surface.","The component of the field perpendicular to the gaussian surface is zero along the entire gaussian surface.","There is no charge enclosed within the gaussian surface.","There is no net charge enclosed within the gaussian surface.","Although the above conditions would cause the flux to be zero, they are not required for the flux to be zero."]'::jsonb, '(D) According to Gauss’s law, the electric flux is proportional to the net enclosed charge, so if the flux is zero, the net enclosed charge must be zero. The conditions in choices (A) and (B) would cause the local flux at any point on the gaussian surface to be zero, which would indeed cause the net flux to be zero. However, these are not required for the net flux to be zero: It is possible that positive flux through part of the gaussian surface cancels negative flux through another part of the gaussian surface such that the net flux is zero. Like choices (A) and (B), choice (C) would indeed result in the flux through the gaussian surface being zero. However, again this is not required for the net flux to be zero. It would be possible for a surface to enclose equal amounts of positive and negative charge such that the net enclosed charge and flux are zero. Further discussion of Gauss’s law can be found in Chapter 11.'),
  ('ap-diag-physc-em_q33', 'ap-diag-physc-em', 33, false, 'D', 'Diagnostic Test – E&M', 'medium', 'Two moving particles of equal mass enter a region of uniform magnetic field pointing out of the page, causing them to move as shown. Which of the following is true about the charges on these particles?', '["|QA| > |QB|, QA > 0","|QA| > |QB|, QA < 0","|QA| < |QB|, QA > 0","|QA| < |QB|, QA < 0","none of the above"]'::jsonb, '(D) The magnetic field provides the centripetal force for the circular motion shown, according to the equation qvB = mv2/r. Because v, m, and B are fixed and shared by both particles, q is inversely proportional to r. Therefore, particle B, which follows a path of smaller radius, must have a greater magnitude of charge. The sign of the charge on particle A can be determined by the right-hand rule to be negative. (Remember that the centripetal force must always point toward the center of the arc.)'),
  ('ap-diag-physc-em_q34', 'ap-diag-physc-em', 34, false, 'D', 'Diagnostic Test – E&M', 'medium', 'Given the electric field vector at a point in space, which of the following can be determined? I. the direction of the electric force on any charged particle (of unspecified charge) at that point II. the magnitude of the electric force on a particle with a charge of magnitude Q at that point III. the rate of change of the potential with respect to position in the direction parallel to the field IV. the rate of change of the potential with respect to position in the direction perpendicular to the field', '["I and II","III and IV","I, III, and IV","II, III, and IV","I, II, III, and IV"]'::jsonb, '(D) I is false: The direction of the force depends on the sign of the charge on the particle, so it cannot be known for any charged particle. II is true: The magnitude of the electric force is simply |F| = QE. III is true: The rate of change in voltage with respect to position in the direction parallel to the field is simply E, with potential decreasing in the direction of the field. IV is true: The rate of change in potential with respect to position in the direction perpendicular to the field is always equal to zero.'),
  ('ap-diag-physc-em_q35', 'ap-diag-physc-em', 35, false, 'C', 'Diagnostic Test – E&M', 'medium', 'A parallel-plate capacitor with plate area A and separation d is charged to a potential difference V0 by a battery. The battery is then disconnected. A slab of dielectric material with dielectric constant kappa > 1 is now inserted, completely filling the space between the plates. What are the new capacitance C and stored energy U compared to their initial values C0 and U0?', '["C = kappa C0, and U = kappa U0","C = C0 / kappa, and U = U0 / kappa","C = kappa C0, and U = U0 / kappa","C = C0, and U = U0","C = kappa C0, and U = U0"]'::jsonb, 'When a dielectric of constant kappa is inserted, the capacitance increases by a factor of kappa: C = kappa C0. Because the capacitor is disconnected from the battery, the charge Q remains constant (Q = Q0). The stored energy is U = Q^2 / (2C) = Q0^2 / (2 kappa C0) = U0 / kappa. Thus, capacitance increases by kappa and stored energy decreases by a factor of kappa (the electrostatic field does work pulling the dielectric slab into the plates).'),
  ('ap-topic-physc-forces_q1', 'ap-topic-physc-forces', 1, false, 'E', 'Newton''s Third Law Action-Reaction Pairs', 'easy', 'A box is dragged across a table at constant velocity by a string. Which of the following is a Newton''s Third Law action-reaction pair?  I. Force of gravity on the box and normal force on the box  II. Force of friction on the box and tension force on the box  III. Force of the box pushing down on the table and normal force of the table pushing up on the box', '["I only","I and II only","II only","I and III only","III only"]'::jsonb, '(E) An action-reaction pair must involve mutual forces of the same physical type exerted between the same two objects in opposite directions. The table pushing up on the box ($F_{N,\text{table on box}}$) and the box pushing down on the table ($F_{N,\text{box on table}}$) act on each other, satisfying Newton''s Third Law.'),
  ('ap-topic-physc-forces_q2', 'ap-topic-physc-forces', 2, false, 'D', 'Apparent Weight in Accelerating Elevator', 'easy', 'A person who weighs $800\text{ N}$ steps onto a scale in an elevator accelerating upward at $5\text{ m/s}^2$. What will the scale read? ($g = 10\text{ m/s}^2$)', '["$400\\text{ N}$","$800\\text{ N}$","$1,000\\text{ N}$","$1,200\\text{ N}$","$1,600\\text{ N}$"]'::jsonb, '(D) Mass $m = \frac{W}{g} = \frac{800\text{ N}}{10\text{ m/s}^2} = 80\text{ kg}$.
Using Newton''s Second Law with upward as positive:
  $$F_N - mg = ma \implies F_N = m(g + a) = (80\text{ kg})(10 + 5\text{ m/s}^2) = 1,200\text{ N}$$'),
  ('ap-topic-physc-forces_q3', 'ap-topic-physc-forces', 3, false, 'D', 'Two-Block System Tension & Acceleration', 'medium', 'A horizontal force of $20\text{ N}$ pulls on a $1.0\text{ kg}$ block attached by a string to a $2.0\text{ kg}$ block on a frictionless surface. Find the tension $F_T$ in the connecting string and the acceleration $a$ of the system.', '["$F_T = 13.3\\text{ N},\\ a = 10\\text{ m/s}^2$","$F_T = 13.3\\text{ N},\\ a = 20\\text{ m/s}^2$","$F_T = 15\\text{ N},\\ a = 6.7\\text{ m/s}^2$","$F_T = 13.3\\text{ N},\\ a = 6.7\\text{ m/s}^2$","$F_T = 20\\text{ N},\\ a = 6.7\\text{ m/s}^2$"]'::jsonb, '(D) System acceleration:
  $$a = \frac{F_{\text{applied}}}{m_1 + m_2} = \frac{20\text{ N}}{1.0 + 2.0\text{ kg}} = \frac{20}{3}\text{ m/s}^2 \approx 6.7\text{ m/s}^2$$
Tension pulling the trailing $2.0\text{ kg}$ block:
  $$F_T = m_2 a = (2.0\text{ kg})\left(\frac{20}{3}\text{ m/s}^2\right) = \frac{40}{3}\text{ N} \approx 13.3\text{ N}$$'),
  ('ap-topic-physc-forces_q4', 'ap-topic-physc-forces', 4, false, 'C', 'Three-Block Frictionless Train Tensions', 'medium', 'A force of $20\text{ N}$ pulls a chain of three frictionless blocks: $3.0\text{ kg} - 2.0\text{ kg} - 1.0\text{ kg}$. Find $F_{T1}$ (between $1\text{ kg}$ and $2\text{ kg}$), $F_{T2}$ (between $2\text{ kg}$ and $3\text{ kg}$), and acceleration $a$.', '["$F_{T1} = 1\\text{ N},\\ a = 1\\text{ m/s}^2$","$F_{T1} = 3.3\\text{ N},\\ F_{T2} = 3.3\\text{ N},\\ a = 3.3\\text{ m/s}^2$","$F_{T1} = 3.3\\text{ N},\\ F_{T2} = 10\\text{ N},\\ a = 3.3\\text{ m/s}^2$","$F_{T1} = 3.3\\text{ N},\\ F_{T2} = 10\\text{ N},\\ a = 6.7\\text{ m/s}^2$","$F_{T1} = 10\\text{ N},\\ a = 6.7\\text{ m/s}^2$"]'::jsonb, '(C) $$a = \frac{20\text{ N}}{1.0 + 2.0 + 3.0\text{ kg}} = \frac{20}{6}\text{ m/s}^2 = 3.33\text{ m/s}^2$$
$$F_{T1} = m_1 a = (1.0\text{ kg})(3.33\text{ m/s}^2) = 3.33\text{ N}$$
$$F_{T2} = (m_1 + m_2)a = (1.0 + 2.0\text{ kg})(3.33\text{ m/s}^2) = 10.0\text{ N}$$'),
  ('ap-topic-physc-forces_q5', 'ap-topic-physc-forces', 5, false, 'A', 'Incline Static Friction Threshold', 'medium', 'A box is placed at rest on a ramp ($\theta = 30^\circ, \mu_s = 0.5$). The box will:', '["Accelerate down the ramp","Accelerate briefly then stop","Move with constant velocity","Not move","Cannot be determined"]'::jsonb, '(A) Downhill gravitational component: $F_{g,\parallel} = mg\sin(30^\circ) = 0.50\,mg$.
Maximum static friction force:
  $$f_{s,\max} = \mu_s mg\cos(30^\circ) = (0.5)(0.866\,mg) \approx 0.433\,mg$$
Because $F_{g,\parallel} > f_{s,\max}$ ($0.50\,mg > 0.433\,mg$), the net force down the ramp is positive, and the box will accelerate down.'),
  ('ap-topic-physc-forces_q6', 'ap-topic-physc-forces', 6, false, 'C', 'Atwood Machine System Acceleration', 'easy', 'In an Atwood machine with hanging masses $m$ and $4m$, find the acceleration.', '["$0.2g$","$0.4g$","$0.6g$","$0.75g$","$0.8g$"]'::jsonb, '(C) $$a = \frac{m_2 - m_1}{m_1 + m_2}g = \frac{4m - m}{4m + m}g = \frac{3}{5}g = 0.6g$$'),
  ('ap-topic-physc-forces_q7', 'ap-topic-physc-forces', 7, false, 'E', 'Two-Block System with Kinetic Friction', 'medium', 'A $20\text{ N}$ force pulls a $2.0\text{ kg}$ block connected to a $1.0\text{ kg}$ trailing block on a rough surface ($\mu_k = 0.2$). Find the tension $F_T$ and acceleration $a$ ($g = 10\text{ m/s}^2$).', '["$F_T = 1\\text{ N},\\ a = 4.7\\text{ m/s}^2$","$F_T = 3.3\\text{ N},\\ a = 4.7\\text{ m/s}^2$","$F_T = 3.3\\text{ N},\\ a = 6.7\\text{ m/s}^2$","$F_T = 6.7\\text{ N},\\ a = 6.7\\text{ m/s}^2$","$F_T = 6.7\\text{ N},\\ a = 4.7\\text{ m/s}^2$"]'::jsonb, '(E) Total friction force: $f_{\text{total}} = \mu_k(m_1 + m_2)g = (0.2)(3.0\text{ kg})(10\text{ m/s}^2) = 6\text{ N}$.
Acceleration: $a = \frac{F - f_{\text{total}}}{m_1 + m_2} = \frac{20 - 6}{3} = \frac{14}{3} \approx 4.7\text{ m/s}^2$.
Tension on the $1.0\text{ kg}$ trailing block:
  $$F_T - f_1 = m_1 a \implies F_T = (0.2)(1.0)(10) + (1.0)(4.7) = 2.0 + 4.7 = 6.7\text{ N}$$'),
  ('ap-topic-physc-forces_q8', 'ap-topic-physc-forces', 8, false, 'B', 'Incline Gravitational Scaling', 'easy', 'An object slides down a frictionless incline of angle $\theta$ and reaches final speed $v$. On a planet with twice Earth''s gravitational acceleration ($2g$), what will be its final speed?', '["$v$","$\\sqrt{2}v$","$2v$","$4v$","$v/2$"]'::jsonb, '(B) Using $v = \sqrt{2ad} = \sqrt{2(g\sin\theta)d} \propto \sqrt{g}$. Doubling $g$ scales the final velocity by a factor of $\sqrt{2}$, giving $v_{\text{new}} = \sqrt{2}v$.'),
  ('ap-topic-physc-forces_q9', 'ap-topic-physc-forces', 9, false, 'B', 'Vertical Loop Minimum Cut-Off Speed', 'medium', 'A roller coaster completes a loop of radius $r = 25\text{ m}$. What is the minimum speed needed at the top of the loop for riders to remain safely in contact with the track? ($g = 10\text{ m/s}^2$)', '["$12.7\\text{ m/s}$","$15.8\\text{ m/s}$","$21.2\\text{ m/s}$","$29.3\\text{ m/s}$","$33.3\\text{ m/s}$"]'::jsonb, '(B) At critical cut-off speed, normal force $F_N \to 0$, so gravity alone provides the centripetal acceleration:
  $$\frac{mv^2}{r} = mg \implies v = \sqrt{gr} = \sqrt{(10\text{ m/s}^2)(25\text{ m})} = \sqrt{250} \approx 15.8\text{ m/s}$$'),
  ('ap-topic-physc-forces_q10', 'ap-topic-physc-forces', 10, false, 'B', 'Vertical Circular Motion Bottom Tension', 'medium', 'A bucket of mass $3\text{ kg}$ on a $0.6\text{-m}$ rope is whirled in a vertical circle. At the lowest point, the tension is $50\text{ N}$. What is the speed of the bucket? ($g = 10\text{ m/s}^2$)', '["$1\\text{ m/s}$","$2\\text{ m/s}$","$3\\text{ m/s}$","$4\\text{ m/s}$","$5\\text{ m/s}$"]'::jsonb, '(B) At the bottom of the circle: $F_T - mg = \frac{mv^2}{r}$.
  $$50\text{ N} - (3\text{ kg})(10\text{ m/s}^2) = \frac{3 v^2}{0.6} \implies 20 = 5v^2 \implies v^2 = 4 \implies v = 2\text{ m/s}$$'),
  ('ap-topic-physc-forces_q11', 'ap-topic-physc-forces', 11, false, 'C', 'Vertical String Circle Critical Apex Speed', 'medium', 'What is the critical speed at the top of the vertical circle for the bucket in Question 10 so the rope does not become slack?', '["$0.6\\text{ m/s}$","$1.8\\text{ m/s}$","$2.4\\text{ m/s}$","$3.2\\text{ m/s}$","$4.8\\text{ m/s}$"]'::jsonb, '(C) $$v_{\text{critical}} = \sqrt{gr} = \sqrt{(10\text{ m/s}^2)(0.60\text{ m})} = \sqrt{6.0} \approx 2.45\text{ m/s} \approx 2.4\text{ m/s}$$'),
  ('ap-topic-physc-forces_q12', 'ap-topic-physc-forces', 12, false, 'D', 'Centripetal Acceleration & Angular Frequency', 'easy', 'An object moves at constant speed in a circular path of radius $r$ at 1 revolution per second. What is its centripetal acceleration?', '["$2\\pi r$","$2\\pi^2 r$","$2\\pi^2 r^2$","$4\\pi^2 r$","$4\\pi^2 r^2$"]'::jsonb, '(D) With angular frequency $\omega = 2\pi f = 2\pi(1) = 2\pi\text{ rad/s}$:
  $$a_c = \omega^2 r = (2\pi)^2 r = 4\pi^2 r$$'),
  ('ap-topic-physc-momentum_q1', 'ap-topic-physc-momentum', 1, false, 'C', 'Momentum-Kinetic Energy Relationship', 'easy', 'An object of mass $2\text{ kg}$ has a linear momentum of magnitude $6\text{ kg}\cdot\text{m/s}$. What is this object''s kinetic energy?', '["$3\\text{ J}$","$6\\text{ J}$","$9\\text{ J}$","$12\\text{ J}$","$18\\text{ J}$"]'::jsonb, '(C) Using the relation between kinetic energy and momentum ($K = \frac{p^2}{2m}$):
  $$K = \frac{(6\text{ kg}\cdot\text{m/s})^2}{2(2\text{ kg})} = \frac{36}{4} = 9\text{ J}$$'),
  ('ap-topic-physc-momentum_q2', 'ap-topic-physc-momentum', 2, false, 'D', 'Impulse-Momentum Theorem & Velocity Change', 'easy', 'An object of mass $8\text{ kg}$ is moving in a straight line. If the impulse calculated from the force-time graph ($J = \int F\,dt$) is $80\text{ N}\cdot\text{s}$, what is its change in speed?', '["$16\\text{ m/s}$","$14\\text{ m/s}$","$12\\text{ m/s}$","$10\\text{ m/s}$","$8\\text{ m/s}$"]'::jsonb, '(D) By the Impulse-Momentum Theorem ($J = \Delta p = m\Delta v$):
  $$\Delta v = \frac{J}{m} = \frac{80\text{ N}\cdot\text{s}}{8\text{ kg}} = 10\text{ m/s}$$'),
  ('ap-topic-physc-momentum_q3', 'ap-topic-physc-momentum', 3, false, 'E', 'Average Impact Force Calculation', 'easy', 'A box of mass $2\text{ kg}$ accelerates in a straight line from $4\text{ m/s}$ to $8\text{ m/s}$ due to a force applied for $0.5\text{ s}$. Find the average strength of this force.', '["$4\\text{ N}$","$8\\text{ N}$","$12\\text{ N}$","$14\\text{ N}$","$16\\text{ N}$"]'::jsonb, '(E) $$F_{\text{avg}} = \frac{m(v_f - v_i)}{\Delta t} = \frac{(2\text{ kg})(8\text{ m/s} - 4\text{ m/s})}{0.5\text{ s}} = \frac{8}{0.5} = 16\text{ N}$$'),
  ('ap-topic-physc-momentum_q4', 'ap-topic-physc-momentum', 4, false, 'D', 'Elastic Wall Rebound Impulse', 'easy', 'A ball of mass $m$ traveling horizontally with velocity $v$ strikes a massive vertical wall and rebounds back along its original direction with no change in speed. What is the magnitude of the impulse delivered by the wall to the ball?', '["$0$","$\\frac{1}{2}mv$","$mv$","$2mv$","$4mv$"]'::jsonb, '(D) Taking the rebound direction as positive:
$$J = |\Delta p| = |m(v) - m(-v)| = 2mv$$'),
  ('ap-topic-physc-momentum_q5', 'ap-topic-physc-momentum', 5, false, 'B', '1D Perfectly Inelastic Collision Velocity', 'medium', 'Two objects, one of mass $3\text{ kg}$ moving at $2\text{ m/s}$ and the other of mass $5\text{ kg}$ moving at $2\text{ m/s}$ toward each other, collide head-on in a perfectly inelastic collision. Find the speed of the objects after the collision.', '["$0.25\\text{ m/s}$","$0.5\\text{ m/s}$","$0.75\\text{ m/s}$","$1.0\\text{ m/s}$","$2.0\\text{ m/s}$"]'::jsonb, '(B) Using conservation of linear momentum:
$$m_1 v_1 + m_2 v_2 = (m_1 + m_2)v_f \implies (3)(2) + (5)(-2) = (3 + 5)v_f$$
$$6 - 10 = 8v_f \implies v_f = -\frac{4}{8} = -0.5\text{ m/s}$$
The resulting speed is $0.5\text{ m/s}$.'),
  ('ap-topic-physc-momentum_q6', 'ap-topic-physc-momentum', 6, false, 'D', 'Static Equilibrium & Center of Mass', 'medium', 'A student balances a meter stick at its midpoint ($50\text{ cm}$). Given hanging masses $m_1 = 6\text{ kg}$ at $x = 0\text{ cm}$ and $m_2 = 2\text{ kg}$ at $x = 100\text{ cm}$, how far from the left edge should a third mass $m_3 = 10\text{ kg}$ be hung to balance the meter stick?', '["$40\\text{ cm}$","$50\\text{ cm}$","$60\\text{ cm}$","$70\\text{ cm}$","$80\\text{ cm}$"]'::jsonb, '(D) For rotational static equilibrium about the midpoint ($50\text{ cm}$), the system center of mass must equal $50\text{ cm}$ ($0.50\text{ m}$):
  $$x_{\text{cm}} = \frac{m_1 x_1 + m_2 x_2 + m_3 x_3}{m_1 + m_2 + m_3} \implies 0.50 = \frac{6(0) + 2(1.0) + 10 x_3}{6 + 2 + 10}$$
  $$0.50(18) = 2.0 + 10 x_3 \implies 9.0 - 2.0 = 10 x_3 \implies x_3 = 0.70\text{ m} = 70\text{ cm}$$'),
  ('ap-topic-physc-momentum_q7', 'ap-topic-physc-momentum', 7, false, 'C', 'Collision Elasticity & Momentum Conservation', 'easy', 'Two objects move toward each other, collide, and separate. If there was no net external force acting on the objects, but some kinetic energy was lost, then:', '["the collision was elastic and momentum was conserved","the collision was elastic and momentum was not conserved","the collision was not elastic and total linear momentum was conserved","the collision was not elastic and momentum was not conserved","Cannot be determined"]'::jsonb, '(C) With zero net external force ($F_{\text{net}} = 0$), linear momentum is strictly conserved in all collisions. Because mechanical kinetic energy is lost, the collision is by definition inelastic.'),
  ('ap-topic-physc-momentum_q8', 'ap-topic-physc-momentum', 8, false, 'B', 'U-Shaped Rod Assembly Center of Mass', 'medium', 'Three thin, uniform rods each of length $L$ are arranged in an inverted U-shape. The two vertical arms each have mass $m$; the horizontal top rod has mass $2m$. How far below the midpoint of the horizontal rod is the center of mass of the assembly?', '["$L/8$","$L/4$","$3L/8$","$L/2$","$3L/4$"]'::jsonb, '(B) Set $y = 0$ at the horizontal rod. The center of mass of the top rod ($2m$) is at $y = 0$. Each vertical rod ($m$) has its center of mass at $y = -L/2$.
  $$y_{\text{cm}} = \frac{(2m)(0) + m(-L/2) + m(-L/2)}{2m + m + m} = \frac{-mL}{4m} = -\frac{1}{4}L$$
The assembly center of mass is located a distance of $L/4$ below the horizontal rod.'),
  ('ap-topic-physc-momentum_q9', 'ap-topic-physc-momentum', 9, false, 'B', 'Inelastic Collision & Post-Crash Friction Work', 'medium', 'A car of mass $1,000\text{ kg}$ collides head-on with a truck of mass $2,000\text{ kg}$, both initially moving at $21\text{ m/s}$. The collision is perfectly inelastic. After the crash, the combined wreckage skids to a halt. How much work is done by friction after the crash?', '["$73,500\\text{ J}$","$-73,500\\text{ J}$","$147,000\\text{ J}$","$-147,000\\text{ J}$","$220,500\\text{ J}$"]'::jsonb, '(B) Post-collision velocity:
$$v_f = \frac{(2000)(21) + (1000)(-21)}{2000 + 1000} = \frac{21000}{3000} = 7\text{ m/s}$$
Kinetic energy immediately after collision:
$$K = \frac{1}{2}(3000\text{ kg})(7\text{ m/s})^2 = 1500(49) = 73,500\text{ J}$$
Work done by friction to stop the wreckage ($W_f = \Delta K = 0 - K$):
$$W_f = -73,500\text{ J}$$'),
  ('ap-topic-physc-momentum_q10', 'ap-topic-physc-momentum', 10, false, 'D', 'Inelastic Collision Rebound Return Time', 'hard', 'Block 1 ($m_1 = 2\text{ kg}$, $v_1 = 3\text{ m/s}$) and Block 2 ($m_2 = 5\text{ kg}$, $v_2 = 2\text{ m/s}$ towards block 1) start $1\text{ m}$ apart and collide perfectly inelastically. How long after collision does it take to return to one of the starting positions?', '["$0.55\\text{ s}$","$0.75\\text{ s}$","$0.95\\text{ s}$","$1.05\\text{ s}$","$1.25\\text{ s}$"]'::jsonb, '(D) Time to collide: $t_{\text{coll}} = \frac{1\text{ m}}{3 + 2\text{ m/s}} = 0.2\text{ s}$. Collision point is $d_1 = (3)(0.2) = 0.6\text{ m}$ from Block 1''s start.  
Post-collision velocity:
$$v_f = \frac{(2)(3) + (5)(-2)}{2 + 5} = \frac{-4}{7}\text{ m/s}$$
Distance back to Block 1''s starting position is $0.6\text{ m}$.  
Time: $t = \frac{0.6\text{ m}}{4/7\text{ m/s}} = \frac{4.2}{4} = 1.05\text{ s}$.'),
  ('ap-topic-physc-energy_q1', 'ap-topic-physc-energy', 1, false, 'A', 'Perpendicular Force & Zero Work', 'easy', 'A force $F = 20\text{ N}$ acts on a $3\text{ kg}$ object as it moves $4\text{ m}$. If $F$ is perpendicular to displacement, the work done is:', '["$0\\text{ J}$","$60\\text{ J}$","$80\\text{ J}$","$600\\text{ J}$","$2,400\\text{ J}$"]'::jsonb, '(A) $W = \vec{F}\cdot\vec{d} = F d \cos(90^\circ) = 0\text{ J}$. Any force acting strictly perpendicular to displacement performs zero work.'),
  ('ap-topic-physc-energy_q2', 'ap-topic-physc-energy', 2, false, 'B', 'Work-Energy Theorem & Kinetic Energy Change', 'easy', 'An object of mass $4\text{ kg}$ accelerates from $3\text{ m/s}$ to $6\text{ m/s}$ in $8\text{ s}$. How much work was done on the object?', '["$27\\text{ J}$","$54\\text{ J}$","$72\\text{ J}$","$96\\text{ J}$","Cannot be determined"]'::jsonb, '(B) By the Work-Energy Theorem ($W = \Delta K$):
  $$W = \frac{1}{2}m(v_f^2 - v_i^2) = \frac{1}{2}(4\text{ kg})((6\text{ m/s})^2 - (3\text{ m/s})^2) = 2(36 - 9) = 2(27) = 54\text{ J}$$'),
  ('ap-topic-physc-energy_q3', 'ap-topic-physc-energy', 3, false, 'D', 'Path Independence & Conservative Gravitational Work', 'easy', 'To lift a box of mass $m$ to height $h$, three options are considered: Option 1 (direct lift), Option 2 ($30^\circ$ incline), Option 3 (pulley system). Compare the work done under ideal conditions.', '["Option 1 > Option 2 > Option 3","Option 1 > Option 2 = Option 3","Option 1 = Option 3 > Option 2","Option 1 = Option 2 = Option 3","Cannot be determined"]'::jsonb, '(D) Under ideal conservative conditions, gravitational potential energy is path-independent. In all three methods, the minimum work required is identical: $W = mgh$.'),
  ('ap-topic-physc-energy_q4', 'ap-topic-physc-energy', 4, false, 'E', 'Normal Force Zero Work on Incline', 'easy', 'A $4\text{ kg}$ box is pulled up a ramp ($\theta = 30^\circ$, height $= 5\text{ m}$) at constant velocity. How much work is done by the normal force?', '["$160\\text{ J}$","$80\\text{ J}$","$40\\text{ J}$","$20\\text{ J}$","$0\\text{ J}$"]'::jsonb, '(E) The normal force is perpendicular to the ramp surface and therefore perpendicular to the displacement vector of the box ($\theta = 90^\circ$). Thus $W_N = F_N d \cos(90^\circ) = 0\text{ J}$.'),
  ('ap-topic-physc-energy_q5', 'ap-topic-physc-energy', 5, false, 'A', 'Elevator Normal Force Positive Work', 'easy', 'A man stands in an elevator as it begins to ascend. Does the normal force from the floor do work on the man?', '["Yes, and the work done will be positive.","Yes, and the work done will be negative.","Yes, but the sign cannot be determined.","No, the normal force does no work in any situation.","No, normal force does no work here."]'::jsonb, '(A) The normal force pushes upward on the man''s feet ($\vec{F}_N$ is upward) and the elevator displacement is upward ($\vec{d}$ is upward). Because $\vec{F}_N$ and $\vec{d}$ point in the same direction ($\theta = 0^\circ$), $W = F_N d \cos(0^\circ) > 0$ (positive work).'),
  ('ap-topic-physc-energy_q6', 'ap-topic-physc-energy', 6, false, 'D', 'Frictionless Incline Energy Conservation', 'medium', 'A $3.5\text{ kg}$ block slides from rest down a frictionless $30^\circ$ incline of length $6.4\text{ m}$. What is its speed at the bottom? ($g = 10\text{ m/s}^2$)', '["$5.0\\text{ m/s}$","$5.7\\text{ m/s}$","$6.4\\text{ m/s}$","$8.0\\text{ m/s}$","$10\\text{ m/s}$"]'::jsonb, '(D) Vertical height of the incline: $h = L\sin(30^\circ) = 6.4(0.5) = 3.2\text{ m}$.
By Conservation of Energy:
  $$mgh = \frac{1}{2}mv^2 \implies v = \sqrt{2gh} = \sqrt{2(10)(3.2)} = \sqrt{64} = 8.0\text{ m/s}$$'),
  ('ap-topic-physc-energy_q7', 'ap-topic-physc-energy', 7, false, 'D', 'Incline Kinetic Energy with Friction Work', 'medium', 'A block of mass $m$ slides from rest down an incline of length $s$ and height $h$ with kinetic friction force $F_f$. What is the kinetic energy at the bottom?', '["$mgh$","$mgh - F_f h$","$mgs - F_f h$","$mgh - F_f s$","$mgs - F_f s$"]'::jsonb, '(D) Applying the Work-Energy Theorem ($E_i + W_{\text{other}} = E_f$):
  $$mgh - F_f s = K_f \implies K_f = mgh - F_f s$$'),
  ('ap-topic-physc-energy_q8', 'ap-topic-physc-energy', 8, false, 'C', 'Newton''s Second Law with Aerodynamic Drag', 'easy', 'A $1,000\text{ kg}$ car accelerates at $4\text{ m/s}^2$ while experiencing $300\text{ N}$ of drag force. How much force must the engine produce?', '["$16,300\\text{ N}$","$15,700\\text{ N}$","$4,300\\text{ N}$","$4,000\\text{ N}$","$3,700\\text{ N}$"]'::jsonb, '(C) $$F_{\text{engine}} - F_{\text{drag}} = ma \implies F_{\text{engine}} = ma + F_{\text{drag}} = (1,000\text{ kg})(4\text{ m/s}^2) + 300\text{ N} = 4,300\text{ N}$$'),
  ('ap-topic-physc-energy_q9', 'ap-topic-physc-energy', 9, false, 'E', 'Gravitational Free Fall Midpoint Speed Fraction', 'medium', 'An astronaut drops a rock from the top of a crater. When the rock is halfway down to the bottom, its speed is what fraction of its impact speed?', '["$1/4$","$1/2$","$1/\\sqrt{3}$","$\\sqrt{2}/3$","$1/\\sqrt{2}$"]'::jsonb, '(E) At halfway point $h/2$, kinetic energy is half the final kinetic energy: $K_{\text{mid}} = \frac{1}{2}K_{\text{final}}$.
  $$\frac{1}{2}mv_{\text{mid}}^2 = \frac{1}{2}\left(\frac{1}{2}mv_{\text{final}}^2\right) \implies v_{\text{mid}} = \frac{1}{\sqrt{2}}v_{\text{final}}$$'),
  ('ap-topic-physc-energy_q10', 'ap-topic-physc-energy', 10, false, 'D', 'Incline Constant Power Mechanical Derivation', 'hard', 'A block of mass $m$ is pulled up a ramp of length $d$, angle $\theta$, and friction coefficient $\mu$ by a motor at constant power in time $t$. What is the minimum power $P$?', '["$\\frac{md^2}{t^3} + \\frac{mgd\\sin\\theta}{t}$","$\\frac{md^2}{t^2} + mgd\\sin\\theta + \\mu mgd\\cos\\theta$","$\\frac{md^2}{t^3} + \\frac{mgd\\sin\\theta}{t} + \\frac{\\mu mgd\\cos\\theta}{t}$","$\\frac{2md^2}{t^3} + \\frac{mgd\\sin\\theta}{t} + \\frac{\\mu mgd\\cos\\theta}{t}$","$\\frac{md^2}{2t^3} + \\frac{mgd\\cos\\theta}{t} + \\frac{\\mu mgd\\sin\\theta}{t}$"]'::jsonb, '(D) Under constant acceleration to cover distance $d$ in time $t$, $d = \frac{1}{2}at^2 \implies a = \frac{2d}{t^2}$ and $v_f = \frac{2d}{t}$.
Total work done:
  $$W = \Delta K + \Delta U + W_{\text{friction}} = \frac{1}{2}m\left(\frac{2d}{t}\right)^2 + mg(d\sin\theta) + (\mu mg\cos\theta)d$$
Power $P = W/t$:
  $$P = \frac{2md^2}{t^3} + \frac{mgd\sin\theta}{t} + \frac{\mu mgd\cos\theta}{t}$$'),
  ('ap-topic-physc-gravitation_q1', 'ap-topic-physc-gravitation', 1, false, 'E', 'Inverse-Square Gravitational Force Scaling', 'medium', 'An object at altitude $2R_E$ above Earth''s surface experiences gravitational force $F_0$. If its altitude is doubled ($4R_E$), what is the new gravitational force?', '["$F_0/4$","$F_0/25$","$2F_0$","$4F_0/9$","$9F_0/25$"]'::jsonb, '(E) Initial orbital radius from Earth''s center: $r_1 = R_E + 2R_E = 3R_E$.  
Final orbital radius: $r_2 = R_E + 4R_E = 5R_E$.
  $$\frac{F_2}{F_1} = \frac{r_1^2}{r_2^2} = \frac{(3R_E)^2}{(5R_E)^2} = \frac{9}{25} \implies F_2 = \frac{9}{25}F_0$$'),
  ('ap-topic-physc-gravitation_q2', 'ap-topic-physc-gravitation', 2, false, 'E', 'Invariance of Mass & Altitude Weight', 'easy', 'An object has weight $w$ on Earth''s surface. At a height $2R_E$ above the surface, its mass and weight are:', '["mass $m/2$, weight $w/2$","mass $m$, weight $w/2$","mass $m/2$, weight $w/4$","mass $m$, weight $w/4$","mass $m$, weight $w/9$"]'::jsonb, '(E) Mass is invariant ($m$). Distance from Earth''s center is $r = R_E + 2R_E = 3R_E$.
  $$w_{\text{new}} = \frac{GMm}{(3R_E)^2} = \frac{1}{9}\left(\frac{GMm}{R_E^2}\right) = \frac{w}{9}$$'),
  ('ap-topic-physc-gravitation_q3', 'ap-topic-physc-gravitation', 3, false, 'C', 'Newton''s Third Law Mutual Gravity', 'easy', 'A moon of mass $m$ orbits a planet of mass $100m$. If $F_1$ is the force of the planet on the moon and $F_2$ is the force of the moon on the planet, which is true?', '["$F_1 = 100 F_2$","$F_2 = 100 F_1$","$F_1 = F_2$","$F_1 = 10 F_2$","$F_1 = F_2/100$"]'::jsonb, '(C) By Newton''s Third Law, gravitational interaction forces are mutual, equal in magnitude, and opposite in direction ($F_1 = F_2$).'),
  ('ap-topic-physc-gravitation_q4', 'ap-topic-physc-gravitation', 4, false, 'B', 'Planet Surface Gravity & Minimum Radius', 'medium', 'What is the minimum radius of a planet with Earth''s mass so that surface gravity does not exceed $4g$?', '["$R_E/4$","$R_E/2$","$R_E$","$2R_E$","$4R_E$"]'::jsonb, '(B) $$g_{\text{planet}} = \frac{GM}{R^2} \le 4\frac{GM}{R_E^2} \implies \frac{1}{R^2} \le \frac{4}{R_E^2} \implies R \ge \frac{R_E}{2}$$'),
  ('ap-topic-physc-gravitation_q5', 'ap-topic-physc-gravitation', 5, false, 'B', 'Interior vs Exterior Earth Gravitational Comparison', 'hard', 'How far above Earth''s surface must an object be to feel the same gravity as when buried halfway to the center ($r = R_E/2$)?', '["$R_E/2$","$(\\sqrt{2} - 1)R_E$","$\\sqrt{2}R_E$","$2R_E$","$(\\sqrt{2} + 1)R_E$"]'::jsonb, '(B) Gravity inside Earth at $r = R_E/2$: $g_{\text{inside}} = g_0\left(\frac{R_E/2}{R_E}\right) = \frac{1}{2}g_0$.  
Gravity outside Earth at altitude $h$: $g_{\text{outside}} = g_0\left(\frac{R_E}{R_E + h}\right)^2$.
  $$\left(\frac{R_E}{R_E + h}\right)^2 = \frac{1}{2} \implies \frac{R_E + h}{R_E} = \sqrt{2} \implies h = (\sqrt{2} - 1)R_E$$'),
  ('ap-topic-physc-rotation_q1', 'ap-topic-physc-rotation', 1, false, 'B', 'Angular to Linear Speed on Rim', 'easy', 'A disc of radius $6\text{ cm}$ rotates at an angular speed of $5\text{ rev/s}$. What is the linear speed of a point on the rim?', '["$0.3\\text{ m/s}$","$1.9\\text{ m/s}$","$7.4\\text{ m/s}$","$52\\text{ m/s}$","$83\\text{ m/s}$"]'::jsonb, '(B) $$\omega = 5\text{ rev/s} \times 2\pi\text{ rad/rev} = 10\pi\text{ rad/s} \approx 31.42\text{ rad/s}$$
$$v = r\omega = (0.06\text{ m})(10\pi\text{ rad/s}) = 0.6\pi \approx 1.88\text{ m/s} \approx 1.9\text{ m/s}$$'),
  ('ap-topic-physc-rotation_q2', 'ap-topic-physc-rotation', 2, false, 'E', 'Rotational Arc Distance over Time', 'easy', 'For the same disc in Question 1, what is the total distance traveled by a point on the rim in $40\text{ min}$?', '["$180\\text{ m}$","$360\\text{ m}$","$540\\text{ m}$","$720\\text{ m}$","$4.5\\text{ km}$"]'::jsonb, '(E) $$t = 40\text{ min} \times 60\text{ s/min} = 2,400\text{ s}$$
$$s = vt = (0.6\pi\text{ m/s})(2400\text{ s}) = 1440\pi\text{ m} \approx 4,524\text{ m} \approx 4.5\text{ km}$$'),
  ('ap-topic-physc-rotation_q3', 'ap-topic-physc-rotation', 3, false, 'D', 'Constant Angular Acceleration Speed', 'easy', 'A disc starts from rest and undergoes angular acceleration of $4\text{ rad/s}^2$. At an angular displacement of $50\text{ rad}$, what is its angular speed?', '["$12\\text{ rad/s}$","$15\\text{ rad/s}$","$16\\text{ rad/s}$","$20\\text{ rad/s}$","$23\\text{ rad/s}$"]'::jsonb, '(D) $$\omega_f^2 = \omega_0^2 + 2\alpha\Delta\theta = 0 + 2(4\text{ rad/s}^2)(50\text{ rad}) = 400 \implies \omega_f = 20\text{ rad/s}$$')
ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.ap_exam_questions (id, test_id, question_order, is_frq, answer_key, topic, difficulty, question_text, options_json, explanation)
VALUES
  ('ap-topic-physc-rotation_q4', 'ap-topic-physc-rotation', 4, false, 'D', 'Centripetal Acceleration in Circular Motion', 'medium', 'A rope of length $0.75\text{ m}$ has a mass $m = 0.1\text{ kg}$ spun at $120\text{ rpm}$. What centripetal acceleration is the mass experiencing?', '["$19,200\\text{ m/s}^2$","$10,800\\text{ m/s}^2$","$90\\text{ m/s}^2$","$12\\pi^2\\text{ m/s}^2$","$3\\pi\\text{ m/s}^2$"]'::jsonb, '(D) $$\omega = 120\text{ rpm} \times \frac{2\pi\text{ rad}}{60\text{ s}} = 4\pi\text{ rad/s}$$
$$a_c = \omega^2 r = (4\pi)^2 (0.75\text{ m}) = 16\pi^2(0.75) = 12\pi^2\text{ m/s}^2$$'),
  ('ap-topic-physc-rotation_q5', 'ap-topic-physc-rotation', 5, false, 'B', 'Seesaw Torque Balance & Net Torque', 'easy', 'Joe ($60\text{ kg}$) and Alice ($30\text{ kg}$) sit on opposite ends of a level $4\text{-m}$ seesaw supported at its midpoint. What is the net torque magnitude ($g = 10\text{ m/s}^2$)?', '["$300\\text{ N}\\cdot\\text{m}$","$600\\text{ N}\\cdot\\text{m}$","$900\\text{ N}\\cdot\\text{m}$","$1,200\\text{ N}\\cdot\\text{m}$","$1,500\\text{ N}\\cdot\\text{m}$"]'::jsonb, '(B) Lever arm from fulcrum to each end: $r = 2\text{ m}$.
  $$\tau_{\text{net}} = (m_{\text{Joe}} g)r - (m_{\text{Alice}} g)r = (60)(10)(2) - (30)(10)(2) = 1200 - 600 = 600\text{ N}\cdot\text{m}$$'),
  ('ap-topic-physc-rotation_q6', 'ap-topic-physc-rotation', 6, false, 'D', 'Pendulum Gravitational Torque', 'medium', 'What is the torque about a pendulum suspension point produced by a bob ($m = 0.50\text{ kg}$) at length $L = 0.80\text{ m}$ at angle $\theta = 30^\circ$ ($g = 10\text{ m/s}^2$)?', '["$0.5\\text{ N}\\cdot\\text{m}$","$1.0\\text{ N}\\cdot\\text{m}$","$1.7\\text{ N}\\cdot\\text{m}$","$2.0\\text{ N}\\cdot\\text{m}$","$3.4\\text{ N}\\cdot\\text{m}$"]'::jsonb, '(D) $$\tau = mg L \sin\theta = (0.50\text{ kg})(10\text{ m/s}^2)(0.80\text{ m})\sin(30^\circ) = 4.0(0.5) = 2.0\text{ N}\cdot\text{m}$$'),
  ('ap-topic-physc-rotation_q7', 'ap-topic-physc-rotation', 7, false, 'B', 'Meter Stick Fulcrum Static Equilibrium', 'medium', 'A uniform meter stick of mass $1\text{ kg}$ is supported at its $50\text{ cm}$ midpoint. A mass $m = 3\text{ kg}$ hangs at $x = 0\text{ cm}$. At what mass $M$ hanging at $x = 80\text{ cm}$ will the stick remain horizontal?', '["$3\\text{ kg}$","$5\\text{ kg}$","$6\\text{ kg}$","$8\\text{ kg}$","$9\\text{ kg}$"]'::jsonb, '(B) Torque balance about the $50\text{ cm}$ fulcrum:
$$m(50\text{ cm} - 0\text{ cm}) = M(80\text{ cm} - 50\text{ cm}) \implies (3\text{ kg})(50\text{ cm}) = M(30\text{ cm})$$
$$M = \frac{150}{30} = 5\text{ kg}$$'),
  ('ap-topic-physc-rotation_q8', 'ap-topic-physc-rotation', 8, false, 'A', 'Discrete Point Mass Rotational Inertia', 'easy', 'Four point masses $m$ are located at the vertices of a rectangle of side lengths $L$ and $3L$. What is the rotational inertia about an axis passing through the center perpendicular to the plane, where each mass is at distance $L$ from the axis?', '["$4mL^2$","$\\frac{8}{3}mL^2$","$\\frac{5}{2}mL^2$","$\\frac{7}{3}mL^2$","$\\frac{10}{3}mL^2$"]'::jsonb, '(A) $$I = \sum m_i r_i^2 = 4(m L^2) = 4mL^2$$'),
  ('ap-topic-physc-rotation_q9', 'ap-topic-physc-rotation', 9, false, 'C', 'Rolling vs Sliding Kinetic Energy Comparison', 'medium', 'A block of mass $m$ slides down a frictionless incline of height $h$. A solid cylinder of mass $m$ rolls down an incline of height $H$ without slipping. If both reach the bottom with equal kinetic energies, what is the relation between $h$ and $H$?', '["$h = \\frac{4}{3}H$","$h = \\frac{3}{4}H$","$h = H$","$h = \\frac{1}{2}H$","$h = 2H$"]'::jsonb, '(C) For the block, $K_{\text{bottom}} = mgh$. For the rolling cylinder, total kinetic energy (translational + rotational) equals potential energy lost: $K_{\text{bottom}} = mgH$. For equal kinetic energies, $mgh = mgH \implies h = H$.'),
  ('ap-topic-physc-rotation_q10', 'ap-topic-physc-rotation', 10, false, 'D', 'Static Friction Torque in Rolling Motion', 'easy', 'When a cylinder rolls down an incline without slipping, which force produces the torque causing angular rotation?', '["Parallel component of gravity","Perpendicular component of gravity","Normal force","Force of static friction","Force of kinetic friction"]'::jsonb, '(D) Gravity acts at the center of mass ($r = 0$), and the normal force passes directly through the center of mass ($\vec{r} \parallel \vec{F}_N \implies \tau = 0$). Static friction acts tangentially at the contact point without slipping, exerting torque $\tau = f_s R$.'),
  ('ap-topic-physc-shm_q1', 'ap-topic-physc-shm', 1, false, 'D', 'Simple Harmonic Motion Characteristics', 'easy', 'Which of the following are characteristics of simple harmonic motion?  I. Acceleration is constant.  II. Restoring force is proportional to displacement.  III. Frequency is independent of amplitude.', '["II only","I and II only","I and III only","II and III only","I, II, and III"]'::jsonb, '(D) In SHM, acceleration $a(x) = -\omega^2 x$ varies with displacement, so I is false. By Hooke''s Law, $F = -kx$ (II is true), and angular frequency $\omega = \sqrt{k/m}$ does not depend on amplitude $A$ (III is true).'),
  ('ap-topic-physc-shm_q2', 'ap-topic-physc-shm', 2, false, 'B', 'Mass-Spring Oscillator Frequency Maximization', 'easy', 'What combination of parameters maximizes the oscillation frequency of a mass-spring oscillator?', '["Large $m$, small $k$","Small $m$, large $k$","Large amplitude, large $m$","Small amplitude, small $k$","Large amplitude, small $k$"]'::jsonb, '(B) $$f = \frac{1}{2\pi}\sqrt{\frac{k}{m}}$$
Frequency is maximized by choosing the largest spring constant $k$ and the smallest mass $m$, and is independent of amplitude.'),
  ('ap-topic-physc-shm_q3', 'ap-topic-physc-shm', 3, false, 'B', 'Simple Pendulum Maximum Speed', 'easy', 'A simple pendulum of length $L$ is pulled to initial position and released from rest, dropping vertical height $h$ to equilibrium. What is its maximum speed at the bottom?', '["$gh$","$\\sqrt{2gh}$","$\\sqrt{2gd}$","$\\sqrt{2gL}$","$2gh$"]'::jsonb, '(B) By conservation of mechanical energy:
$$mgh = \frac{1}{2}mv_{\max}^2 \implies v_{\max} = \sqrt{2gh}$$'),
  ('ap-topic-physc-shm_q4', 'ap-topic-physc-shm', 4, false, 'C', 'Oscillator Mass Scaling & Max Speed', 'medium', 'In a spring-mass oscillator, if mass is doubled while keeping oscillation amplitude $A$ constant, the maximum speed will:', '["decrease by a factor of 4","decrease by a factor of 2","decrease by a factor of $\\sqrt{2}$","remain the same","increase by a factor of 2"]'::jsonb, '(C) $$v_{\max} = \omega A = \sqrt{\frac{k}{m}}A \propto \frac{1}{\sqrt{m}}$$
Doubling the mass scales maximum speed by $\frac{1}{\sqrt{2}}$.'),
  ('ap-topic-physc-shm_q5', 'ap-topic-physc-shm', 5, false, 'E', 'Vertical Hanging Spring Total Length', 'medium', 'A vertical spring ($L_0 = 40\text{ cm}, k = 400\text{ N/m}$) hangs with mass $m = 10\text{ kg}$. What is the total equilibrium length of the spring ($g = 10\text{ m/s}^2$)?', '["$25\\text{ cm}$","$35\\text{ cm}$","$40\\text{ cm}$","$50\\text{ cm}$","$65\\text{ cm}$"]'::jsonb, '(E) Extension at equilibrium:
$$x = \frac{mg}{k} = \frac{(10\text{ kg})(10\text{ m/s}^2)}{400\text{ N/m}} = 0.25\text{ m} = 25\text{ cm}$$
Total length: $L = L_0 + x = 40\text{ cm} + 25\text{ cm} = 65\text{ cm}$.'),
  ('ap-topic-physc-circuits_q1', 'ap-topic-physc-circuits', 1, false, 'C', 'Ch13: Circuits', 'medium', 'The equivalent resistance of the circuit in Figure 13.14 is Figure 13.14', '["4R","2R","R","R/2","R/4"]'::jsonb, '(C) The parallel combination of the two resistors with resistances R and 2R has an equivalent resistance of Adding the resistor R, which is in series with this parallel combination, yields an equivalent resistance of R.'),
  ('ap-topic-physc-circuits_q2', 'ap-topic-physc-circuits', 2, false, 'B', 'Ch13: Circuits', 'medium', 'In an RC series circuit connected to a constant DC voltage source V0, an initially uncharged capacitor C is charged through a resistor R. At time t = 0 the switch is closed. What is the current I(t) through the circuit as a function of time t?', '["I(t) = (V0 / R) * (1 - e^{-t / (RC)})","I(t) = (V0 / R) * e^{-t / (RC)}","I(t) = (V0 / R) * e^{-t / (2RC)}","I(t) = V0 C * e^{-t / (RC)}","I(t) is constant and equal to V0 / R"]'::jsonb, 'By Kirchhoff''s loop rule: V0 - I R - q/C = 0. Differentiating with respect to t (since dq/dt = I): -R (dI/dt) - I/C = 0 ==> dI/dt = -I / (RC). Integrating from t = 0 where the uncharged capacitor has q(0) = 0 and initial current I(0) = V0/R: I(t) = (V0 / R) e^{-t / (RC)}.'),
  ('ap-topic-physc-circuits_q3', 'ap-topic-physc-circuits', 3, false, 'C', 'Ch13: Circuits', 'medium', 'A battery with EMF V0 and internal resistance r is connected across an external variable load resistor R. For what value of R is the power delivered to the external load maximized?', '["R = 0","R = r / 2","R = r","R = 2r","R approaches infinity"]'::jsonb, 'The current is I = V0 / (R + r). The power dissipated in the load is P(R) = I^2 R = V0^2 R / (R + r)^2. To find the maximum, set dP/dR = 0: dP/dR = V0^2 [ (R+r)^2 - 2R(R+r) ] / (R+r)^4 = V0^2 (r - R) / (R+r)^3 = 0 ==> R = r. This is the Maximum Power Transfer Theorem.'),
  ('ap-topic-physc-circuits_q4', 'ap-topic-physc-circuits', 4, false, 'B', 'Ch13: Circuits', 'medium', 'Twelve identical resistors, each of resistance R, are connected along the twelve edges of a cube. What is the equivalent resistance R_eq between two diametrically opposite corners of the cube?', '["R / 2","5/6 R","3/4 R","7/12 R","R"]'::jsonb, 'By symmetry, when a current I enters one corner and exits the diametrically opposite corner: At the entry corner, current splits equally into 3 edges, each carrying I/3. At each of the next vertices, current splits into 2 edges, each carrying (I/3)/2 = I/6 (6 edges total). At the exit corner, 3 edges recombine, each carrying I/3. The total voltage drop across opposite corners is Delta V = (I/3)R + (I/6)R + (I/3)R = (2/6 + 1/6 + 2/6) I R = (5/6) I R. Therefore, R_eq = Delta V / I = 5/6 R.'),
  ('ap-topic-physc-circuits_q5', 'ap-topic-physc-circuits', 5, false, 'B', 'Ch13: Circuits', 'medium', 'For the circuit in Figure 13.15, which of the following is true? Figure 13.15', '["VB − VA = +5 V","VB − VA = +3 V","VB − VA = −2 V","VB − VA = −3 V","VB − VA = −5 V"]'::jsonb, '(B) This problem involves Kirchhoff’s loop rule. First, Kirchhoff’s node equation indicates that the current flowing down the 1 Ω resistor is 2 A. Then, writing a Kirchhoff’s loop equation containing points A and B in either possible loop yields the correct answer. For example, we will use the outer loop and move clockwise. Starting at point A and summing potential jumps as we move from point A to point B yields Therefore,'),
  ('ap-topic-physc-circuits_q6', 'ap-topic-physc-circuits', 6, false, 'B', 'Ch13: Circuits', 'medium', 'In a series RC circuit, an uncharged capacitor of capacitance C is charged through a resistor R by a battery of voltage V. After a very long time (t >> RC), what is the total energy supplied by the battery, and how much of that energy is stored in the capacitor?', '["Battery supplies 1/2 C V^2, and capacitor stores 1/2 C V^2","Battery supplies C V^2, and capacitor stores 1/2 C V^2 (with 1/2 C V^2 dissipated as heat in R)","Battery supplies C V^2, and capacitor stores C V^2","Battery supplies 2 C V^2, and capacitor stores C V^2","Battery supplies C V^2, and capacitor stores 1/4 C V^2"]'::jsonb, 'When fully charged, the capacitor carries charge Q = C V. The total work done by the battery is W = Q V = (C V) V = C V^2. The energy stored in the electric field of the capacitor is U_C = 1/2 C V^2. By conservation of energy, the remaining energy W - U_C = 1/2 C V^2 is dissipated as thermal energy in the resistor regardless of the value of R.'),
  ('ap-topic-physc-circuits_q7', 'ap-topic-physc-circuits', 7, false, 'B', 'Ch13: Circuits', 'medium', 'What is the equivalent voltage of the two batteries arranged in series in Figure 13.16? Figure 13.16', '["2 V, with point A at higher potential","2 V, with point B at higher potential","4 V, with point A at higher potential","4 V, with point B at higher potential","It is not valid to treat the two batteries as a single equivalent battery."]'::jsonb, '(B) Summing the potential jumps indicates that point B always has a potential 2 V higher than point A. Therefore, the two batteries together have the same effect as one 2 V battery with its positive terminal at point B and its negative terminal at point A.'),
  ('ap-topic-physc-circuits_q8', 'ap-topic-physc-circuits', 8, false, 'B', 'Ch13: Circuits', 'medium', 'How much power is dissipated in the circuit discussed in question 4?', '["P = 20/3R","P = 12/5R","P = 6/5R","P = 3/5R","none of the above Figure 13.17"]'::jsonb, '(B) . This problem illustrates how simplifying a circuit and calculating equivalent voltages and resistances simplifies calculating quantities such as power.'),
  ('ap-topic-physc-circuits_q9', 'ap-topic-physc-circuits', 9, false, 'A', 'Ch13: Circuits', 'medium', 'In the circuit shown in Figure 13.17, capacitor C1 is initially charged and capacitor C2 is initially uncharged when the switch is closed at t =', '["Directly proportional to the parameter (linear scaling)","Inversely proportional to the square of the separation","Equal in magnitude with opposite orientation","Independent of the mass and charge distribution","Zero net change under static equilibrium conditions"]'::jsonb, '(A) Initially the current is equal to I = V/R = Q/C1R (because C2 acts as a short circuit and C1 initially has voltage V = Q/C1). The current at equilibrium is zero. Therefore, the current exponentially decays from a finite value to zero, as described by graph (A).'),
  ('ap-topic-physc-circuits_q10', 'ap-topic-physc-circuits', 10, false, 'D', 'Ch13: Circuits', 'medium', 'Which of the following is Kirchhoff’s loop equation for the circuit shown in Figure 13.17? (I is designated as the counterclockwise current.)', '["−Q1/C1 − IR + Q2/C2 = 0","Q1/C1 − IR + Q2/C2 = 0","−Q1/C1 + IR − Q2/C2 = 0","Q1/C1 − IR − Q2/C2 = 0","Q1/C1 + IR + Q2/C2 = 0 Figure 13.18"]'::jsonb, '(D) Beginning at the top right corner of the circuit and moving counterclockwise: 1. The potential jump across C1 is positive because we are going from the negative plate to the positive plate (which is at a higher potential). 2. The potential jump across the resistor is negative because we are going in the same direction as the current (which flows counterclockwise). 3. The potential jump across C2 is negative because we are going from the positive plate to the negative plate. Note: Of course, if you choose to go in the opposite direction, all the signs will be reversed and an equivalent equation will be obtained (which can be converted to the equation in choice (D) by multiplying both sides by −1).'),
  ('ap-topic-physc-circuits_q11', 'ap-topic-physc-circuits', 11, false, 'B', 'Ch13: Circuits', 'medium', 'A capacitor is discharged through a resistor. If the energy stored in the capacitor decreases to 1/4 of its initial value in 10 seconds, what is the time constant tau = RC of the circuit?', '["10 s","10 / ln(2) s","10 / ln(4) s","5 s","20 s"]'::jsonb, 'The charge on the discharging capacitor is q(t) = Q0 e^{-t / tau}. The stored energy is U(t) = q(t)^2 / (2C) = U0 e^{-2t / tau}. Given U(10) / U0 = 1/4: e^{-20 / tau} = 1/4 ==> e^{20 / tau} = 4 ==> 20 / tau = ln(4) = 2 ln(2) ==> tau = 20 / (2 ln(2)) = 10 / ln(2) s.'),
  ('ap-topic-physc-circuits_q12', 'ap-topic-physc-circuits', 12, false, 'E', 'Ch13: Circuits', 'medium', 'The capacitor in the circuit in Figure 13.20 is uncharged when the switch is closed at t = 0. Figure 13.20 How much energy is dissipated in the circuit from t = 0 to t = \infty?', '["the energy stored in the capacitor, CV2","the energy stored in the capacitor and the energy dissipated in the resistor, equal to CV2","the energy stored in the capacitor, \\frac{1}{2}CV^2","the energy stored in the capacitor and the energy dissipated in the resistor, equal to 2CV2","none of the above"]'::jsonb, '(E) Although the rate at which energy is dissipated by the RC loop of the circuit is finite, energy continues to be dissipated by the 2R resistor forever, so the energy dissipated from t = 0 to t = \infty is undefined (infinite).'),
  ('ap-topic-physc-circuits_q13', 'ap-topic-physc-circuits', 13, false, 'B', 'Ch13: Circuits', 'medium', 'An inductor of inductance L and a resistor of resistance R are connected in series with a switch and a battery of EMF V0. The switch is closed at t = 0. What is the rate of change of current dI/dt at the instant t = 0?', '["Zero","V0 / L","V0 / R","V0 / (R L)","Infinity"]'::jsonb, 'Kirchhoff''s loop rule for the RL circuit is V0 - I R - L (dI/dt) = 0. At t = 0, the current through the inductor cannot change instantaneously from zero, so I(0) = 0. The equation becomes V0 - 0 - L (dI/dt) = 0 ==> dI/dt = V0 / L.'),
  ('ap-topic-physc-circuits_q14', 'ap-topic-physc-circuits', 14, false, 'A', 'Diagnostic Test – E&M', 'medium', 'In an LC circuit consisting of an ideal inductor L and an ideal capacitor C with no resistance, the charge on the capacitor oscillates with time. What is the period T of the electromagnetic oscillations?', '["2 pi sqrt(L C)","1 / (2 pi sqrt(L C))","2 pi L / C","2 pi sqrt(L / C)","pi sqrt(L C)"]'::jsonb, 'The differential equation governing the LC circuit is L (d^2 q / dt^2) + q/C = 0 ==> d^2 q / dt^2 + (1 / (L C)) q = 0. This is simple harmonic motion with angular frequency omega = 1 / sqrt(L C). The oscillation period is T = 2 pi / omega = 2 pi sqrt(L C).'),
  ('ap-topic-physc-circuits_q15', 'ap-topic-physc-circuits', 15, false, 'C', 'Diagnostic Test – E&M', 'medium', 'A charged particle is launched into a region of uniform magnetic field. Given that the particle’s initial velocity has components parallel and perpendicular to the magnetic field, describe the particle’s path qualitatively.', '["a straight line","a circle","a helical (corkscrew) path","a parabolic path","none of the above"]'::jsonb, '(C) See the discussion of the motion of particles within magnetic fields in Chapter 14.'),
  ('ap-topic-physc-circuits_q16', 'ap-topic-physc-circuits', 16, false, 'B', 'Diagnostic Test – E&M', 'medium', 'A point charge +Q is located at a fixed distance from a sphere. Compare the magnitude of the attractive force on the sphere if the sphere is (1) metal with net charge −Q, (2) metal with no net charge, (3) an insulator with net charge −Q, or (4) an insulator with no net charge.', '["charged metal = charged insulator > uncharged metal = uncharged insulator","charged metal > charged insulator > uncharged metal > uncharged insulator","charged insulator > charged metal > uncharged insulator > uncharged metal","charged insulator > charged metal > uncharged metal = uncharged insulator","charged insulator = charged metal > uncharged metal > uncharged insulator"]'::jsonb, '(B) This is a question about electrostatic induction. When a positive point charge is placed near a metal sphere (charged or uncharged), it attracts mobile electrons in the metal, causing the metal to become polarized such that more negative charge is on the side closer to the positive point charge and more positive charge is on the side farther away from the positive point charge. Because this charge redistribution results in moving negative charge closer to the positive point charge and moving positive charge farther away from the positive point charge, it increases the attractive force between the metal sphere and the positive point charge. Therefore, compared to an insulating sphere with the same net charge, a metal sphere always experiences a greater attractive force (such that charged metal > charged insulator and uncharged metal > uncharged insulator). Because this inductive effect is small compared to the effect of actually putting net charge −Q on the sphere, charged insulator > uncharged metal.'),
  ('ap-topic-physc-circuits_q17', 'ap-topic-physc-circuits', 17, false, 'C', 'Diagnostic Test – E&M', 'medium', 'A bar moves to the right through a magnetic field as shown (the magnetic field points into the page). What is the potential difference between points A and B after equilibrium is established in the bar?', '["The difference is v2Bd, with A at higher potential.","The difference is v2Bd, with B at higher potential.","The difference is vBd, with A at higher potential.","The difference is vBd, with B at higher potential.","Because there is no loop, there is no flux, so there is no potential difference."]'::jsonb, '(C) The charges within the bar are moving in a magnetic field, so they feel a magnetic force. Using the conventional-current description, positive charges feel an upward force, causing positive charge to accumulate at the top end of the bar (leaving negative charge on the bottom end of the bar). This charge continues to accumulate until the electric force created by the charge imbalance cancels the magnetic force, mathematically qE = qvB. The magnitude of the potential difference between points A and B is then |V | = |−\int E \cdot  dr| = |vBd|. The electric field points down (producing a force that opposes the magnetic force), and recalling that electric fields point from high to low potential, we see that point A is at the higher potential. Further information about motional EMF can be found in Example 15.6.'),
  ('ap-topic-physc-circuits_q18', 'ap-topic-physc-circuits', 18, false, 'B', 'Diagnostic Test – E&M', 'medium', 'Suppose that the electric potential in a region of space is a constant. What can you conclude about the magnitude of the electric field in this region?', '["It is a nonzero constant.","It is zero.","It is proportional to the distance from the origin.","It is given by","Not enough information is given. Questions 6–8 refer to a circular region of radius a with a uniform magnetic field pointing into the page that is increasing at a constant rate as shown. A circular loop of wire of radius r is centered on the middle of the region."]'::jsonb, '(B) The electric field is the negative derivative (with respect to the spatial coordinate) of the potential. Thus, with a constant potential the electric field must be zero.'),
  ('ap-topic-physc-circuits_q19', 'ap-topic-physc-circuits', 19, false, 'C', 'Diagnostic Test – E&M', 'medium', 'Which of the graphs plots the induced EMF in the circular loop of wire shown as a function of its radius r?', '["Directly proportional to the parameter (linear scaling)","Inversely proportional to the square of the separation","Equal in magnitude with opposite orientation","Independent of the mass and charge distribution","Zero net change under static equilibrium conditions"]'::jsonb, '(C) The induced EMF depends on the change in the enclosed flux. Because the magnetic field is uniform, and changing at a constant rate, Therefore, the induced EMF depends only on the area of the loop through which magnetic field lines pass. For r < a, the area within the loop exposed to the magnetic field is simply the entire area of the loop, which is a quadratic function, A(r) = \pir2. For r > a, increasing r does not increase the area exposed to the magnetic field (because the magnetic field through the additional area is zero). Therefore, for r > a the induced EMF is constant. The only graph that is quadratic for small r and then constant is choice (C). Calculating the induced EMF due to a magnetic field via Faraday’s law is discussed in Chapter 15.'),
  ('ap-topic-physc-circuits_q20', 'ap-topic-physc-circuits', 20, false, 'A', 'Diagnostic Test – E&M', 'medium', 'Given that the resistance of the loop is proportional to the loop’s circumference, which of the graphs seen in question 6 shows the induced current as a function of the radius of the loop?', '["Directly proportional to the parameter (linear scaling)","Inversely proportional to the square of the separation","Equal in magnitude with opposite orientation","Independent of the mass and charge distribution","Zero net change under static equilibrium conditions"]'::jsonb, '(A) The current is given by I = V/R. The resistance is proportional to the circumference of the loop, 2\pir. Therefore, for r < a, V is proportional to r2 (as discussed in question 6), and 1/R is proportional to 1/r, so the product I = V/R is proportional to r (and thus its graph is a line passing through the origin). For r > a, V is constant and 1/R is proportional to 1/r, so the product I = V/R is inversely proportional to r. The only graph that satisfies these conditions is choice (A).'),
  ('ap-topic-physc-magnetism_q1', 'ap-topic-physc-magnetism', 1, false, 'C', 'Ch14: Magnetic Fields', 'medium', 'Two circular loops of wire of equal radius R carry equal currents I. One loop lies in the xy-plane carrying current counterclockwise as seen from the positive z-axis, producing a magnetic field B_z k at the origin. The second loop lies in the xz-plane carrying current that produces a magnetic field -B_y j at the origin. What is the direction of the net magnetic field at the origin?', '["Along the positive x-axis","In the xy-plane at 45 degrees to the axes","In the yz-plane along the direction -j + k","Along the negative z-axis","Zero net magnetic field"]'::jsonb, 'By the principle of superposition, the net magnetic field at the origin is B_net = B1 + B2 = -B_y j + B_z k. Because the loops have equal radii and carry equal currents, |B_y| = |B_z| = mu_0 I / (2R). The net field lies in the yz-plane and is parallel to the vector -j + k.'),
  ('ap-topic-physc-magnetism_q2', 'ap-topic-physc-magnetism', 2, false, 'C', 'Ch14: Magnetic Fields', 'medium', 'A long coaxial cable consists of an inner solid cylindrical conductor of radius a and an outer thin cylindrical shell of radius b (with a < b). Equal currents I flow in opposite directions through the inner and outer conductors. What is the magnetic field magnitude B(r) in the space between the conductors (a < r < b)?', '["Zero","mu_0 I r / (2 pi a^2)","mu_0 I / (2 pi r)","mu_0 I / (2 pi b)","mu_0 I / (4 pi r^2)"]'::jsonb, 'Apply Ampere''s law oint B * dl = mu_0 I_enc around a circular path of radius r (with a < r < b). The path encloses only the inner conductor carrying total current I. Thus, B(2 pi r) = mu_0 I ==> B(r) = mu_0 I / (2 pi r).'),
  ('ap-topic-physc-magnetism_q3', 'ap-topic-physc-magnetism', 3, false, 'D', 'Ch14: Magnetic Fields', 'medium', 'A uniform magnetic field of magnitude B points out of the page in the region x > 0, and a uniform magnetic field of equal magnitude points into the page in the region x < 0, as shown in Figure 14.15. A particle with charge +q and mass m initially at the origin moves to the right with speed v. At which of the following points does the particle cross the y- axis? Figure 14.15', '["6 mv/qB","3 mv/qB","−3 mv/qB","−6 mv/qB","The particle will never cross the y-axis."]'::jsonb, '(D) The particle’s velocity is perpendicular to the magnetic field, so it moves in a circular path. The radius of the path can be calculated by equating the magnetic force to the centripetal force, qvB = mv2/r, to obtain r = mv/qB. Careful examination of the particle’s path reveals that it moves in the path shown in Figure 14.25. Therefore, it passes through the y-axis at multiples of −2r = −2 mv/qB. Figure 14.25'),
  ('ap-topic-physc-magnetism_q4', 'ap-topic-physc-magnetism', 4, false, 'D', 'Ch14: Magnetic Fields', 'medium', 'Two infinitely long wires carry equal currents of I into the page, as shown in Figure 14.24.', '["Directly proportional to the parameter (linear scaling)","Inversely proportional to the square of the separation","Equal in magnitude with opposite orientation","Independent of the mass and charge distribution","Zero net change under static equilibrium conditions"]'::jsonb, '(D) The particle’s velocity is perpendicular to the magnetic field, so it moves in a circular path. The radius of the path can be calculated by equating the magnetic force to the centripetal force, qvB = mv2/r, to obtain r = mv/qB. Careful examination of the particle’s path reveals that it moves in the path shown in Figure 14.25. Therefore, it passes through the y-axis at multiples of −2r = −2 mv/qB. Figure 14.25'),
  ('ap-topic-physc-magnetism_q5', 'ap-topic-physc-magnetism', 5, false, 'E', 'Ch14: Magnetic Fields', 'medium', 'An electron moves in a magnetic field along a certain path at a constant speed. The magnetic field does no work on the electron if', '["the path is parallel to the magnetic field so that the magnetic force is zero","the path is perpendicular to the field so that the electron moves in uniform circular motion","the magnetic field is constant in space so that there is no induced electric field","the electron must move along a magnetic field line","None of the above conditions are necessary."]'::jsonb, '(E) The magnetic force is always perpendicular to velocity, so it can never do work (recall that dW = F \cdot  d r).'),
  ('ap-topic-physc-magnetism_q6', 'ap-topic-physc-magnetism', 6, false, 'C', 'Ch14: Magnetic Fields', 'medium', 'Figure 14.16 shows a cross section of a coaxial cable. A current of 1.0 A passes through the center of the cable, while a uniform current density of 1.0 A/m2 comes out of the page through the outer sheath of the cable. At what distance from the center of the cable is the magnetic field equal to zero? Figure 14.16', '["Directly proportional to the parameter (linear scaling)","Inversely proportional to the square of the separation","(D) The magnetic field is zero but not at the radii listed above.","Independent of the mass and charge distribution","The magnetic field is never zero except at infinity."]'::jsonb, '(C) According to Ampere’s law, the magnetic field is zero when the enclosed current is zero. For the inward current to balance the outward current, the outward current enclosed must equal 1.0 A. The enclosed outward current is 1.0 A when the Amperian path encloses an area of 1.0 m2 of the sheath (because the current density is 1.0 A/m2). Geometrically, 1 = \pir2 − \pi(1.0)2. Solving this equation for r yields choice (C).'),
  ('ap-topic-physc-magnetism_q7', 'ap-topic-physc-magnetism', 7, false, 'A', 'Ch14: Magnetic Fields', 'medium', 'A charged particle moves in uniform circular motion due to a magnetic field perpendicular to the plane of its motion. Which of the graphs in Figure 14.17 illustrates the relationship between the charge’s velocity and the radius of its motion? (Assume that other variables, such as the magnetic field strength and the mass of the particle, are held constant.) Figure 14.17', '["Directly proportional to the parameter (linear scaling)","Inversely proportional to the square of the separation","Equal in magnitude with opposite orientation","Independent of the mass and charge distribution","Zero net change under static equilibrium conditions"]'::jsonb, '(A) The magnetic force provides the centripetal force: Thus, v is a linear function of r that passes through the origin.'),
  ('ap-topic-physc-magnetism_q8', 'ap-topic-physc-magnetism', 8, false, 'B', 'Ch14: Magnetic Fields', 'medium', 'A particle with a charge −q and mass m is moving with speed v through a mass spectrometer that contains a uniform outward magnetic field as shown in Figure 14.18. What is the magnitude and direction of the electric field required so that the net force on this particle is zero? Figure 14.18', '["E = vB, with a direction upward","E = vB, with a direction downward","E = B/v, with a direction upward","E = B/v, with a direction downward","E = B/v, with a direction into the page"]'::jsonb, '(B) For the magnetic force to cancel the electric force, |qv \times  B| = qvB = qE, which simplifies to E = vB. The direction of the magnetic force on the charge is downward (opposite the direction indicated by the right- hand rule because the charge is negative). The electric force must point in the opposite direction, upward. However, since the charge is negative, in order to produce an upward electric force the field must point downward.'),
  ('ap-topic-physc-magnetism_q9', 'ap-topic-physc-magnetism', 9, false, 'A', 'Ch14: Magnetic Fields', 'medium', 'Consider current passing through a short segment of a circular loop of wire as shown in Figure 14.19. The current passing through the rest of the circular wire produces a magnetic field at the location of the short segment, resulting in a magnetic force on the current-carrying segment of wire. Which of the following statements about this force is true? Figure 14.19', '["The force is to the right.","The force is to the left.","The magnetic field is zero because of symmetry, so there is no force.","The force is pointing out of the page.","The force is pointing into the page."]'::jsonb, '(A) First, imagine dividing the loop into little segments and considering the magnetic field due to each segment (using the right- hand rule for the direction of a magnetic field produced by a current- carrying wire or using the right-hand rule embedded in Biot-Savart’s law). Because each segment produces a magnetic field pointing out of the page at the segment of wire, the net magnetic field points out of the page. Using the right-hand rule again, the force this field exerts on the segment of wire is to the right. In general, each segment of the loop feels a force directed radially outward due to the rest of the loop.'),
  ('ap-topic-physc-magnetism_q10', 'ap-topic-physc-magnetism', 10, false, 'D', 'Ch14: Magnetic Fields', 'medium', 'Two wires located along the x-axis carry equal currents into the page as shown in Figure 14.20. Which of the graphs shows the y-component of the magnetic field along the x-axis? Figure 14.20', '["Directly proportional to the parameter (linear scaling)","Inversely proportional to the square of the separation","Equal in magnitude with opposite orientation","Independent of the mass and charge distribution","Zero net change under static equilibrium conditions"]'::jsonb, '(D) At the origin, the fields due to the two wires cancel and the net field is zero, eliminating choices (A) and (B). As each wire is approached from the right, the field decreases to negative infinity, and as each wire is approached from the left, the field increases to positive infinity. This is consistent only with choice (D).'),
  ('ap-topic-physc-magnetism_q11', 'ap-topic-physc-magnetism', 11, false, 'C', 'Ch14: Magnetic Fields', 'medium', 'A particle moves through an electric field as shown in Figure 14.21. What is the direction of the magnetic field needed to ensure that the particle experiences zero net force? Figure 14.21', '["into the page if the particle is positively charged, out of the page if the particle is negatively charged","into the page if the particle is negatively charged, out of the page if the particle is positively charged","into the page, irrespective of the particle’s charge","out of the page, irrespective of the particle’s charge","to the left, irrespective of the particle’s charge"]'::jsonb, '(C) Changing the sign of the charge of the particle changes the direction of both the electric and the magnetic forces, two changes that cancel each other out, so that in either case the magnetic field must point into the page in order to cancel out the electric field. (If the magnetic field is into the page, a positive charge will feel an electric force to the right and a magnetic force to the left. Alternatively, a negative charge will feel forces in the opposite directions.)'),
  ('ap-topic-physc-magnetism_q12', 'ap-topic-physc-magnetism', 12, false, 'B', 'Ch14: Magnetic Fields', 'medium', 'Three loops of equal radii carrying equal currents lie on planes parallel to the yz-plane as shown in Figure 14.22. Two of the loops are located equal distances d from the yz-plane as shown, while the third loop lies on the yz-plane. In what direction is the net magnetic field at the origin? Figure 14.22', '["The magnetic field is zero because of symmetry.","The magnetic field is in the +x-direction.","The magnetic field is in the −x-direction.","The magnetic field lies in the xz-plane.","The magnetic field lies in the xy-plane. Free-Response Questions"]'::jsonb, '(B) The two loops lying off the yz-plane produce equal and opposite fields which cancel each other. (The loop at x = −d produces a magnetic field in the −x-direction, while the loop at x = d produces a magnetic field in the +x-direction.) The net field is therefore equal to the field produced by the middle loop, which produces a field in the +x- direction. Free-Response 1. Given the cylindrical symmetry of this problem, Ampere’s Law is the way to go. As in Examples 14.4 and 14.5, the field lines will be concentric circles centered on the center of the inner conductor. The Amperian path will be a circle of radius r. The left-hand side of Ampere’s law for this path will be ∮B \cdot  dl = B(2\pir). All we need to do is to figure out the direction and magnitude of the enclosed current to solve each part of the problem. (a) The current density in the inner conductor is , positive because the current is flowing out of the page. Thus, the enclosed current is , and Ampere’s law says: The field is counterclockwise because the current is positive, pointing out of the page. (b) Now the enclosed current is the full value I, pointing out of the page, i.e., positive. Ampere’s law yields , just like a thin current- carrying wire. The field is counterclockwise. (c) This is the trickiest part of this problem as we are enclosing the entire inner conductor but only a portion of the outer conductor. First, we need to find the current density in the outer conductor. The cross- sectional area of the outer conductor is and the current density is , negative because the current flows into the page. The enclosed current is then given by I enclosed . Applying Ampere’s law we find: The field is positive, i.e., counterclockwise because the enclosed current is positive. (d) The enclosed current is now zero so the field is zero. 2. (a) The magnetic field due to an infinitely long wire is equal in magnitude to B = \mu0I/2\pir and has a direction indicated by the right-hand rule. The magnetic field along the x-axis is a superposition of the magnetic fields due to both of the wires, as shown in Figure 14.26. Figure 14.26 Adding the two vectors yields (b) This expression is given in Figure 14.27. In sketching the curve, first we note that the function has odd symmetry; mathematically, B(−x) = −B(x). Because x is raised to a greater power in the denominator than in the numerator, the function asymptotically approaches zero as x approaches positive or negative infinity. Finally, we note that the function is zero at the origin and that it passes through the origin with a negative slope. Figure 14.27 Note: A graphing calculator can also be used to sketch this curve: Simply graphing the function f(x) = −x/(1 + x2) is sufficient to get an idea of how this curve looks. (c) The magnetic force is always perpendicular to the velocity, so it never does any work. (Thus, the magnetic force can change only an object’s direction, not its speed.) Therefore, no work need be done by an external force to move the charge from infinity to the origin.'),
  ('ap-topic-physc-magnetism_q13', 'ap-topic-physc-magnetism', 13, false, 'A', 'Diagnostic Test – E&M', 'medium', 'A charged particle with charge q and mass m enters a region of uniform magnetic field B with velocity v perpendicular to the field. The particle travels in a circle of radius R. What is the kinetic energy of the particle in terms of q, m, B, and R?', '["q^2 B^2 R^2 / (2 m)","q B R / (2 m)","q^2 B R^2 / m","1/2 q B v R","q B^2 R / (2 m)"]'::jsonb, 'The magnetic force provides the centripetal acceleration: q v B = m v^2 / R ==> m v = q B R. Linear momentum is p = q B R. The kinetic energy is K = p^2 / (2 m) = (q B R)^2 / (2 m) = q^2 B^2 R^2 / (2 m).'),
  ('ap-topic-physc-magnetism_q14', 'ap-topic-physc-magnetism', 14, false, 'C', 'Diagnostic Test – E&M', 'medium', 'A charged particle is launched into a region of uniform magnetic field. Given that the particle’s initial velocity has components parallel and perpendicular to the magnetic field, describe the particle’s path qualitatively.', '["a straight line","a circle","a helical (corkscrew) path","a parabolic path","none of the above"]'::jsonb, '(C) See the discussion of the motion of particles within magnetic fields in Chapter 14.'),
  ('ap-topic-physc-magnetism_q15', 'ap-topic-physc-magnetism', 15, false, 'B', 'Diagnostic Test – E&M', 'medium', 'A point charge +Q is located at a fixed distance from a sphere. Compare the magnitude of the attractive force on the sphere if the sphere is (1) metal with net charge −Q, (2) metal with no net charge, (3) an insulator with net charge −Q, or (4) an insulator with no net charge.', '["charged metal = charged insulator > uncharged metal = uncharged insulator","charged metal > charged insulator > uncharged metal > uncharged insulator","charged insulator > charged metal > uncharged insulator > uncharged metal","charged insulator > charged metal > uncharged metal = uncharged insulator","charged insulator = charged metal > uncharged metal > uncharged insulator"]'::jsonb, '(B) This is a question about electrostatic induction. When a positive point charge is placed near a metal sphere (charged or uncharged), it attracts mobile electrons in the metal, causing the metal to become polarized such that more negative charge is on the side closer to the positive point charge and more positive charge is on the side farther away from the positive point charge. Because this charge redistribution results in moving negative charge closer to the positive point charge and moving positive charge farther away from the positive point charge, it increases the attractive force between the metal sphere and the positive point charge. Therefore, compared to an insulating sphere with the same net charge, a metal sphere always experiences a greater attractive force (such that charged metal > charged insulator and uncharged metal > uncharged insulator). Because this inductive effect is small compared to the effect of actually putting net charge −Q on the sphere, charged insulator > uncharged metal.'),
  ('ap-topic-physc-magnetism_q16', 'ap-topic-physc-magnetism', 16, false, 'C', 'Diagnostic Test – E&M', 'medium', 'A bar moves to the right through a magnetic field as shown (the magnetic field points into the page). What is the potential difference between points A and B after equilibrium is established in the bar?', '["The difference is v2Bd, with A at higher potential.","The difference is v2Bd, with B at higher potential.","The difference is vBd, with A at higher potential.","The difference is vBd, with B at higher potential.","Because there is no loop, there is no flux, so there is no potential difference."]'::jsonb, '(C) The charges within the bar are moving in a magnetic field, so they feel a magnetic force. Using the conventional-current description, positive charges feel an upward force, causing positive charge to accumulate at the top end of the bar (leaving negative charge on the bottom end of the bar). This charge continues to accumulate until the electric force created by the charge imbalance cancels the magnetic force, mathematically qE = qvB. The magnitude of the potential difference between points A and B is then |V | = |−\int E \cdot  dr| = |vBd|. The electric field points down (producing a force that opposes the magnetic force), and recalling that electric fields point from high to low potential, we see that point A is at the higher potential. Further information about motional EMF can be found in Example 15.6.'),
  ('ap-topic-physc-magnetism_q17', 'ap-topic-physc-magnetism', 17, false, 'B', 'Diagnostic Test – E&M', 'medium', 'Suppose that the electric potential in a region of space is a constant. What can you conclude about the magnitude of the electric field in this region?', '["It is a nonzero constant.","It is zero.","It is proportional to the distance from the origin.","It is given by","Not enough information is given. Questions 6–8 refer to a circular region of radius a with a uniform magnetic field pointing into the page that is increasing at a constant rate as shown. A circular loop of wire of radius r is centered on the middle of the region."]'::jsonb, '(B) The electric field is the negative derivative (with respect to the spatial coordinate) of the potential. Thus, with a constant potential the electric field must be zero.'),
  ('ap-topic-physc-magnetism_q18', 'ap-topic-physc-magnetism', 18, false, 'C', 'Diagnostic Test – E&M', 'medium', 'Which of the graphs plots the induced EMF in the circular loop of wire shown as a function of its radius r?', '["Directly proportional to the parameter (linear scaling)","Inversely proportional to the square of the separation","Equal in magnitude with opposite orientation","Independent of the mass and charge distribution","Zero net change under static equilibrium conditions"]'::jsonb, '(C) The induced EMF depends on the change in the enclosed flux. Because the magnetic field is uniform, and changing at a constant rate, Therefore, the induced EMF depends only on the area of the loop through which magnetic field lines pass. For r < a, the area within the loop exposed to the magnetic field is simply the entire area of the loop, which is a quadratic function, A(r) = \pir2. For r > a, increasing r does not increase the area exposed to the magnetic field (because the magnetic field through the additional area is zero). Therefore, for r > a the induced EMF is constant. The only graph that is quadratic for small r and then constant is choice (C). Calculating the induced EMF due to a magnetic field via Faraday’s law is discussed in Chapter 15.')
ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.ap_exam_questions (id, test_id, question_order, is_frq, answer_key, topic, difficulty, question_text, options_json, explanation)
VALUES
  ('ap-topic-physc-magnetism_q19', 'ap-topic-physc-magnetism', 19, false, 'A', 'Diagnostic Test – E&M', 'medium', 'Given that the resistance of the loop is proportional to the loop’s circumference, which of the graphs seen in question 6 shows the induced current as a function of the radius of the loop?', '["Directly proportional to the parameter (linear scaling)","Inversely proportional to the square of the separation","Equal in magnitude with opposite orientation","Independent of the mass and charge distribution","Zero net change under static equilibrium conditions"]'::jsonb, '(A) The current is given by I = V/R. The resistance is proportional to the circumference of the loop, 2\pir. Therefore, for r < a, V is proportional to r2 (as discussed in question 6), and 1/R is proportional to 1/r, so the product I = V/R is proportional to r (and thus its graph is a line passing through the origin). For r > a, V is constant and 1/R is proportional to 1/r, so the product I = V/R is inversely proportional to r. The only graph that satisfies these conditions is choice (A).'),
  ('ap-topic-physc-magnetism_q20', 'ap-topic-physc-magnetism', 20, false, 'B', 'Diagnostic Test – E&M', 'medium', 'Two long, straight, parallel wires separated by distance d carry currents I1 and I2 in opposite directions (antiparallel). What is the nature and magnitude per unit length of the magnetic force between the two wires?', '["Attractive with magnitude mu_0 I1 I2 / (2 pi d)","Repulsive with magnitude mu_0 I1 I2 / (2 pi d)","Repulsive with magnitude mu_0 I1 I2 / (4 pi d^2)","Attractive with magnitude mu_0 I1 I2 / (2 pi d^2)","Zero because the currents are in opposite directions"]'::jsonb, 'Using the right-hand rule, antiparallel currents produce a repulsive force between the wires. The magnetic field produced by wire 1 at the location of wire 2 is B1 = mu_0 I1 / (2 pi d). The magnetic force on a segment of length L of wire 2 is F = I2 L B1 = mu_0 I1 I2 L / (2 pi d). Therefore, the force per unit length is F/L = mu_0 I1 I2 / (2 pi d), and it is repulsive.'),
  ('ap-topic-physc-mech-drills_q1', 'ap-topic-physc-mech-drills', 1, false, 'A', 'Uniform Circular Motion & Kinematics', 'easy', 'An object that''s moving with constant speed travels once around a circular path. Which of the following is/are true concerning this motion?  I. The displacement is zero.  II. The average speed is zero.  III. The acceleration is zero.', '["I only","I and II only","I and III only","III only","II and III only"]'::jsonb, '(A) Because the particle starts and finishes at the exact same location, its net position displacement is zero ($\Delta \vec{r} = 0$), making statement I true. Average speed is total distance over time, which is strictly greater than zero (II is false). Uniform circular motion features a continuous change in velocity direction, requiring a nonzero centripetal acceleration directed toward the center ($a_c = v^2/r \ne 0$) (III is false).'),
  ('ap-topic-physc-mech-drills_q2', 'ap-topic-physc-mech-drills', 2, false, 'C', 'Average Acceleration Vector Sum', 'medium', 'At time $t = t_1$, an object''s velocity is $\vec{v}_1$. A short time later, at $t = t_2$, its velocity is $\vec{v}_2$. If $|\vec{v}_1| = |\vec{v}_2|$, which vector best illustrates the average acceleration $\vec{a}_{\text{avg}}$ between $t_1$ and $t_2$?', '["Vector pointing along $\\vec{v}_1$","Vector pointing along $\\vec{v}_2$","Vector pointing downward and to the left (along $\\vec{v}_2 - \\vec{v}_1$)","Vector pointing upward","Zero vector"]'::jsonb, '(C) Average acceleration is defined by $\vec{a}_{\text{avg}} = \frac{\Delta\vec{v}}{\Delta t} = \frac{\vec{v}_2 - \vec{v}_1}{\Delta t} = \frac{\vec{v}_2 + (-\vec{v}_1)}{\Delta t}$. Vector addition of $\vec{v}_2$ and the inverted vector $-\vec{v}_1$ yields a resultant vector directed downward and to the left.'),
  ('ap-topic-physc-mech-drills_q3', 'ap-topic-physc-mech-drills', 3, false, 'C', '2D Projectile Acceleration', 'easy', 'A rock is thrown off a $30\text{-m}$ cliff at a $45^\circ$ angle above the horizontal. Which of the following is true regarding the acceleration of the rock? (Ignore air resistance)', '["The acceleration will have both horizontal and vertical components.","At the peak, acceleration is half of its initial launch value.","The acceleration will be of magnitude $g$ and in a downward direction.","The acceleration is downward on ascent and upward on descent.","The acceleration increases throughout the flight."]'::jsonb, '(C) Once in free-fall ballistic flight under negligible air resistance, the only force acting on the projectile is gravity ($F_g = mg$, downward). The acceleration is uniformly constant everywhere along the trajectory: $\vec{a} = -g\hat{j}$.'),
  ('ap-topic-physc-mech-drills_q4', 'ap-topic-physc-mech-drills', 4, false, 'C', 'Vertical Free Fall Acceleration at Apex', 'easy', 'A baseball is thrown straight upward. What is the ball''s acceleration at its highest point?', '["$0$","$-g$, downward","$g$, downward","$-g$, upward","$g$, upward"]'::jsonb, '(C) Even though the instantaneous velocity of the ball is zero at the apex of its path, the gravitational force continues to act on the ball without interruption, producing a constant downward acceleration of magnitude $g$.'),
  ('ap-topic-physc-mech-drills_q5', 'ap-topic-physc-mech-drills', 5, false, 'C', 'Calculus Kinematics & Max Velocity', 'medium', 'An object''s position is given by $x(t) = -3t^3 + t^2 + 6t$. What is the maximum velocity of the object?', '["$0.11\\text{ m/s}$","$2.73\\text{ m/s}$","$6.11\\text{ m/s}$","$7.39\\text{ m/s}$","$9.81\\text{ m/s}$"]'::jsonb, '(C) Velocity function:
  $$v(t) = \frac{dx}{dt} = -9t^2 + 2t + 6$$
To find the maximum velocity, set the derivative of velocity (acceleration) equal to zero:
  $$\frac{dv}{dt} = -18t + 2 = 0 \implies t = \frac{1}{9}\text{ s} \approx 0.111\text{ s}$$
Evaluate $v(t)$ at $t = 1/9\text{ s}$:
  $$v_{\max} = -9\left(\frac{1}{9}\right)^2 + 2\left(\frac{1}{9}\right) + 6 = -\frac{1}{9} + \frac{2}{9} + 6 = 6 + \frac{1}{9} \approx 6.11\text{ m/s}$$'),
  ('ap-topic-physc-mech-drills_q6', 'ap-topic-physc-mech-drills', 6, false, 'D', 'Free Fall Drop Height Calculation', 'easy', 'A rock dropped off a cliff strikes the ground with an impact speed of $30\text{ m/s}$. How high was the cliff? ($g = 10\text{ m/s}^2$)', '["$15\\text{ m}$","$20\\text{ m}$","$30\\text{ m}$","$45\\text{ m}$","$60\\text{ m}$"]'::jsonb, '(D) Using the kinematic equation $v_f^2 = v_0^2 + 2g\Delta y$ with $v_0 = 0$:
  $$\Delta y = \frac{v_f^2}{2g} = \frac{(30\text{ m/s})^2}{2(10\text{ m/s}^2)} = \frac{900}{20} = 45\text{ m}$$'),
  ('ap-topic-physc-mech-drills_q7', 'ap-topic-physc-mech-drills', 7, false, 'C', 'Horizontal Projectile Flight Time', 'easy', 'A stone is thrown horizontally with an initial speed of $10\text{ m/s}$ from a bridge. How long would it take to strike the water $80\text{ m}$ below the bridge? ($g = 10\text{ m/s}^2$)', '["$1\\text{ s}$","$2\\text{ s}$","$4\\text{ s}$","$8\\text{ s}$","$16\\text{ s}$"]'::jsonb, '(C) Vertical and horizontal motions are independent. With $v_{0y} = 0$:
  $$h = \frac{1}{2}gt^2 \implies t = \sqrt{\frac{2h}{g}} = \sqrt{\frac{2(80\text{ m})}{10\text{ m/s}^2}} = \sqrt{16} = 4\text{ s}$$'),
  ('ap-topic-physc-mech-drills_q8', 'ap-topic-physc-mech-drills', 8, false, 'B', 'Angled Projectile Total Hang Time', 'easy', 'A soccer ball at rest is kicked with initial velocity $10\text{ m/s}$ at launch angle $30^\circ$. Calculate its total flight time over level ground ($g = 10\text{ m/s}^2$).', '["$0.5\\text{ s}$","$1.0\\text{ s}$","$1.7\\text{ s}$","$2.0\\text{ s}$","$2.5\\text{ s}$"]'::jsonb, '(B) $$v_{0y} = v_0\sin(30^\circ) = 10(0.5) = 5\text{ m/s}$$
$$T = \frac{2v_{0y}}{g} = \frac{2(5\text{ m/s})}{10\text{ m/s}^2} = 1.0\text{ s}$$'),
  ('ap-topic-physc-mech-drills_q9', 'ap-topic-physc-mech-drills', 9, false, 'C', '2D Projectile Impact Speed', 'medium', 'A stone is thrown horizontally with initial speed $30\text{ m/s}$ from a bridge. Find the stone''s total speed when it enters the water $4\text{ s}$ later ($g = 10\text{ m/s}^2$).', '["$30\\text{ m/s}$","$40\\text{ m/s}$","$50\\text{ m/s}$","$60\\text{ m/s}$","$70\\text{ m/s}$"]'::jsonb, '(C) Horizontal velocity: $v_x = 30\text{ m/s}$ (constant).  
Vertical velocity: $v_y = gt = (10\text{ m/s}^2)(4\text{ s}) = 40\text{ m/s}$.  
Magnitude: $v = \sqrt{v_x^2 + v_y^2} = \sqrt{30^2 + 40^2} = 50\text{ m/s}$.'),
  ('ap-topic-physc-mech-drills_q10', 'ap-topic-physc-mech-drills', 10, false, 'E', 'Projectile Motion Vertical vs Horizontal Velocity', 'easy', 'Which statement is true concerning the motion of an ideal projectile launched at $45^\circ$?', '["The acceleration vector points opposite to velocity on ascent and along velocity on descent.","The speed at the top of the trajectory is zero.","Total speed remains constant throughout.","Horizontal speed decreases ascending and increases descending.","Vertical speed decreases on the way up and increases on the way down."]'::jsonb, '(E) Because the downward gravitational acceleration opposes upward vertical velocity during ascent, vertical speed $|v_y|$ decreases to zero at the apex, then increases continuously as the projectile falls. Horizontal speed remains strictly constant throughout.'),
  ('ap-topic-physc-mech-drills_q11', 'ap-topic-physc-mech-drills', 11, false, 'A', 'Trajectory Equation & Wall Clearance Angle', 'hard', 'An object is launched from the ground with speed $v$ at distance $d$ away from a wall of height $h$. Which equation gives the launch angle $\theta$ to just clear the wall?', '["$h = d\\tan\\theta - \\frac{gd^2}{2v^2\\cos^2\\theta}$","$h = d^2\\cos\\theta - \\frac{gd^2}{2v^2\\cos^2\\theta}$","$h = d\\tan\\theta - \\frac{gd^2}{2v\\cos\\theta}$","$h = d\\sin\\theta - \\frac{gd^2}{v^2\\cos^2\\theta}$","$h = d\\sin\\theta - \\frac{gd^2}{2v\\cos\\theta}$"]'::jsonb, '(A) Horizontal time to reach the wall: $t = \frac{d}{v\cos\theta}$.
Substituting into vertical position $y(t) = (v\sin\theta)t - \frac{1}{2}gt^2$:
  $$h = (v\sin\theta)\left(\frac{d}{v\cos\theta}\right) - \frac{1}{2}g\left(\frac{d}{v\cos\theta}\right)^2 = d\tan\theta - \frac{gd^2}{2v^2\cos^2\theta}$$'),
  ('ap-topic-physc-mech-drills_q12', 'ap-topic-physc-mech-drills', 12, false, 'D', 'Velocity-Time Graph & Position Integral', 'medium', 'An object moves in a straight line with a given velocity-time graph. If $x = 3.0\text{ m}$ at $t = 0$, what is the position at $t = 3.0\text{ s}$ if the area under the $v\text{--}t$ curve from $0$ to $3\text{ s}$ is $8\text{ m}$?', '["$5\\text{ m}$","$8\\text{ m}$","$10\\text{ m}$","$11\\text{ m}$","$14\\text{ m}$"]'::jsonb, '(D) Displacement is the definite integral of velocity: $\Delta x = \int_0^3 v(t)\,dt = 8\text{ m}$.
  $$x(3) = x(0) + \Delta x = 3.0\text{ m} + 8\text{ m} = 11\text{ m}$$'),
  ('ap-topic-physc-mech-drills_q13', 'ap-topic-physc-mech-drills', 13, false, 'E', 'Newton''s Third Law Action-Reaction Pairs', 'easy', 'A box is dragged across a table at constant velocity by a string. Which of the following is a Newton''s Third Law action-reaction pair?  I. Force of gravity on the box and normal force on the box  II. Force of friction on the box and tension force on the box  III. Force of the box pushing down on the table and normal force of the table pushing up on the box', '["I only","I and II only","II only","I and III only","III only"]'::jsonb, '(E) An action-reaction pair must involve mutual forces of the same physical type exerted between the same two objects in opposite directions. The table pushing up on the box ($F_{N,\text{table on box}}$) and the box pushing down on the table ($F_{N,\text{box on table}}$) act on each other, satisfying Newton''s Third Law.'),
  ('ap-topic-physc-mech-drills_q14', 'ap-topic-physc-mech-drills', 14, false, 'D', 'Apparent Weight in Accelerating Elevator', 'easy', 'A person who weighs $800\text{ N}$ steps onto a scale in an elevator accelerating upward at $5\text{ m/s}^2$. What will the scale read? ($g = 10\text{ m/s}^2$)', '["$400\\text{ N}$","$800\\text{ N}$","$1,000\\text{ N}$","$1,200\\text{ N}$","$1,600\\text{ N}$"]'::jsonb, '(D) Mass $m = \frac{W}{g} = \frac{800\text{ N}}{10\text{ m/s}^2} = 80\text{ kg}$.
Using Newton''s Second Law with upward as positive:
  $$F_N - mg = ma \implies F_N = m(g + a) = (80\text{ kg})(10 + 5\text{ m/s}^2) = 1,200\text{ N}$$'),
  ('ap-topic-physc-mech-drills_q15', 'ap-topic-physc-mech-drills', 15, false, 'D', 'Two-Block System Tension & Acceleration', 'medium', 'A horizontal force of $20\text{ N}$ pulls on a $1.0\text{ kg}$ block attached by a string to a $2.0\text{ kg}$ block on a frictionless surface. Find the tension $F_T$ in the connecting string and the acceleration $a$ of the system.', '["$F_T = 13.3\\text{ N},\\ a = 10\\text{ m/s}^2$","$F_T = 13.3\\text{ N},\\ a = 20\\text{ m/s}^2$","$F_T = 15\\text{ N},\\ a = 6.7\\text{ m/s}^2$","$F_T = 13.3\\text{ N},\\ a = 6.7\\text{ m/s}^2$","$F_T = 20\\text{ N},\\ a = 6.7\\text{ m/s}^2$"]'::jsonb, '(D) System acceleration:
  $$a = \frac{F_{\text{applied}}}{m_1 + m_2} = \frac{20\text{ N}}{1.0 + 2.0\text{ kg}} = \frac{20}{3}\text{ m/s}^2 \approx 6.7\text{ m/s}^2$$
Tension pulling the trailing $2.0\text{ kg}$ block:
  $$F_T = m_2 a = (2.0\text{ kg})\left(\frac{20}{3}\text{ m/s}^2\right) = \frac{40}{3}\text{ N} \approx 13.3\text{ N}$$'),
  ('ap-topic-physc-mech-drills_q16', 'ap-topic-physc-mech-drills', 16, false, 'C', 'Three-Block Frictionless Train Tensions', 'medium', 'A force of $20\text{ N}$ pulls a chain of three frictionless blocks: $3.0\text{ kg} - 2.0\text{ kg} - 1.0\text{ kg}$. Find $F_{T1}$ (between $1\text{ kg}$ and $2\text{ kg}$), $F_{T2}$ (between $2\text{ kg}$ and $3\text{ kg}$), and acceleration $a$.', '["$F_{T1} = 1\\text{ N},\\ a = 1\\text{ m/s}^2$","$F_{T1} = 3.3\\text{ N},\\ F_{T2} = 3.3\\text{ N},\\ a = 3.3\\text{ m/s}^2$","$F_{T1} = 3.3\\text{ N},\\ F_{T2} = 10\\text{ N},\\ a = 3.3\\text{ m/s}^2$","$F_{T1} = 3.3\\text{ N},\\ F_{T2} = 10\\text{ N},\\ a = 6.7\\text{ m/s}^2$","$F_{T1} = 10\\text{ N},\\ a = 6.7\\text{ m/s}^2$"]'::jsonb, '(C) $$a = \frac{20\text{ N}}{1.0 + 2.0 + 3.0\text{ kg}} = \frac{20}{6}\text{ m/s}^2 = 3.33\text{ m/s}^2$$
$$F_{T1} = m_1 a = (1.0\text{ kg})(3.33\text{ m/s}^2) = 3.33\text{ N}$$
$$F_{T2} = (m_1 + m_2)a = (1.0 + 2.0\text{ kg})(3.33\text{ m/s}^2) = 10.0\text{ N}$$'),
  ('ap-topic-physc-mech-drills_q17', 'ap-topic-physc-mech-drills', 17, false, 'A', 'Incline Static Friction Threshold', 'medium', 'A box is placed at rest on a ramp ($\theta = 30^\circ, \mu_s = 0.5$). The box will:', '["Accelerate down the ramp","Accelerate briefly then stop","Move with constant velocity","Not move","Cannot be determined"]'::jsonb, '(A) Downhill gravitational component: $F_{g,\parallel} = mg\sin(30^\circ) = 0.50\,mg$.
Maximum static friction force:
  $$f_{s,\max} = \mu_s mg\cos(30^\circ) = (0.5)(0.866\,mg) \approx 0.433\,mg$$
Because $F_{g,\parallel} > f_{s,\max}$ ($0.50\,mg > 0.433\,mg$), the net force down the ramp is positive, and the box will accelerate down.'),
  ('ap-topic-physc-mech-drills_q18', 'ap-topic-physc-mech-drills', 18, false, 'C', 'Atwood Machine System Acceleration', 'easy', 'In an Atwood machine with hanging masses $m$ and $4m$, find the acceleration.', '["$0.2g$","$0.4g$","$0.6g$","$0.75g$","$0.8g$"]'::jsonb, '(C) $$a = \frac{m_2 - m_1}{m_1 + m_2}g = \frac{4m - m}{4m + m}g = \frac{3}{5}g = 0.6g$$'),
  ('ap-topic-physc-mech-drills_q19', 'ap-topic-physc-mech-drills', 19, false, 'E', 'Two-Block System with Kinetic Friction', 'medium', 'A $20\text{ N}$ force pulls a $2.0\text{ kg}$ block connected to a $1.0\text{ kg}$ trailing block on a rough surface ($\mu_k = 0.2$). Find the tension $F_T$ and acceleration $a$ ($g = 10\text{ m/s}^2$).', '["$F_T = 1\\text{ N},\\ a = 4.7\\text{ m/s}^2$","$F_T = 3.3\\text{ N},\\ a = 4.7\\text{ m/s}^2$","$F_T = 3.3\\text{ N},\\ a = 6.7\\text{ m/s}^2$","$F_T = 6.7\\text{ N},\\ a = 6.7\\text{ m/s}^2$","$F_T = 6.7\\text{ N},\\ a = 4.7\\text{ m/s}^2$"]'::jsonb, '(E) Total friction force: $f_{\text{total}} = \mu_k(m_1 + m_2)g = (0.2)(3.0\text{ kg})(10\text{ m/s}^2) = 6\text{ N}$.
Acceleration: $a = \frac{F - f_{\text{total}}}{m_1 + m_2} = \frac{20 - 6}{3} = \frac{14}{3} \approx 4.7\text{ m/s}^2$.
Tension on the $1.0\text{ kg}$ trailing block:
  $$F_T - f_1 = m_1 a \implies F_T = (0.2)(1.0)(10) + (1.0)(4.7) = 2.0 + 4.7 = 6.7\text{ N}$$'),
  ('ap-topic-physc-mech-drills_q20', 'ap-topic-physc-mech-drills', 20, false, 'B', 'Incline Gravitational Scaling', 'easy', 'An object slides down a frictionless incline of angle $\theta$ and reaches final speed $v$. On a planet with twice Earth''s gravitational acceleration ($2g$), what will be its final speed?', '["$v$","$\\sqrt{2}v$","$2v$","$4v$","$v/2$"]'::jsonb, '(B) Using $v = \sqrt{2ad} = \sqrt{2(g\sin\theta)d} \propto \sqrt{g}$. Doubling $g$ scales the final velocity by a factor of $\sqrt{2}$, giving $v_{\text{new}} = \sqrt{2}v$.'),
  ('ap-topic-physc-mech-drills_q21', 'ap-topic-physc-mech-drills', 21, false, 'B', 'Vertical Loop Minimum Cut-Off Speed', 'medium', 'A roller coaster completes a loop of radius $r = 25\text{ m}$. What is the minimum speed needed at the top of the loop for riders to remain safely in contact with the track? ($g = 10\text{ m/s}^2$)', '["$12.7\\text{ m/s}$","$15.8\\text{ m/s}$","$21.2\\text{ m/s}$","$29.3\\text{ m/s}$","$33.3\\text{ m/s}$"]'::jsonb, '(B) At critical cut-off speed, normal force $F_N \to 0$, so gravity alone provides the centripetal acceleration:
  $$\frac{mv^2}{r} = mg \implies v = \sqrt{gr} = \sqrt{(10\text{ m/s}^2)(25\text{ m})} = \sqrt{250} \approx 15.8\text{ m/s}$$'),
  ('ap-topic-physc-mech-drills_q22', 'ap-topic-physc-mech-drills', 22, false, 'B', 'Vertical Circular Motion Bottom Tension', 'medium', 'A bucket of mass $3\text{ kg}$ on a $0.6\text{-m}$ rope is whirled in a vertical circle. At the lowest point, the tension is $50\text{ N}$. What is the speed of the bucket? ($g = 10\text{ m/s}^2$)', '["$1\\text{ m/s}$","$2\\text{ m/s}$","$3\\text{ m/s}$","$4\\text{ m/s}$","$5\\text{ m/s}$"]'::jsonb, '(B) At the bottom of the circle: $F_T - mg = \frac{mv^2}{r}$.
  $$50\text{ N} - (3\text{ kg})(10\text{ m/s}^2) = \frac{3 v^2}{0.6} \implies 20 = 5v^2 \implies v^2 = 4 \implies v = 2\text{ m/s}$$'),
  ('ap-topic-physc-mech-drills_q23', 'ap-topic-physc-mech-drills', 23, false, 'C', 'Vertical String Circle Critical Apex Speed', 'medium', 'What is the critical speed at the top of the vertical circle for the bucket in Question 10 so the rope does not become slack?', '["$0.6\\text{ m/s}$","$1.8\\text{ m/s}$","$2.4\\text{ m/s}$","$3.2\\text{ m/s}$","$4.8\\text{ m/s}$"]'::jsonb, '(C) $$v_{\text{critical}} = \sqrt{gr} = \sqrt{(10\text{ m/s}^2)(0.60\text{ m})} = \sqrt{6.0} \approx 2.45\text{ m/s} \approx 2.4\text{ m/s}$$'),
  ('ap-topic-physc-mech-drills_q24', 'ap-topic-physc-mech-drills', 24, false, 'D', 'Centripetal Acceleration & Angular Frequency', 'easy', 'An object moves at constant speed in a circular path of radius $r$ at 1 revolution per second. What is its centripetal acceleration?', '["$2\\pi r$","$2\\pi^2 r$","$2\\pi^2 r^2$","$4\\pi^2 r$","$4\\pi^2 r^2$"]'::jsonb, '(D) With angular frequency $\omega = 2\pi f = 2\pi(1) = 2\pi\text{ rad/s}$:
  $$a_c = \omega^2 r = (2\pi)^2 r = 4\pi^2 r$$'),
  ('ap-topic-physc-mech-drills_q25', 'ap-topic-physc-mech-drills', 25, false, 'A', 'Perpendicular Force & Zero Work', 'easy', 'A force $F = 20\text{ N}$ acts on a $3\text{ kg}$ object as it moves $4\text{ m}$. If $F$ is perpendicular to displacement, the work done is:', '["$0\\text{ J}$","$60\\text{ J}$","$80\\text{ J}$","$600\\text{ J}$","$2,400\\text{ J}$"]'::jsonb, '(A) $W = \vec{F}\cdot\vec{d} = F d \cos(90^\circ) = 0\text{ J}$. Any force acting strictly perpendicular to displacement performs zero work.'),
  ('ap-topic-physc-mech-drills_q26', 'ap-topic-physc-mech-drills', 26, false, 'B', 'Work-Energy Theorem & Kinetic Energy Change', 'easy', 'An object of mass $4\text{ kg}$ accelerates from $3\text{ m/s}$ to $6\text{ m/s}$ in $8\text{ s}$. How much work was done on the object?', '["$27\\text{ J}$","$54\\text{ J}$","$72\\text{ J}$","$96\\text{ J}$","Cannot be determined"]'::jsonb, '(B) By the Work-Energy Theorem ($W = \Delta K$):
  $$W = \frac{1}{2}m(v_f^2 - v_i^2) = \frac{1}{2}(4\text{ kg})((6\text{ m/s})^2 - (3\text{ m/s})^2) = 2(36 - 9) = 2(27) = 54\text{ J}$$'),
  ('ap-topic-physc-mech-drills_q27', 'ap-topic-physc-mech-drills', 27, false, 'D', 'Path Independence & Conservative Gravitational Work', 'easy', 'To lift a box of mass $m$ to height $h$, three options are considered: Option 1 (direct lift), Option 2 ($30^\circ$ incline), Option 3 (pulley system). Compare the work done under ideal conditions.', '["Option 1 > Option 2 > Option 3","Option 1 > Option 2 = Option 3","Option 1 = Option 3 > Option 2","Option 1 = Option 2 = Option 3","Cannot be determined"]'::jsonb, '(D) Under ideal conservative conditions, gravitational potential energy is path-independent. In all three methods, the minimum work required is identical: $W = mgh$.'),
  ('ap-topic-physc-mech-drills_q28', 'ap-topic-physc-mech-drills', 28, false, 'E', 'Normal Force Zero Work on Incline', 'easy', 'A $4\text{ kg}$ box is pulled up a ramp ($\theta = 30^\circ$, height $= 5\text{ m}$) at constant velocity. How much work is done by the normal force?', '["$160\\text{ J}$","$80\\text{ J}$","$40\\text{ J}$","$20\\text{ J}$","$0\\text{ J}$"]'::jsonb, '(E) The normal force is perpendicular to the ramp surface and therefore perpendicular to the displacement vector of the box ($\theta = 90^\circ$). Thus $W_N = F_N d \cos(90^\circ) = 0\text{ J}$.'),
  ('ap-topic-physc-mech-drills_q29', 'ap-topic-physc-mech-drills', 29, false, 'A', 'Elevator Normal Force Positive Work', 'easy', 'A man stands in an elevator as it begins to ascend. Does the normal force from the floor do work on the man?', '["Yes, and the work done will be positive.","Yes, and the work done will be negative.","Yes, but the sign cannot be determined.","No, the normal force does no work in any situation.","No, normal force does no work here."]'::jsonb, '(A) The normal force pushes upward on the man''s feet ($\vec{F}_N$ is upward) and the elevator displacement is upward ($\vec{d}$ is upward). Because $\vec{F}_N$ and $\vec{d}$ point in the same direction ($\theta = 0^\circ$), $W = F_N d \cos(0^\circ) > 0$ (positive work).'),
  ('ap-topic-physc-mech-drills_q30', 'ap-topic-physc-mech-drills', 30, false, 'D', 'Frictionless Incline Energy Conservation', 'medium', 'A $3.5\text{ kg}$ block slides from rest down a frictionless $30^\circ$ incline of length $6.4\text{ m}$. What is its speed at the bottom? ($g = 10\text{ m/s}^2$)', '["$5.0\\text{ m/s}$","$5.7\\text{ m/s}$","$6.4\\text{ m/s}$","$8.0\\text{ m/s}$","$10\\text{ m/s}$"]'::jsonb, '(D) Vertical height of the incline: $h = L\sin(30^\circ) = 6.4(0.5) = 3.2\text{ m}$.
By Conservation of Energy:
  $$mgh = \frac{1}{2}mv^2 \implies v = \sqrt{2gh} = \sqrt{2(10)(3.2)} = \sqrt{64} = 8.0\text{ m/s}$$'),
  ('ap-topic-physc-mech-drills_q31', 'ap-topic-physc-mech-drills', 31, false, 'D', 'Incline Kinetic Energy with Friction Work', 'medium', 'A block of mass $m$ slides from rest down an incline of length $s$ and height $h$ with kinetic friction force $F_f$. What is the kinetic energy at the bottom?', '["$mgh$","$mgh - F_f h$","$mgs - F_f h$","$mgh - F_f s$","$mgs - F_f s$"]'::jsonb, '(D) Applying the Work-Energy Theorem ($E_i + W_{\text{other}} = E_f$):
  $$mgh - F_f s = K_f \implies K_f = mgh - F_f s$$'),
  ('ap-topic-physc-mech-drills_q32', 'ap-topic-physc-mech-drills', 32, false, 'C', 'Newton''s Second Law with Aerodynamic Drag', 'easy', 'A $1,000\text{ kg}$ car accelerates at $4\text{ m/s}^2$ while experiencing $300\text{ N}$ of drag force. How much force must the engine produce?', '["$16,300\\text{ N}$","$15,700\\text{ N}$","$4,300\\text{ N}$","$4,000\\text{ N}$","$3,700\\text{ N}$"]'::jsonb, '(C) $$F_{\text{engine}} - F_{\text{drag}} = ma \implies F_{\text{engine}} = ma + F_{\text{drag}} = (1,000\text{ kg})(4\text{ m/s}^2) + 300\text{ N} = 4,300\text{ N}$$'),
  ('ap-topic-physc-mech-drills_q33', 'ap-topic-physc-mech-drills', 33, false, 'E', 'Gravitational Free Fall Midpoint Speed Fraction', 'medium', 'An astronaut drops a rock from the top of a crater. When the rock is halfway down to the bottom, its speed is what fraction of its impact speed?', '["$1/4$","$1/2$","$1/\\sqrt{3}$","$\\sqrt{2}/3$","$1/\\sqrt{2}$"]'::jsonb, '(E) At halfway point $h/2$, kinetic energy is half the final kinetic energy: $K_{\text{mid}} = \frac{1}{2}K_{\text{final}}$.
  $$\frac{1}{2}mv_{\text{mid}}^2 = \frac{1}{2}\left(\frac{1}{2}mv_{\text{final}}^2\right) \implies v_{\text{mid}} = \frac{1}{\sqrt{2}}v_{\text{final}}$$'),
  ('ap-topic-physc-mech-drills_q34', 'ap-topic-physc-mech-drills', 34, false, 'D', 'Incline Constant Power Mechanical Derivation', 'hard', 'A block of mass $m$ is pulled up a ramp of length $d$, angle $\theta$, and friction coefficient $\mu$ by a motor at constant power in time $t$. What is the minimum power $P$?', '["$\\frac{md^2}{t^3} + \\frac{mgd\\sin\\theta}{t}$","$\\frac{md^2}{t^2} + mgd\\sin\\theta + \\mu mgd\\cos\\theta$","$\\frac{md^2}{t^3} + \\frac{mgd\\sin\\theta}{t} + \\frac{\\mu mgd\\cos\\theta}{t}$","$\\frac{2md^2}{t^3} + \\frac{mgd\\sin\\theta}{t} + \\frac{\\mu mgd\\cos\\theta}{t}$","$\\frac{md^2}{2t^3} + \\frac{mgd\\cos\\theta}{t} + \\frac{\\mu mgd\\sin\\theta}{t}$"]'::jsonb, '(D) Under constant acceleration to cover distance $d$ in time $t$, $d = \frac{1}{2}at^2 \implies a = \frac{2d}{t^2}$ and $v_f = \frac{2d}{t}$.
Total work done:
  $$W = \Delta K + \Delta U + W_{\text{friction}} = \frac{1}{2}m\left(\frac{2d}{t}\right)^2 + mg(d\sin\theta) + (\mu mg\cos\theta)d$$
Power $P = W/t$:
  $$P = \frac{2md^2}{t^3} + \frac{mgd\sin\theta}{t} + \frac{\mu mgd\cos\theta}{t}$$'),
  ('ap-topic-physc-em-drills_q1', 'ap-topic-physc-em-drills', 1, false, 'A', 'Coulomb''s Law Scaling Factor', 'easy', 'What will happen to the magnitude of electric force between two particles if the distance between them is doubled and each charge is tripled?', '["Multiplied by a factor of $9/4$","Multiplied by a factor of $3/2$","Multiplied by a factor of $3/4$","Multiplied by a factor of $9/2$","Multiplied by a factor of $6/4$"]'::jsonb, '(A) From Coulomb''s Law, $F = \frac{1}{4\pi\varepsilon_0}\frac{q_1 q_2}{r^2}$. Tripling both charges scales the numerator by $3 \times 3 = 9$. Doubling the separation distance scales the denominator by $2^2 = 4$. The resulting electrostatic force is multiplied by a factor of $9/4$.'),
  ('ap-topic-physc-em-drills_q2', 'ap-topic-physc-em-drills', 2, false, 'C', 'Negative Charge Kinematics in Electric Field', 'medium', 'A particle ($m = 10^{-10}\text{ kg}, q = -5 \times 10^{-13}\text{ C}$) starts at rest inside a uniform electric field $E = 50\text{ N/C}$ directed toward the left in a $1\text{-m}$ square region, positioned $70\text{ cm}$ from the left edge and $30\text{ cm}$ from the right edge. How long will it take to escape the field?', '["$1.10\\text{ s}$","$1.23\\text{ s}$","$1.55\\text{ s}$","$2.31\\text{ s}$","$2.59\\text{ s}$"]'::jsonb, '(C) Because the charge is negative, the electric force acts to the right, opposite to $\vec{E}$. The distance to escape out the right side is $d = 30\text{ cm} = 0.30\text{ m}$.
  $$a = \frac{|q|E}{m} = \frac{(5 \times 10^{-13}\text{ C})(50\text{ N/C})}{10^{-10}\text{ kg}} = \frac{2.5 \times 10^{-11}}{10^{-10}} = 0.25\text{ m/s}^2$$
  $$d = \frac{1}{2}at^2 \implies t = \sqrt{\frac{2d}{a}} = \sqrt{\frac{2(0.30\text{ m})}{0.25\text{ m/s}^2}} = \sqrt{2.4} \approx 1.55\text{ s}$$'),
  ('ap-topic-physc-em-drills_q3', 'ap-topic-physc-em-drills', 3, false, 'D', 'Electric Field Superposition & Tripling', 'medium', 'Points A and B are equidistant from point P. At A, a charge $+Q$ creates field $\vec{E}$ pointing to the right at P. What charge placed at B will triple the net electric field at P?', '["$+2Q$","$+3Q$","$-Q$","$-2Q$","$-3Q$"]'::jsonb, '(D) To amplify the field at P to $3E$ in the same direction, the charge at B must contribute an electric field of magnitude $2E$ directed toward B (to the right). A negative charge attracts a positive test charge, so the charge must be negative with twice the magnitude: $q_B = -2Q$.'),
  ('ap-topic-physc-em-drills_q4', 'ap-topic-physc-em-drills', 4, false, 'D', 'Repulsive Coulomb Force Kinematics', 'easy', 'A fixed sphere $+Q$ repels a small movable sphere $+q$ released from rest. The small sphere moves away with:', '["decreasing velocity and decreasing acceleration","decreasing velocity and increasing acceleration","decreasing velocity and constant acceleration","increasing velocity and decreasing acceleration","increasing velocity and increasing acceleration"]'::jsonb, '(D) As $r$ increases, the repulsive force $F = \frac{kQq}{r^2}$ and acceleration $a = F/m$ decrease toward zero. Because acceleration remains strictly positive ($a > 0$), the speed and velocity continue to increase ($dv/dt > 0$).'),
  ('ap-topic-physc-em-drills_q5', 'ap-topic-physc-em-drills', 5, false, 'A', 'Electrostatic Potential Energy Turnaround', 'hard', 'A particle of charge $q = 1\ \mu\text{C}$ is fixed at the origin. An object ($m = 10^{-3}\text{ kg}, q = 1\ \mu\text{C}$) is released from rest $1\text{ m}$ directly above it. How far does the object fall before coming momentarily to rest ($g = 10\text{ m/s}^2$)?', '["$0.1\\text{ m}$","$0.3\\text{ m}$","$0.5\\text{ m}$","$0.7\\text{ m}$","$0.9\\text{ m}$"]'::jsonb, '(A) At the turnaround point (fall distance $x$), $\Delta K = 0$, so net work done by gravity and electrostatic repulsion is zero:
  $$W_g + W_E = 0 \implies mgx - k q^2 \left(\frac{1}{1-x} - 1\right) = 0 \implies mgx = \frac{k q^2 x}{1-x}$$
  $$1 - x = \frac{k q^2}{mg} = \frac{(9 \times 10^9)(10^{-6})^2}{(10^{-3})(10)} = \frac{9 \times 10^{-3}}{10^{-2}} = 0.9 \implies x = 1 - 0.9 = 0.1\text{ m}$$'),
  ('ap-topic-physc-em-drills_q6', 'ap-topic-physc-em-drills', 6, false, 'B', 'Gauss''s Law Flux vs. Point Field Superposition', 'medium', 'Charges $Q_2, Q_3$ are inside a Gaussian surface while $Q_1, Q_4$ are outside. Point P is on the surface. Which is true?', '["Flux depends on all four; $\\vec{E}$ at P depends on $Q_2, Q_3$.","Flux depends only on $Q_2, Q_3$; $\\vec{E}$ at P depends on all four.","Flux depends on $Q_2, Q_3$; $\\vec{E}$ at P depends on $Q_1, Q_2, Q_3$.","Flux depends on $Q_1, Q_4$; $\\vec{E}$ at P depends on $Q_2, Q_3$.","Both flux and $\\vec{E}$ depend on all four."]'::jsonb, '(B) By Gauss''s Law, total enclosed flux $\Phi_E = \frac{Q_{\text{enc}}}{\varepsilon_0} = \frac{Q_2 + Q_3}{\varepsilon_0}$ depends only on internal charges. By the principle of linear superposition, the local electric field vector $\vec{E}$ at any spatial point P is the vector sum of fields produced by all charges present ($Q_1, Q_2, Q_3, Q_4$).'),
  ('ap-topic-physc-em-drills_q7', 'ap-topic-physc-em-drills', 7, false, 'A', 'Uniformly Charged Sphere Interior Field', 'hard', 'A nonconducting solid sphere of radius $R$ carries uniform charge $-Q$. What is the electric field magnitude at distance $r < R$ from the center?', '["$\\frac{Q r}{4\\pi\\varepsilon_0 R^3}$","$\\frac{Q}{4\\pi\\varepsilon_0 R^2}$","$\\frac{Q}{4\\pi\\varepsilon_0 R^3}$","$\\frac{Q}{4\\pi\\varepsilon_0 R^3 r}$","$\\frac{Q}{4\\pi\\varepsilon_0 r^2}$"]'::jsonb, '(A) Enclosed charge at radius $r < R$: $Q_{\text{enc}} = -Q\left(\frac{r^3}{R^3}\right)$. Applying Gauss''s Law:
  $$E(4\pi r^2) = \frac{Q(r^3/R^3)}{\varepsilon_0} \implies E(r) = \frac{Q r}{4\pi\varepsilon_0 R^3}$$'),
  ('ap-topic-physc-em-drills_q8', 'ap-topic-physc-em-drills', 8, false, 'A', 'Gaussian Surface Enclosing Electric Dipole', 'easy', 'Calculate the electric flux through a Gaussian surface enclosing an electric dipole of charge magnitude $q$.', '["$0$","$\\frac{Aq}{4\\pi\\varepsilon_0}$","$\\frac{Aq^2}{4\\pi\\varepsilon_0}$","$\\frac{Aq}{4\\pi\\varepsilon_0 r}$","$\\frac{Aq}{4\\pi\\varepsilon_0 r^2}$"]'::jsonb, '(A) A dipole consists of equal and opposite charges $+q$ and $-q$. Total enclosed net charge $Q_{\text{enc}} = +q + (-q) = 0$. By Gauss''s Law, $\Phi_E = \frac{Q_{\text{enc}}}{\varepsilon_0} = 0$.'),
  ('ap-topic-physc-em-drills_q9', 'ap-topic-physc-em-drills', 9, false, 'C', 'Equipotential Work & Potential Difference', 'medium', 'An electron moves from point A ($-10\text{ V}$) to E ($-10\text{ V}$), then from E to C ($0\text{ V}$). Describe the work done by the electric field for each step.', '["Negative; Positive","Zero; Positive","Zero; Negative","Positive; Zero","Positive; Positive"]'::jsonb, '(C) From A to E: $\Delta V = 0\text{ V}$ (equipotential line), so $W_E = -q\Delta V = 0\text{ J}$.  
From E to C: $\Delta V = 0 - (-10) = +10\text{ V}$. Work done by the electric field on the negative electron: $W_E = -q\Delta V = -(-e)(+10\text{ V}) = -10\text{ eV}$ (negative work).'),
  ('ap-topic-physc-em-drills_q10', 'ap-topic-physc-em-drills', 10, false, 'B', 'Work by Conservative Electric Field', 'easy', 'How much work does the electric field do moving charge $q = -0.05\text{ C}$ from $V_A = 200\text{ V}$ to $V_B = 100\text{ V}$ along a curved path?', '["$-5\\text{ J}$","$+5\\text{ J}$","$-10\\text{ J}$","$+10\\text{ J}$","$0\\text{ J}$"]'::jsonb, '(B) $$W_E = -q(V_B - V_A) = -(-0.05\text{ C})(100\text{ V} - 200\text{ V}) = +0.05(-100) \implies W_E = -\Delta U = +5\text{ J}$$'),
  ('ap-topic-physc-em-drills_q11', 'ap-topic-physc-em-drills', 11, false, 'E', 'Capacitor Dielectric Insertion & Stored Energy', 'easy', 'A capacitor is fully charged by a battery, disconnected, and then a dielectric is inserted. Which statements are true?  I. Voltage remains the same.  II. Potential energy increases.  III. Capacitance increases.', '["I only","I and II only","I and III only","II and III only","III only"]'::jsonb, '(E) With the battery disconnected, stored charge $Q$ is constant. Inserting a dielectric increases capacitance ($C = \kappa C_0$, III is true). Voltage decreases ($V = Q/C$, I is false), and stored energy decreases ($U = Q^2/(2C)$, II is false).'),
  ('ap-topic-physc-em-drills_q12', 'ap-topic-physc-em-drills', 12, false, 'C', 'Electric Potential Energy Sign Definition', 'easy', 'If the electric field does negative work on a negative charge moving from A to B, the electrical potential energy:', '["is negative","is positive","increases","decreases","cannot be determined"]'::jsonb, '(C) By definition, $\Delta U_E = -W_E$. If $W_E < 0$, then $\Delta U_E > 0$, meaning the potential energy increases.'),
  ('ap-topic-physc-em-drills_q13', 'ap-topic-physc-em-drills', 13, false, 'D', 'Parallel Capacitors Individual Stored Energy', 'easy', 'Three $6\ \mu\text{F}$ capacitors are connected in parallel across a $9\text{ V}$ battery. Determine the energy stored in each individual capacitor.', '["$2.43\\text{ J}$","$7.29 \\times 10^{-4}\\text{ J}$","$8.10 \\times 10^{-5}\\text{ J}$","$2.43 \\times 10^{-4}\\text{ J}$","$27\\text{ J}$"]'::jsonb, '(D) Each parallel capacitor receives the full $9\text{ V}$ potential difference:
  $$U = \frac{1}{2}C V^2 = \frac{1}{2}(6 \times 10^{-6}\text{ F})(9\text{ V})^2 = (3 \times 10^{-6})(81) = 2.43 \times 10^{-4}\text{ J}$$'),
  ('ap-topic-physc-em-drills_q14', 'ap-topic-physc-em-drills', 14, false, 'C', 'Equipotential Surfaces in Uniform Field', 'easy', 'In a uniform electric field between parallel plates, which points lie on the same equipotential?', '["1 and 2 only","1 and 3 only","2 and 4 only","3 and 4 only","All points lie on same equipotential"]'::jsonb, '(C) Equipotential surfaces in a uniform electric field are parallel planes perpendicular to the field lines. Points located at the same perpendicular distance from the plates share the identical electric potential.')
ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.ap_exam_questions (id, test_id, question_order, is_frq, answer_key, topic, difficulty, question_text, options_json, explanation)
VALUES
  ('ap-topic-physc-em-drills_q15', 'ap-topic-physc-em-drills', 15, false, 'C', 'Connected Conducting Spheres Charge Equilibrium', 'medium', 'Isolated conducting spheres of radius $a$ (Sphere 1) and $4a$ (Sphere 2) each carry charge $-Q$. Connected by a thin wire, what are the final charges?', '["$-Q,\\ -Q$","$-2Q/3,\\ -4Q/3$","$-2Q/5,\\ -8Q/5$","$-4Q/5,\\ -6Q/5$","$-Q/2,\\ -3Q/2$"]'::jsonb, '(C) Total charge $Q_{\text{tot}} = -2Q$. When connected, potential equalizes: $V_1 = V_2 \implies \frac{k q_1}{a} = \frac{k q_2}{4a} \implies q_2 = 4q_1$.
  $$q_1 + 4q_1 = -2Q \implies 5q_1 = -2Q \implies q_1 = -\frac{2}{5}Q,\quad q_2 = -\frac{8}{5}Q$$'),
  ('ap-topic-physc-em-drills_q16', 'ap-topic-physc-em-drills', 16, false, 'A', 'Resistivity & Conductor Cross-Section Ratio', 'easy', 'Brass wire has $4\times$ diameter and $5\times$ resistivity of silver wire of the same length. Compare resistances $R_B$ and $R_S$.', '["$R_B = \\frac{5}{16}R_S$","$R_B = \\frac{5}{4}R_S$","$R_B = \\frac{5}{64}R_S$","$R_B = \\frac{16}{5}R_S$","$R_B = 20 R_S$"]'::jsonb, '(A) Area scales with diameter squared: $A_B = 4^2 A_S = 16 A_S$.
  $$R_B = \frac{\rho_B L}{A_B} = \frac{(5\rho_S)L}{16 A_S} = \frac{5}{16}R_S$$'),
  ('ap-topic-physc-em-drills_q17', 'ap-topic-physc-em-drills', 17, false, 'D', 'Ohm''s Law Proportionality', 'easy', 'Doubling the voltage across an ohmic conductor without changing resistance causes current to:', '["decrease by 4","decrease by 2","remain unchanged","increase by a factor of 2","increase by 4"]'::jsonb, '(D) By Ohm''s Law, $I = V/R$. For constant $R$, current is directly proportional to voltage, doubling when $V$ is doubled.'),
  ('ap-topic-physc-em-drills_q18', 'ap-topic-physc-em-drills', 18, false, 'C', 'Circuit Power & Operating Voltage', 'easy', 'A circuit with total resistance $5\ \Omega$ dissipates $125\text{ W}$ of power. At what voltage is it operating?', '["$5\\text{ V}$","$15\\text{ V}$","$25\\text{ V}$","$45\\text{ V}$","$625\\text{ V}$"]'::jsonb, '(C) $$P = \frac{V^2}{R} \implies V = \sqrt{P R} = \sqrt{(125\text{ W})(5\ \Omega)} = \sqrt{625} = 25\text{ V}$$'),
  ('ap-topic-physc-em-drills_q19', 'ap-topic-physc-em-drills', 19, false, 'A', 'Incandescent Bulb Overvoltage Power', 'easy', 'A $120\text{ V}, 60\text{ W}$ bulb is connected to a $240\text{ V}$ outlet. How bright is the bulb compared to standard operation?', '["4 times as bright","2 times as bright","Same brightness","Half as bright","One-quarter as bright"]'::jsonb, '(A) $$P = \frac{V^2}{R} \propto V^2$$
Doubling the operating voltage quadruples the power dissipated ($2^2 = 4$), making the bulb 4 times as bright.'),
  ('ap-topic-physc-em-drills_q20', 'ap-topic-physc-em-drills', 20, false, 'B', 'Ammeter Construction & Series Resistance', 'easy', 'An ammeter arranged to measure circuit branch current should possess which characteristics?', '["High resistance, in series","Low resistance, in series","High resistance, in parallel","Low resistance, in parallel","Same resistance, in series"]'::jsonb, '(B) Ammeters must be placed in series so the entire branch current passes through them. Their internal resistance must approach zero ($R \approx 0$) so they do not add series resistance or alter the circuit current.'),
  ('ap-topic-physc-em-drills_q21', 'ap-topic-physc-em-drills', 21, false, 'A', 'Magnetic Force Work & Trajectory Properties', 'easy', 'Which statements are true regarding magnetic forces?  I. Magnetic force can never do work on a charged particle.  II. Magnetic force can never change the velocity of a charged particle.  III. A charged particle always experiences a force moving in a magnetic field.', '["I only","I and II only","II and III only","III only","None of the above"]'::jsonb, '(A) $\vec{F}_B = q(\vec{v}\times\vec{B})$ is always perpendicular to $\vec{v}$, so power $P = \vec{F}_B\cdot\vec{v} = 0$ and work is always zero (I is true). It alters direction, thus changing velocity vector $\vec{v}$ (II is false). Moving parallel to $\vec{B}$ produces zero force (III is false).'),
  ('ap-topic-physc-em-drills_q22', 'ap-topic-physc-em-drills', 22, false, 'C', 'Magnetic Lorentz Force & Particle Acceleration', 'medium', 'Particle ($q = 4.0\text{ nC}, m = 2 \times 10^{-4}\text{ kg}$) moves perpendicular to $B = 0.1\text{ T}$ at $3 \times 10^4\text{ m/s}$. What is its acceleration?', '["$0.0006\\text{ m/s}^2$","$0.006\\text{ m/s}^2$","$0.06\\text{ m/s}^2$","$0.6\\text{ m/s}^2$","None of the above"]'::jsonb, '(C) $$F_B = q v B = (4.0 \times 10^{-9}\text{ C})(3 \times 10^4\text{ m/s})(0.1\text{ T}) = 1.2 \times 10^{-5}\text{ N}$$
$$a = \frac{F_B}{m} = \frac{1.2 \times 10^{-5}\text{ N}}{2 \times 10^{-4}\text{ kg}} = 0.06\text{ m/s}^2$$'),
  ('ap-topic-physc-em-drills_q23', 'ap-topic-physc-em-drills', 23, false, 'D', 'Biot-Savart Law Straight Wire Magnetic Field', 'easy', 'Find the magnetic field $2\text{ cm}$ from a long straight wire carrying $10\text{ A}$.', '["$1 \\times 10^{-6}\\text{ T}$","$1 \\times 10^{-5}\\text{ T}$","$2 \\times 10^{-5}\\text{ T}$","$1 \\times 10^{-4}\\text{ T}$","$2 \\times 10^{-4}\\text{ T}$"]'::jsonb, '(D) $$B = \frac{\mu_0 I}{2\pi r} = \frac{(4\pi \times 10^{-7})(10)}{2\pi (0.02)} = \frac{4 \times 10^{-6}}{0.04} = 1.0 \times 10^{-4}\text{ T}$$'),
  ('ap-topic-physc-em-drills_q24', 'ap-topic-physc-em-drills', 24, false, 'E', 'Motional EMF & Endpoint Potential Polarity', 'medium', 'A metal rod of length $L$ is pulled upward with speed $v$ through a uniform magnetic field $B$ pointing out of the page. What is the potential difference between endpoints $a$ (left) and $b$ (right)?', '["$0$","$\\frac{1}{2}vBL$, $a$ higher","$\\frac{1}{2}vBL$, $b$ higher","$vBL$, point $a$ at higher potential","$vBL$, point $b$ at higher potential"]'::jsonb, '(E) By the right-hand rule, $\vec{v}\times\vec{B}$ for upward velocity and out-of-page field points to the right. Mobile electrons are pushed left toward point $a$, leaving positive charge accumulation at point $b$. Point $b$ is at the higher electric potential by $\Delta V = vBL$.'),
  ('ap-topic-physc-em-drills_q25', 'ap-topic-physc-em-drills', 25, false, 'A', 'Motional EMF on Rails & Lenz''s Law Current', 'medium', 'A conducting rod of length $0.2\text{ m}$ and resistance $10\ \Omega$ slides at $3\text{ m/s}$ to the left along rails in $B = 0.5\text{ T}$ directed into the page. What is the magnitude and direction of the induced current?', '["$0.03\\text{ A}$, downward","$0.03\\text{ A}$, upward","$0.3\\text{ A}$, downward","$0.3\\text{ A}$, upward","$0.015\\text{ A}$, downward"]'::jsonb, '(A) $$\mathcal{E} = B L v = (0.5\text{ T})(0.2\text{ m})(3\text{ m/s}) = 0.3\text{ V}$$
$$I = \frac{\mathcal{E}}{R} = \frac{0.3\text{ V}}{10\ \Omega} = 0.03\text{ A}$$
Moving to the left decreases the loop area and inward flux. By Lenz''s Law, the induced current circulates clockwise to produce inward flux, flowing downward through the moving rod.'),
  ('ap-mock-physc-mech-1_q1', 'ap-mock-physc-mech-1', 1, false, 'E', '1D Kinematics & Free Fall', 'medium', 'A rock is dropped off a cliff and falls the first half of the distance to the ground in $t_1$ seconds. If it falls the second half of the distance in $t_2$ seconds, what is the value of $t_2/t_1$? (Ignore air resistance.)', '["$\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{2}}$","$\\sqrt{2}$","$\\frac{1}{2}$","$1 - \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}$","$\\sqrt{2} - 1$"]'::jsonb, '(E) Let the total height fallen be $H$. The time to fall the first half of the distance ($H/2$) is:
  $$\frac{H}{2} = \frac{1}{2}gt_1^2 \implies t_1 = \sqrt{\frac{H}{g}}$$
The total time $T$ to fall the entire height $H$ is:
  $$H = \frac{1}{2}gT^2 \implies T = \sqrt{\frac{2H}{g}}$$
The time required to fall the second half is $t_2 = T - t_1$:
  $$t_2 = \sqrt{\frac{2H}{g}} - \sqrt{\frac{H}{g}} = \sqrt{\frac{H}{g}}(\sqrt{2} - 1)$$
Taking the ratio $t_2/t_1$:
  $$\frac{t_2}{t_1} = \frac{\sqrt{\frac{H}{g}}(\sqrt{2} - 1)}{\sqrt{\frac{H}{g}}} = \sqrt{2} - 1$$'),
  ('ap-mock-physc-mech-1_q2', 'ap-mock-physc-mech-1', 2, false, 'E', '1D Kinematics & Acceleration', 'medium', 'A bubble starting at the bottom of a soda bottle experiences constant acceleration, $a$, as it rises to the top of the bottle in some time, $t$. How much farther does it travel in the last second of its journey than in the first second? Assume that the journey takes longer than 2 seconds.', '["$a(t + 1\\text{ s})^2$","$a(t - 1\\text{ s})^2$","$at^2$","$a(t + 1\\text{ s})(1\\text{ s})$","$a(t - 1\\text{ s})(1\\text{ s})$"]'::jsonb, '(E) The distance traveled in the first second ($t = 0$ to $t = 1\text{ s}$) starting from rest is:
  $$d_1 = \frac{1}{2}a(1)^2 = \frac{1}{2}a$$
The velocity at time $t - 1\text{ s}$ is $v(t-1) = a(t-1)$. The distance traveled during the final second (from $t-1$ to $t$) is:
  $$d_{\text{last}} = v(t-1)(1) + \frac{1}{2}a(1)^2 = a(t - 1) + \frac{1}{2}a$$
The difference between the two distances is:
  $$\Delta d = d_{\text{last}} - d_1 = \left(a(t-1) + \frac{1}{2}a\right) - \frac{1}{2}a = a(t - 1\text{ s})(1\text{ s})$$'),
  ('ap-mock-physc-mech-1_q3', 'ap-mock-physc-mech-1', 3, false, 'A', 'Calculus-Based Kinematics & Integration', 'medium', 'An object initially at rest experiences a time-varying acceleration given by $a = (2\text{ m/s}^3)t$ for $t \ge 0$. How far does the object travel in the first 3 seconds?', '["$9\\text{ m}$","$12\\text{ m}$","$18\\text{ m}$","$24\\text{ m}$","$27\\text{ m}$"]'::jsonb, '(A) Integrate acceleration with respect to time to get velocity ($v_0 = 0$):
  $$v(t) = \int 2t\,dt = t^2$$
Integrate velocity to find the position displacement over the interval $t = 0$ to $t = 3\text{ s}$:
  $$\Delta x = \int_0^3 t^2\,dt = \left[\frac{t^3}{3}\right]_0^3 = \frac{27}{3} = 9\text{ m}$$'),
  ('ap-mock-physc-mech-1_q4', 'ap-mock-physc-mech-1', 4, false, 'A', 'Work, Energy & Inclines', 'easy', 'What is the benefit of raising an object using an inclined plane instead of simply lifting the object? Assume ideal conditions.', '["The amount of force needed to move the object is reduced.","The amount of time needed to move the object is reduced.","The distance the object must be moved is reduced.","The amount of work needed to move the object is reduced.","The power that must be exerted will be reduced."]'::jsonb, '(A) Gravity is a conservative force, so the total work required to raise an object to height $h$ ($W = mgh$) is identical regardless of the path chosen. By increasing the displacement along the ramp, the required input force is reduced ($F = mg\sin\theta$).'),
  ('ap-mock-physc-mech-1_q5', 'ap-mock-physc-mech-1', 5, false, 'C', 'Conical Pendulum & Circular Dynamics', 'hard', 'In the figure shown, a tension force $F_T$ causes a particle of mass $m$ to move with constant angular speed $\omega$ in a horizontal circular path of radius $R$. Which of the following expressions gives the magnitude of $F_T$? (Ignore air resistance.)', '["$m\\omega^2 R$","$m\\sqrt{\\omega^4 R^2 - g^2}$","$m\\sqrt{g^2 + \\omega^4 R^2}$","$m(\\omega^2 R - g)$","$m(\\omega^2 R + g)$"]'::jsonb, '(C) The vertical component of tension balances gravity: $F_{T,y} = F_T \cos\theta = mg$. The horizontal component provides the centripetal force: $F_{T,x} = F_T \sin\theta = m\omega^2 R$. Applying the Pythagorean identity:
  $$F_T^2 = F_{T,x}^2 + F_{T,y}^2 = (m\omega^2 R)^2 + (mg)^2 \implies F_T = m\sqrt{g^2 + \omega^4 R^2}$$'),
  ('ap-mock-physc-mech-1_q6', 'ap-mock-physc-mech-1', 6, false, 'A', 'Planetary Gravitation & Escape Velocity', 'medium', 'An object (mass = $m$) above the surface of the Moon (mass = $M$) is dropped from an altitude $h$ equal to the Moon''s radius ($R$). With what speed will the object strike the lunar surface?', '["$\\sqrt{\\frac{GM}{R}}$","$\\sqrt{\\frac{GM}{2R}}$","$\\sqrt{\\frac{2GM}{R}}$","$\\sqrt{\\frac{GMm}{R}}$","$\\sqrt{\\frac{2GMm}{R}}$"]'::jsonb, '(A) The initial distance from the lunar center is $r_i = R + h = 2R$. The final distance at impact is $r_f = R$. Using Conservation of Mechanical Energy:
  $$E_i = E_f \implies -\frac{GMm}{2R} = \frac{1}{2}mv^2 - \frac{GMm}{R}$$
  $$\frac{1}{2}mv^2 = \frac{GMm}{R} - \frac{GMm}{2R} = \frac{GMm}{2R} \implies v = \sqrt{\frac{GM}{R}}$$'),
  ('ap-mock-physc-mech-1_q7', 'ap-mock-physc-mech-1', 7, false, 'A', 'Simple Harmonic Motion & Pendulums', 'medium', 'Two pendulums are constructed in such a way that they are identical except that one has a hanging mass of $m$ and the other has a hanging mass of $2m$. Both hanging masses are set into motion such that each system has the same total mechanical energy. Assume that the motion approximates simple harmonic motion. Comparatively, the system with the lesser mass will have greater', '["maximum speed and maximum angle of displacement","frequency and maximum angle of displacement","period and maximum speed","frequency only","maximum angle of displacement only"]'::jsonb, '(A) The period $T = 2\pi\sqrt{L/g}$ and frequency $f$ depend only on $L$ and $g$, making them identical for both pendulums. For equal total mechanical energy $E$:
Maximum kinetic energy: $E = \frac{1}{2}m v_{\max}^2 \implies v_{\max} = \sqrt{2E/m}$, so the smaller mass has the greater maximum speed.
Maximum potential energy: $E = mgh_{\max} \implies h_{\max} = E/(mg)$, so the smaller mass reaches a greater vertical height, which requires a larger maximum angular displacement.'),
  ('ap-mock-physc-mech-1_q8', 'ap-mock-physc-mech-1', 8, false, 'D', 'Rolling Motion & Rotational Dynamics', 'hard', 'A uniform cylinder of mass $m$ and radius $r$ unrolls without slipping from two strings tied to a vertical support. If the rotational inertia of the cylinder is $\frac{1}{2}mr^2$, find the acceleration of its center of mass.', '["$\\frac{1}{3}g$","$\\frac{1}{2}g$","$\\frac{3}{5}g$","$\\frac{2}{3}g$","$g$"]'::jsonb, '(D) Let $F_T$ be the tension in each of the two strings.
Translational motion: $mg - 2F_T = ma$
Rotational motion about CM: $\tau = (2F_T)r = I\alpha = \left(\frac{1}{2}mr^2\right)\left(\frac{a}{r}\right) \implies 2F_T = \frac{1}{2}ma$
Substituting into the translational equation:
  $$mg - \frac{1}{2}ma = ma \implies mg = \frac{3}{2}ma \implies a = \frac{2}{3}g$$'),
  ('ap-mock-physc-mech-1_q9', 'ap-mock-physc-mech-1', 9, false, 'B', 'Work, Power & Constant Velocity', 'easy', 'A space shuttle is launched from Earth. As it travels up, it moves at a constant velocity of $150\text{ m/s}$ straight up. If its engines provide $1.5 \times 10^8\text{ W}$ of power, what is the shuttle''s mass? You may assume that the shuttle''s mass and the acceleration due to gravity are constant ($g = 10\text{ m/s}^2$).', '["$6.7 \\times 10^2\\text{ kg}$","$1.0 \\times 10^5\\text{ kg}$","$6.7 \\times 10^5\\text{ kg}$","$1.0 \\times 10^6\\text{ kg}$","$2.3 \\times 10^6\\text{ kg}$"]'::jsonb, '(B) At constant velocity, engine thrust equals gravitational weight: $F = mg$.
  $$P = Fv = mgv \implies m = \frac{P}{gv} = \frac{1.5 \times 10^8\text{ W}}{(10\text{ m/s}^2)(150\text{ m/s})} = \frac{1.5 \times 10^8}{1500} = 1.0 \times 10^5\text{ kg}$$'),
  ('ap-mock-physc-mech-1_q10', 'ap-mock-physc-mech-1', 10, false, 'B', 'Newton''s 2nd Law & Force Graphs', 'easy', 'A uniform cylinder, initially at rest on a frictionless, horizontal surface, is pulled by a constant force $F$ from time $t = 0$ to time $t = T$. From time $t = T$ on, this force is removed. Which graph best illustrates the speed, $v$, of the cylinder''s center of mass from $t = 0$ onwards?', '["Velocity increases linearly to $T$, then decreases linearly to zero","Velocity increases linearly from $0$ to $T$, then remains constant","Velocity increases parabolically from $0$ to $T$, then remains constant","Velocity stays constant from $0$ to $T$, then drops to zero","Velocity increases exponentially"]'::jsonb, '(B) For $0 \le t \le T$, the constant net force causes a constant acceleration $a = F/m$, meaning $v(t) = at$ increases linearly. For $t > T$, $F = 0 \implies a = 0$, so velocity remains at a constant horizontal value.'),
  ('ap-mock-physc-mech-1_q11', 'ap-mock-physc-mech-1', 11, false, 'A', 'Gravitational Potential & Orbital Energy', 'hard', 'A satellite is in circular orbit around Earth (radius = $R$). If the work required to lift the satellite to its orbital height is equal to the satellite''s kinetic energy while in this orbit, how high above the surface of Earth is the satellite?', '["$R/2$","$R$","$3R/2$","$2R$","$4R$"]'::jsonb, '(A) Work to lift the satellite to altitude $h$:
  $$W = \Delta U = -\frac{GMm}{R+h} - \left(-\frac{GMm}{R}\right) = \frac{GMmh}{R(R+h)}$$
For a circular orbit at radius $r = R+h$, the orbital speed satisfies $v^2 = \frac{GM}{R+h}$, so kinetic energy is:
  $$K = \frac{1}{2}mv^2 = \frac{GMm}{2(R+h)}$$
Equating $W = K$:
  $$\frac{GMmh}{R(R+h)} = \frac{GMm}{2(R+h)} \implies \frac{h}{R} = \frac{1}{2} \implies h = \frac{R}{2}$$'),
  ('ap-mock-physc-mech-1_q12', 'ap-mock-physc-mech-1', 12, false, 'E', 'Inelastic Collisions & Center of Mass', 'hard', 'A block of length $l_1 = 10\text{ cm}$, mass $m_1 = 10\text{ kg}$, and $v_1 = 2\text{ m/s}$ moves toward a second block of length $l_2 = 20\text{ cm}$, $m_2 = 2\text{ kg}$, and $v_2 = 8\text{ m/s}$ moving toward the first. The blocks'' closest edges are $1\text{ m}$ apart. The blocks experience a perfectly inelastic collision. How long after the collision does it take for the center of mass of the combined object to cross the point midway between the blocks'' starting positions?', '["$0.225\\text{ s}$","$0.325\\text{ s}$","$0.525\\text{ s}$","$0.900\\text{ s}$","$0.975\\text{ s}$"]'::jsonb, '(E) Time to collision: $t_{\text{coll}} = \frac{1.0\text{ m}}{2\text{ m/s} + 8\text{ m/s}} = 0.1\text{ s}$. The collision point is $0.2\text{ m}$ from block 1''s starting position.  
Velocity of combined mass:
  $$v_f = \frac{(10\text{ kg})(2\text{ m/s}) + (2\text{ kg})(-8\text{ m/s})}{10\text{ kg} + 2\text{ kg}} = \frac{4}{12} = \frac{1}{3}\text{ m/s}$$
Midpoint of the initial separation is $0.5\text{ m}$ from block 1''s starting position.  
The distance from the collision point to the midpoint is $0.5 - 0.2 = 0.3\text{ m}$. Taking the center of mass position offset within the combined block into account adds $0.025\text{ m}$, giving a total travel distance of $d = 0.325\text{ m}$.  
Time: $t = \frac{0.325\text{ m}}{1/3\text{ m/s}} = 0.975\text{ s}$.'),
  ('ap-mock-physc-mech-1_q13', 'ap-mock-physc-mech-1', 13, false, 'C', 'Impulse-Momentum Theorem', 'medium', 'A rubber ball (mass = $0.08\text{ kg}$) is dropped from a height of $3.2\text{ m}$ and rebounds almost to its original height. If the impact time with the floor is $0.04\text{ s}$, what average force did the floor exert on the ball?', '["$0.16\\text{ N}$","$16\\text{ N}$","$32\\text{ N}$","$36\\text{ N}$","$64\\text{ N}$"]'::jsonb, '(C) Impact speed: $v = \sqrt{2gh} = \sqrt{2(10)(3.2)} = 8\text{ m/s}$.
Change in momentum taking upward as positive:
  $$\Delta p = m(v_f - v_i) = 0.08(8 - (-8)) = 1.28\text{ kg}\cdot\text{m/s}$$
Average force:
  $$F_{\text{avg}} = \frac{\Delta p}{\Delta t} = \frac{1.28\text{ N}\cdot\text{s}}{0.04\text{ s}} = 32\text{ N}$$'),
  ('ap-mock-physc-mech-1_q14', 'ap-mock-physc-mech-1', 14, false, 'D', 'Rotational Kinematics & Arc Length', 'easy', 'A disk of radius $0.1\text{ m}$ initially at rest undergoes an angular acceleration of $2.0\text{ rad/s}^2$. If the disk only rotates, find the total distance traveled by a point on the rim in $4.0\text{ s}$.', '["$0.4\\text{ m}$","$0.8\\text{ m}$","$1.2\\text{ m}$","$1.6\\text{ m}$","$2.0\\text{ m}$"]'::jsonb, '(D) Angular displacement:
  $$\Delta\theta = \frac{1}{2}\alpha t^2 = \frac{1}{2}(2.0\text{ rad/s}^2)(4.0\text{ s})^2 = 16\text{ rad}$$
Arc distance on rim:
  $$s = r\Delta\theta = (0.1\text{ m})(16\text{ rad}) = 1.6\text{ m}$$'),
  ('ap-mock-physc-mech-1_q15', 'ap-mock-physc-mech-1', 15, false, 'C', 'Energy Conservation & Projectile Launch', 'medium', 'A small object slides down a frictionless quarter-circular slide of radius $R$ starting from rest at a height equal to $2R$ above a horizontal surface. Find its horizontal displacement, $x$, from the starting point at the moment it strikes the surface.', '["$R$","$2R$","$3R$","$4R$","$5R$"]'::jsonb, '(C) The object leaves the slide horizontally at height $R$ with speed $v = \sqrt{2gR}$.
Time to fall the remaining height $R$: $t = \sqrt{2R/g}$.
Horizontal distance traveled in air:
  $$\Delta x_{\text{air}} = v t = \sqrt{2gR}\sqrt{\frac{2R}{g}} = 2R$$
Total horizontal displacement from the starting position: $x_{\text{total}} = R + 2R = 3R$.'),
  ('ap-mock-physc-mech-1_q16', 'ap-mock-physc-mech-1', 16, false, 'A', 'Uniform Circular Motion & Projections', 'hard', 'A particle executes uniform circular motion in a circle of radius $R$. When the shadow on Screen 1 is at position $x = -0.5R$ and moving in the $+x$ direction, what is true about the position and velocity of the shadow on Screen 2 at that same instant?', '["$y = -0.866R$; velocity in $-y$ direction","$y = -0.866R$; velocity in $+y$ direction","$y = -0.5R$; velocity in $-y$ direction","$y = +0.866R$; velocity in $-y$ direction","$y = +0.866R$; velocity in $+y$ direction"]'::jsonb, '(A) Position along $x$: $x = R\cos\theta = -0.5R \implies \theta = 120^\circ$ or $240^\circ$.
Because the shadow moves in $+x$ direction, $v_x = -\omega R\sin\theta > 0 \implies \sin\theta < 0$, placing the angle at $\theta = 240^\circ$.
Therefore:
  $$y = R\sin(240^\circ) = -R\frac{\sqrt{3}}{2} \approx -0.866R$$
  $$v_y = \omega R\cos(240^\circ) = -0.5\omega R < 0 \quad (\text{directed along }-y)$$'),
  ('ap-mock-physc-mech-1_q17', 'ap-mock-physc-mech-1', 17, false, 'C', 'Rotational Inertia & Torque', 'hard', 'Two objects of masses $m$ and $2m$ are attached to the ends of a rigid massless rod of length $L$ at rest on a frictionless table. When a force $F$ is applied perpendicularly at mass $2m$, the resulting rotational acceleration of the rod about its center of mass is $kF/(mL)$. What is $k$?', '["$1/4$","$1/3$","$1/2$","$2/3$","$1$"]'::jsonb, '(C) Distance of CM from mass $m$: $y_{\text{cm}} = \frac{m(0) + 2m(L)}{3m} = \frac{2}{3}L$.
Distance from CM to mass $2m$: $L - \frac{2}{3}L = \frac{1}{3}L$.
Rotational inertia about CM:
  $$I_{\text{cm}} = m\left(\frac{2}{3}L\right)^2 + 2m\left(\frac{1}{3}L\right)^2 = \frac{4}{9}mL^2 + \frac{2}{9}mL^2 = \frac{2}{3}mL^2$$
Torque about CM: $\tau = F\left(\frac{1}{3}L\right)$.
Angular acceleration:
  $$\alpha = \frac{\tau}{I_{\text{cm}}} = \frac{F L / 3}{\frac{2}{3}mL^2} = \frac{1}{2}\frac{F}{mL} \implies k = \frac{1}{2}$$'),
  ('ap-mock-physc-mech-1_q18', 'ap-mock-physc-mech-1', 18, false, 'E', 'Impulse, Momentum & Collision Safety', 'medium', 'Old cars were made with rigid frames; modern cars are made with frames that crumple in a collision. If each vehicle crashes into a wall and comes to a complete stop from the same initial mass and speed, which of the following would NOT be true?', '["The modern vehicle would experience less force.","The modern vehicle would experience less impulse.","The modern vehicle''s magnitude of acceleration would be less.","The modern vehicle''s collision would take less time.","More than one of the above would not be true."]'::jsonb, '(E) Because both vehicles have the same mass and initial speed and both come to rest, $\Delta p$ is identical for both. Since Impulse $J = \Delta p$, both experience the same impulse (making B false). The crumpling frame extends the stopping distance and increases collision time, so it takes more time (making D false). Because both B and D are false, (E) is the correct choice.'),
  ('ap-mock-physc-mech-1_q19', 'ap-mock-physc-mech-1', 19, false, 'D', 'Static Equilibrium & Torque', 'easy', 'A homogeneous bar lying on a flat table is acted upon by exactly two external horizontal forces, $F_1$ and $F_2$. If the net force on the rod is zero, which of the following is true?', '["The net torque on the bar must also be zero.","The bar cannot accelerate translationally or rotationally.","The bar can accelerate translationally if $F_1$ and $F_2$ are not applied at the same point.","The net torque will be zero if $F_1$ and $F_2$ are applied at the same point.","None of the above"]'::jsonb, '(D) Since $\vec{F}_{\text{net}} = \vec{F}_1 + \vec{F}_2 = 0$, we have $\vec{F}_2 = -\vec{F}_1$. If both forces are applied at the same point, their position vectors $\vec{r}$ from any chosen pivot are identical, resulting in $\vec{\tau}_{\text{net}} = \vec{r}\times\vec{F}_1 + \vec{r}\times(-\vec{F}_1) = 0$.'),
  ('ap-mock-physc-mech-1_q20', 'ap-mock-physc-mech-1', 20, false, 'B', 'Universal Gravitation & Weight', 'easy', 'An astronaut lands on a planet whose mass and radius are each twice that of Earth. If the astronaut weighs $800\text{ N}$ on Earth, how much will he weigh on this planet?', '["$200\\text{ N}$","$400\\text{ N}$","$800\\text{ N}$","$1,600\\text{ N}$","$3,200\\text{ N}$"]'::jsonb, '(B) $$g_{\text{planet}} = \frac{G(2M)}{(2R)^2} = \frac{2}{4}\frac{GM}{R^2} = \frac{1}{2}g_{\text{Earth}}$$
$$W_{\text{planet}} = m g_{\text{planet}} = \frac{1}{2}W_{\text{Earth}} = \frac{1}{2}(800\text{ N}) = 400\text{ N}$$'),
  ('ap-mock-physc-mech-1_q21', 'ap-mock-physc-mech-1', 21, false, 'C', 'Work-Energy Theorem & Variable Forces', 'medium', 'A particle of mass $m = 1.0\text{ kg}$ is acted upon by a variable force $F(x)$. The area under the $F(x)$ curve from $x = 0$ to $x = 10\text{ m}$ is $50\text{ N}\cdot\text{m}$. If the particle''s speed was zero at $x = 0$, what is its speed at $x = 10\text{ m}$?', '["$5.0\\text{ m/s}$","$8.7\\text{ m/s}$","$10\\text{ m/s}$","$14\\text{ m/s}$","$20\\text{ m/s}$"]'::jsonb, '(C) By the Work-Energy Theorem:
  $$W = \Delta K \implies 50\text{ J} = \frac{1}{2}mv^2 - 0 \implies v = \sqrt{\frac{2(50)}{1.0}} = 10\text{ m/s}$$'),
  ('ap-mock-physc-mech-1_q22', 'ap-mock-physc-mech-1', 22, false, 'B', 'Angular Momentum Conservation & Collapse', 'medium', 'The radius of a collapsing spinning star decreases to $\frac{1}{4}$ of its initial value. What is the ratio of the final rotational kinetic energy to the initial rotational kinetic energy ($K_f / K_i$)?', '["$4$","$16$","$64$","$256$","$1/16$"]'::jsonb, '(B) Angular momentum is conserved: $L = I_i\omega_i = I_f\omega_f \implies \omega_f = \omega_i(I_i/I_f)$.
For a sphere, $I \propto R^2$. Since $R_f = R_i/4$, $I_f = I_i/16$, which yields $\omega_f = 16\omega_i$.
The rotational kinetic energy ratio is:
  $$\frac{K_f}{K_i} = \frac{\frac{1}{2}I_f\omega_f^2}{\frac{1}{2}I_i\omega_i^2} = \left(\frac{1}{16}\right)(16)^2 = 16$$'),
  ('ap-mock-physc-mech-1_q23', 'ap-mock-physc-mech-1', 23, false, 'C', '2D Projectile Motion', 'easy', 'A ball is projected with an initial velocity of magnitude $v_0 = 40\text{ m/s}$ at an angle of $60^\circ$ toward a vertical wall $20\text{ m}$ away. How long does the ball take to reach the wall?', '["$0.25\\text{ s}$","$0.50\\text{ s}$","$1.0\\text{ s}$","$2.0\\text{ s}$","$3.0\\text{ s}$"]'::jsonb, '(C) The horizontal velocity is constant:
$$v_x = v_0 \cos 60^\circ = 40(0.5) = 20\text{ m/s}$$
$$t = \frac{x}{v_x} = \frac{20\text{ m}}{20\text{ m/s}} = 1.0\text{ s}$$'),
  ('ap-mock-physc-mech-1_q24', 'ap-mock-physc-mech-1', 24, false, 'B', 'Dimensional Analysis', 'easy', 'If $C, M, L,$ and $T$ represent the dimensions of charge, mass, length, and time respectively, what are the dimensions of the permittivity of free space ($\varepsilon_0$)?', '["$T^2C^2 / (M^2L^2)$","$T^2C^2 / (ML^3)$","$ML^3 / (T^2C^2)$","$C^2M / (T^2L^2)$","$T^2L^2 / (C^2M)$"]'::jsonb, '(B) From Coulomb''s Law $F = \frac{1}{4\pi\varepsilon_0}\frac{q^2}{r^2} \implies \varepsilon_0 = \frac{q^2}{F r^2}$.
Using base dimensions $[q] = C$, $[r] = L$, and $[F] = MLT^{-2}$:
$$[\varepsilon_0] = \frac{C^2}{(MLT^{-2})(L^2)} = \frac{T^2C^2}{ML^3}$$'),
  ('ap-mock-physc-mech-1_q25', 'ap-mock-physc-mech-1', 25, false, 'D', '2D Inelastic Collisions & Vectors', 'hard', 'Two clay balls moving toward each other on a frictionless surface collide perfectly inelastically. Ball 1 has mass $m_1$ and moves at $45^\circ$; ball 2 has mass $m_2$ and moves at $60^\circ$. If $m_1 = 2m_2$ and the post-collision velocity is parallel to the negative $y$-axis, what is $v_2$?', '["$v_1(\\sin 60^\\circ)$","$v_1(\\cos 60^\\circ)$","$v_1(2\\cos 60^\\circ)$","$v_1(2\\sin 45^\\circ)/(\\sin 60^\\circ)$","$v_1(\\tan 60^\\circ)$"]'::jsonb, '(D) Since the combined mass moves purely along the $-y$ axis, horizontal momentum must sum to zero:
  $$m_1 v_1 \sin 45^\circ - m_2 v_2 \sin 60^\circ = 0$$
Substituting $m_1 = 2m_2$:
  $$(2m_2)v_1 \sin 45^\circ = m_2 v_2 \sin 60^\circ \implies v_2 = \frac{2v_1 \sin 45^\circ}{\sin 60^\circ}$$'),
  ('ap-mock-physc-mech-1_q26', 'ap-mock-physc-mech-1', 26, false, 'B', 'SHM & Static Friction Threshold', 'hard', 'The coefficient of static friction between two blocks is $0.80$. If the blocks oscillate horizontally with a frequency of $2.0\text{ Hz}$, what is the maximum amplitude of the oscillations if the small block is not to slip on the large block?', '["$3.1\\text{ cm}$","$5.0\\text{ cm}$","$6.2\\text{ cm}$","$7.5\\text{ cm}$","$9.4\\text{ cm}$"]'::jsonb, '(B) Maximum acceleration in SHM is $a_{\max} = \omega^2 A = (2\pi f)^2 A$. To prevent slipping, static friction must provide this acceleration:
  $$\mu_s g \ge (2\pi f)^2 A \implies A \le \frac{\mu_s g}{4\pi^2 f^2} = \frac{(0.80)(9.8)}{4\pi^2 (2.0)^2} \approx 0.050\text{ m} = 5.0\text{ cm}$$'),
  ('ap-mock-physc-mech-1_q27', 'ap-mock-physc-mech-1', 27, false, 'D', 'Coefficient of Restitution', 'medium', 'When two objects collide, the coefficient of restitution is $e = v_{\text{rel,after}} / v_{\text{rel,before}}$. If a ball dropped from height $H_1$ rebounds to height $H_2$, what is $e$?', '["$H_1/H_2$","$H_2/H_1$","$\\sqrt{H_1/H_2}$","$\\sqrt{H_2/H_1}$","$(H_1/H_2)^2$"]'::jsonb, '(D) Impact speed is $v_1 = \sqrt{2gH_1}$ and rebound speed is $v_2 = \sqrt{2gH_2}$.
  $$e = \frac{v_2}{v_1} = \frac{\sqrt{2gH_2}}{\sqrt{2gH_1}} = \sqrt{\frac{H_2}{H_1}}$$'),
  ('ap-mock-physc-mech-1_q28', 'ap-mock-physc-mech-1', 28, false, 'D', 'Torque & Lever Arm', 'easy', 'A square metal plate of side length $40\text{ cm}$ lies flat on a table. A force $F$ of magnitude $10\text{ N}$ is applied along one of the corners. Determine the torque produced by $F$ relative to the center of rotation (center of mass).', '["$0\\text{ N}\\cdot\\text{m}$","$1.0\\text{ N}\\cdot\\text{m}$","$1.4\\text{ N}\\cdot\\text{m}$","$2.0\\text{ N}\\cdot\\text{m}$","$4.0\\text{ N}\\cdot\\text{m}$"]'::jsonb, '(D) The lever arm from the geometric center to the line of action along any side of the square is $r_\perp = s/2 = 0.40/2 = 0.20\text{ m}$.
  $$\tau = F r_\perp = (10\text{ N})(0.20\text{ m}) = 2.0\text{ N}\cdot\text{m}$$'),
  ('ap-mock-physc-mech-1_q29', 'ap-mock-physc-mech-1', 29, false, 'A', 'Conservative Gravitational Work', 'easy', 'A block of mass $m = 2.0\text{ kg}$ is pushed upward from $(0,0)$ to $(3\text{ m}, 3\text{ m})$. How much work is done by gravity during this displacement?', '["$-60\\text{ J}$","$-80\\text{ J}$","$-90\\text{ J}$","$-100\\text{ J}$","$-120\\text{ J}$"]'::jsonb, '(A) Gravity is conservative and depends only on vertical displacement $\Delta z = 3\text{ m}$:
  $$W_g = -mg\Delta z = -(2.0\text{ kg})(10\text{ m/s}^2)(3\text{ m}) = -60\text{ J}$$'),
  ('ap-mock-physc-mech-1_q30', 'ap-mock-physc-mech-1', 30, false, 'C', 'SHM Spring-Block Collision', 'hard', 'A horizontal spring with constant $k$ oscillates between $-A$ and $+A$ with mass $M$. A bullet of mass $m$ with speed $v$ collides inelastically with $M$ at $x = -A$. How far beyond $x = -A$ will the spring compress?', '["$\\frac{mv}{\\sqrt{kM}}$","$\\frac{mv}{k(M+m)}$","$\\frac{mv}{\\sqrt{k(M+m)}}$","$\\frac{Mv}{\\sqrt{k(M+m)}}$","$\\frac{mv}{\\sqrt{km}}$"]'::jsonb, '(C) At $x = -A$, the block was momentarily at rest. Immediately after the collision, the combined velocity is $v'' = \frac{mv}{M+m}$. The additional kinetic energy is converted into extra compression $x_{\text{extra}}$:
  $$\frac{1}{2}k x_{\text{extra}}^2 = \frac{1}{2}(M+m)(v'')^2 = \frac{1}{2}\frac{m^2v^2}{M+m} \implies x_{\text{extra}} = \frac{mv}{\sqrt{k(M+m)}}$$'),
  ('ap-mock-physc-mech-1_q31', 'ap-mock-physc-mech-1', 31, false, 'D', 'Calculus Moment of Inertia Integration', 'hard', 'A rod pivoting about $x = 0$ has linear density $\lambda(x) = kx$. Determine the rod''s moment of inertia in terms of length $L$ and total mass $M$.', '["$\\frac{1}{3}ML^2$","$\\frac{1}{4}ML^2$","$\\frac{2}{5}ML^2$","$\\frac{1}{2}ML^2$","$2ML^2$"]'::jsonb, '(D) Total mass: $M = \int_0^L kx\,dx = \frac{1}{2}kL^2 \implies k = \frac{2M}{L^2}$.
Moment of inertia:
  $$I = \int_0^L x^2\,dm = \int_0^L x^2(kx\,dx) = \frac{1}{4}kL^4 = \frac{1}{4}\left(\frac{2M}{L^2}\right)L^4 = \frac{1}{2}ML^2$$'),
  ('ap-mock-physc-mech-1_q32', 'ap-mock-physc-mech-1', 32, false, 'D', 'Potential Energy Curves & Stability', 'medium', 'A conservative force has potential energy $U(x) = x^3 - 12x$. Which position represents a point of stable equilibrium?', '["$x = -4\\text{ m}$","$x = -2\\text{ m}$","$x = 0\\text{ m}$","$x = 2\\text{ m}$","$x = 4\\text{ m}$"]'::jsonb, '(D) Equilibrium requires $\frac{dU}{dx} = 3x^2 - 12 = 0 \implies x = \pm 2\text{ m}$.
Stable equilibrium requires a local minimum ($\frac{d^2U}{dx^2} > 0$):
  $$\frac{d^2U}{dx^2} = 6x \implies \text{At } x = 2\text{ m}, \frac{d^2U}{dx^2} = 12 > 0\text{ (Stable)}$$'),
  ('ap-mock-physc-mech-1_q33', 'ap-mock-physc-mech-1', 33, false, 'D', 'Atwood Machine & Inertial Frames', 'medium', 'A light frictionless pulley attached to an elevator ceiling supports masses $m$ and $M$ ($M > m$). The elevator car descends at a constant velocity. Determine the acceleration of the masses relative to the elevator.', '["$(M - m)g$","$(M + m)g$","$\\frac{M + m}{M - m}g$","$\\frac{M - m}{M + m}g$","$\\frac{2Mm}{M + m}g$"]'::jsonb, '(D) Because the elevator travels at constant velocity, it acts as an inertial frame of reference ($a_{\text{frame}} = 0$). The Atwood acceleration is:
  $$a = \frac{M - m}{M + m}g$$'),
  ('ap-mock-physc-mech-1_q34', 'ap-mock-physc-mech-1', 34, false, 'E', 'Variable Friction Ramp Energy Tracking', 'hard', 'A block ($m = 2\text{ kg}$) starts at rest on a ramp ($\sin\theta = 0.45, \cos\theta = 0.9$). The ramp consists of alternating frictionless sections of $1\text{ m}$ and rough sections ($\mu = 2/3$) of increasing length ($1\text{ m}, 2\text{ m}, 3\text{ m}, \dots$). How far along the ramp does the block travel before stopping?', '["$4\\text{ m}$","$8\\text{ m}$","$12\\text{ m}$","$19\\text{ m}$","$24\\text{ m}$"]'::jsonb, '(E) $\Delta PE$ gained per meter: $mg\sin\theta = (2)(10)(0.45) = +9\text{ J/m}$.  
Friction work per rough meter: $-\mu mg\cos\theta = -(2/3)(2)(10)(0.9) = -12\text{ J/m}$.  
Net change on rough segments: $+9 - 12 = -3\text{ J/m}$.
Tracking energy across the sequence:
0–1 m (smooth): $+9\text{ J}$
1–2 m (rough 1m): $9 - 3 = 6\text{ J}$
2–3 m (smooth): $6 + 9 = 15\text{ J}$
3–5 m (rough 2m): $15 - 6 = 9\text{ J}$
5–6 m (smooth): $9 + 9 = 18\text{ J}$
6–9 m (rough 3m): $18 - 9 = 9\text{ J}$
9–10 m (smooth): $9 + 9 = 18\text{ J}$
10–14 m (rough 4m): $18 - 12 = 6\text{ J}$
14–15 m (smooth): $6 + 9 = 15\text{ J}$
15–20 m (rough 5m): $15 - 15 = 0\text{ J}$  
20–21 m (smooth): $+9\text{ J}$  
Next rough segment: travels $9/3 = 3\text{ m}$ before stopping.
Total distance = $21 + 3 = 24\text{ m}$.'),
  ('ap-mock-physc-mech-1_q35', 'ap-mock-physc-mech-1', 35, false, 'E', 'Vector Force Sums & Newton''s 2nd Law', 'easy', 'An object of mass $2\text{ kg}$ is acted upon by three external forces, each of magnitude $4\text{ N}$. Which of the following could NOT be the resulting acceleration of the object?', '["$0\\text{ m/s}^2$","$2\\text{ m/s}^2$","$4\\text{ m/s}^2$","$6\\text{ m/s}^2$","$8\\text{ m/s}^2$"]'::jsonb, '(E) The maximum possible net force occurs when all three forces act in the same direction: $F_{\text{net,max}} = 4 + 4 + 4 = 12\text{ N}$.
  $$a_{\max} = \frac{12\text{ N}}{2\text{ kg}} = 6\text{ m/s}^2$$
An acceleration of $8\text{ m/s}^2$ is impossible.'),
  ('ap-mock-physc-em-1_q1', 'ap-mock-physc-em-1', 1, false, 'C', 'Electrostatic Induction & Polarization', 'easy', 'A nonconducting sphere is given a nonzero net electric charge, $+Q$, and then brought close to a neutral conducting sphere of the same radius. Which of the following will be true?', '["An electric field will be induced within the conducting sphere.","The conducting sphere will develop a net electric charge of $-Q$.","The spheres will experience an electrostatic attraction.","The spheres will experience an electrostatic repulsion.","The spheres will experience no electrostatic interaction."]'::jsonb, '(C) The positive charge $+Q$ on the nonconducting sphere induces negative mobile electrons to migrate toward the near side of the neutral conducting sphere, leaving equal positive bound charge on the far side. Because electrostatic force varies inversely with the square of distance ($F \propto 1/r^2$), the attractive force on the closer negative charges is strictly greater than the repulsive force on the farther positive charges, resulting in a net attractive electrostatic force.'),
  ('ap-mock-physc-em-1_q2', 'ap-mock-physc-em-1', 2, false, 'E', 'Electric Power & Resistance Relationships', 'easy', 'If the total resistance of a circuit were doubled, the power dissipated by that same circuit would', '["increase by a factor of 4","increase by a factor of 2","decrease by a factor of 2","decrease by a factor of 4","Cannot be determined"]'::jsonb, '(E) Power dissipation can be expressed as $P = I^2 R$ or $P = V^2/R$. If the circuit is operated under a constant voltage source, doubling $R$ cuts power in half ($P \propto 1/R$). If operated under a constant current source, doubling $R$ doubles the power ($P \propto R$). Without specifying whether the voltage or current remains fixed, the change in power dissipation cannot be determined.'),
  ('ap-mock-physc-em-1_q3', 'ap-mock-physc-em-1', 3, false, 'E', 'Magnetic Lorentz Force & Orbital Radius', 'medium', 'Each of the following ionized isotopes is projected with the same speed into a uniform magnetic field $B$ such that the isotope''s initial velocity is perpendicular to $B$. Which combination of mass and charge would result in a circular path with the largest radius?', '["$m = 16\\text{ u},\\ q = -5e$","$m = 18\\text{ u},\\ q = -2e$","$m = 19\\text{ u},\\ q = -2e$","$m = 16\\text{ u},\\ q = +e$","$m = 20\\text{ u},\\ q = +e$"]'::jsonb, '(E) The magnetic Lorentz force supplies the centripetal acceleration:
  $$|q|vB = \frac{mv^2}{r} \implies r = \frac{mv}{|q|B}$$
Since $v$ and $B$ are identical for all particles, the radius $r$ is directly proportional to the mass-to-charge magnitude ratio $m/|q|$. Evaluating $m/|q|$:
(A) $16/5 = 3.2$
(B) $18/2 = 9.0$
(C) $19/2 = 9.5$
(D) $16/1 = 16.0$
(E) $20/1 = 20.0$
Option (E) yields the largest ratio, producing the largest orbit radius.'),
  ('ap-mock-physc-em-1_q4', 'ap-mock-physc-em-1', 4, false, 'A', 'Electric Field Symmetry & Superposition', 'medium', 'The picture shows 4 charges of equal magnitude fixed at the corners of a rectangle ($2\text{ cm} \times 4\text{ cm}$). How many locations on either Line A (vertical bisector) or Line B (horizontal bisector) would be places with zero net electric field?', '["1","2","All of Line A","All of Line B","All of both Line A and Line B"]'::jsonb, '(A) By symmetry, only at the exact geometric center of the rectangle do the electric field vectors from all four symmetrically placed identical charges cancel out vectorially to yield $\vec{E}_{\text{net}} = 0$. Moving away from the center along Line A or Line B introduces asymmetric distances to the pairs of charges, creating a nonzero net electric field component along the perpendicular axis. Thus, there is only $1$ point of zero net electric field.')
ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.ap_exam_questions (id, test_id, question_order, is_frq, answer_key, topic, difficulty, question_text, options_json, explanation)
VALUES
  ('ap-mock-physc-em-1_q5', 'ap-mock-physc-em-1', 5, false, 'B', 'Resistivity & Conductor Geometry', 'easy', 'Four wires are each made of aluminum. Which wire will have the greatest electrical resistance?', '["Wire A (short, thick)","Wire B (longest, thinnest cross-section)","Wire C (short, thin)","Wire D (long, thick)","All the wires have the same resistance."]'::jsonb, '(B) The resistance of a cylindrical conductor of resistivity $\rho$, length $L$, and cross-sectional area $A$ is given by $R = \frac{\rho L}{A}$. Because all wires have the same resistivity $\rho$, resistance is maximized by choosing the maximum length $L$ and minimum cross-sectional area $A$ (Wire B).'),
  ('ap-mock-physc-em-1_q6', 'ap-mock-physc-em-1', 6, false, 'E', 'Magnetic Units & Dimensional Analysis', 'easy', 'Which of the following is NOT equal to one tesla?', '["$1\\text{ J}/(\\text{A}\\cdot\\text{m}^2)$","$1\\text{ kg}/(\\text{C}\\cdot\\text{s})$","$1\\text{ N}/(\\text{A}\\cdot\\text{m})$","$1\\text{ V}\\cdot\\text{s}/\\text{m}^2$","$1\\text{ A}\\cdot\\text{N}/\\text{V}$"]'::jsonb, '(E) From $F = qvB = ILB$, $1\text{ T} = 1\frac{\text{N}}{\text{A}\cdot\text{m}}$.
$\frac{\text{J}}{\text{A}\cdot\text{m}^2} = \frac{\text{N}\cdot\text{m}}{\text{A}\cdot\text{m}^2} = \frac{\text{N}}{\text{A}\cdot\text{m}} = 1\text{ T}$
$\frac{\text{kg}}{\text{C}\cdot\text{s}} = \frac{\text{kg}\cdot\text{m/s}^2}{(\text{C/s})\cdot\text{m}} = \frac{\text{N}}{\text{A}\cdot\text{m}} = 1\text{ T}$
$1\text{ V}\cdot\text{s}/\text{m}^2 = \frac{\text{J/C}\cdot\text{s}}{\text{m}^2} = \frac{\text{N}\cdot\text{m}}{\text{A}\cdot\text{m}^2} = 1\text{ T}$
Option (E): $1\frac{\text{A}\cdot\text{N}}{\text{V}} = \frac{\text{A}\cdot\text{N}}{\text{J/C}} = \frac{\text{A}^2\text{s}\cdot\text{N}}{\text{N}\cdot\text{m}} = \frac{\text{A}^2\text{s}}{\text{m}} \ne 1\text{ T}$.'),
  ('ap-mock-physc-em-1_q7', 'ap-mock-physc-em-1', 7, false, 'C', 'Gauss''s Law & Enclosed Electric Flux', 'easy', 'A cube with side length $d$ and a sphere with diameter $d$ each enclose the same net electric charge $+Q$. If $\Phi_C$ is the flux through the cube and $\Phi_S$ is the flux through the sphere, which of the following is true?', '["$\\Phi_C > \\Phi_S$","$\\Phi_C < \\Phi_S$","$\\Phi_C = \\Phi_S$","$\\Phi_C = 0$","$\\Phi_S = 0$"]'::jsonb, '(C) By Gauss''s Law, the total electric flux through any closed surface depends exclusively on the net enclosed charge: $\Phi_E = \oint \vec{E}\cdot d\vec{A} = \frac{Q_{\text{enc}}}{\varepsilon_0}$. Because both closed surfaces enclose identical net charge $+Q$, their total electric fluxes are equal: $\Phi_C = \Phi_S = \frac{Q}{\varepsilon_0}$.'),
  ('ap-mock-physc-em-1_q8', 'ap-mock-physc-em-1', 8, false, 'E', 'Electrostatic Potential Wells & Oscillations', 'medium', 'Two positive charges $Q_1$ and $Q_2$ of equal magnitude are fixed at $x = 0$ and $x = 100\text{ cm}$. A third positive charge $q$ is released from rest at $x = 70\text{ cm}$. At what position would it return to rest?', '["$x = 30\\text{ cm}$","$x = 50\\text{ cm}$","$x = 70\\text{ cm}$","Cannot be determined without $q$","It will not stop."]'::jsonb, '(E) In ideal electrostatic conditions without friction or radiation damping, energy is conserved. The symmetric potential energy well causes the charge to oscillate periodically between turning points $x = 70\text{ cm}$ and $x = 30\text{ cm}$, passing through $x = 50\text{ cm}$ at maximum speed. It continuously oscillates back and forth and does not come to a permanent stop.'),
  ('ap-mock-physc-em-1_q9', 'ap-mock-physc-em-1', 9, false, 'A', 'Elementary Charge Quantization', 'easy', 'An object carries a charge of $-1\text{ C}$. How many excess electrons does it contain?', '["$6.25 \\times 10^{18}$","$8.00 \\times 10^{18}$","$1.60 \\times 10^{19}$","$3.20 \\times 10^{19}$","$6.25 \\times 10^{19}$"]'::jsonb, '(A) Using the elementary charge $e = 1.6 \times 10^{-19}\text{ C}$:
  $$N = \frac{|Q|}{e} = \frac{1.0\text{ C}}{1.6 \times 10^{-19}\text{ C/electron}} = 6.25 \times 10^{18}\text{ electrons}$$'),
  ('ap-mock-physc-em-1_q10', 'ap-mock-physc-em-1', 10, false, 'E', 'DC Resistor Networks & Kirchhoff''s Rules', 'medium', 'Four $200\ \Omega$ resistors are configured such that $R_2, R_3, R_4$ are in parallel with each other, and that entire parallel block is in series with $R_1$ across a $24\text{ V}$ ideal battery. How much total current is provided by the source?', '["$30\\text{ mA}$","$48\\text{ mA}$","$64\\text{ mA}$","$72\\text{ mA}$","$90\\text{ mA}$"]'::jsonb, '(E) Equivalent resistance of three identical $200\ \Omega$ resistors in parallel:
  $$R_{234} = \frac{200\ \Omega}{3} \approx 66.67\ \Omega$$
Total series resistance:
  $$R_{\text{total}} = R_1 + R_{234} = 200 + \frac{200}{3} = \frac{800}{3}\ \Omega \approx 266.67\ \Omega$$
Total source current:
  $$I_{\text{total}} = \frac{V}{R_{\text{total}}} = \frac{24\text{ V}}{\frac{800}{3}\ \Omega} = \frac{72}{800}\text{ A} = 0.090\text{ A} = 90\text{ mA}$$'),
  ('ap-mock-physc-em-1_q11', 'ap-mock-physc-em-1', 11, false, 'E', 'Power Dissipation in Resistor Networks', 'medium', 'For the same circuit in Question 10, what is the ratio of the power dissipated by $R_1$ to the power dissipated by $R_4$?', '["$1/9$","$1/3$","$1$","$3$","$9$"]'::jsonb, '(E) The entire current $I$ passes through $R_1$, whereas in the parallel bank of three identical resistors, the current through $R_4$ is $I_4 = \frac{1}{3}I$. Since $R_1 = R_4 = R$, the ratio of power dissipation is:
  $$\frac{P_1}{P_4} = \frac{I^2 R_1}{I_4^2 R_4} = \frac{I^2 R}{\left(\frac{1}{3}I\right)^2 R} = \frac{I^2}{\frac{1}{9}I^2} = 9$$'),
  ('ap-mock-physc-em-1_q12', 'ap-mock-physc-em-1', 12, false, 'D', 'RC Time Constant & Dimensional Analysis', 'easy', 'What is the value and unit of the product: $20\ \mu\text{F} \times 500\ \Omega$?', '["$0.01\\text{ henry}$","$0.01\\text{ ampere per coulomb}$","$0.01\\text{ weber}$","$0.01\\text{ second}$","$0.01\\text{ volt per ampere}$"]'::jsonb, '(D) Numerical value: $(20 \times 10^{-6}\text{ F}) \times (500\ \Omega) = 0.010$.
Dimensional analysis of the $RC$ time constant ($\tau = RC$):
  $$[\text{Farad}] \times [\text{Ohm}] = \left(\frac{\text{Coulomb}}{\text{Volt}}\right) \times \left(\frac{\text{Volt}}{\text{Ampere}}\right) = \frac{\text{C}}{\text{C/s}} = \text{second}$$
Thus, the result is $0.01\text{ second}$.'),
  ('ap-mock-physc-em-1_q13', 'ap-mock-physc-em-1', 13, false, 'B', 'Faraday''s Law & Induced Electric Fields', 'hard', 'A copper wire circle of radius $1\text{ m}$ is immersed in a magnetic field $B(t) = 2t(1-t) = 2t - 2t^2$ perpendicular into the page. What is the magnitude of the induced electric field in the wire at time $t = 1\text{ s}$?', '["$(1/\\pi)\\text{ N/C}$","$1\\text{ N/C}$","$2\\text{ N/C}$","$\\pi\\text{ N/C}$","$2\\pi\\text{ N/C}$"]'::jsonb, '(B) Faraday''s Law in non-electrostatic form relating $\vec{E}$ and magnetic flux $\Phi_B$:
  $$\oint \vec{E}\cdot d\vec{l} = -\frac{d\Phi_B}{dt} = -A\frac{dB}{dt}$$
  $$E(2\pi r) = -\pi r^2 \frac{d}{dt}(2t - 2t^2) = -\pi r^2 (2 - 4t)$$
  $$E = \frac{r}{2}|2 - 4t|$$
At $t = 1\text{ s}$ with $r = 1\text{ m}$:
  $$E = \frac{1}{2}|2 - 4(1)| = \frac{1}{2}(2) = 1\text{ N/C} \text{ (or V/m)}$$'),
  ('ap-mock-physc-em-1_q14', 'ap-mock-physc-em-1', 14, false, 'A', 'Magnetic Force on Current Loops & Equilibrium', 'medium', 'The top half of a rectangular loop of wire ($x\text{ m} \times y\text{ m}$) hangs vertically in a uniform magnetic field $B$ pointing into the page, supporting a mass $m$. Find the magnitude and direction of the loop current to balance the mass''s weight.', '["$I = \\frac{mg}{xB}$, clockwise","$I = \\frac{mg}{xB}$, counterclockwise","$I = \\frac{mg}{(x + \\frac{1}{2}y)B}$, clockwise","$I = \\frac{mg}{(x + \\frac{1}{2}y)B}$, counterclockwise","$I = \\frac{mg}{(x + y)B}$, clockwise"]'::jsonb, '(A) The magnetic forces on the vertical segments of length $y/2$ are equal and opposite, canceling each other out. The top horizontal wire of length $x$ must produce an upward magnetic force balancing gravity:
  $$F_B = I x B = mg \implies I = \frac{mg}{xB}$$
By the right-hand rule ($\vec{F}_B = I\vec{L}\times\vec{B}$), with $\vec{B}$ directed into the page, an upward force requires current pointing to the right along the top wire, which corresponds to a clockwise circulation.'),
  ('ap-mock-physc-em-1_q15', 'ap-mock-physc-em-1', 15, false, 'C', 'Electrostatic Potential Energy & Energy Conservation', 'medium', 'Particle $q_1 = 10\text{ nC}$ is fixed in place. Particle $q_2 = -5\text{ nC}$ of mass $m = 5 \times 10^{-10}\text{ kg}$ is released from rest $2\text{ cm}$ away from $q_1$. How fast will it move when it is $1\text{ cm}$ away?', '["$100\\text{ m/s}$","$200\\text{ m/s}$","$300\\text{ m/s}$","$400\\text{ m/s}$","$500\\text{ m/s}$"]'::jsonb, '(C) Change in electric potential from $r_i = 0.02\text{ m}$ to $r_f = 0.01\text{ m}$:
  $$\Delta V = k q_1 \left(\frac{1}{r_f} - \frac{1}{r_i}\right) = (9 \times 10^9)(10 \times 10^{-9})\left(\frac{1}{0.01} - \frac{1}{0.02}\right) = 90(100 - 50) = +4500\text{ V}$$
Change in electric potential energy:
  $$\Delta U_E = q_2 \Delta V = (-5 \times 10^{-9}\text{ C})(4500\text{ V}) = -2.25 \times 10^{-5}\text{ J}$$
By Conservation of Energy ($\Delta K = -\Delta U_E$):
  $$\frac{1}{2}m v_f^2 = 2.25 \times 10^{-5}\text{ J} \implies v_f = \sqrt{\frac{2(2.25 \times 10^{-5}\text{ J})}{5 \times 10^{-10}\text{ kg}}} = \sqrt{9 \times 10^4} = 300\text{ m/s}$$'),
  ('ap-mock-physc-em-1_q16', 'ap-mock-physc-em-1', 16, false, 'A', 'Magnetic Fields of Parallel Current Wires', 'medium', 'Two long straight parallel wires carry current: Wire 1 carries $I$ to the right, and Wire 2 carries $2I$ to the left. At which of the marked points is the net magnetic field zero?', '["Point 1 only (above Wire 1)","Points 1 and 2 only","Point 2 only","Points 3 and 4 only","Point 3 only"]'::jsonb, '(A) Between the wires, right-hand rule shows both currents produce magnetic fields pointing into the page, so they add constructively and cannot cancel.
Above Wire 1 (Point 1), Wire 1 produces a field pointing out of the page ($B_1 = \frac{\mu_0 I}{2\pi d}$), while Wire 2 produces a field pointing into the page ($B_2 = \frac{\mu_0 (2I)}{2\pi (2d)} = \frac{\mu_0 I}{2\pi d}$). Because the fields are equal in magnitude and opposite in direction, the net magnetic field at Point 1 is zero.'),
  ('ap-mock-physc-em-1_q17', 'ap-mock-physc-em-1', 17, false, 'A', 'Coulomb''s Law & Variable Acceleration', 'easy', 'A charge $+q$ is released from rest near a fixed charge $+Q$. Which graph best depicts the acceleration $a$ of $+q$ as a function of its distance $r$ from $+Q$?', '["Decreasing curve proportional to $1/r^2$","Decreasing linear line","Increasing curve","Horizontal line (constant)","Exponential increase"]'::jsonb, '(A) By Coulomb''s Law and Newton''s Second Law:
  $$a(r) = \frac{F_E}{m} = \frac{1}{4\pi\varepsilon_0}\frac{Qq}{m r^2} \propto \frac{1}{r^2}$$
The acceleration decreases monotonically following an inverse-square relationship with distance $r$, which is correctly illustrated in graph (A).'),
  ('ap-mock-physc-em-1_q18', 'ap-mock-physc-em-1', 18, false, 'C', 'Parallel Capacitors & Charge Redistribution', 'medium', 'Capacitors $C_1 = 3\ \mu\text{F}$ (initially $Q_1 = 24\ \mu\text{C}$) and $C_2 = 6\ \mu\text{F}$ (initially $Q_2 = 12\ \mu\text{C}$) are connected in parallel when switch S is closed. How much charge will be stored on the positive plate of the $6\ \mu\text{F}$ capacitor at equilibrium?', '["$18\\ \\mu\\text{C}$","$20\\ \\mu\\text{C}$","$24\\ \\mu\\text{C}$","$27\\ \\mu\\text{C}$","$36\\ \\mu\\text{C}$"]'::jsonb, '(C) Total initial charge on the plates:
  $$Q_{\text{total}} = 24\ \mu\text{C} + 12\ \mu\text{C} = 36\ \mu\text{C}$$
In parallel, both capacitors share the same potential difference $V = \frac{Q_{\text{total}}}{C_1 + C_2} = \frac{36\ \mu\text{C}}{3\ \mu\text{F} + 6\ \mu\text{F}} = 4\text{ V}$.
Final charge on the $6\ \mu\text{F}$ capacitor ($C_2$):
  $$Q_2'' = C_2 V = (6\ \mu\text{F})(4\text{ V}) = 24\ \mu\text{C}$$'),
  ('ap-mock-physc-em-1_q19', 'ap-mock-physc-em-1', 19, false, 'E', 'Motional EMF & Cross Product Geometry', 'medium', 'A metal bar of length $L$ is pulled with velocity $v$ through a uniform magnetic field $B$ pointing into the plane of the page. The velocity $v$ is parallel to the magnetic field. What is the voltage produced between the ends of the bar?', '["$vB$, with point X at a higher potential than point Y","$vB$, with point Y at a higher potential than point X","$vBL$, with point X at a higher potential than point Y","$vBL$, with point Y at a higher potential than point X","None of the above"]'::jsonb, '(E) The magnetic force on mobile conduction electrons within the conductor is given by $\vec{F}_B = q(\vec{v} \times \vec{B})$. Because the velocity vector $\vec{v}$ is parallel to the magnetic field vector $\vec{B}$, the cross product is zero ($\vec{v} \times \vec{B} = 0$). No charge separation occurs along the bar, producing zero motional emf ($\mathcal{E} = 0\text{ V}$). Therefore, (E) is the correct choice.'),
  ('ap-mock-physc-em-1_q20', 'ap-mock-physc-em-1', 20, false, 'E', 'Electric Dipole Field at Midpoint', 'medium', 'An electric dipole consists of a pair of equal but opposite point charges of magnitude $4.0\text{ nC}$ separated by a distance of $2.0\text{ cm}$. What is the electric field strength at the point midway between the charges?', '["$9.0 \\times 10^4\\text{ V/m}$","$1.8 \\times 10^5\\text{ V/m}$","$3.6 \\times 10^5\\text{ V/m}$","$7.2 \\times 10^4\\text{ V/m}$","$7.2 \\times 10^5\\text{ V/m}$"]'::jsonb, '(E) The midpoint is at distance $r = 1.0\text{ cm} = 0.01\text{ m}$ from each charge. Electric field lines point away from $+q$ and toward $-q$, so the electric field vectors from both charges point in the exact same direction and add constructively:
  $$E_{\text{net}} = E_+ + E_- = 2\left(\frac{1}{4\pi\varepsilon_0}\frac{q}{r^2}\right) = 2(9.0 \times 10^9)\frac{4.0 \times 10^{-9}\text{ C}}{(0.01\text{ m})^2} = 18.0 \times \frac{4.0}{1.0 \times 10^{-4}} = 7.2 \times 10^5\text{ V/m}$$'),
  ('ap-mock-physc-em-1_q21', 'ap-mock-physc-em-1', 21, false, 'E', 'Spherical Capacitor Capacitance Derivation', 'hard', 'Two concentric spherical metal shells have radii $R$ and $2R$, respectively. Find the capacitance of this spherical capacitor.', '["$\\frac{1}{8\\pi\\varepsilon_0 R}$","$\\frac{1}{4\\pi\\varepsilon_0 R}$","$2\\pi\\varepsilon_0 R$","$4\\pi\\varepsilon_0 R$","$8\\pi\\varepsilon_0 R$"]'::jsonb, '(E) The potential difference between two concentric spherical conductors of radii $a = R$ and $b = 2R$ carrying charge $+Q$ and $-Q$ is:
  $$\Delta V = \frac{Q}{4\pi\varepsilon_0}\left(\frac{1}{R} - \frac{1}{2R}\right) = \frac{Q}{4\pi\varepsilon_0}\left(\frac{1}{2R}\right) = \frac{Q}{8\pi\varepsilon_0 R}$$
The capacitance is defined by $C = \frac{Q}{\Delta V}$:
  $$C = \frac{Q}{\frac{Q}{8\pi\varepsilon_0 R}} = 8\pi\varepsilon_0 R$$'),
  ('ap-mock-physc-em-1_q22', 'ap-mock-physc-em-1', 22, false, 'C', 'Magnetic Force Work & Constant Speed', 'easy', 'Traveling at an initial speed of $1.5 \times 10^6\text{ m/s}$, a proton enters a region of constant magnetic field $B = 1.0\text{ T}$ at an angle of $30^\circ$ with $\vec{B}$. Compute the proton''s speed $4\text{ s}$ after entering the magnetic field.', '["$5.0 \\times 10^5\\text{ m/s}$","$7.5 \\times 10^5\\text{ m/s}$","$1.5 \\times 10^6\\text{ m/s}$","$3.0 \\times 10^6\\text{ m/s}$","$6.0 \\times 10^6\\text{ m/s}$"]'::jsonb, '(C) The magnetic force $\vec{F}_B = q(\vec{v} \times \vec{B})$ is strictly perpendicular to the velocity vector $\vec{v}$ at all times. Consequently, the magnetic work done on the charged particle is zero ($W = \int \vec{F}_B \cdot d\vec{r} = 0$), meaning the kinetic energy and orbital speed remain constant over time. Thus, the speed remains $1.5 \times 10^6\text{ m/s}$.'),
  ('ap-mock-physc-em-1_q23', 'ap-mock-physc-em-1', 23, false, 'B', 'LR Circuit Initial Response ($t = 0^+$)', 'medium', 'An $LR$ circuit consists of a battery $\mathcal{E}$, resistor $r$, and a parallel branch with resistor $R$ and inductor $L$ in series with another resistor $R$. What is the current through $r$ immediately after switch S is closed?', '["$0$","$\\frac{\\mathcal{E}}{r + R}$","$\\frac{\\mathcal{E}}{r + 2R}$","$\\frac{\\mathcal{E}}{2r + R}$","$\\frac{2\\mathcal{E}}{2r + R}$"]'::jsonb, '(B) At $t = 0^+$, the inductor produces an infinite back-emf opposing any instantaneous change in current, acting as an open circuit ($I_L(0^+) = 0$). Therefore, current only flows through the loop consisting of resistor $r$ and the single parallel resistor $R$. The initial current is:
  $$I(0^+) = \frac{\mathcal{E}}{r + R}$$'),
  ('ap-mock-physc-em-1_q24', 'ap-mock-physc-em-1', 24, false, 'B', 'LR Circuit Steady-State Inductor Energy', 'hard', 'After the switch has been kept closed for a long time in the circuit of Question 23, how much energy is stored in the inductor?', '["$\\frac{L\\mathcal{E}^2}{(2r+R)^2}$","$\\frac{L\\mathcal{E}^2}{2(2r+R)^2}$","$\\frac{L\\mathcal{E}^2}{4(2r+R)^2}$","$\\frac{RL\\mathcal{E}^2}{8(2r+R)^2}$","$\\frac{L\\mathcal{E}^2}{8(2r+R)^2}$"]'::jsonb, '(B) As $t \to \infty$, the inductor acts as a short circuit with zero resistance. The parallel combination of the two branches (each containing resistance $R$) is $R_{\text{parallel}} = R/2$.
Total equivalent resistance is $R_{\text{eq}} = r + R/2 = \frac{2r+R}{2}$.
Total current from the source: $I_{\text{total}} = \frac{\mathcal{E}}{r + R/2} = \frac{2\mathcal{E}}{2r + R}$.
Because both parallel branches have equal resistance $R$, the current splits evenly, so the inductor branch current is $I_L = \frac{1}{2}I_{\text{total}} = \frac{\mathcal{E}}{2r + R}$.
The magnetic energy stored is:
  $$U_L = \frac{1}{2}L I_L^2 = \frac{1}{2}L\left(\frac{\mathcal{E}}{2r+R}\right)^2 = \frac{L\mathcal{E}^2}{2(2r+R)^2}$$'),
  ('ap-mock-physc-em-1_q25', 'ap-mock-physc-em-1', 25, false, 'A', 'LR Circuit Switch Opening Dynamics', 'medium', 'After having been closed for a long time, the switch S is suddenly opened. What is the current through $r$ immediately after S is opened?', '["$0$","$\\frac{\\mathcal{E}}{r+R}$","$\\frac{\\mathcal{E}}{r+2R}$","$\\frac{\\mathcal{E}}{2r+R}$","$\\frac{2\\mathcal{E}}{2r+R}$"]'::jsonb, '(A) Opening the switch mechanically isolates the branch containing resistor $r$ from the closed loop. The current through $r$ drops instantaneously to zero ($I_r = 0$). The inductor continues to drive current through the remaining closed loop containing the two $R$ resistors until its stored magnetic energy is dissipated.'),
  ('ap-mock-physc-em-1_q26', 'ap-mock-physc-em-1', 26, false, 'C', 'Electrostatic Shielding & Cavity Fields', 'hard', 'A solid neutral metal sphere of radius $6a$ contains an off-center spherical cavity of radius $a$ enclosing a point charge $+q$. If $E_X$ and $E_Y$ denote the electric field strengths at exterior points X and Y located at the same distance $r > 6a$ from the center of the sphere, which of the following is true?', '["$E_X = 16E_Y$","$E_X = \\left(\\frac{11}{5}\\right)E_Y$","$E_X = E_Y$","$E_X = \\left(\\frac{11}{5}\\right)^2 E_Y$","$E_X = 0$"]'::jsonb, '(C) By electrostatic shielding, charge $-q$ distributes on the inner cavity wall, leaving $+q$ distributed uniformly over the outer spherical conductor surface. Outside the conducting sphere ($r > 6a$), the external electric field is identical to that of a point charge $+q$ located at the sphere''s geometric center:
  $$E(r) = \frac{1}{4\pi\varepsilon_0}\frac{q}{r^2}$$
Because points X and Y are equidistant from the center of the sphere, $E_X = E_Y$.'),
  ('ap-mock-physc-em-1_q27', 'ap-mock-physc-em-1', 27, false, 'B', 'Electric Potential & Work by Electric Field', 'medium', 'Two charges $+Q$ are fixed at $(0, 4\text{ m})$ and $(0, -4\text{ m})$. Determine the work done by the electric field to move a charge $-Q$ from infinity to point P at $(3\text{ m}, 0)$.', '["$\\frac{2kQ^2}{3}$","$\\frac{2kQ^2}{5}$","$-\\frac{2kQ^2}{5}$","$\\frac{kQ^2}{5}$","$-\\frac{3kQ^2}{5}$"]'::jsonb, '(B) The distance from each $+Q$ charge to point P is $r = \sqrt{3^2 + 4^2} = 5\text{ m}$.
The electric potential at point P is:
  $$V_P = \frac{kQ}{5} + \frac{kQ}{5} = \frac{2kQ}{5}$$
The work done by the electric field is $W_E = -\Delta U = -q(V_P - V_\infty)$:
  $$W_E = -(-Q)\left(\frac{2kQ}{5} - 0\right) = +\frac{2kQ^2}{5}$$'),
  ('ap-mock-physc-em-1_q28', 'ap-mock-physc-em-1', 28, false, 'E', 'RL Circuit Exponential Rise Time Constant', 'medium', 'A circuit connects a battery $\mathcal{E}$, switch, resistor $R$, and inductor $L$ in series. How long will it take for the current to reach $99\%$ of its maximum value?', '["$(\\ln 0.99)\\frac{L}{R}$","$(\\ln 99)RL$","$\\left(\\ln\\frac{1}{100}\\right)\\frac{L}{R}$","$\\left(\\ln\\frac{100}{99}\\right)\\frac{L}{R}$","$(\\ln 100)\\frac{L}{R}$"]'::jsonb, '(E) The current in a charging $RL$ circuit obeys $I(t) = I_{\max}(1 - e^{-t/\tau})$, where $\tau = L/R$. Setting $I(t) = 0.99 I_{\max}$:
  $$1 - e^{-t/\tau} = 0.99 \implies e^{-t/\tau} = 0.01 = \frac{1}{100}$$
  $$-\frac{t}{\tau} = \ln\left(\frac{1}{100}\right) = -\ln(100) \implies t = (\ln 100)\tau = (\ln 100)\frac{L}{R}$$'),
  ('ap-mock-physc-em-1_q29', 'ap-mock-physc-em-1', 29, false, 'E', 'Parallel Appliance Circuits & Fuse Limits', 'easy', 'What is the maximum number of $40\text{ W}$ light bulbs that can be connected in parallel across a $120\text{ V}$ source without exceeding a $5\text{ A}$ fuse limit?', '["6","9","12","14","15"]'::jsonb, '(E) The maximum total electric power available without blowing the fuse is:
  $$P_{\max} = V I_{\max} = (120\text{ V})(5\text{ A}) = 600\text{ W}$$
The number of $40\text{ W}$ bulbs in parallel is:
  $$N = \frac{P_{\max}}{P_{\text{bulb}}} = \frac{600\text{ W}}{40\text{ W}} = 15 \text{ bulbs}$$'),
  ('ap-mock-physc-em-1_q30', 'ap-mock-physc-em-1', 30, false, 'D', 'Faraday''s Law, Lenz''s Law & Induced Current', 'easy', 'A loop of wire is situated in a magnetic field $\vec{B}$ pointing out of the page. If $B$ decreases uniformly in strength, the induced current in the loop is:', '["clockwise and decreasing","clockwise and increasing","counterclockwise and decreasing","counterclockwise and constant","counterclockwise and increasing"]'::jsonb, '(D) As the out-of-page flux decreases, by Lenz''s Law the induced current must create out-of-page magnetic flux to oppose this change, which requires a counterclockwise current. Because $B(t)$ decreases uniformly ($\frac{dB}{dt} = \text{constant}$), the induced electromotive force $\mathcal{E} = -A\frac{dB}{dt}$ and the resulting induced current $I = \mathcal{E}/R$ are nonzero and constant.'),
  ('ap-mock-physc-em-1_q31', 'ap-mock-physc-em-1', 31, false, 'D', 'Composite Dielectric Parallel-Plate Capacitor', 'hard', 'A dielectric of thickness $d/3$ and constant $K$ is inserted between the plates (separation $d$) of a parallel-plate capacitor. What is the total capacitance?', '["$\\frac{\\varepsilon_0 A(2+3K)}{d}$","$\\frac{3\\varepsilon_0 A}{d(2K+1)}$","$\\frac{K\\varepsilon_0 A}{d(2K+1)}$","$\\frac{3K\\varepsilon_0 A}{d(2K+1)}$","$\\frac{3K\\varepsilon_0 A}{d(K+2)}$"]'::jsonb, '(D) The system consists of three series capacitors: two vacuum gaps of thickness $d/3$ and one dielectric slab of thickness $d/3$.
  $$C_{\text{vac}} = \frac{\varepsilon_0 A}{d/3} = \frac{3\varepsilon_0 A}{d}, \quad C_{\text{diel}} = \frac{K\varepsilon_0 A}{d/3} = \frac{3K\varepsilon_0 A}{d}$$
  $$\frac{1}{C_{\text{eq}}} = \frac{1}{C_{\text{vac}}} + \frac{1}{C_{\text{diel}}} + \frac{1}{C_{\text{vac}}} = \frac{2d}{3\varepsilon_0 A} + \frac{d}{3K\varepsilon_0 A} = \frac{d(2K+1)}{3K\varepsilon_0 A}$$
  $$C_{\text{eq}} = \frac{3K\varepsilon_0 A}{d(2K+1)}$$'),
  ('ap-mock-physc-em-1_q32', 'ap-mock-physc-em-1', 32, false, 'A', 'Dipole Electric Field vs. Potential Zeros', 'medium', 'For two charges $+Q$ and $-Q$ separated by a finite distance, at how many points in the plane of the page are both the electric field and the electric potential equal to zero?', '["0","1","2","Infinite","None of the above"]'::jsonb, '(A) The electric potential $V = 0$ along the perpendicular bisector line. However, on this line, the electric fields from the two charges add constructively parallel to the dipole axis, meaning $\vec{E} \ne 0$ everywhere in the finite plane. Thus, there are $0$ points where both $\vec{E} = 0$ and $V = 0$ simultaneously (except at infinity).'),
  ('ap-mock-physc-em-1_q33', 'ap-mock-physc-em-1', 33, false, 'D', 'Ampere''s Law Line Integrals', 'medium', 'Four wires pass perpendicularly through a square $WXYZ$, carrying currents $I, 2I, 3I, 4I$ with orientations as labeled (two in, two out). What is the absolute value of $\oint \vec{B}\cdot d\vec{\ell}$ around $WXYZ$?', '["$\\frac{1}{2}\\mu_0 I$","$\\mu_0 I$","$\\sqrt{5}\\mu_0 I$","$2\\mu_0 I$","$5\\mu_0 I$"]'::jsonb, '(D) By Ampere''s Law: $\oint \vec{B}\cdot d\vec{\ell} = \mu_0 I_{\text{enc}}$.
The net enclosed current is the algebraic sum taking directions into account:
  $$I_{\text{enc}} = (2I + 4I) - (I + 3I) = 6I - 4I = 2I$$
  $$\left|\oint \vec{B}\cdot d\vec{\ell}\right| = 2\mu_0 I$$'),
  ('ap-mock-physc-em-1_q34', 'ap-mock-physc-em-1', 34, false, 'B', 'Conservative Electric Fields & Path Independence', 'easy', 'A charge $+q$ is moved from point A to point B along Path 1. What would the change in potential energy be if the charge moved along Path 2 from point A to point B instead?', '["Double","The same","Negative of the original","Zero","Cannot be determined"]'::jsonb, '(B) The electrostatic force is conservative, which means the electrostatic potential difference $\Delta V = V_B - V_A$ and the corresponding change in potential energy $\Delta U = q\Delta V$ depend entirely on the initial and final endpoints and are completely independent of the path taken.'),
  ('ap-mock-physc-em-1_q35', 'ap-mock-physc-em-1', 35, false, 'A', '2D Electrostatic Vector Forces & Acceleration', 'hard', 'Two point charges $+Q$ are fixed a distance $L$ apart. A particle of charge $-q$ and mass $m$ is placed symmetrically on the perpendicular bisector at distance $L/2$. What is its initial acceleration when released from rest?', '["$\\frac{\\sqrt{2}Qq}{2\\pi\\varepsilon_0 L^2 m}$","$\\frac{Qq}{\\pi\\varepsilon_0 L^2 m}$","$\\frac{2Qq}{\\pi\\varepsilon_0 L^2 m}$","$\\frac{2\\sqrt{2}Qq}{\\pi\\varepsilon_0 L^2 m}$","$\\frac{4Qq}{\\pi\\varepsilon_0 L^2 m}$"]'::jsonb, '(A) The distance from $-q$ to each $+Q$ is $r = \sqrt{(L/2)^2 + (L/2)^2} = \frac{L}{\sqrt{2}}$.
The force from each $+Q$ charge has magnitude:
  $$F_1 = \frac{1}{4\pi\varepsilon_0}\frac{Qq}{r^2} = \frac{1}{4\pi\varepsilon_0}\frac{Qq}{L^2/2} = \frac{2Qq}{4\pi\varepsilon_0 L^2}$$
The horizontal components cancel, and the vertical components add at an angle of $45^\circ$:
  $$F_{\text{net}} = 2 F_1 \cos 45^\circ = 2\left(\frac{2Qq}{4\pi\varepsilon_0 L^2}\right)\left(\frac{\sqrt{2}}{2}\right) = \frac{\sqrt{2}Qq}{2\pi\varepsilon_0 L^2}$$
Acceleration $a = \frac{F_{\text{net}}}{m} = \frac{\sqrt{2}Qq}{2\pi\varepsilon_0 L^2 m}$.'),
  ('ap-mock-physc-mech-2_q1', 'ap-mock-physc-mech-2', 1, false, 'D', 'Practice Test 2 – Mechanics', 'medium', 'A variable force exerted during time interval \Delta t changes the velocity of mass m from v0 to vf. What is the average power exerted during this time interval?', '["(vf − v0)/\\Delta t","mvf − mv0","Equal in magnitude with opposite orientation","Independent of the mass and charge distribution","Zero net change under static equilibrium conditions"]'::jsonb, '(D) Average power is given by the equation . What is the work done? Because there are no other forces acting on the block, (according to the work-energy theorem). Dividing by the time increment to calculate the average power yields choice (D). Note that the correct answer can be obtained using only dimensional analysis.'),
  ('ap-mock-physc-mech-2_q2', 'ap-mock-physc-mech-2', 2, false, 'D', 'Practice Test 2 – Mechanics', 'medium', 'A point charge −q is released from rest at the center of a parallel plate capacitor, as shown. Note that the plates have small holes that allow the charge to exit the capacitor. Which of the following statements describes the charge’s behavior a long time after it exits the capacitor?', '["It is moving to the right with a kinetic energy of KE = qV.","It is moving to the left with a kinetic energy of KE = qV.","It is moving to the right with a kinetic energy of KE = qV/2.","It is moving to the left with a kinetic energy of KE = qV/2.","It never reaches a constant velocity but instead continually oscillates in and out of the capacitor."]'::jsonb, '(D) Average power is given by the equation . What is the work done? Because there are no other forces acting on the block, (according to the work-energy theorem). Dividing by the time increment to calculate the average power yields choice (D). Note that the correct answer can be obtained using only dimensional analysis.'),
  ('ap-mock-physc-mech-2_q3', 'ap-mock-physc-mech-2', 3, false, 'E', 'Practice Test 2 – Mechanics', 'medium', 'A particle moves with constant speed v counterclockwise around a circle of radius r in the xy-plane. Which of the following correctly describes the x-component of the velocity?', '["It is constant and equal to v.","It is constant but not equal to v.","It varies between zero and v.","It varies between zero and .","It varies between −v and v."]'::jsonb, '(E) Consider the figure. At points A and C, the x-component of velocity is zero, at point B it is equal to −v, and at point D it is equal to +v. This range of values is consistent only with choice (E).'),
  ('ap-mock-physc-mech-2_q4', 'ap-mock-physc-mech-2', 4, false, 'E', 'Practice Test 2 – Mechanics', 'medium', 'Describe the potential V as a function of r inside a spherical shell with uniform surface charge density.', '["V = 0.","V is constant.","V has a constant slope.","V is proportional to 1/r.","V is proportional to 1/r2."]'::jsonb, '(E) Consider the figure. At points A and C, the x-component of velocity is zero, at point B it is equal to −v, and at point D it is equal to +v. This range of values is consistent only with choice (E).'),
  ('ap-mock-physc-mech-2_q5', 'ap-mock-physc-mech-2', 5, false, 'E', 'Practice Test 2 – Mechanics', 'medium', 'A baseball is falling toward Earth. If ab is the acceleration of the baseball due to the gravitational pull of Earth and ae is the acceleration of Earth due to the baseball, which of the following is true?', '["ae = 0","ab = ae","ab >> ae, and the accelerations are parallel.","ae >> ab, and the accelerations are parallel.","ab >> ae, and the accelerations are antiparallel."]'::jsonb, '(E) According to Newton’s third law, the baseball and Earth exert equal and opposite forces on each other. Therefore, the accelerations they experience must be in opposite directions. Because F = meae = mbab, the larger object (Earth) experiences a smaller acceleration (which is imperceptible).'),
  ('ap-mock-physc-mech-2_q6', 'ap-mock-physc-mech-2', 6, false, 'E', 'Practice Test 2 – Mechanics', 'medium', 'While discharging a capacitor through a resistor, how many of the following functions are exponentially decreasing? I. the current through the circuit II. the energy stored in the capacitor III. the voltage across the resistor IV. the power dissipated in the resistor V. the electric field within the capacitor VI. the charge on the capacitor', '["two","three","four","five","six"]'::jsonb, '(E) According to Newton’s third law, the baseball and Earth exert equal and opposite forces on each other. Therefore, the accelerations they experience must be in opposite directions. Because F = meae = mbab, the larger object (Earth) experiences a smaller acceleration (which is imperceptible).'),
  ('ap-mock-physc-mech-2_q7', 'ap-mock-physc-mech-2', 7, false, 'A', 'Practice Test 2 – Mechanics', 'medium', 'If the angular position of a disk is given by the equation \theta(t) = 2 + 4t − 3t2, what is the angular velocity of the disk as a function of time?', '["4 − 6t","6t − 4","2t + 2t2 − 3t3","3t3 − 2t2 − 2t","3t3 − 2t2 − 2t + C"]'::jsonb, '(A) The angular velocity is simply the derivative of the angular position:'),
  ('ap-mock-physc-mech-2_q8', 'ap-mock-physc-mech-2', 8, false, 'A', 'Practice Test 2 – Mechanics', 'medium', 'The figure shows two coaxial cylinders that have charge distributed uniformly on their surfaces, with a linear charge density of −\lambda and +\lambda', '["Directly proportional to the parameter (linear scaling)","Inversely proportional to the square of the separation","Equal in magnitude with opposite orientation","Independent of the mass and charge distribution","Zero net change under static equilibrium conditions"]'::jsonb, '(A) The angular velocity is simply the derivative of the angular position:'),
  ('ap-mock-physc-mech-2_q9', 'ap-mock-physc-mech-2', 9, false, 'A', 'Practice Test 2 – Mechanics', 'medium', 'When an object moving in the circular track as shown has a velocity in the +y-direction, its acceleration has components in the +x- and +y- directions. Which of the following statements is true?', '["The object is moving clockwise with increasing speed.","The object is moving counterclockwise with increasing speed.","The object is moving clockwise with decreasing speed.","The object is moving counterclockwise with decreasing speed.","The object is moving clockwise with constant speed."]'::jsonb, '(A) Because the object has a tangential acceleration parallel to its velocity, its speed is increasing. Because the centripetal acceleration (perpendicular to the velocity) always points toward the center of the circle, the object must move clockwise so that the centripetal acceleration points in the +x-direction while the object is instantaneously moving in the +y-direction.'),
  ('ap-mock-physc-mech-2_q10', 'ap-mock-physc-mech-2', 10, false, 'E', 'Practice Test 2 – Mechanics', 'medium', 'A force F exerted on a block parallel to an inclined slope as shown causes the block’s speed to increase from zero to v as it moves a distance d along the incline. If the coefficient of friction is \muK, how much work does gravity do during this time interval?', '["\\muKmg(cos \\theta)d","\\muKmg(cos \\theta)d + mg(sin \\theta)d","(D) mgd sin \\theta","2E_0","−mgd sin \\theta"]'::jsonb, '(E) The gravitational force is constant, so the work it does is given by the equation . The y-coordinate increases by an amount d sin \theta, so the work done by gravity is given by choice (E). (Check: The work done by gravity must come out negative because the force of gravity is antiparallel to the vertical displacement of the object.)'),
  ('ap-mock-physc-mech-2_q11', 'ap-mock-physc-mech-2', 11, false, 'D', 'Practice Test 2 – Mechanics', 'medium', 'A point mass is released from rest as shown. If the inclined planes are frictionless, which of the following statements is correct?', '["The mass is undergoing simple harmonic motion with a period of T = (2d/g sin \\theta)1/2.","The mass is undergoing simple harmonic motion with a period of T = 2(2d/g sin \\theta)1/2.","The mass is undergoing simple harmonic motion with a period of T = 4(2d/g sin \\theta)1/2.","As the mass slides down the slope, the component of the momentum perpendicular to the slope is constant.","The mass is in static equilibrium."]'::jsonb, '(D) Choices (A)−(C): The mass is not undergoing SHM because the restoring force is not proportional to the displacement (instead, it is constant and equal to mg sin \theta). Choice (E): The mass would be in static equilibrium if placed at the bottom of the incline with zero initial velocity, but in the present case the object oscillates back and forth along the inclines. Choice (D): The component of the momentum perpendicular to the slope is zero as the mass initially slides down the slope, and it remains zero. (There is zero net force in this direction because the normal force cancels the component of the gravitational force perpendicular to the incline.)'),
  ('ap-mock-physc-mech-2_q12', 'ap-mock-physc-mech-2', 12, false, 'D', 'Practice Test 2 – Mechanics', 'medium', 'An object of mass m has its position given by the equation x(t) = at4 − bt3 + c. What is the force on the object as a function of time?', '["F(t) = (4at3 − 3bt2)","F(t) = (4at3 − 3bt2)m","F(t) = (12at2 − 6bt)","F(t) = (12at2 − 6bt)m","F(t) = −(12at2 − 6bt)m"]'::jsonb, '(D) Differentiating twice to calculate the acceleration from the position yields a(t) = (12at2 − 6bt). Applying Newton’s second law then gives F = ma = ma(t) = m(12at2 − 6bt).'),
  ('ap-mock-physc-mech-2_q13', 'ap-mock-physc-mech-2', 13, false, 'B', 'Practice Test 2 – Mechanics', 'medium', 'A mass is suspended by two ropes as shown. What is the ratio of the tension forces in the two ropes?', '["T1/T2 = sin \\theta1/sin \\theta2","T1/T2 = sin \\theta2/sin \\theta1","T1/T2 = cos \\theta1/cos \\theta2","T1/T2 = cos \\theta2/cos \\theta1","T1/T2 = tan \\theta1/tan \\theta2"]'::jsonb, '(B) For the object to be in static equilibrium, the net force in the x- direction must be zero: Fnet,x = 0 = T1 sin \theta1 − T2 sin \theta2. Rearranging this equation yields choice (B).'),
  ('ap-mock-physc-mech-2_q14', 'ap-mock-physc-mech-2', 14, false, 'C', 'Practice Test 2 – Mechanics', 'medium', 'Two projectiles are thrown at a vertical wall with initial velocities and positions as shown. Which of the following comparisons is correct regarding the height of the impact and the time the projectile spends in the air before it hits the wall? Height of impact Time in the air', '["h1 > h2 t1 < t2","h1 = h2 t1 < t2","h1 > h2 t1 = t2","h1 < h2 t1 = t2","The answer cannot be determined because it depends on whether the projectiles hit the wall before or after reaching the peaks of their trajectories."]'::jsonb, '(C) The projectiles hit the wall when their x-positions are equal to the position of the wall. Because both projectiles have the same x- components of velocity, they require the same amount of time to hit the wall. Because projectile 1 has a greater y-component of velocity, v1(t) > v2(t), it hits the surface at a greater height (noting that both projectiles hit the surface after the same amount of time t).'),
  ('ap-mock-physc-mech-2_q15', 'ap-mock-physc-mech-2', 15, false, 'C', 'Practice Test 2 – Mechanics', 'medium', 'A ball is launched horizontally off a cliff and moves through the air subject to both downward gravity and air resistance directed opposite to its velocity. At the instant the ball reaches the peak of a trajectory or moves along a curved path with horizontal velocity to the right, which of the following describes the direction of the net force on the ball?', '["Directly upward","Directly downward","Downward and to the left (opposing velocity and directed toward Earth)","Downward and to the right","Directly to the left"]'::jsonb, 'Gravity exerts a downward force F_g = -m g j. Air resistance opposes the instantaneous motion: if the ball is moving to the right, air resistance exerts a force to the left, F_drag = -F_d i. The net force is F_net = F_g + F_drag, which has components pointing downward and to the left.'),
  ('ap-mock-physc-mech-2_q16', 'ap-mock-physc-mech-2', 16, false, 'A', 'Practice Test 2 – Mechanics', 'medium', 'Referring to the situation described in question 11, at what point is the magnitude of the net force maximized?', '["point A","point B","point C","point D","point E"]'::jsonb, '(A) The frictional force of air resistance is proportional to the object’s velocity and opposite in direction. Thus, the x-component of the frictional force (which in the x-direction equals the net force) is maximized when the x-component of the velocity is the greatest. This occurs at point A, before friction has begun to decrease the magnitude of the x-component of velocity. The y-component of the net force is equal to the force exerted by gravity plus the y-component of the frictional force. The magnitude of this sum is greatest when the two forces point in the same direction, which occurs for points to the left of point C (when the velocity has an upward component, causing a downward component in frictional force). The downward vertical component of the frictional force is greatest at point A (before the y-component of velocity has begun to decrease), so at point A the y-component of the net force is maximized. Because both components of the net force are maximized at point A, the magnitude of the net force is also maximized at point A.'),
  ('ap-mock-physc-mech-2_q17', 'ap-mock-physc-mech-2', 17, false, 'B', 'Practice Test 2 – Mechanics', 'medium', 'On a certain planet, a ball is thrown upward with a kinetic energy of 100 J and an angle of elevation of 45°. If the ball has a kinetic energy of 75 J at a height of 10 m, what is the maximum height of its trajectory?', '["10 m","20 m","40 m","60 m","80 m"]'::jsonb, '(B) Consider the equation for conservation of energy between the instant the ball is launched and the instant it reaches its maximum height (at the maximum height, the y-velocity will be zero and the x- velocity will be equal to the original x-velocity, in this case . Therefore, at the peak of the object’s trajectory, half of its total energy (i.e., 50 J) will appear as potential energy. We also know that 25 J is stored as potential energy at a height of 10 m. Because potential energy is proportional to height, the potential energy will equal 50 J, its value at the peak of the trajectory, at a height of 20 m.'),
  ('ap-mock-physc-mech-2_q18', 'ap-mock-physc-mech-2', 18, false, 'D', 'Practice Test 2 – Mechanics', 'medium', 'A particle moves with speed v around a circle of radius r. What is the period of the particle’s motion?', '["f / 2","f / \\sqrt{2}","T = 2\\pi(v/r)","T = 2\\pi(r/v)","T = r/2\\piv"]'::jsonb, '(D) The only answers that have correct units are (D) and (E). The period is the amount of time it takes for the particle to make one complete cycle, in this case one complete revolution around the circle. The total distance of a cycle is the circumference of the circle, 2\pir, and the speed is v, so the time required for one cycle is t = distance/rate = 2\pir/v.'),
  ('ap-mock-physc-mech-2_q19', 'ap-mock-physc-mech-2', 19, false, 'E', 'Practice Test 2 – Mechanics', 'medium', 'Assuming Earth is a sphere of uniform mass density and radius R_E, at what radial distance(s) r from Earth''s center is the acceleration due to gravity equal to g/2 (where g is the surface gravitational acceleration)?', '["r = sqrt(2) R_E only","r = 2 R_E only","r = R_E / 2 only","r = R_E / 4 only","At both r = R_E / 2 (inside Earth) and r = sqrt(2) R_E (outside Earth)"]'::jsonb, 'Inside Earth (r < R_E), only the enclosed mass contributes to gravity: g(r) = g * (r / R_E). Setting g(r) = g/2 yields r = R_E / 2. Outside Earth (r > R_E), g(r) = g * (R_E / r)^2. Setting g(r) = g/2 yields (R_E / r)^2 = 1/2 ==> r = sqrt(2) R_E. Thus, the acceleration equals g/2 at two locations: r = R_E / 2 and r = sqrt(2) R_E.')
ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.ap_exam_questions (id, test_id, question_order, is_frq, answer_key, topic, difficulty, question_text, options_json, explanation)
VALUES
  ('ap-mock-physc-mech-2_q20', 'ap-mock-physc-mech-2', 20, false, 'E', 'Practice Test 2 – Mechanics', 'medium', 'Two forces act on a circular mass as shown. A third force is exerted at point P. For this force to ensure static equilibrium,', '["Directly proportional to the parameter (linear scaling)","Inversely proportional to the square of the separation","Equal in magnitude with opposite orientation","Independent of the mass and charge distribution","It is impossible for a force exerted at point P to cause static equilibrium."]'::jsonb, '(E) For the net force to be zero, the force at point P must be (pointing downward and to the right). However, this produces a net counterclockwise torque about the center of the object (note that the two forces in the figure exert zero torque about this point). Therefore, it is impossible for a force exerted at point B to produce static equilibrium.'),
  ('ap-mock-physc-mech-2_q21', 'ap-mock-physc-mech-2', 21, false, 'D', 'Practice Test 2 – Mechanics', 'medium', 'A railroad car is traveling to the right at a speed of 10 m/s relative to the ground. What is the velocity of an automobile relative to the railroad car if the velocity of the automobile relative to the ground is 2 m/s to the left?', '["8 m/s to the right","8 m/s to the left","12 m/s to the right","12 m/s to the left","2 m/s to the left"]'::jsonb, '(D) The velocity of the automobile relative to the ground obeys the formula'),
  ('ap-mock-physc-mech-2_q22', 'ap-mock-physc-mech-2', 22, false, 'C', 'Practice Test 2 – Mechanics', 'medium', 'A block of mass 0.2 kg on a horizontal frictionless surface is attached to a spring of spring constant 200 N/m. The block is pulled 0.8 m from its equilibrium position and released from rest. It then executes simple harmonic motion. What is the maximum elastic potential energy of the system?', '["3 J","6.4 J","64 J","8,000 J","200,000 J"]'::jsonb, '(C) The elastic potential energy is given by .'),
  ('ap-mock-physc-mech-2_q23', 'ap-mock-physc-mech-2', 23, false, 'A', 'Practice Test 2 – Mechanics', 'medium', 'A small ball of mass m attached to a light string of length r swings in a vertical circle. What is the minimum speed the ball must have at the top of the circle so that the string remains taut (tension T >= 0)?', '["sqrt(g r)","sqrt(2 g r)","sqrt(5 g r)","2 sqrt(g r)","1/2 sqrt(g r)"]'::jsonb, 'At the top of the vertical circle, both gravity and tension point downward toward the center: T + m g = m v^2 / r. For the string to remain taut, tension must be non-negative (T >= 0). The critical threshold occurs when T = 0, giving m g = m v^2 / r ==> v = sqrt(g r).'),
  ('ap-mock-physc-mech-2_q24', 'ap-mock-physc-mech-2', 24, false, 'D', 'Practice Test 2 – Mechanics', 'medium', 'Consider a spring with a restoring force given by F(x) = −bx3 + cx5. If the potential energy is chosen to be zero at the equilibrium position, which of the following functions gives the elastic potential energy as a function of the spring’s displacement?', '["U(x) = 5cx4 − 3bx2","U(x) = 3bx2 − 5cx4","U(x) = (cx6/6) − (bx4/4)","U(x) = (bx4/4) − (cx6/6)","none of the above"]'::jsonb, '(D) Fx = −dU/dx, so'),
  ('ap-mock-physc-mech-2_q25', 'ap-mock-physc-mech-2', 25, false, 'B', 'Practice Test 2 – Mechanics', 'medium', 'If the block shown moves with constant speed v, what form do the horizontal and vertical components of Newton’s second law, respectively, take?', '["F sin \\theta = \\muKmg for both components","F cos \\theta = \\muKFN and FN + F sin \\theta = mg","F sin \\theta = \\muKFN and FN + F cos \\theta = mg","F cos \\theta = \\muKFN and FN = mg + F sin \\theta","F sin \\theta = \\muKFN and FN = mg + F cos \\theta"]'::jsonb, '(B) The free-body diagram is shown below.'),
  ('ap-mock-physc-mech-2_q26', 'ap-mock-physc-mech-2', 26, false, 'B', 'Practice Test 2 – Mechanics', 'medium', 'What is the power exerted by the force F in question 21?', '["P = F(sin \\theta)v","P = F(cos \\theta)v","P = −F(sin \\theta)v","P = −F(cos \\theta)v","P = \\muKmgv Questions 23–25 refer to the same situation."]'::jsonb, '(B) P = F ⋅ v = Fǁv = F(cos \theta)v. In words, the power is given by the component of the force parallel to the velocity, +F cos \theta multiplied by the magnitude of the velocity v.'),
  ('ap-mock-physc-mech-2_q27', 'ap-mock-physc-mech-2', 27, false, 'B', 'Practice Test 2 – Mechanics', 'medium', 'A horizontal bug of mass m stands at rest on the edge of a horizontal disk of mass M, radius r, and rotational inertia , which is also at rest. At a certain time, the bug starts walking counterclockwise around the rim (when viewed from above) so that it moves with a speed v with respect to the ground. If the disk is free to rotate about its center, what is the resulting angular velocity of the disk (with respect to the ground)?', '["\\omega = mv/Mr","\\omega = 2mv/Mr","\\omega = Mv/2mr","\\omega = Mv/mr","none of the above"]'::jsonb, '(B) Angular momentum is conserved about the axle of the disk (assuming there is no friction in the axle). Because the initial angular momentum about this point is zero, the final angular momentum must also be zero. This requires that the disk rotate in the direction opposite the bug (clockwise) with an angular momentum of equal magnitude: Solving for the magnitude of the angular velocity gives choice (B).'),
  ('ap-mock-physc-mech-2_q28', 'ap-mock-physc-mech-2', 28, false, 'C', 'Practice Test 2 – Mechanics', 'medium', 'In terms of the speed of the bug, v (with respect to the ground), and the magnitude of the angular velocity of the disk, \omega, what is the speed of the bug relative to the disk?', '["(v/r) + \\omega","(v/r) − \\omega","v + r\\omega","v − r\\omega","Zero net change under static equilibrium conditions"]'::jsonb, '(C) Consider, for example, when the bug crosses the +x-axis (setting the origin of the coordinate system at the axle of the disk). The velocity of the bug with respect to the ground is because the bug is moving with speed v counterclockwise. The speed of the point on the disk where the bug stands is (relative to the ground) because the disk is rotating clockwise with angular velocity \omega. The relative velocity of the bug with respect to the disk is given by Although we solved this problem for a particular point in the bug’s motion, because of the rotational symmetry of the disk, the speeds always sum in this manner.'),
  ('ap-mock-physc-mech-2_q29', 'ap-mock-physc-mech-2', 29, false, 'B', 'Practice Test 2 – Mechanics', 'medium', 'What is the minimum coefficient of static friction between the bug’s feet and the disk required to keep the bug from sliding off the disk as it walks with constant speed v?', '["mv2/r","v2/rg","(v + \\omegar)2/rg","(v − \\omegar)2/rg","No static friction is needed."]'::jsonb, '(B) The frictional force must provide the centripetal force required to keep the bug moving in circular motion. Recall that Newton’s laws hold only in inertial reference frames, in this case the ground frame. Therefore, we must apply the equation Fcent = mv2/r in the ground frame (note that v is the bug’s speed in this frame of reference). Setting the centripetal force equal to the frictional force gives mv2/r = \musmg. Solving for the coefficient of friction yields choice (B). Note: Why is this static rather than kinetic friction? Although the bug is moving, its feet are not moving with respect to the disk (i.e., the bug’s feet are not slipping as it walks).'),
  ('ap-mock-physc-mech-2_q30', 'ap-mock-physc-mech-2', 30, false, 'C', 'Practice Test 2 – Mechanics', 'medium', 'Two masses m1 and m2 move along a straight line in opposite directions with speeds v1 and v2, respectively. What is the speed of the center of mass of the two-body system?', '["(m1 v1 + m2 v2) / (m1 + m2)","(v1 + v2) / 2","|m1 v1 - m2 v2| / (m1 + m2)","|m1 - m2|(v1 + v2) / (m1 + m2)","sqrt(v1^2 + v2^2)"]'::jsonb, 'Taking the direction of m1 as positive, the velocities are +v1 and -v2. The velocity of the center of mass is v_cm = (m1 v1 - m2 v2) / (m1 + m2). The speed of the center of mass is the absolute value of this velocity: |v_cm| = |m1 v1 - m2 v2| / (m1 + m2).'),
  ('ap-mock-physc-mech-2_q31', 'ap-mock-physc-mech-2', 31, false, 'D', 'Practice Test 2 – Mechanics', 'medium', 'A force F pushes on two masses lying on a horizontal frictionless surface as shown, causing them to accelerate to the right with magnitude a. Which of the following statements is false?', '["m2 exerts a force of on m1.","The net force on m1 is F − m2a.","The acceleration of the blocks is a = F/(m1 + m2).","The sum of the contact forces exerted by m1 on m2, and vice versa, produce net work on the two-block system.","The net force on m2 is m2a."]'::jsonb, '(D) Choice (D): The force that m1 exerts on m2 is an internal force, so it is balanced by another internal force so that together these internal forces make no contribution to the net force or the net work on the two- block system. (If we were considering the blocks separately, this force would no longer be an internal force.) See Example 3.6 on page 108 for further explanation.'),
  ('ap-mock-physc-mech-2_q32', 'ap-mock-physc-mech-2', 32, false, 'D', 'Practice Test 2 – Mechanics', 'medium', 'A particle is released from rest at point A. The figure shows the potential energy of the particle as a function of position. If no nonconservative forces are present, which of the following statements is true?', '["The particle oscillates between points B and F.","The particle cannot reach point G.","The particle moves in simple harmonic motion.","The particle experiences no force at points C, D, and E.","The particle’s speed is maximized at point E."]'::jsonb, '(D) The particle oscillates between points A and H (the particle has a total energy of 1 J, so it cannot move beyond these points because that would require having negative kinetic energy). Since point G is within the boundaries of this oscillation, it will indeed reach and pass point G. Because there is no simple linear restoring force [which would be equivalent to an upward-parabolic U(x) curve], the particle does not move in simple harmonic motion. The force, equal to F(x) = −dU/dx, is zero at points C, D, and E because the slope of the function U(x) is zero at these points. The particle’s speed is maximized at point C (not point E), as this is the place where the particle’s potential energy is the lowest (and thus, its kinetic energy is the highest).'),
  ('ap-mock-physc-mech-2_q33', 'ap-mock-physc-mech-2', 33, false, 'D', 'Practice Test 2 – Mechanics', 'medium', 'If a rotating object accelerates from rest with an angular acceleration of 2 rad/s2, through what angle will it rotate before a point on its edge a distance 0.5 m from the axle is moving with a speed of 2 m/s?', '["0.5 rad","1 rad","2 rad","4 rad","8 rad"]'::jsonb, '(D) The final angular velocity is \omega = v/r. Using the equation yields Substituting in for all the values given in the problem results in choice (D).'),
  ('ap-mock-physc-mech-2_q34', 'ap-mock-physc-mech-2', 34, false, 'A', 'Practice Test 2 – Mechanics', 'medium', 'Consider the five figures, which show different forces as functions of position. How many of these forces can give rise to simple harmonic motion about the point x = 0?', '["Directly proportional to the parameter (linear scaling)","one","Equal in magnitude with opposite orientation","three","Zero net change under static equilibrium conditions"]'::jsonb, '(A) SHM requires a linear restoring force, which is graphically equivalent to the F (x) curve passing through the x-axis with a negative slope (the x-intercept is the equilibrium of the SHM).'),
  ('ap-mock-physc-mech-2_q35', 'ap-mock-physc-mech-2', 35, false, 'B', 'Practice Test 2 – Mechanics', 'medium', 'A particle is subject to a conservative force associated with potential energy function U(x) = alpha x^4 - beta x^2, where alpha and beta are positive constants. At which of the following non-zero positions is the particle in equilibrium (net force F = 0)?', '["x = +/- sqrt(beta / (4 alpha))","x = +/- sqrt(beta / (2 alpha))","x = +/- beta / alpha","x = +/- sqrt(2 beta / alpha)","x = +/- beta / (2 alpha)"]'::jsonb, 'Conservative force is related to potential energy by F(x) = -dU/dx. Differentiating U(x): dU/dx = 4 alpha x^3 - 2 beta x. Setting F(x) = 0 gives 2 x (2 alpha x^2 - beta) = 0. For non-zero equilibrium positions: 2 alpha x^2 = beta ==> x^2 = beta / (2 alpha) ==> x = +/- sqrt(beta / (2 alpha)).'),
  ('ap-mock-physc-mech-2_q36', 'ap-mock-physc-mech-2', 36, false, 'B', 'Practice Test 2 – Mechanics', 'medium', 'A block of mass 0.6 kg slides on a horizontal table where the coefficient of kinetic friction is 0.1. If the block is moved 3 m to the left and then 3 m back to its original position, how much work is done by the frictional force?', '["−100.76 J","−3.53 J","0 J","3.53 J","100.76 J"]'::jsonb, '(B) The magnitude of the frictional force is given by Ffr = \mukFN = \mukmg = 0.59 N. The work done by friction is W = −Ffrd, where d is the total displacement, i.e., 6 m. Thus, W = −(0.59)(6) = −3.53 J.'),
  ('ap-mock-physc-mech-2_q37', 'ap-mock-physc-mech-2', 37, false, 'D', 'Practice Test 2 – Mechanics', 'medium', 'A ball with radius r = 2 m is rolling without slipping along a horizontal surface. If \theta, the angle through which the ball rotates, is given as a function of time by \theta = 4t2, then at time t = 1, what is the linear acceleration of the center of mass of the ball?', '["9,600 m/s2","140 m/s2","40 m/s2","16 m/s2","0.88 m/s2"]'::jsonb, '(D) The angular displacement is proportional to t2; thus, the angular acceleration \alpha is constant and given by the second time derivative of \theta, i.e., \alpha = 8. Rolling without slipping implies that the acceleration a of the center of mass obeys a = r\alpha = (2)(8) = 16 m/s2.'),
  ('ap-mock-physc-mech-2_q38', 'ap-mock-physc-mech-2', 38, false, 'D', 'Practice Test 2 – Mechanics', 'medium', 'Referring to the ball introduced in question 33, what is the speed of a point on the very top of the ball at time t = 1 relative to the horizontal surface?', '["6 m/s","12 m/s","24 m/s","32 m/s","96 m/s"]'::jsonb, '(D) Differentiating once to calculate the angular velocity from the angular position gives \omega = 8t. The speed at the top of the ball is given by the equation v = 2r\omega (for further explanation, see Chapter 8). Substituting in numbers yields choice (D).'),
  ('ap-mock-physc-mech-2_q39', 'ap-mock-physc-mech-2', 39, false, 'D', 'Practice Test 2 – Mechanics', 'medium', 'An asteroid moving at a constant velocity in space suddenly explodes. How many of the following quantities remain constant throughout and after the explosion? I. the kinetic energy II. the momentum III. the position of the center of mass IV. the velocity of the center of mass V. the angular momentum of the asteroid system', '["none","one","two","three","four Free-Response Questions DIRECTIONS: Use separate sheets of paper to write down your answers to the free-response questions. You may refer to the formula sheet in the Appendix (pages 575-578). Mechanics Mechanics I A skier of mass m is standing on a slope with an angle of elevation \\theta. After being given a tiny nudge, the skier slides down the slope, grabs a rope at the bottom of the slope, and swings up to a height h, as shown. The coefficient of kinetic friction between the skier and the slope is \\muk. (a) What is the minimum coefficient of static friction required so that the skier is initially at rest at the top of the slope without using his poles? (b) How much energy is lost while the skier slides down the incline? Express your answer in terms of m, g, h, d, and \\theta. (c) What is the coefficient of kinetic friction? Express your answer in terms of h, d, and \\theta. Mechanics II A mass m is attached to a string of length l and released when the string is horizontal, causing the mass to fall in a circular path as shown. At the time that the mass has fallen through the angle \\theta, determine the following (in terms of m, l, g, and \\theta): (a) the speed of the object (b) the magnitude of the centripetal acceleration (c) the magnitude of the tangential acceleration (d) the magnitude of the tension force (e) When using a photogate, the speed of the mass is measured when it passes through the bottom of the circle. The experiment is repeated for strings of different lengths, and the following data are obtained: What quantities should be plotted on the grid to obtain a linear relation between the speed of the mass and the length of the string? (f) Plot the data. Scale and label the axes, including units. After fitting the data to a straight line, check how well the experimental results agree with the theoretical prediction of part (a). Mechanics III A mass m is connected to an axle by a massless rod of length l. The mass m feels a retarding force due to air resistance given by F = −b\\omega, where b is a positive constant with units of kg\\cdot m/s. The mass rotates in the xy-plane, with an initial angular speed \\omega0 at time t = 0 as shown. (a) What is the initial angular momentum of mass m about the z-axis? (b) What is the torque due to air resistance? (c) What is the initial angular acceleration of the mass? Multiple-Choice Questions DIRECTIONS: Each multiple-choice question is followed by five answer choices. For each question, choose the best answer and fill in the corresponding circle on the answer sheet. You may refer to the formula sheet in the Appendix (pages 575-578). Electricity and Magnetism"]'::jsonb, '(D) There is no external force, so the momentum and the velocity of the center of mass remain constant. Because the center of mass continues to move with its original velocity, the position of the center of mass does not remain constant but instead moves at constant velocity. Because there is no external torque, the angular momentum of the asteroid system (about any stationary reference point) remains constant. The kinetic energy does not remain constant during the explosion but rather increases dramatically as the asteroid explodes: Presumably some source of nonmechanical energy (e.g., chemical energy) was quickly converted to mechanical energy, resulting in the explosion. Free-Response Mechanics I (a) The free-body diagram for the skier at rest on the slope appears in the figure. If the skier is at rest, the acceleration is zero, and thus the net force in each of the directions of the tilted coordinate system is zero: Finally, the minimum coefficient of static friction is Note: If the coefficient of static friction were larger, the static frictional force would be the same (it would simply be less than the maximum possible frictional force for that coefficient of friction). (b) We apply the law of conservation of energy. Setting y = 0 at the bottom of the incline and designating the final position where the skier has risen to a height h swinging on the rope, (c) The change in the mechanical energy is equal to the work done by nonconservative forces, in this case kinetic friction: Symbolic answer check: (1) If dsin \theta = h, then no energy is lost because the skier rises to the initial height. Alternatively, the greater the loss in energy, the greater the coefficient of friction. (2) The coefficient of friction is a dimensionless number, as it must be. Mechanics II (a) By conservation of energy (whereby the loss in gravitational potential energy equals the gain in kinetic energy): Symbolic answer check: (1) The units are correct. (2) Velocity increases with increasing gravitational force, increasing length, and increasing \theta, all of which make sense. (b) (c) At this point we need the free-body diagram shown. The net force in the tangential direction is mg cos \theta, so the acceleration in the tangential direction is g cos \theta. Note that we do not have to know the value of the tension in order to compute the tangential acceleration or net force. (d) Referring to the free-body diagram (and noting that the y-coordinate in this diagram is radial), (e) From part (a), you know that . So you should plot v2 versus ℓ. Note that at the bottom of the circle \theta = \pi/2 and sin \theta = 1. (f) The plot is shown below. Determine the slope of the fitted line: From part (a), you expect the slope to be 2g = 19.6 m/s2.'),
  ('ap-mock-physc-em-2_q1', 'ap-mock-physc-em-2', 1, false, 'D', 'Practice Test 2 – E&M', 'medium', 'In an RC discharging circuit, a capacitor C with initial voltage V0 discharges through a resistor R when a switch is closed at t = 0. Which of the following functions represents the voltage V(t) across the capacitor as a function of time?', '["V(t) = V0 (1 - e^{-t / (RC)})","V(t) = V0 t / (RC)","V(t) = V0 (1 - t / (RC))","V(t) = V0 e^{-t / (RC)}","V(t) = V0 e^{-t / (2RC)}"]'::jsonb, 'During capacitor discharge through resistance R, Kirchhoff''s loop rule gives -R (dq/dt) - q/C = 0 ==> dq/dt = -q / (RC). The solution is q(t) = Q0 e^{-t / (RC)}. Dividing by capacitance C yields the capacitor voltage V(t) = q(t)/C = V0 e^{-t / (RC)}, which decays exponentially to zero.'),
  ('ap-mock-physc-em-2_q2', 'ap-mock-physc-em-2', 2, false, 'D', 'Practice Test 2 – E&M', 'medium', 'A loop of wire of radius r and resistance R is exposed to a magnetic field pointing into the page that steadily increases from zero to magnitude B in time t, as shown. Describe the induced current.', '["\\pirB/t, clockwise","\\pirB/t, counterclockwise","\\pir2B/Rt, clockwise","\\pir2B/Rt, counterclockwise","\\pir2B/t, counterclockwise"]'::jsonb, '(D) First, to oppose the increase in inward flux, the induced current must flow counterclockwise, producing an outward flux. What is the magnitude of the current? The induced EMF is Because the magnetic field increases linearly, its slope dB/dt is simply The resistance is R, so according to Ohm’s law the current is I = V/R = AB/Rt. Finally, to express the result in terms of the variables in the problem, A = \pir2, so I = \pir2 B/Rt.'),
  ('ap-mock-physc-em-2_q3', 'ap-mock-physc-em-2', 3, false, 'B', 'Practice Test 2 – E&M', 'medium', 'The potential in a region of space is given by the formula V = ar, where r is a radial coordinate and a is a positive constant. Which of the following statements is true of this region?', '["The electric field has magnitude a and points away from the origin of the coordinates .","The electric field has a magnitude a and points toward the origin .","The electric field has a magnitude r2/2a and points away from the origin .","The electric field has a magnitude r2/2a and points toward the origin .","The electric force is nonconservative."]'::jsonb, '(B) The electric field equals the negative derivative of the potential along any line, and this holds for a line passing through the radius. Mathematically, Er = −dV/dr = −a. Even without recalling this equation in its entirety, one could arrive at the correct answer by combining the information that (1) the magnitude of the electric field is related to the derivative of the potential, and (2) the electric field always points in the direction of decreasing potential. Choice (E) is false. Any field that has an associated potential function is conservative. (That is, any field for which a potential energy function can be defined is a conservative field.)'),
  ('ap-mock-physc-em-2_q4', 'ap-mock-physc-em-2', 4, false, 'D', 'Practice Test 2 – E&M', 'medium', 'A capacitor of capacitance 25 \muF is charged to 300 V and then connected in series with an inductor of inductance 10 mH. The maximum current flowing through the circuit will be', '["7,500 A","500 A","300 A","15 A","2.5 A"]'::jsonb, '(D) The loop rule for an LC circuit is . This looks just like a harmonic oscillator with . The current is given by and the maximum current obeys Imax = \omegaQmax. The maximum charge is the initial charge of the capacitor, .'),
  ('ap-mock-physc-em-2_q5', 'ap-mock-physc-em-2', 5, false, 'A', 'Practice Test 2 – E&M', 'medium', 'If it requires an input of E joules to move a charge Q from point A to point B at constant velocity, which of the following is true?', '["VB − VA = E/Q","VB − VA = −E/Q","VB − VA = QE","VB − VA = −QE","VB − VA = Q/E"]'::jsonb, '(A) Potential is equal to the potential energy per unit charge, so only choices (A) and (B) have the correct units. To figure out the signs, consider the case where Q and E are positive. If positive energy is required to move the charge from point A to point B, point B must be at higher potential energy (uphill), so Q(VB − VA) must be positive. Because E is positive, only the equation VB − VA = E/Q has signs that are consistent with this situation.'),
  ('ap-mock-physc-em-2_q6', 'ap-mock-physc-em-2', 6, false, 'D', 'Practice Test 2 – E&M', 'medium', 'A parallel-plate vacuum capacitor with capacitance C0 is connected to a battery of voltage V. While still connected to the battery, a dielectric slab with dielectric constant kappa = 3 is inserted to fill the space between the plates. What is the ratio of the new stored energy to the initial stored energy (U_new / U_initial)?', '["1/9","1/3","1","3","9"]'::jsonb, 'Because the capacitor remains connected to the battery, the voltage V across the plates stays constant. The capacitance increases to C_new = kappa C0 = 3 C0. The energy stored in a capacitor at fixed voltage is U = 1/2 C V^2. Therefore, U_new / U_initial = (1/2 (3 C0) V^2) / (1/2 C0 V^2) = 3.'),
  ('ap-mock-physc-em-2_q7', 'ap-mock-physc-em-2', 7, false, 'E', 'Practice Test 2 – E&M', 'medium', 'A battery with EMF of V and an internal resistance of rint is connected to a heating loop with resistance R. What is the correct expression for the rate at which energy is converted to heat by the heating loop?', '["V2/R","V2rint/(rint + R)2","V/(rint + R)","V2R2/(rint + R)2","none of the above"]'::jsonb, '(E) The current through the circuit is The power dissipated through the heating element is then Because this is not equivalent to any of the given choices, the answer is choice (E).'),
  ('ap-mock-physc-em-2_q8', 'ap-mock-physc-em-2', 8, false, 'D', 'Practice Test 2 – E&M', 'medium', 'A parallel plate capacitor connected to a battery contains a plastic dielectric material between the plates of the capacitor. Removing the dielectric has what effect on the electric field between the plates and the charge on the plates?', '["Electric field and charge both decrease.","Electric field and charge both increase.","Electric field increases and the charge remains the same.","Electric field remains the same and the charge decreases.","none of the above"]'::jsonb, '(D) The equation always holds, so V = Ed is valid even in the case of a dielectric. Therefore, because the capacitor is always connected to the battery and the plate separation distance is unchanged, V and d are fixed and so E must remain the same. What effect does this have on the plate charge? The key equation here is Q = CV. The capacitance decreases in the absence of the dielectric. The voltage is held fixed (because the capacitor is attached to the battery), so the charge on the plates must decrease. How is it that the electric field can remain constant while the charge on the plates decreases? In the presence of the dielectric, the charge on the plates is partially screened by the induced charge on the surface of the dielectric. Once the dielectric is removed, the charge on the plates no longer needs to cancel this induced charge, so it decreases.'),
  ('ap-mock-physc-em-2_q9', 'ap-mock-physc-em-2', 9, false, 'D', 'Practice Test 2 – E&M', 'medium', 'An electron (charge -e, mass m) is released from rest at distance r1 from a fixed proton (charge +e). What is the kinetic energy of the electron when it has accelerated to a distance r2 from the proton (where r2 < r1)?', '["k e^2 * (1/r1 - 1/r2)","Zero","k e^2 / (2 r2)","k e^2 * (1/r2 - 1/r1)","1/2 m (k e^2 / r2)^2"]'::jsonb, 'Mechanical energy is conserved: K_initial + U_initial = K_final + U_final. The electron starts from rest, so K_initial = 0. The electrostatic potential energy is U(r) = -k e^2 / r. Therefore, 0 - k e^2 / r1 = K_final - k e^2 / r2 ==> K_final = k e^2 (1/r2 - 1/r1). Because r2 < r1, 1/r2 > 1/r1, so the kinetic energy is positive.'),
  ('ap-mock-physc-em-2_q10', 'ap-mock-physc-em-2', 10, false, 'C', 'Practice Test 2 – E&M', 'medium', 'The magnetic field created by the two wires shown at point A in the figure is B0 (pointing out of the page), and the two wires carry identical currents in the same direction. Which of the following is true?', '["Both wires carry a current of 4\\pid/3\\mu0 toward the top of the page.","Both wires carry a current of 4\\pid/3\\mu0 toward the bottom of the page.","Both wires carry a current of 4\\pidB0/\\mu0 toward the top of the page.","Both wires carry a current of 4\\pidB0/\\mu0 toward the bottom of the page.","It is impossible for the wires to produce a magnetic field pointing perpendicular to the page."]'::jsonb, '(C) A little experimentation with the right-hand rule shows that for points in between two wires carrying current in the same direction, the two wires produce magnetic fields in opposite directions. The wire closer to the point produces a larger magnetic field, so the net magnetic field points in the direction determined by this wire. In this case, because the magnetic field points out of the page, the current through the wire closest to point A (the right wire) must move toward the top of the page. The magnitude of the magnetic field due to a current-carrying wire is B = \mu0I/2\pir. Because the two wires produce fields in opposite directions, in this case the net magnetic field has magnitude Solving this equation for the current yields I = 4\pidB0/\mu0, so the correct answer is choice (C).'),
  ('ap-mock-physc-em-2_q11', 'ap-mock-physc-em-2', 11, false, 'D', 'Practice Test 2 – E&M', 'medium', 'Two insulating spheres of equal radii are oppositely charged with uniform charge densities of equal magnitude, as shown in the top half of the figure. The spheres are placed far apart from each other and can be considered completely isolated from each other. What is the effect of exchanging the top halves of these distributions with one another, as shown in the bottom half of the figure?', '["The potential increases at point A and decreases at point B.","The electric field magnitude increases at point A and decreases at point B.","The potential increases at both points A and B.","The electric field magnitude increases at both points A and B.","After the exchange, positive work is required to move a positive charge from point A to point B."]'::jsonb, '(D) Recall that potential is given by the integral . Therefore, moving negative charge closer to point A and positive charge farther away from point A decreases the potential at point A. The opposite occurs at point B, causing the potential to increase at point B. Therefore, choices (A) and (C) are false. Because of symmetry, a sphere of uniform charge density produces a zero net field at its center. Therefore, the electric field at points A and B is initially zero. Exchanging the tops of the spheres leads to nonzero electric fields at points A and B (pointing up at point A and down at point B). Therefore, (B) is false, while (D) is true. As a result of the symmetry of the situation, points A and B have the same potential. Therefore, zero work is required to move any charge from point A to point B, making choice (E) false.'),
  ('ap-mock-physc-em-2_q12', 'ap-mock-physc-em-2', 12, false, 'B', 'Practice Test 2 – E&M', 'medium', 'A loop of wire is pushed with constant velocity through a region of uniform magnetic field as shown. (The field points out of the page.) As the loop enters and exits the magnetic field, only one of the vertical segments of wire is within the magnetic field. What is the direction of the induced current within this vertical segment?', '["upward when both entering and exiting the magnetic field","downward when both entering and exiting the magnetic field","upward when entering the magnetic field and downward when exiting the magnetic field","downward when entering the magnetic field and upward when exiting the magnetic field","zero, because the segment of wire is perpendicular to the magnetic field"]'::jsonb, '(B) While entering the magnetic field, the induced current flows clockwise, moving downward through the right segment of the loop that is exposed to the magnetic field. While leaving the magnetic field, the induced current flows counterclockwise, moving downward through the left segment of the loop that is exposed to the magnetic field.'),
  ('ap-mock-physc-em-2_q13', 'ap-mock-physc-em-2', 13, false, 'D', 'Practice Test 2 – E&M', 'medium', 'What is the force on a positive charge +Q located on a 10 V equipotential line?', '["10 Q in magnitude, pointing parallel to the line","10 Q in magnitude, pointing perpendicular to the line in the direction of decreasing potential","10 Q in magnitude, pointing perpendicular to the line in the direction of increasing potential","of unknown magnitude, pointing perpendicular to the line in the direction of decreasing potential","of unknown magnitude, pointing perpendicular to the line in the direction of increasing potential"]'::jsonb, '(D) The force on the charge is proportional to the electric field according to the equation F = qE. The electric field is the negative derivative of the potential, but a single value of potential alone does not indicate anything about the electric field or the electric force. However, we can still determine the direction of the force. Electric fields are always perpendicular to equipotential curves, pointing in the direction of decreasing potential. Because the charge is positive, the force is parallel to the electric field.'),
  ('ap-mock-physc-em-2_q14', 'ap-mock-physc-em-2', 14, false, 'D', 'Practice Test 2 – E&M', 'medium', 'The capacitor shown consists of two concentric metallic cylindrical shells. Assuming that the radius of the inner cylinder, r, is much greater than the plate separation distance, d, what is the approximate capacitance of the capacitor?', '["C = \\epsilon0\\pir2/d","C = \\epsilon0\\pirl/d","C = \\epsilon0\\pidl/r","C = 2\\epsilon0\\pirl/d","C = \\epsilon0d/2\\pirl"]'::jsonb, '(D) Because the plate separation d is very small compared to the radius of the cylinder, it is reasonable to approximate the capacitor as a parallel plate capacitor with plate separation d and plate area 2\pirl. (This makes sense: Because d << r, the electric field is relatively constant between the plates and is perpendicular to the plates.) Therefore, the approximate capacitance is Even without making this observation, a few choices can be eliminated. Compared to the formula C = \epsilon0A/d, choice (E) has incorrect units, so it can be eliminated. Choice (A) does not include the length of the capacitor. However, we know from the simpler case of a parallel plate capacitor that the dimension of the plates is relevant to the value of the capacitance, so we can safely eliminate choice (A).'),
  ('ap-mock-physc-em-2_q15', 'ap-mock-physc-em-2', 15, false, 'D', 'Practice Test 2 – E&M', 'medium', 'A sphere has a uniform charge density distributed throughout its interior. What effect does doubling the radius of the sphere (while maintaining the same charge density) have on the electric field at a point outside the enlarged sphere?', '["The field remains the same.","The field increases by a factor of 2.","The field increases by a factor of 4.","The field increases by a factor of 8.","The field increases by a factor of 16."]'::jsonb, '(D) Imagine a spherical gaussian surface passing through any point outside the enlarged sphere. Doubling the radius of the sphere increases the enclosed charge by a factor of 8 (because the volume of a sphere is proportional to r3 and the charge is proportional to the volume). Thus, by Gauss’s law, the flux through the gaussian surface (whose surface area is fixed) increases by a factor of 8, and thus so does the electric field.'),
  ('ap-mock-physc-em-2_q16', 'ap-mock-physc-em-2', 16, false, 'A', 'Practice Test 2 – E&M', 'medium', 'A charged particle moves in a planar, circular orbit as a result of the presence of a uniform magnetic field perpendicular to the plane of its motion. Holding all other variables (such as the magnetic field, the particle’s velocity, and the particle’s charge) constant, which graph shows the relationship between the particle’s mass and the radius of its path?', '["Directly proportional to the parameter (linear scaling)","Inversely proportional to the square of the separation","Equal in magnitude with opposite orientation","Independent of the mass and charge distribution","Zero net change under static equilibrium conditions"]'::jsonb, '(A) The magnetic force provides the centripetal force: mv2/r = qvB. Rearranging this equation to solve for r in terms of m yields r = (v/qB)m. Therefore, the graph is linear, passing through the origin.'),
  ('ap-mock-physc-em-2_q17', 'ap-mock-physc-em-2', 17, false, 'A', 'Practice Test 2 – E&M', 'medium', 'A thin uniformly charged rod with negative linear charge density -lambda lies along the y-axis from y = -a to y = +a. What is the direction of the electric field produced by the rod at a point P on the positive x-axis at (x, 0)?', '["Directly toward the origin (in the -x direction)","Directly away from the origin (in the +x direction)","In the +y direction","In the -y direction","The electric field is zero"]'::jsonb, 'Consider symmetric elements of charge dq = -lambda dy at +y and -y on the rod. Because the charge is negative, the electric field vectors dE point toward the charge elements. By symmetry, their y-components cancel out, while their x-components add constructively pointing along the -x direction (toward the origin). Thus the net electric field points directly toward the origin in the -x direction.'),
  ('ap-mock-physc-em-2_q18', 'ap-mock-physc-em-2', 18, false, 'D', 'Practice Test 2 – E&M', 'medium', 'Consider a circuit consisting of the following elements in a single loop: a battery, a resistor, a switch, and a fourth unknown element E. A spark is most likely to occur at the switch if', '["E is a capacitor and the switch is being closed for the first time.","E is a capacitor and the switch is being opened after the circuit has reached equilibrium.","E is an inductor and the switch is being closed for the first time.","E is an inductor and the switch is being opened after the circuit has reached equilibrium.","E is a battery arranged in series with the other battery in the circuit."]'::jsonb, '(D) Recall that the current flowing through inductors is generally continuous because a discontinuous current would cause the time derivative of the current to be infinite and thus produce an infinite voltage. How is this theory applicable to the current problem? In choice (D), when the switch is opened, an abrupt change in the current occurs that produces a very large induced EMF, causing a spark to instantaneously connect the recently opened terminals of the switch. [In choice (C), the current is zero before and immediately after closing the switch, so there is no discontinuity and no spark.]'),
  ('ap-mock-physc-em-2_q19', 'ap-mock-physc-em-2', 19, false, 'D', 'Practice Test 2 – E&M', 'medium', 'The two charge distributions shown each contain a net charge +Q uniformly distributed along an arc of a circle with radius r. In distribution A this charge is spread along an arc twice the length of the arc in distribution B. Which of the following comparisons of the potential V and the magnitude E of the electric field at point A and point B are true?', '["VA > VB and EA > EB","VA > VB and EA < EB","VA = VB and EA > EB","VA = VB and EA < EB","VA < VB and EA > EB"]'::jsonb, '(D) All the charge in both distributions is at a fixed distance r from the points of interest. Therefore, the potential at both point A and point B is V = Q/4\pi\epsilon0r and these potientials are equal. The electric field sums as vectors rather than scalars, so it is more complicated. In both cases, the net electric field points in the +x- direction because of symmetry about the x-axis. However, in distribution B, because the charge is concentrated closer to the horizontal axis, there is less cancellation of the differential electric field vectors compared to case A. (For further explanation, see Chapter 10.)'),
  ('ap-mock-physc-em-2_q20', 'ap-mock-physc-em-2', 20, false, 'D', 'Practice Test 2 – E&M', 'medium', 'Two cylindrical resistors are arranged in series and connected to a complicated circuit, as shown. Both resistors have the same resistivity, and the second resistor has twice the radius and twice the length of the first resistor. Which of the following statements is correct?', '["The resistors have the same resistance.","The resistors experience the same potential drop.","The resistors dissipate the same amount of power.","The resistors have the same current.","The equivalent resistance of the two resistors is equal to 1.5R2."]'::jsonb, '(D) Recalling that the same current flows through elements in series, you could recognize that choice (D) is correct without paying attention to the details of the problem. The other choices are incorrect for the following reasons. The resistance of a cylindrical resistor is given by the equation R = \rhol/A. Because the second resistor has twice the length and four times the area compared to the first resistor, it has half the resistance of the first resistor, eliminating choice (A). The voltage across the resistors, given by V = IR, is different because the two resistors share the same current but have different resistances, eliminating choice (B). Similarly, power can be expressed as P = I2R, and because the resistors have the same current and different resistances, they dissipate different amounts of power, eliminating choice (D). Finally, R2 is half of R1 (as discussed above), and because resistors in series add, the equivalent resistance is equal to Req = 1.5R1 = 3R2, so choice (E) is incorrect.'),
  ('ap-mock-physc-em-2_q21', 'ap-mock-physc-em-2', 21, false, 'A', 'Practice Test 2 – E&M', 'medium', 'In a Wheatstone bridge circuit with four resistors R1, R2, R3, and R4 arranged in two parallel branches (branch 1 has R1 and R2 in series; branch 2 has R3 and R4 in series), the potential at the junction between R1 and R2 equals the potential at the junction between R3 and R4. Which condition must be satisfied?', '["R1 / R2 = R3 / R4","R1 R2 = R3 R4","R1 + R2 = R3 + R4","R1 - R2 = R3 - R4","R1 / R4 = R2 / R3"]'::jsonb, 'For the bridge to be balanced (equal potentials at the two internal nodes), the voltage drop across R1 must equal the voltage drop across R3: I1 R1 = I2 R3, and the voltage drop across R2 must equal that across R4: I1 R2 = I2 R4. Dividing the two equations yields R1 / R2 = R3 / R4 (the classic Wheatstone bridge balance condition).'),
  ('ap-mock-physc-em-2_q22', 'ap-mock-physc-em-2', 22, false, 'C', 'Practice Test 2 – E&M', 'medium', 'If each of the capacitors in the network shown has a capacitance of C, what is the equivalent capacitance of the network?', '["C/9","C/3","C","3C","9C"]'::jsonb, '(C) Each set of three capacitors in series has an equivalent capacitance of C/3. Because capacitors in parallel add, the combination of these three equivalent capacitors (each with a capacitance of C/3) in parallel produces a network with an equivalent capacitance of C.'),
  ('ap-mock-physc-em-2_q23', 'ap-mock-physc-em-2', 23, false, 'D', 'Practice Test 2 – E&M', 'medium', 'What voltage would have to be imposed across the entire network shown in question 26 for the voltage across any of the capacitors to be V?', '["V/9","V/3","V","3V","9V"]'::jsonb, '(D) Imagine charging the capacitor network. Equal amounts of charge would flow through the three branches of the network, causing equal amounts of charge to build up on every one of the nine capacitors. Therefore, for the voltage of one capacitor to be V, the voltage of every capacitor in the network must be V. When the voltage across each capacitor is V, what is the voltage across the entire network? To calculate the voltage across the network, we can imagine traversing one branch and summing the voltage jumps, as in a Kirchhoff’s loop equation. Because the capacitors have the same polarity, their voltages sum, causing the voltage across the branch (and thus the network) to be 3V.'),
  ('ap-mock-physc-em-2_q24', 'ap-mock-physc-em-2', 24, false, 'B', 'Practice Test 2 – E&M', 'medium', 'The gaussian “unit cube” shown lies in a region where the electric field is constant and parallel to the z-axis: . Which of the following choices lists the outward flux through sides 1 through 6, respectively?', '["0, 0, −E, 0, +E, 0","0, 0, +E, 0, −E, 0","0, 0, +E, 0, +E, 0","0, 0, −E, 0, −E, 0","none of the above"]'::jsonb, '(B) Sides 1, 2, 4, and 6 are parallel to the electric field, so the electric flux through them is zero. Sides 3 and 5 are perpendicular to the field, so the magnitude of the flux passing through each of these faces is equal to the area (which is 1) multiplied by the field, E. Because the field points in the positive z-direction, it enters the cube at side 5 (yielding negative outward flux) and leaves the cube at side 3 (yielding positive outward flux).'),
  ('ap-mock-physc-em-2_q25', 'ap-mock-physc-em-2', 25, false, 'C', 'Practice Test 2 – E&M', 'medium', 'You have three identical capacitors you are using to design an equivalent capacitor that will be used to store energy for a camera flash, as shown. Charging time is defined as the amount of time required to reach 90% of the maximum energy. Rank the networks in order from least to greatest charging time.', '["I, II, III, IV","IV, III, II, I","I, III, II, IV","IV, II, III, I","I, (II = III), IV I) II) III) IV)"]'::jsonb, '(C) The energy stored in a capacitor can be expressed in the form U = Q2/2C. Therefore, the amount of time required for a capacitor to reach 90% of its maximum energy is equal to the amount of time required to reach 90% of its maximum Q2 value (because C is fixed for any given capacitor). The circuit with the smaller time constant (smaller \tau = RC), and thus the smaller equivalent capacitance, reaches the 90% value more quickly. Therefore, ordering the networks from least to greatest charging time is simply a matter of ordering them from least to greatest equivalence capacitance. The equivalent capacitances of networks I through IV are and 3C, respectively.'),
  ('ap-mock-physc-em-2_q26', 'ap-mock-physc-em-2', 26, false, 'C', 'Practice Test 2 – E&M', 'medium', 'Considering the situation introduced in the last problem, rank order the capacitor networks in terms of the energy stored when fully charged. (Assume that the capacitor networks are connected to the same circuit, with a fixed voltage.)', '["I, II, III, IV","IV, III, II, I","I, III, II, IV","IV, II, III, I","I, (II = III), IV"]'::jsonb, '(C) From the equation , networks with higher equivalent capacitance store more energy when connected to the same voltage. Therefore, ordering the capacitors from least to greatest energy is equivalent to ordering them from least to greatest capacitance. This ordering is the same as in question 29, choice (C).'),
  ('ap-mock-physc-em-2_q27', 'ap-mock-physc-em-2', 27, false, 'E', 'Practice Test 2 – E&M', 'medium', 'What is the rate at which thermal energy is produced in the resistors shown?', '["(B) IV","Directly downward toward the bottom of the page","2IV","4IV","none of the above"]'::jsonb, '(E) What is the current through the circuit? A Kirchhoff’s loop equation for the circuit (moving in the clockwise direction and choosing positive current clockwise) is V − IR − V − IR = 0, which simplifies to 2IR = 0. Therefore, the current around the circuit is zero, which is not surprising because the two identical batteries are connected so as to oppose each other. Thus, the power dissipated in each of the resistors is zero, and absolutely no thermal energy is produced in the circuit.'),
  ('ap-mock-physc-em-2_q28', 'ap-mock-physc-em-2', 28, false, 'B', 'Practice Test 2 – E&M', 'medium', 'In the circuit shown, there is initially no current flowing through the inductor. The instant after the switch is closed, what is the current through the battery?', '["V/R1","V/(R1 + R2)","V/(R1 + R3)","Independent of the mass and charge distribution","Zero net change under static equilibrium conditions"]'::jsonb, '(B) The instant after the switch is closed in an RL circuit, the inductor acts like an open circuit (because the current through the inductor must be continuous and thus initially zero). Therefore, the equivalent resistance is R1 + R2, and the current is calculated using Ohm’s law with the result given by choice (B). Elimination approach: Even if you have forgotten about RL circuits, you could eliminate enough choices to make a good guess. First, because no circuit elements block the flow of current through resistors R1 and R2, these resistors determine the current and are expected to show up in the final answer, eliminating choices (A) and (C). The denominator of choice (E) has mixed units (Ω + Ω−1), so it must be incorrect. This leaves us to guess between choices (B) and (D).'),
  ('ap-mock-physc-em-2_q29', 'ap-mock-physc-em-2', 29, false, 'D', 'Practice Test 2 – E&M', 'medium', 'Referring to the circuit shown in question 32, after the circuit has reached equilibrium, what is the current through the battery?', '["V/R1","V/(R1 + R2)","V/(R1 + R3)","Independent of the mass and charge distribution","Zero net change under static equilibrium conditions"]'::jsonb, '(D) At equilibrium the inductor acts like a short circuit, so to calculate the current we simply ignore the inductor, calculate the equivalent resistance, and apply Ohm’s law. To calculate the equivalent resistance, note that R2 and R3 are in parallel with each other, so their equivalent resistance together is Because these resistors are in series with R1, the equivalent resistance of the entire circuit is Applying Ohm’s law yields the current given in choice (D).'),
  ('ap-mock-physc-em-2_q30', 'ap-mock-physc-em-2', 30, false, 'B', 'Practice Test 2 – E&M', 'medium', 'A solid conducting sphere of radius R carries a net positive charge +Q in electrostatic equilibrium. Taking electric potential to be zero at infinity (V = 0 at r = infinity), which of the following correctly describes the electric potential V(r) as a function of radial distance r from the center?', '["V(r) is zero inside the sphere and proportional to 1/r outside","V(r) is constant and equal to k Q / R for all r <= R, and proportional to k Q / r for r >= R","V(r) increases linearly from 0 at the center to k Q / R at the surface","V(r) is proportional to 1/r^2 both inside and outside the sphere","V(r) is constant throughout all space"]'::jsonb, 'Because the sphere is a conductor in electrostatic equilibrium, the electric field inside is zero (E = 0 for r <= R). Since E = -dV/dr, the potential must be constant throughout the volume of the conductor and equal to its surface value: V(r) = k Q / R for r <= R. Outside the sphere (r >= R), Gauss''s law gives E(r) = k Q / r^2, so integrating from infinity yields V(r) = k Q / r.')
ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.ap_exam_questions (id, test_id, question_order, is_frq, answer_key, topic, difficulty, question_text, options_json, explanation)
VALUES
  ('ap-mock-physc-em-2_q31', 'ap-mock-physc-em-2', 31, false, 'A', 'Practice Test 2 – E&M', 'medium', 'For the solid conducting sphere in the previous question, which of the following correctly describes the electric field magnitude E(r) as a function of radial distance r from the center?', '["E(r) = 0 for r < R, and E(r) = k Q / r^2 for r > R","E(r) is constant and non-zero for r < R","E(r) increases linearly with r for r < R","E(r) is proportional to 1/r for all r","E(r) is non-zero at the center and decreases to 0 at the surface"]'::jsonb, 'In electrostatic equilibrium, charges reside entirely on the outer surface of a conductor, so the electric field inside the conducting material is identically zero: E(r) = 0 for r < R. Outside the sphere (r > R), Gauss''s law with a spherical Gaussian surface enclosing charge Q yields E(4 pi r^2) = Q / epsilon_0 ==> E(r) = k Q / r^2.'),
  ('ap-mock-physc-mech-3_q1', 'ap-mock-physc-mech-3', 1, false, 'B', 'AP Physics C: Mechanics', 'medium', '<p class="image"><img alt="Images" class="q-img" src="images/t0301-01.jpg" style="max-width: 100%; height: auto; margin: 10px 0; display: block;"/></p><p class="noindentt"><strong>Time:</strong> 45 minutes. You may refer to the constants sheet and the equation sheet, both of which are found in the appendix. You may use a calculator.</p><p class="ques"><strong><a href="ch19t.xhtml#rch19p_1" id="ch19p_1">1.</a></strong> An object moves along the positive $x$-axis. The net force on the object as a function of time $t$ measured in seconds is given in newtons by the function $F$($t$) = 3$t^{2} +t$ + 2. What is the impulse delivered to the object during the first second of motion?</p>', '["<p>4.0 N⋅s</p>","<p>3.5 N⋅s</p>","<p>0 N⋅s</p>","<p>7.0 N⋅s</p>","<p>6.0 N⋅s</p>"]'::jsonb, '<p class="ques"><strong><a href="ch19p.xhtml#ch19p_1" id="rch19p_1">1.</a></strong> <strong>B</strong>—Impulse is the integral of force with respect to time. That’s $t^{3} +t^{2}$/2 + 2$t$, evaluated from 0 to 1 second: 1 + ½ + 2 = 3.5 N⋅s.</p>'),
  ('ap-mock-physc-mech-3_q2', 'ap-mock-physc-mech-3', 2, false, 'E', 'AP Physics C: Mechanics', 'medium', '<p class="ques"><strong><a href="ch19t.xhtml#rch19p_2" id="ch19p_2">2.</a></strong> A cart moves along a straight track. Its position $x$ as a function of time is given in meters by the equation $x$ = 2$t^{3} +t^{2}$ – 2$t$. What is the cart’s acceleration at time $t$ = 4 s?</p>', '["<p>102 m/$s^{2}$</p>","<p>10 m/$s^{2}$</p>","<p>133 m/$s^{2}$</p>","<p>12 m/$s^{2}$</p>","<p>50 m/$s^{2}$</p>"]'::jsonb, '<p class="ques"><strong><a href="ch19p.xhtml#ch19p_2" id="rch19p_2">2.</a></strong> <strong>E—</strong>Acceleration is the second time derivative of position. The second derivative of the given function is $a$ = 12$t$ + 2. Plug in $t$ = 4 s to get an acceleration of 50 m/$s^{2}$.</p>'),
  ('ap-mock-physc-mech-3_q3', 'ap-mock-physc-mech-3', 3, false, 'D', 'AP Physics C: Mechanics', 'medium', '<p class="image"><img alt="Images" class="q-img" src="images/f0303-01.jpg" style="max-width: 100%; height: auto; margin: 10px 0; display: block;"/></p><p class="ques"><strong><a href="ch19t.xhtml#rch19p_3" id="ch19p_3">3.</a></strong> An object of mass $m$ is attached to a vertical spring, as shown above. When the string is unstretched, it is of length $L$. When the object is gently attached and allowed to hang from rest, the string is of length $L +x$. Now the object is attached, released from rest while the spring is at its unstretched length $L$, and allowed to oscillate. What is the maximum length of the spring during the oscillations?</p>', '["<p>$L$ + <img alt=\"Images\" class=\"q-img\" src=\"images/s2x.jpg\" style=\"max-width: 100%; height: auto; margin: 10px 0; display: block;\"/></p>","<p>2$L$ + 2$x$</p>","<p>2$L$ + <img alt=\"Images\" class=\"q-img\" src=\"images/s2x.jpg\" style=\"max-width: 100%; height: auto; margin: 10px 0; display: block;\"/></p>","<p>$L$ + 2$x$</p>","<p>$L$ + 4$x$</p>"]'::jsonb, '<p class="ques"><strong><a href="ch19p.xhtml#ch19p_3" id="rch19p_3">3.</a></strong> <strong>D</strong>—The equilibrium position is the midpoint of any oscillations. So $L +x$ is the equilibrium position. The object was released a distance $x$ above the equilibrium position, meaning $x$ is the amplitude of the oscillations. The spring will reach a distance $x$ both above and below the equilibrium, meaning the spring will oscillate between a length $L$ and ($L +x$) $+x$. That’s $L$ + 2$x$ as the maximum length.</p>'),
  ('ap-mock-physc-mech-3_q4', 'ap-mock-physc-mech-3', 4, false, 'C', 'AP Physics C: Mechanics', 'medium', '<div class="passage-box" style="background: rgba(255,255,255,0.03); padding: 12px 16px; border-radius: 8px; margin-bottom: 14px; border-left: 3px solid #7c3aed;"><p class="image"><img alt="Images" class="q-img" src="images/f0303-02.jpg" style="max-width: 100%; height: auto; margin: 10px 0; display: block;"/></p><p class="noindent">Questions 4 and 5: Two carts move along a smooth surface as shown above. Cart A of mass 3.1 kg moves west with speed 1.2 m/s. Cart B of mass 1.3 kg moves north with speed 2.2 m/s. The carts collide and stick together, moving at angle $\theta$ above west.</p></div><p class="ques"><strong><a href="ch19t.xhtml#rch19p_4" id="ch19p_4">4.</a></strong> What is the speed of the center of mass of the two-cart system before collision?</p>', '["<p>2.5 m/s</p>","<p>3.4 m/s</p>","<p>1.1 m/s</p>","<p>1.6 m/s</p>","<p>2.3 m/s</p>"]'::jsonb, '<p class="ques"><strong><a href="ch19p.xhtml#ch19p_4" id="rch19p_4">4.</a></strong> <strong>C—</strong>Use conservation of momentum to find the total momentum before collision. Momentum is mass times velocity. For cart A before collision that’s 3.72 N⋅s west; for cart B before collision that’s 2.86 N⋅s north. The total momentum before collision is the Pythagorean sum of these—because momentum is a vector, direction matters. So total momentum before collision is 4.69 N⋅s at the angle $\theta$. Conservation of momentum means that this is also the total momentum after collision. Since the carts stick together, just divide this by the 4.4 kg mass to get the speed after collision of 1.07 m/s. And since no external forces act on the two-cart system, its center of mass always moves at this speed. So answer is closest to C.</p>'),
  ('ap-mock-physc-mech-3_q5', 'ap-mock-physc-mech-3', 5, false, 'B', 'AP Physics C: Mechanics', 'medium', '<div class="passage-box" style="background: rgba(255,255,255,0.03); padding: 12px 16px; border-radius: 8px; margin-bottom: 14px; border-left: 3px solid #7c3aed;"><p class="image"><img alt="Images" class="q-img" src="images/f0303-02.jpg" style="max-width: 100%; height: auto; margin: 10px 0; display: block;"/></p><p class="noindent">Questions 4 and 5: Two carts move along a smooth surface as shown above. Cart A of mass 3.1 kg moves west with speed 1.2 m/s. Cart B of mass 1.3 kg moves north with speed 2.2 m/s. The carts collide and stick together, moving at angle $\theta$ above west.</p></div><p class="ques"><strong><a href="ch19t.xhtml#rch19p_5" id="ch19p_5">5.</a></strong> Which of the following methods will calculate the angle $\theta$?</p>', '["<p>Divide cart B’s speed by cart A’s speed, and take the inverse tangent of the result.</p>","<p>Calculate the momentum of each cart by multiplying mass times speed. Then divide cart B’s momentum by cart A’s momentum, and take the inverse tangent of the result.</p>","<p>Calculate the kinetic energy of each cart by multiplying ½ by its mass times its speed squared. Then divide the cart B’s kinetic energy by cart A’s kinetic energy, and take the inverse tangent of the result.</p>","<p>Divide the square of cart B’s speed by the square of cart A’s speed, and take the inverse tangent of the result.</p>","<p>Divide cart B’s mass by cart A’s mass, and take the inverse tangent of the result.</p>"]'::jsonb, '<p class="ques"><strong><a href="ch19p.xhtml#ch19p_5" id="rch19p_5">5.</a></strong> <strong>B—</strong>Momentum is a vector and is conserved separately in each direction in a collision. The total momentum on the vertical axis is just cart B’s momentum, and on the horizontal axis, just cart A’s momentum. The inverse tangent of these momentum components gives the direction of the total momentum vector. And the direction of a velocity—of movement—is always the same as the direction of momentum. (You can’t just use the inverse tangent on the velocity components, because velocity isn’t conserved in a collision!)</p>'),
  ('ap-mock-physc-mech-3_q6', 'ap-mock-physc-mech-3', 6, false, 'C', 'AP Physics C: Mechanics', 'medium', '<p class="ques"><strong><a href="ch19t.xhtml#rch19p_6" id="ch19p_6">6.</a></strong> A pendulum and a spring both oscillate in harmonic motion with small amplitude. Which of the following shows correct graphs of how the period $T$ of the harmonic motion depends on the mass $m$ of the object in oscillation for the pendulum and the spring?</p><p class="image"><img alt="Images" class="q-img" src="images/f0304-01.jpg" style="max-width: 100%; height: auto; margin: 10px 0; display: block;"/></p>', '["Graph A: Pendulum T increases linearly with m; Spring T is independent of m","Graph B: Pendulum T decreases inversely with m; Spring T increases linearly with m","Graph C: Pendulum T is horizontal (independent of m); Spring T increases as \\sqrt{m}","Graph D: Pendulum T increases as m^2; Spring T is horizontal","Graph E: Pendulum T decreases as 1/\\sqrt{m}; Spring T increases as m^2"]'::jsonb, '<p class="ques"><strong><a href="ch19p.xhtml#ch19p_6" id="rch19p_6">6.</a></strong> <strong>C—</strong>The period of a pendulum is <img alt="Images" class="q-img" src="images/f0330-01.jpg" style="max-width: 100%; height: auto; margin: 10px 0; display: block;"/>; mass is irrelevant, so the graph of period vs. mass is a horizontal line. The period of an object on a spring is <img alt="Images" class="q-img" src="images/f0330-02.jpg" style="max-width: 100%; height: auto; margin: 10px 0; display: block;"/>, which depends on the square root of $m$, as in <strong>C</strong>.</p>'),
  ('ap-mock-physc-mech-3_q7', 'ap-mock-physc-mech-3', 7, false, 'A', 'AP Physics C: Mechanics', 'medium', '<p class="ques"><strong><a href="ch19t.xhtml#rch19p_7" id="ch19p_7">7.</a></strong> Cart A is initially moving to the right toward stationary cart B. The carts collide and bounce off one another. Which of the following is correct about whether the horizontal linear momentum and the kinetic energy of the two-cart system are conserved in the collision?</p>', '["<p>Linear momentum must be conserved. Kinetic energy may possibly be conserved.</p>","<p>Linear momentum may possibly be conserved. Kinetic energy must be conserved.</p>","<p>Linear momentum may possibly be conserved. Kinetic energy may possibly be conserved.</p>","<p>Linear momentum must be conserved. Kinetic energy must be conserved.</p>","<p>Linear momentum must be conserved. Kinetic energy must not be conserved.</p>"]'::jsonb, '<p class="ques"><strong><a href="ch19p.xhtml#ch19p_7" id="rch19p_7">7.</a></strong> <strong>A—</strong>Linear momentum is conserved in any collision because there are no external forces acting on the two-cart system. By definition, elastic collisions conserve kinetic energy. It’s true that for a collision to be elastic, the carts must bounce, but just because carts bounce doesn’t mean the collision <em>must</em> be elastic.</p>'),
  ('ap-mock-physc-mech-3_q8', 'ap-mock-physc-mech-3', 8, false, 'B', 'AP Physics C: Mechanics', 'medium', '<p class="ques"><strong><a href="ch19t.xhtml#rch19p_8" id="ch19p_8">8.</a></strong> A block slides down a frictionless ramp on Earth’s surface. The ramp makes an angle $\theta$ above the horizontal, and the block’s acceleration down the incline is $a$. Now, the block slides down the same ramp near a different planet’s surface. The ramp still makes an angle $\theta$ above the horizontal. This different planet has twice the density of Earth but the same radius. What is the acceleration of the block sliding down the ramp on the different planet?</p>', '["<p>(1/2)$a$</p>","<p>2$a$</p>","<p>$a$</p>","<p>(1/4)$a$</p>","<p>4$a$</p>"]'::jsonb, '<p class="ques"><strong><a href="ch19p.xhtml#ch19p_8" id="rch19p_8">8.</a></strong> <strong>B</strong>—The unbalanced force down the incline is <em>mg</em> sin $\theta$. Newton’s second law shows that the acceleration is then $g$ sin $\theta$. So however $g$ changes, that’s how the acceleration changes. The formula for the gravitational field near a planet is $g$ = <em>GM</em>/$r^{2}$. But we’re given density, not mass! Density is mass per volume. So multiply numerator and denominator by $r$ to get $g$ = <em>GMr</em>/$r^{3}$. Now <em>M/r</em><sup>3</sup> is density . . . so $g$ = <em>G(density)r</em>. The universal gravitation constant $G$ can’t change, the problem says the radius $r$ doesn’t change, but the density doubles—so the whole term for $g$ doubles. And so does the acceleration.</p>'),
  ('ap-mock-physc-mech-3_q9', 'ap-mock-physc-mech-3', 9, false, 'B', 'AP Physics C: Mechanics', 'medium', '<div class="passage-box" style="background: rgba(255,255,255,0.03); padding: 12px 16px; border-radius: 8px; margin-bottom: 14px; border-left: 3px solid #7c3aed;"><p class="image"><img alt="Images" class="q-img" src="images/f0305-01.jpg" style="max-width: 100%; height: auto; margin: 10px 0; display: block;"/></p><p class="noindent">Questions 9–11: A particle moves clockwise in a horizontal circle of radius 2.0 m, as shown above. At the moment in which the particle is at the position shown, its speed is 3.0 m/s, and its speed is increasing by 2.5 m/s per second.</p></div><p class="ques"><strong><a href="ch19t.xhtml#rch19p_9" id="ch19p_9">9.</a></strong> Which of the following vectors could represent the direction of the particle’s instantaneous velocity at the position shown?</p>', '["<p><img alt=\"Images\" class=\"q-img\" src=\"images/larrow.jpg\" style=\"max-width: 100%; height: auto; margin: 10px 0; display: block;\"/></p>","<p><img alt=\"Images\" class=\"q-img\" src=\"images/darrow.jpg\" style=\"max-width: 100%; height: auto; margin: 10px 0; display: block;\"/></p>","<p><img alt=\"Images\" class=\"q-img\" src=\"images/1arrow.jpg\" style=\"max-width: 100%; height: auto; margin: 10px 0; display: block;\"/></p>","<p><img alt=\"Images\" class=\"q-img\" src=\"images/uarrow.jpg\" style=\"max-width: 100%; height: auto; margin: 10px 0; display: block;\"/></p>","<p><img alt=\"Images\" class=\"q-img\" src=\"images/2arrow.jpg\" style=\"max-width: 100%; height: auto; margin: 10px 0; display: block;\"/></p>"]'::jsonb, '<p class="ques"><strong><a href="ch19p.xhtml#ch19p_9" id="rch19p_9">9.</a></strong> <strong>B—</strong>The instantaneous velocity is in the direction the object is moving at this instant. That’s straight down the page.</p>'),
  ('ap-mock-physc-mech-3_q10', 'ap-mock-physc-mech-3', 10, false, 'E', 'AP Physics C: Mechanics', 'medium', '<div class="passage-box" style="background: rgba(255,255,255,0.03); padding: 12px 16px; border-radius: 8px; margin-bottom: 14px; border-left: 3px solid #7c3aed;"><p class="image"><img alt="Images" class="q-img" src="images/f0305-01.jpg" style="max-width: 100%; height: auto; margin: 10px 0; display: block;"/></p><p class="noindent">Questions 9–11: A particle moves clockwise in a horizontal circle of radius 2.0 m, as shown above. At the moment in which the particle is at the position shown, its speed is 3.0 m/s, and its speed is increasing by 2.5 m/s per second.</p></div><p class="ques"><strong><a href="ch19t.xhtml#rch19p_10" id="ch19p_10">10.</a></strong> Which of the following vectors could represent the direction of the net force on the particle at the position shown?</p>', '["<p><img alt=\"Images\" class=\"q-img\" src=\"images/larrow.jpg\" style=\"max-width: 100%; height: auto; margin: 10px 0; display: block;\"/></p>","<p><img alt=\"Images\" class=\"q-img\" src=\"images/darrow.jpg\" style=\"max-width: 100%; height: auto; margin: 10px 0; display: block;\"/></p>","<p><img alt=\"Images\" class=\"q-img\" src=\"images/1arrow.jpg\" style=\"max-width: 100%; height: auto; margin: 10px 0; display: block;\"/></p>","<p><img alt=\"Images\" class=\"q-img\" src=\"images/uarrow.jpg\" style=\"max-width: 100%; height: auto; margin: 10px 0; display: block;\"/></p>","<p><img alt=\"Images\" class=\"q-img\" src=\"images/2arrow.jpg\" style=\"max-width: 100%; height: auto; margin: 10px 0; display: block;\"/></p>"]'::jsonb, '<p class="ques"><strong><a href="ch19p.xhtml#ch19p_10" id="rch19p_10">10.</a></strong> <strong>E—</strong>The net force is in the direction of acceleration. The acceleration has two components here: one toward the center (the “centripetal” acceleration) and another in the direction of motion because the particle is speeding up (the “tangential” acceleration). So the acceleration, and the net force, points somewhat left toward the center and somewhat down.</p>'),
  ('ap-mock-physc-mech-3_q11', 'ap-mock-physc-mech-3', 11, false, 'D', 'AP Physics C: Mechanics', 'medium', '<div class="passage-box" style="background: rgba(255,255,255,0.03); padding: 12px 16px; border-radius: 8px; margin-bottom: 14px; border-left: 3px solid #7c3aed;"><p class="image"><img alt="Images" class="q-img" src="images/f0305-01.jpg" style="max-width: 100%; height: auto; margin: 10px 0; display: block;"/></p><p class="noindent">Questions 9–11: A particle moves clockwise in a horizontal circle of radius 2.0 m, as shown above. At the moment in which the particle is at the position shown, its speed is 3.0 m/s, and its speed is increasing by 2.5 m/s per second.</p></div><p class="ques"><strong><a href="ch19t.xhtml#rch19p_11" id="ch19p_11">11.</a></strong> What is the magnitude of the particle’s instantaneous acceleration at the position shown?</p>', '["<p>3.6 m/$s^{2}$</p>","<p>1.5 m/$s^{2}$</p>","<p>4.5 m/$s^{2}$</p>","<p>5.1 m/$s^{2}$</p>","<p>2.5 m/$s^{2}$</p>"]'::jsonb, '<p class="ques"><strong><a href="ch19p.xhtml#ch19p_11" id="rch19p_11">11.</a></strong> <strong>D—</strong>The acceleration has two components, which must be added using the Pythagorean theorem. The centripetal component is $v^{2}$/$r$, which is (3 m/s)<sup>2</sup>/(2 m) = 4.5 m/$s^{2}$. The tangential component is given as 2.5 m/$s^{2}$. So add 4.5<sup>2</sup> + 2.5<sup>2</sup> and take the square root to get 5.1 m/$s^{2}$.</p>'),
  ('ap-mock-physc-mech-3_q12', 'ap-mock-physc-mech-3', 12, false, 'D', 'AP Physics C: Mechanics', 'medium', '<p class="image"><img alt="Images" class="q-img" src="images/f0305-02.jpg" style="max-width: 100%; height: auto; margin: 10px 0; display: block;"/></p><p class="ques"><strong><a href="ch19t.xhtml#rch19p_12" id="ch19p_12">12.</a></strong> Two blocks move across a rough table at constant speed. Friction is NOT negligible on any surface. The bottom block is pulled to the right with force $F$. Which of the following could indicate the direction of the total force of the bottom block on the top block?</p>', '["<p><img alt=\"Images\" class=\"q-img\" src=\"images/rarrow.jpg\" style=\"max-width: 100%; height: auto; margin: 10px 0; display: block;\"/></p>","<p><img alt=\"Images\" class=\"q-img\" src=\"images/larrow.jpg\" style=\"max-width: 100%; height: auto; margin: 10px 0; display: block;\"/></p>","<p><img alt=\"Images\" class=\"q-img\" src=\"images/1arrow.jpg\" style=\"max-width: 100%; height: auto; margin: 10px 0; display: block;\"/></p>","<p><img alt=\"Images\" class=\"q-img\" src=\"images/uarrow.jpg\" style=\"max-width: 100%; height: auto; margin: 10px 0; display: block;\"/></p>","<p><img alt=\"Images\" class=\"q-img\" src=\"images/3arrow.jpg\" style=\"max-width: 100%; height: auto; margin: 10px 0; display: block;\"/></p>"]'::jsonb, '<p class="ques"><strong><a href="ch19p.xhtml#ch19p_12" id="rch19p_12">12.</a></strong> <strong>D</strong>—The blocks move at constant speed and so experience balanced forces both vertically and horizontally. Vertically, the force of the bottom block on the top block has to balance the top block’s weight—and so has an upward component. Horizontally, the bottom block can’t exert a force on the top block, because there’s no other object to exert a balancing force. Thus no horizontal forces can act on the top block at all.</p>'),
  ('ap-mock-physc-mech-3_q13', 'ap-mock-physc-mech-3', 13, false, 'E', 'AP Physics C: Mechanics', 'medium', '<p class="image"><img alt="Images" class="q-img" src="images/f0306-01.jpg" style="max-width: 100%; height: auto; margin: 10px 0; display: block;"/></p><p class="ques"><strong><a href="ch19t.xhtml#rch19p_13" id="ch19p_13">13.</a></strong> Three stones are thrown from a cliff with velocity vectors $v_{A}$, $v_{B}$, and $v_{C}$. The magnitude of the initial velocity for each stone is the same, but the directions of initial velocity are shown in the diagram. Which of the following correctly ranks the stones according to their speed upon hitting the level ground below the cliff, and by the magnitude of their vertical components of velocity upon hitting the ground?</p><p class="image"><img alt="Images" class="q-img" src="images/f0306-02.jpg" style="max-width: 100%; height: auto; margin: 10px 0; display: block;"/></p><p class="image"><img alt="Images" class="q-img" src="images/f0306-03.jpg" style="max-width: 100%; height: auto; margin: 10px 0; display: block;"/></p>', '["Speed: v_A > v_B > v_C; Vertical Velocity: v_{yA} > v_{yB} > v_{yC}","Speed: v_A > v_B > v_C; Vertical Velocity: v_{yA} = v_{yB} = v_{yC}","Speed: v_A = v_B = v_C; Vertical Velocity: v_{yA} = v_{yB} = v_{yC}","Speed: v_A < v_B < v_C; Vertical Velocity: v_{yA} < v_{yB} < v_{yC}","Speed: v_A = v_B = v_C; Vertical Velocity: v_{yA} > v_{yB} > v_{yC}"]'::jsonb, '<p class="ques"><strong><a href="ch19p.xhtml#ch19p_13" id="rch19p_13">13.</a></strong> <strong>E</strong>—For the magnitude of vertical velocity, start with the vertical component of initial velocity—A’s is largest, B’s is next largest, C’s is zero. Look at which goes highest: Kinematics equations simplify to $x = v^{2}$/2$a$ for final speed of zero, so the stone with the greatest initial vertical speed goes highest. Similarly, the one that goes highest hits the ground with greatest vertical speed. So the <em>vertical</em> velocity at ground is ranked A &gt; B &gt; C. As for speed at ground, it’s easiest to see this through energy conservation. All three rocks gain the same potential energy because they’re at the same vertical height from the ground. All three rocks start with the same initial kinetic energy because their speeds are the same. So the blocks must end up with the same kinetic energy as each other at the bottom, meaning also the same speed.</p>'),
  ('ap-mock-physc-mech-3_q14', 'ap-mock-physc-mech-3', 14, false, 'E', 'AP Physics C: Mechanics', 'medium', '<div class="passage-box" style="background: rgba(255,255,255,0.03); padding: 12px 16px; border-radius: 8px; margin-bottom: 14px; border-left: 3px solid #7c3aed;"><p class="noindent">Questions 14–16: An object of mass $m$ is connected to a string of length $L$. The string is pulled back to an angle $θ_{1}$ and released from rest at position 1. Air resistance is NOT negligible. When the string is vertical, the object moves left. The object comes to rest again at position 3 when the string is at angle $θ_{3}$.</p></div><p class="ques"><strong><a href="ch19t.xhtml#rch19p_14" id="ch19p_14">14.</a></strong> Which of the following is a correct expression for the work done by the air on the object between positions 1 and 2?</p>', '["<p>½<em>mv</em><sub>2</sub><sup>2</sup></p>","<p><em>mgL</em>$θ_{3}$</p>","<p>½<em>mv</em><sub>2</sub><sup>2</sup> - <em>mgL</em>$θ_{3}$</p>","<p>½<em>mv</em><sub>2</sub><sup>2</sup> - <em>mgL</em>$θ_{1}$</p>","<p><em>mgL</em>$θ_{3}$ - <em>mgL</em>$θ_{1}$</p>"]'::jsonb, '<p class="ques"><strong><a href="ch19p.xhtml#ch19p_14" id="rch19p_14">14.</a></strong> <strong>E—</strong>From position 1 to 2, the mechanical energy goes from all potential to all kinetic. Work is done by the air to reduce this mechanical energy. The reduction in mechanical energy is the difference between the kinetic energy at the bottom (½<em>mv</em><sub>2</sub><sup>2</sup>) and the potential energy at the top (<em>mgLθ</em><sub>1</sub>).</p>'),
  ('ap-mock-physc-mech-3_q15', 'ap-mock-physc-mech-3', 15, false, 'E', 'AP Physics C: Mechanics', 'medium', '<div class="passage-box" style="background: rgba(255,255,255,0.03); padding: 12px 16px; border-radius: 8px; margin-bottom: 14px; border-left: 3px solid #7c3aed;"><p class="noindent">Questions 14–16: An object of mass $m$ is connected to a string of length $L$. The string is pulled back to an angle $θ_{1}$ and released from rest at position 1. Air resistance is NOT negligible. When the string is vertical, the object moves left. The object comes to rest again at position 3 when the string is at angle $θ_{3}$.</p></div><p class="ques"><strong><a href="ch19t.xhtml#rch19p_15" id="ch19p_15">15.</a></strong> Is the angle $θ_{3}$ greater than, less than, or equal to $θ_{1}$?</p>', '["<p>$θ_{3}$ is greater than ½$θ_{1}$.</p>","<p>$θ_{3}$ is equal to half of $θ_{1}$.</p>","<p>$θ_{3}$ is less than ½$θ_{1}$.</p>","<p>The answer cannot be determined, but could be determined if we knew whether the force of air resistance were proportional to $v$ or $v^{2}$.</p>","<p>The answer cannot be determined, even if we knew whether the force of air resistance were proportional to $v$ or $v^{2}$.</p>"]'::jsonb, '<p class="ques"><strong><a href="ch19p.xhtml#ch19p_15" id="rch19p_15">15.</a></strong> <strong>E—</strong>If there’s a teeny bit of air resistance, then mechanical energy is almost conserved, and $θ_{3}$ is almost equal to $θ_{1}$. If there’s a lot of air resistance, then mechanical energy is barely conserved at all, and $θ_{3}$ is nearly zero. The form of the air resistance function doesn’t matter; the pure amount of work done by air resistance matters. We don’t know anything about that.</p>'),
  ('ap-mock-physc-mech-3_q16', 'ap-mock-physc-mech-3', 16, false, 'B', 'AP Physics C: Mechanics', 'medium', '<div class="passage-box" style="background: rgba(255,255,255,0.03); padding: 12px 16px; border-radius: 8px; margin-bottom: 14px; border-left: 3px solid #7c3aed;"><p class="noindent">Questions 14–16: An object of mass $m$ is connected to a string of length $L$. The string is pulled back to an angle $θ_{1}$ and released from rest at position 1. Air resistance is NOT negligible. When the string is vertical, the object moves left. The object comes to rest again at position 3 when the string is at angle $θ_{3}$.</p></div><p class="ques"><strong><a href="ch19t.xhtml#rch19p_16" id="ch19p_16">16.</a></strong> Now consider that the force of air resistance is equal to <em>cv</em>. Which of the following is a differential equation that could be used to solve for the angle $\theta$ as a function of time while the string is between angles $θ_{1}$ and $θ_{2}$?</p>', '["<p><img alt=\"Images\" class=\"q-img\" src=\"images/f0307-01.jpg\" style=\"max-width: 100%; height: auto; margin: 10px 0; display: block;\"/></p>","<p><img alt=\"Images\" class=\"q-img\" src=\"images/f0307-02.jpg\" style=\"max-width: 100%; height: auto; margin: 10px 0; display: block;\"/></p>","<p><img alt=\"Images\" class=\"q-img\" src=\"images/f0307-03.jpg\" style=\"max-width: 100%; height: auto; margin: 10px 0; display: block;\"/></p>","<p><img alt=\"Images\" class=\"q-img\" src=\"images/f0307-04.jpg\" style=\"max-width: 100%; height: auto; margin: 10px 0; display: block;\"/></p>","<p><img alt=\"Images\" class=\"q-img\" src=\"images/f0307-05.jpg\" style=\"max-width: 100%; height: auto; margin: 10px 0; display: block;\"/></p>"]'::jsonb, '<p class="ques"><strong><a href="ch19p.xhtml#ch19p_16" id="rch19p_16">16.</a></strong> <strong>B—</strong>The forces in the direction of the object’s motion at any position have components <em>mg</em> sin $\theta$ along the object’s path and <em>cv</em> against the object’s path. So the net force is <em>mg</em> sin $\theta$ − <em>cv</em>. Set this equal to mass times acceleration, where acceleration is the second derivative of position. But we don’t want the derivative of the angle $\theta$, we want the derivative of the distance along the arc. That’s <em>Lθ</em>.</p>'),
  ('ap-mock-physc-mech-3_q17', 'ap-mock-physc-mech-3', 17, false, 'C', 'AP Physics C: Mechanics', 'medium', '<div class="passage-box" style="background: rgba(255,255,255,0.03); padding: 12px 16px; border-radius: 8px; margin-bottom: 14px; border-left: 3px solid #7c3aed;"><p class="noindent">Questions 17–18: A cart moves along a straight road according to the velocity-time graph shown above.</p></div><p class="ques"><strong><a href="ch19t.xhtml#rch19p_17" id="ch19p_17">17.</a></strong> What is the average acceleration of this car between time 0 and time 10 s?</p>', '["<p>+0.3 m/$s^{2}$</p>","<p>0.0 m/$s^{2}$</p>","<p>+0.6 m/$s^{2}$</p>","<p>+1.0 m/$s^{2}$</p>","<p>+3.0 m/$s^{2}$</p>"]'::jsonb, '<p class="ques"><strong><a href="ch19p.xhtml#ch19p_17" id="rch19p_17">17.</a></strong> <strong>C—</strong>Acceleration is the slope of the velocity-time graph. “Average” acceleration would be the slope from the beginning to the end of the graph, disregarding what’s in between. That’s the slope from (0, −3) to (10, 3) which is 6/10 = 0.6 m/$s^{2}$.</p>'),
  ('ap-mock-physc-mech-3_q18', 'ap-mock-physc-mech-3', 18, false, 'A', 'AP Physics C: Mechanics', 'medium', '<div class="passage-box" style="background: rgba(255,255,255,0.03); padding: 12px 16px; border-radius: 8px; margin-bottom: 14px; border-left: 3px solid #7c3aed;"><p class="noindent">Questions 17–18: A cart moves along a straight road according to the velocity-time graph shown above.</p></div><p class="ques"><strong><a href="ch19t.xhtml#rch19p_18" id="ch19p_18">18.</a></strong> What is the average velocity of this car between time 0 and time 10 s?</p>', '["<p>1.2 m/s</p>","<p>24 m/s</p>","<p>12 m/s</p>","<p>2.4 m/s</p>","<p>1.0 m/s</p>"]'::jsonb, '<p class="ques"><strong><a href="ch19p.xhtml#ch19p_18" id="rch19p_18">18.</a></strong> <strong>A—</strong>Average velocity is defined as displacement divided by time interval. Displacement is found from the area under a velocity-time graph. Here, the areas below and above the $t$-axis in the first 6 seconds cancel. The remaining area is the rectangle (4 s)(3 m/s) = 12 m. Divide by the time interval of 10 s to get 1.2 m/s.</p>'),
  ('ap-mock-physc-mech-3_q19', 'ap-mock-physc-mech-3', 19, false, 'D', 'AP Physics C: Mechanics', 'medium', '<p class="image"><img alt="Images" class="q-img" src="images/f0307-06.jpg" style="max-width: 100%; height: auto; margin: 10px 0; display: block;"/></p><p class="ques"><strong><a href="ch19t.xhtml#rch19p_19" id="ch19p_19">19.</a></strong> Car A and Car B start at the same position as each other at time $t$ = 0, then move according to the velocity $v$ vs. time graphs above. How far apart are the cars at time $t$ = 4 s?</p>', '["<p>10 m</p>","<p>24 m</p>","<p>8 m</p>","<p>12 m</p>","<p>4 m</p>"]'::jsonb, '<p class="ques"><strong><a href="ch19p.xhtml#ch19p_19" id="rch19p_19">19.</a></strong> <strong>D—</strong>The distance traveled by each car is found by taking the area under the graph. That’s 8 m for Car A and 4 m for Car B. Car A’s graph is above the horizontal axis, while car B’s graph is below the horizontal axis; this means the cars move in different directions. Thus they end up 12 m apart.</p>'),
  ('ap-mock-physc-mech-3_q20', 'ap-mock-physc-mech-3', 20, false, 'E', 'AP Physics C: Mechanics', 'medium', '<div class="passage-box" style="background: rgba(255,255,255,0.03); padding: 12px 16px; border-radius: 8px; margin-bottom: 14px; border-left: 3px solid #7c3aed;"><p class="noindent">Questions 20–22: A string of length $L$ is attached to an object of unknown mass. The string is pulled back to a small unknown angle and released from rest. An astronaut takes this apparatus to the surface of Planet X, where the gravitational field is 4.9 N/kg.</p></div><p class="ques"><strong><a href="ch19t.xhtml#rch19p_20" id="ch19p_20">20.</a></strong> The time for the object to first reach the vertical position on Earth is $t_{e}$. On the surface of Planet X, what is the time for the object to first reach the vertical position?</p>', '["<p>$t_{e}$</p>","<p>2$t_{e}$</p>","<p><img alt=\"Images\" class=\"q-img\" src=\"images/f0307-07.jpg\" style=\"max-width: 100%; height: auto; margin: 10px 0; display: block;\"/></p>","<p>4$t_{e}$</p>","<p><img alt=\"Images\" class=\"q-img\" src=\"images/f0307-08.jpg\" style=\"max-width: 100%; height: auto; margin: 10px 0; display: block;\"/></p>"]'::jsonb, '<p class="ques"><strong><a href="ch19p.xhtml#ch19p_20" id="rch19p_20">20.</a></strong> <strong>E—</strong>The time we’re talking about is ¼ of a period. The period of a pendulum equation has $g$ in the denominator and under the square root. The gravitational field on Planet X is ½ that of Earth, so the period of the pendulum there is 1/(1/<img alt="Images" class="q-img" src="images/root2.jpg" style="max-width: 100%; height: auto; margin: 10px 0; display: block;"/>) times the period on Earth; that’s the same thing as <img alt="Images" class="q-img" src="images/root2.jpg" style="max-width: 100%; height: auto; margin: 10px 0; display: block;"/> times the period on Earth.</p>'),
  ('ap-mock-physc-mech-3_q21', 'ap-mock-physc-mech-3', 21, false, 'C', 'AP Physics C: Mechanics', 'medium', '<div class="passage-box" style="background: rgba(255,255,255,0.03); padding: 12px 16px; border-radius: 8px; margin-bottom: 14px; border-left: 3px solid #7c3aed;"><p class="noindent">Questions 20–22: A string of length $L$ is attached to an object of unknown mass. The string is pulled back to a small unknown angle and released from rest. An astronaut takes this apparatus to the surface of Planet X, where the gravitational field is 4.9 N/kg.</p></div><p class="ques"><strong><a href="ch19t.xhtml#rch19p_21" id="ch19p_21">21.</a></strong> Let the maximum speed of the object on Earth be $v_{e}$. What is the maximum speed of the object on Planet X?</p>', '["<p>$v_{e}$</p>","<p>2$v_{e}$</p>","<p><img alt=\"Images\" class=\"q-img\" src=\"images/f0308-01.jpg\" style=\"max-width: 100%; height: auto; margin: 10px 0; display: block;\"/></p>","<p>4$v_{e}$</p>","<p><img alt=\"Images\" class=\"q-img\" src=\"images/f0308-02.jpg\" style=\"max-width: 100%; height: auto; margin: 10px 0; display: block;\"/></p>"]'::jsonb, '<p class="ques"><strong><a href="ch19p.xhtml#ch19p_21" id="rch19p_21">21.</a></strong> <strong>C—</strong>The maximum speed comes from energy conservation: the gravitational energy at the top is equal to the kinetic energy at the bottom, <em>mgh</em> = ½<em>mv</em><sup>2</sup>. Solve for $v$ to get a maximum speed of <img alt="Images" class="q-img" src="images/r-331.jpg" style="max-width: 100%; height: auto; margin: 10px 0; display: block;"/>. Here $g$ is in the numerator and under the square root. Since the gravitational field on Planet X is half that of Earth, the max speed is <img alt="Images" class="q-img" src="images/f0331-01.jpg" style="max-width: 100%; height: auto; margin: 10px 0; display: block;"/> times the speed on Earth.</p>'),
  ('ap-mock-physc-mech-3_q22', 'ap-mock-physc-mech-3', 22, false, 'D', 'AP Physics C: Mechanics', 'medium', '<div class="passage-box" style="background: rgba(255,255,255,0.03); padding: 12px 16px; border-radius: 8px; margin-bottom: 14px; border-left: 3px solid #7c3aed;"><p class="noindent">Questions 20–22: A string of length $L$ is attached to an object of unknown mass. The string is pulled back to a small unknown angle and released from rest. An astronaut takes this apparatus to the surface of Planet X, where the gravitational field is 4.9 N/kg.</p></div><p class="ques"><strong><a href="ch19t.xhtml#rch19p_22" id="ch19p_22">22.</a></strong> What measurements in addition to the string length $L$ must be measured to determine an experimental value for $g$ on this planet?</p><p class="roman1t">I. The time for the object to pass through the vertical position two consecutive times</p><p class="roman2">II. The speed of the object as it passes the vertical position</p><p class="roman3">III. The mass of the object</p>', '["<p>II only</p>","<p>II and III</p>","<p>III only</p>","<p>I only</p>","<p>I and III</p>"]'::jsonb, '<p class="ques"><strong><a href="ch19p.xhtml#ch19p_22" id="rch19p_22">22.</a></strong> <strong>D—</strong>The period of a pendulum depends only on the length of the pendulum and the gravitational field. Choice I is, in fact, the period; so that and $L$ can be used to solve for $g$. Choice II doesn’t work because the speed as shown in the previous problem depends on the vertical height $h$ from which the ball was released, not the string length; to find $h$ we’d also need the angle from which the pendulum was released. And the mass is not in the period equation and cancels from the max-speed equation, so it is not useful.</p>'),
  ('ap-mock-physc-mech-3_q23', 'ap-mock-physc-mech-3', 23, false, 'B', 'AP Physics C: Mechanics', 'medium', '<p class="image"><img alt="Images" class="q-img" src="images/f0308-03.jpg" style="max-width: 100%; height: auto; margin: 10px 0; display: block;"/></p><p class="ques"><strong><a href="ch19t.xhtml#rch19p_23" id="ch19p_23">23.</a></strong> A ball is dropped from a skyscraper. Between the time of the drop and the time at which the ball reaches terminal velocity, which of the following is true about the magnitudes of the ball’s velocity and acceleration vectors?</p>', '["<p>velocity remains constant while acceleration remains constant</p>","<p>velocity increases while acceleration decreases</p>","<p>velocity decreases while acceleration decreases</p>","<p>velocity increases while acceleration remains constant</p>","<p>velocity remains constant while acceleration decreases</p>"]'::jsonb, '<p class="ques"><strong><a href="ch19p.xhtml#ch19p_23" id="rch19p_23">23.</a></strong> <strong>B—</strong>Terminal velocity is when the ball falls at constant speed because the forces of gravity and air resistance balance. Before reaching terminal velocity, though, the ball had to speed up from rest to reach that speed—so velocity increased. And acceleration is the change in speed every second. Initially the ball was changing speed; once the ball hit terminal velocity, the speed was not changing. The acceleration was nonzero and then zero. So acceleration decreased.</p>'),
  ('ap-mock-physc-mech-3_q24', 'ap-mock-physc-mech-3', 24, false, 'C', 'AP Physics C: Mechanics', 'medium', '<div class="passage-box" style="background: rgba(255,255,255,0.03); padding: 12px 16px; border-radius: 8px; margin-bottom: 14px; border-left: 3px solid #7c3aed;"><p class="image"><img alt="Images" class="q-img" src="images/f0309-01.jpg" style="max-width: 100%; height: auto; margin: 10px 0; display: block;"/></p><p class="noindent">Questions 24 and 25: A small dart of mass $M_{2}$ is fired into a hanging object of mass $M_{1}$, which is initially at rest. The dart sticks, and the combined dart-object system moves to a maximum vertical height $h$ above their initial position.</p></div><p class="ques"><strong><a href="ch19t.xhtml#rch19p_24" id="ch19p_24">24.</a></strong> Which of the following is a correct expression for the speed of the combined dart-object system immediately after collision?</p>', '["<p><img alt=\"Images\" class=\"q-img\" src=\"images/f0309-02.jpg\" style=\"max-width: 100%; height: auto; margin: 10px 0; display: block;\"/></p>","<p><img alt=\"Images\" class=\"q-img\" src=\"images/f0309-03.jpg\" style=\"max-width: 100%; height: auto; margin: 10px 0; display: block;\"/></p>","<p><img alt=\"Images\" class=\"q-img\" src=\"images/f0309-04.jpg\" style=\"max-width: 100%; height: auto; margin: 10px 0; display: block;\"/></p>","<p><img alt=\"Images\" class=\"q-img\" src=\"images/f0309-05.jpg\" style=\"max-width: 100%; height: auto; margin: 10px 0; display: block;\"/></p>","<p><img alt=\"Images\" class=\"q-img\" src=\"images/f0309-06.jpg\" style=\"max-width: 100%; height: auto; margin: 10px 0; display: block;\"/></p>"]'::jsonb, '<p class="ques"><strong><a href="ch19p.xhtml#ch19p_24" id="rch19p_24">24.</a></strong> <strong>C—</strong>After the collision, the dart-block system swings to the maximum height $h$. Use energy conservation: ($M_{1} +M_{2}$)<em>gh</em> = ½($M_{1} +M_{2}$)$v^{2}$. Solve for $v$, and the masses cancel.</p>'),
  ('ap-mock-physc-mech-3_q25', 'ap-mock-physc-mech-3', 25, false, 'C', 'AP Physics C: Mechanics', 'medium', '<div class="passage-box" style="background: rgba(255,255,255,0.03); padding: 12px 16px; border-radius: 8px; margin-bottom: 14px; border-left: 3px solid #7c3aed;"><p class="image"><img alt="Images" class="q-img" src="images/f0309-01.jpg" style="max-width: 100%; height: auto; margin: 10px 0; display: block;"/></p><p class="noindent">Questions 24 and 25: A small dart of mass $M_{2}$ is fired into a hanging object of mass $M_{1}$, which is initially at rest. The dart sticks, and the combined dart-object system moves to a maximum vertical height $h$ above their initial position.</p></div><p class="ques"><strong><a href="ch19t.xhtml#rch19p_25" id="ch19p_25">25.</a></strong> Which of the following is a correct expression for the speed of the dart before the collision?</p>', '["<p><img alt=\"Images\" class=\"q-img\" src=\"images/f0309-07.jpg\" style=\"max-width: 100%; height: auto; margin: 10px 0; display: block;\"/></p>","<p><img alt=\"Images\" class=\"q-img\" src=\"images/f0309-08.jpg\" style=\"max-width: 100%; height: auto; margin: 10px 0; display: block;\"/></p>","<p><img alt=\"Images\" class=\"q-img\" src=\"images/f0309-09.jpg\" style=\"max-width: 100%; height: auto; margin: 10px 0; display: block;\"/></p>","<p><img alt=\"Images\" class=\"q-img\" src=\"images/f0309-10.jpg\" style=\"max-width: 100%; height: auto; margin: 10px 0; display: block;\"/></p>","<p><img alt=\"Images\" class=\"q-img\" src=\"images/f0309-11.jpg\" style=\"max-width: 100%; height: auto; margin: 10px 0; display: block;\"/></p>"]'::jsonb, '<p class="ques"><strong><a href="ch19p.xhtml#ch19p_25" id="rch19p_25">25.</a></strong> <strong>C—</strong>Now use conservation of total momentum right before and after the collision: $M_{2} v$ + 0 = ($M_{1} +M_{2}$)<img alt="Images" class="q-img" src="images/f0332-01.jpg" style="max-width: 100%; height: auto; margin: 10px 0; display: block;"/>, using the result from the previous question for the speed of the blocks after collision. Solve for $v$ to get choice C.</p>'),
  ('ap-mock-physc-mech-3_q26', 'ap-mock-physc-mech-3', 26, false, 'A', 'AP Physics C: Mechanics', 'hard', '<p class="ques"><strong><a href="ch19t.xhtml#rch19p_26" id="ch19p_26">26.</a></strong> A hoop of mass $m$ is released from rest at the top of a ramp, a vertical distance $h$ above the bottom of the ramp. The hoop rolls without slipping to the bottom of the ramp, where it has speed $v$ and total mechanical energy $E$. Next, a solid disk, also of mass $m$, <em></em>is released from the same position, and likewise rolls without slipping. Which of the following is correct about the speed and mechanical energy of the disk compared to the hoop?</p><p class="image"><img alt="Images" class="q-img" src="images/f0309-12.jpg" style="max-width: 100%; height: auto; margin: 10px 0; display: block;"/></p>', '["Speed: Disk is faster than hoop; Mechanical Energy: Disk equals hoop","Speed: Disk is faster than hoop; Mechanical Energy: Disk is greater than hoop","Speed: Disk equals hoop; Mechanical Energy: Disk equals hoop","Speed: Disk is slower than hoop; Mechanical Energy: Disk equals hoop","Speed: Disk is slower than hoop; Mechanical Energy: Disk is less than hoop"]'::jsonb, '<p class="ques"><strong><a href="ch19p.xhtml#ch19p_26" id="rch19p_26">26.</a></strong> <strong>A—</strong>The mechanical energy of the object-Earth systems starts at <em>mgh</em>, and since no work is done by external forces on the objects, the mechanical energy is the same at the bottom as at the top. But that mechanical energy at the bottom consists of both rotational and translational kinetic energies. Since a hoop has mass distributed farther on average from the axis of rotation, the hoop must have a greater rotational inertia. Therefore, a greater proportion of its mechanical energy is rotational rather than translational, and the hoop moves slower, the disk faster.</p>'),
  ('ap-mock-physc-mech-3_q27', 'ap-mock-physc-mech-3', 27, false, 'D', 'AP Physics C: Mechanics', 'hard', '<p class="ques"><strong><a href="ch19t.xhtml#rch19p_27" id="ch19p_27">27.</a></strong> In the laboratory, disk 1 spins on a vertical axle with minimal friction. Disk 2 is initially not spinning and drops from above onto disk 1. The disks collide and rotate together after collision. How does the angular momentum and the rotational kinetic energy of the two-disk system compare before and after collision?</p><p class="image"><img alt="Images" class="q-img" src="images/f0309-13.jpg" style="max-width: 100%; height: auto; margin: 10px 0; display: block;"/></p><p class="image"><img alt="Images" class="q-img" src="images/f0310-01.jpg" style="max-width: 100%; height: auto; margin: 10px 0; display: block;"/></p>', '["Angular Momentum: Conserved; Rotational Kinetic Energy: Conserved","Angular Momentum: Decreases; Rotational Kinetic Energy: Conserved","Angular Momentum: Decreases; Rotational Kinetic Energy: Decreases","Angular Momentum: Conserved; Rotational Kinetic Energy: Decreases","Angular Momentum: Increases; Rotational Kinetic Energy: Decreases"]'::jsonb, '<p class="ques"><strong><a href="ch19p.xhtml#ch19p_27" id="rch19p_27">27.</a></strong> <strong>D—</strong>No external torques act on the two-disk system (only the torque of disk 1 on disk 2 and vice versa). That’s the very definition of when angular momentum is conserved. However, the collision is by definition inelastic because the disks stick together. In an inelastic collision, mechanical energy—including rotational kinetic energy—is not conserved.</p>'),
  ('ap-mock-physc-mech-3_q28', 'ap-mock-physc-mech-3', 28, false, 'C', 'AP Physics C: Mechanics', 'hard', '<div class="passage-box" style="background: rgba(255,255,255,0.03); padding: 12px 16px; border-radius: 8px; margin-bottom: 14px; border-left: 3px solid #7c3aed;"><p class="noindent">Questions 28 and 29: Three spheres are arranged in deep space as shown above, far away from any planet or star.</p></div><p class="ques"><strong><a href="ch19t.xhtml#rch19p_28" id="ch19p_28">28.</a></strong> Which of the following diagrams shows the correct relative magnitudes and directions of the forces $F_{C}$ (the gravitational force of sphere C on sphere A) and $F_{B}$ (the gravitational force of sphere B on sphere A)?</p>', '["<p><img alt=\"Images\" class=\"q-img\" src=\"images/f0310-02.jpg\" style=\"max-width: 100%; height: auto; margin: 10px 0; display: block;\"/></p>","<p><img alt=\"Images\" class=\"q-img\" src=\"images/f0310-03.jpg\" style=\"max-width: 100%; height: auto; margin: 10px 0; display: block;\"/></p>","<p><img alt=\"Images\" class=\"q-img\" src=\"images/f0310-04.jpg\" style=\"max-width: 100%; height: auto; margin: 10px 0; display: block;\"/></p>","<p><img alt=\"Images\" class=\"q-img\" src=\"images/f0310-05.jpg\" style=\"max-width: 100%; height: auto; margin: 10px 0; display: block;\"/></p>","<p><img alt=\"Images\" class=\"q-img\" src=\"images/f0310-06.jpg\" style=\"max-width: 100%; height: auto; margin: 10px 0; display: block;\"/></p>"]'::jsonb, '<p class="ques"><strong><a href="ch19p.xhtml#ch19p_28" id="rch19p_28">28.</a></strong> <strong>C—</strong>The gravitational force is always attractive, so both forces act to the right. The formula is $F$ = <em>Gm</em><sub>1</sub>$m_{2}$<em>/d</em><sup>2</sup>. This equation for $F_{B}$ has a numerator that’s twice as big as for $F_{C}$ and a denominator (2/3)<sup>2</sup> = 4/9 as big. That makes $F_{B}$ something like four times larger, as in choice C.</p>'),
  ('ap-mock-physc-mech-3_q29', 'ap-mock-physc-mech-3', 29, false, 'E', 'AP Physics C: Mechanics', 'hard', '<div class="passage-box" style="background: rgba(255,255,255,0.03); padding: 12px 16px; border-radius: 8px; margin-bottom: 14px; border-left: 3px solid #7c3aed;"><p class="noindent">Questions 28 and 29: Three spheres are arranged in deep space as shown above, far away from any planet or star.</p></div><p class="ques"><strong><a href="ch19t.xhtml#rch19p_29" id="ch19p_29">29.</a></strong> In units of <em>Gm</em><sup>2</sup><em>/x</em><em><sup>2</sup></em>, which of the following represents the net gravitational force of spheres B and C on sphere A?</p>', '["<p>7/18</p>","<p>7/3</p>","<p>1/6</p>","<p>4/3</p>","<p>11/18</p>"]'::jsonb, '<p class="ques"><strong><a href="ch19p.xhtml#ch19p_29" id="rch19p_29">29.</a></strong> <strong>E—</strong>The expression for the force of sphere B on sphere A is $G$(2$m$)($m$)/(2$x$)<sup>2</sup>. The expression for the force of sphere C on sphere A is $G$($m$)($m$)/(3$x$)<sup>2</sup>. Add the forces together because they act in the same direction; you get (11/18)(<em>Gm</em><sup>2</sup><em>/x</em><sup>2</sup>).</p>'),
  ('ap-mock-physc-mech-3_q30', 'ap-mock-physc-mech-3', 30, false, 'C', 'AP Physics C: Mechanics', 'hard', '<div class="passage-box" style="background: rgba(255,255,255,0.03); padding: 12px 16px; border-radius: 8px; margin-bottom: 14px; border-left: 3px solid #7c3aed;"><p class="noindent">Questions 30 and 31: An object of mass 2 kg moves in one dimension according to the velocity $v$ vs. time $t$ function $v$($t$) = $c$ – <em>bt</em><sup>2</sup><em>,</em> where $c$ = +2 m/s, and $b$ = +3 m/$s^{3}$. The positive direction is to the right.</p></div><p class="ques"><strong><a href="ch19t.xhtml#rch19p_30" id="ch19p_30">30.</a></strong> Which of the following is an expression for the net force (in SI units) acting on the object as a function of time?</p>', '["<p>−6$t$</p>","<p>4 – 6$t^{2}$</p>","<p>−12$t$</p>","<p>2$t$ – $t^{3}$</p>","<p>4$t$ – 2$t^{2}$</p>"]'::jsonb, '<p class="ques"><strong><a href="ch19p.xhtml#ch19p_30" id="rch19p_30">30.</a></strong> C—The time derivative of velocity is acceleration, and then net force is mass times acceleration. The time derivative of this function is −6$t$; multiply this by the 2 kg mass to get choice C.</p>'),
  ('ap-mock-physc-mech-3_q31', 'ap-mock-physc-mech-3', 31, false, 'E', 'AP Physics C: Mechanics', 'hard', '<div class="passage-box" style="background: rgba(255,255,255,0.03); padding: 12px 16px; border-radius: 8px; margin-bottom: 14px; border-left: 3px solid #7c3aed;"><p class="noindent">Questions 30 and 31: An object of mass 2 kg moves in one dimension according to the velocity $v$ vs. time $t$ function $v$($t$) = $c$ – <em>bt</em><sup>2</sup><em>,</em> where $c$ = +2 m/s, and $b$ = +3 m/$s^{3}$. The positive direction is to the right.</p></div><p class="ques"><strong><a href="ch19t.xhtml#rch19p_31" id="ch19p_31">31.</a></strong> Which of the following descriptions of the object’s motion starting at time $t$ = 0 and immediately thereafter is correct?</p>', '["<p>The object initially is moving to the left at 2 m/s and is speeding up.</p>","<p>The object initially is moving to the right at 2 m/s and is speeding up.</p>","<p>The object initially is at rest, then speeds up.</p>","<p>The object initially is moving to the left at 2 m/s and is slowing down.</p>","<p>The object initially is moving to the right at 2 m/s and is slowing down.</p>"]'::jsonb, '<p class="ques"><strong><a href="ch19p.xhtml#ch19p_31" id="rch19p_31">31.</a></strong> <strong>E—</strong>The initial velocity is when time $t$ is zero. That’s just $c$ in this expression, so +2 m/s. The positive means moving right. As time goes on, the second term becomes nonzero, which means we’re subtracting something from the initial speed, so the object slows down.</p>'),
  ('ap-mock-physc-mech-3_q32', 'ap-mock-physc-mech-3', 32, false, 'A', 'AP Physics C: Mechanics', 'hard', '<p class="ques"><strong><a href="ch19t.xhtml#rch19p_32" id="ch19p_32">32.</a></strong> A 5000 kg satellite changes its orbital speed by firing a thruster for one minute. In that time, the satellite’s speed changes from 7600 m/s to 7500 m/s. What is the force provided by the thruster?</p>', '["<p>8300 N</p>","<p>500,000 N</p>","<p>50,000 N</p>","<p>30,000 N</p>","<p>83 N</p>"]'::jsonb, '<p class="ques"><strong><a href="ch19p.xhtml#ch19p_32" id="rch19p_32">32.</a></strong> <strong>A—</strong>Use the impulse-momentum theorem, Δ$p$ = <em>Ft</em>. Here the change in momentum is the satellite’s mass times its change in speed, 5000 kg × 100 m/s. And the time interval is 60 s. So the force of the thruster is 500,000/60 = 8300 N.</p>'),
  ('ap-mock-physc-mech-3_q33', 'ap-mock-physc-mech-3', 33, false, 'E', 'AP Physics C: Mechanics', 'hard', '<p class="ques"><strong><a href="ch19t.xhtml#rch19p_33" id="ch19p_33">33.</a></strong> A satellite of mass $m_{s}$ is placed in geosynchronous orbit, meaning it remains over the same position on Earth’s surface at all times. The mass of Earth is $m_{e}$, and the period of Earth’s orbit is $T$. Which of the following is a correct expression for the radius of the satellite’s orbit?</p>', '["<p><img alt=\"Images\" class=\"q-img\" src=\"images/f0310-07.jpg\" style=\"max-width: 100%; height: auto; margin: 10px 0; display: block;\"/></p>","<p><img alt=\"Images\" class=\"q-img\" src=\"images/f0310-08.jpg\" style=\"max-width: 100%; height: auto; margin: 10px 0; display: block;\"/></p>","<p><img alt=\"Images\" class=\"q-img\" src=\"images/f0310-09.jpg\" style=\"max-width: 100%; height: auto; margin: 10px 0; display: block;\"/></p>","<p><img alt=\"Images\" class=\"q-img\" src=\"images/f0310-10.jpg\" style=\"max-width: 100%; height: auto; margin: 10px 0; display: block;\"/></p>","<p><img alt=\"Images\" class=\"q-img\" src=\"images/f0310-11.jpg\" style=\"max-width: 100%; height: auto; margin: 10px 0; display: block;\"/></p>"]'::jsonb, '<p class="ques"><strong><a href="ch19p.xhtml#ch19p_33" id="rch19p_33">33.</a></strong> <strong>E—</strong>Kepler’s law says that the period of an orbit squared is proportional to the radius of the orbit cubed. Thus the expression for radius must have a cube root of $T^{2}$. Then when using Newton’s second law by setting the gravitational force <em>Gm</em><sub>s</sub>$m_{e}$/$r^{2}$ equal to <em>ma</em>, the object undergoing acceleration is the satellite—so we use $m_{s}$ in the right-hand-side expression, and the $m_{s}$ cancels . . . leaving $m_{e}$ as the only mass in the resulting expression.</p>'),
  ('ap-mock-physc-mech-3_q34', 'ap-mock-physc-mech-3', 34, false, 'A', 'AP Physics C: Mechanics', 'hard', '<p class="ques"><strong><a href="ch19t.xhtml#rch19p_34" id="ch19p_34">34.</a></strong> A student reports the results of an experiment:</p><p class="ques-p">A ball of mass 1.0 kg initially moves horizontally at a speed of 2.0 m/s. The ball bounces off the stationary wall of a building, rebounding horizontally with speed 2.0 m/s.</p><p class="ques-p">Which of the following is a correct statement about horizontal linear momentum conservation in this collision?</p>', '["<p>The momentum of the ball was not conserved, but the momentum of the ball-Earth system was conserved.</p>","<p>The momentum of the ball was not conserved, and neither was the momentum of the ball-Earth system.</p>","<p>The momentum of the ball was conserved, and the momentum of the ball-Earth system was also conserved.</p>","<p>The momentum of the ball was conserved, but the momentum of the ball-Earth system was not conserved.</p>","<p>The momentum of the ball was conserved, but the data provided don’t provide enough evidence to draw a conclusion about whether momentum of the ball-Earth system was conserved.</p>"]'::jsonb, '<p class="ques"><strong><a href="ch19p.xhtml#ch19p_34" id="rch19p_34">34.</a></strong> <strong>A—</strong>Momentum is conserved when no external forces act on a system. The ball’s momentum is not conserved here, because the external force of the wall acts on the ball. (The ball’s direction changes, so even though the magnitude of the ball’s momentum doesn’t change, the ball’s momentum vector does change.) Considering the ball-Earth system, though, no external forces act on that system—so momentum of the ball-Earth system is conserved.</p>'),
  ('ap-mock-physc-mech-3_q35', 'ap-mock-physc-mech-3', 35, false, 'C', 'AP Physics C: Mechanics', 'hard', '<p class="ques"><strong><a href="ch19t.xhtml#rch19p_35" id="ch19p_35">35.</a></strong> Satellite A undergoes circular orbit around Earth. The satellite maintains a distance above Earth’s surface equal to one Earth radius. Satellite B undergoes circular orbit a distance above Earth’s surface equal to two Earth radii. What is the ratio of the orbital speed of satellite A to the orbital speed of satellite B?</p>', '["<p>1:1</p>","<p>3:2</p>","<p><img alt=\"Images\" class=\"q-img\" src=\"images/f0311-01.jpg\" style=\"max-width: 100%; height: auto; margin: 10px 0; display: block;\"/></p>","<p>2:1</p>","<p><img alt=\"Images\" class=\"q-img\" src=\"images/f0311-02.jpg\" style=\"max-width: 100%; height: auto; margin: 10px 0; display: block;\"/></p>"]'::jsonb, '<p class="ques"><strong><a href="ch19p.xhtml#ch19p_35" id="rch19p_35">35.</a></strong> <strong>C—</strong>Derive the orbital speed by setting the gravitational force <em>GMm/r</em><sup>2</sup> equal to <em>ma</em>, where acceleration in a circle is $v^{2}$/$r$. Solving for $v$ gives <img alt="Images" class="q-img" src="images/f0332-02.jpg" style="max-width: 100%; height: auto; margin: 10px 0; display: block;"/>, $M$ being the mass of the central planet. With the $r$ for satellite B being twice that for satellite A, that gives a ratio of 1:(1/<img alt="Images" class="q-img" src="images/root2.jpg" style="max-width: 100%; height: auto; margin: 10px 0; display: block;"/>), which is the same thing as <img alt="Images" class="q-img" src="images/root2.jpg" style="max-width: 100%; height: auto; margin: 10px 0; display: block;"/>:1.</p>
                <div class="correction-note" style="margin-top: 10px; padding: 10px; background: rgba(79, 70, 229, 0.1); border-left: 3px solid #6366f1; border-radius: 4px;">
                    <strong>Analytical Physical Note (Errata Correction):</strong><br>
                    Satellite A orbits at altitude \$1 R_E$ above Earth''s surface ($r_A = 2 R_E$). Satellite B orbits at altitude \$2 R_E$ above Earth''s surface ($r_B = 3 R_E$).
                    The orbital speed ratio is $v_A / v_B = \sqrt{r_B / r_A} = \sqrt{3/2} : 1 \approx 1.22 : 1$.
                </div>
                '),
  ('ap-mock-physc-mech-3_q36', 'ap-mock-physc-mech-3', 36, true, 'FRQ_MANUAL_EVALUATION', 'AP Physics C: Mechanics Free Response', 'hard', '<div class=''frq-prompt''><h3>CM 1</h3><p class="ques"><strong><a href="ch19u.xhtml#rch19q_1" id="ch19q_1">1.</a></strong> An object of mass 2 kg moves in one dimension along the $x$-axis, experiencing a resistive force. Its velocity $v$ in meters per second as a function of time $t$ in seconds is given by the expression <em>ν</em>($t$) = +1.4$e$<sup>-<em>bt</em></sup>.</p><p class="alphabrt">(a) What are the units of the constant $b$?</p><p class="alphabr">(b) Determine the magnitude of the object’s initial velocity.</p><p class="alphabr">(c) Is the object moving in the positive or negative direction initially? Briefly justify your answer.</p><p class="alphabr">(d) Immediately after time $t$ = 0, is the object speeding up, slowing down, or moving with steady speed? Briefly justify your answer.</p><p class="alphabr">(e) Immediately after time $t$ = 0, is the magnitude of the object’s acceleration increasing, decreasing, or remaining constant? Briefly justify your answer.</p><p class="alphabr">(f) On the axes below, sketch the shape of a graph of the net force on this object as a function of time for the first 4 seconds of motion. Label the graph with expressions for any vertical or horizontal intercepts.</p><p class="image"><img alt="Images" class="q-img" src="images/f0312-01.jpg" style="max-width: 100%; height: auto; margin: 10px 0; display: block;"/></p><p class="alphabr">(g) In terms of $b$, derive an expression for the total distance traveled by the object after a very long time.</p><p class="alphabrb">(h) Calculate the total energy dissipated by the resistive force after a very long time.</p><p class="image"><img alt="Images" class="q-img" src="images/f0313-01.jpg" style="max-width: 100%; height: auto; margin: 10px 0; display: block;"/></p></div>', '["Submit analytical derivation in workspace."]'::jsonb, '<p class="ques"><strong><a href="ch19q.xhtml#ch19q_1" id="rch19q_1">1.</a></strong> (15 points)</p><p class="alphabrt">(a) The exponent must be unitless, so units of $b$ must cancel the units of time.</p><p class="hangp"><strong>1 pt</strong> for the correct answer, 1/s or s<sup>−1</sup></p><p class="alphabrt">(b) Initial velocity is when $t$ = 0. And $e^{0}$ is 1. So the initial velocity is 1.4 m/s.</p><p class="hangp"><strong>1 pt</strong> for the correct answer</p><p class="alphabrt">(c) Positive—when $t$ = 0, the $e$<sup>−</sup><em>bt</em> term becomes 1, and the remaining expression is positive.</p><p class="hangp"><strong>1 pt</strong> for the correct answer</p><p class="hangp"><strong>1 pt</strong> for justification</p><p class="alphabrt">(d) As $t$ increases, the $e$<sup>−</sup><em>bt</em> term gets smaller. So the whole expression gets smaller, and the speed decreases.</p><p class="hangp"><strong>1 pt</strong> for the correct answer</p><p class="hangp"><strong>1 pt</strong> for justification</p><p class="alphabrt">(e) The acceleration is the time derivative of the velocity. For this function, that’s −1.4<em>be</em><sup>−</sup><em>bt</em>. Again, the $e$<sup>−</sup><em>bt</em> term gets smaller as time goes on. That makes the absolute value of the whole expression get smaller. The magnitude of the acceleration decreases.</p><p class="hangp"><strong>1 pt</strong> for the correct answer</p><p class="hangp"><strong>1 pt</strong> for justification</p><p class="alphabrt">(f)</p><p class="hangp"><strong>1 pt</strong> for a graph never above the horizontal axis</p><p class="hangp"><strong>1 pt</strong> for an exponential graph decreasing in magnitude, even if in the wrong quadrant</p><p class="hangp"><strong>1 pt</strong> for labeling the $y$-intercept as “2.8$b$,” whether positive or negative—the net force is <em>ma</em>, and $m$ is 2 kg, so the intercept is (2)(1.4$b$) = 2.8$b$.</p><p class="image"><img alt="Images" class="q-img" src="images/f0333-01.jpg" style="max-width: 100%; height: auto; margin: 10px 0; display: block;"/></p><p class="alphabrt">(g)</p><p class="hangp"><strong>1 pt</strong> for setting up an integral of velocity with respect to time <em>with correct limits of 0 to infinity</em></p><p class="hangp"><strong>1 pt</strong> for the correct answer of (positive) 1.4/$b$</p><p class="image"><img alt="Images" class="q-img" src="images/f0333-02.jpg" style="max-width: 100%; height: auto; margin: 10px 0; display: block;"/></p><p class="alphabrt">(h) The total energy dissipated is the change in kinetic energy. The initial kinetic energy is ½<em>mv</em><sup>2</sup>, where $m$ is 2 kg and $v$ is 1.4$b$. The final kinetic energy is zero. Total energy dissipated is ½(2)(1.4$b$)<sup>2</sup> = (1.4$b$)<sup>2</sup> or 2.0$b^{2}$.</p><p class="hangp"><strong>1 pt</strong> for attempting to calculate a change in kinetic energy</p><p class="hangp"><strong>1 pt</strong> for the the correct answer</p>'),
  ('ap-mock-physc-mech-3_q37', 'ap-mock-physc-mech-3', 37, true, 'FRQ_MANUAL_EVALUATION', 'AP Physics C: Mechanics Free Response', 'hard', '<div class=''frq-prompt''><h3>CM 2</h3><p class="ques"><strong><a href="ch19u.xhtml#rch19q_2" id="ch19q_2">2.</a></strong> Satellites A and B are in circular orbit around a planet of mass $M$ and radius $R$. Satellite A has mass $m_{A}$ and orbital radius 2$R$. Satellite B has mass $m_{B}$ and orbital radius 4$R$.</p><p class="alphabrt">(a) Derive an expression for the orbital speed $v_{A}$ of satellite A in terms of given variables and fundamental constants.</p><p class="alphabr">(b) The orbital speed of satellite B is $v_{B}$. Can the ratio $v_{B}$:$v_{A}$ be calculated? If so, calculate this ratio. If not, explain what additional information would be needed and why.</p><p class="alphabr">(c) Derive an expression for the work necessary to move satellite A into satellite B’s orbit.</p><p class="alphabr">(d) Let the period of satellite A’s orbit be $T_{A}$ and the period of satellite B’s orbit be $T_{B}$. Calculate the ratio $T_{B}$/$T_{A}$.</p><p class="alphabr">(e) Now assume each satellite has the same mass $m_{A}$. In that case, which satellite has a larger total mechanical energy in its orbit? Justify your answer.</p><p class="alphabrb">(f) Derive an expression for the minimum speed that satellite A must have at the location shown in order to end up a very long way away from the planet.</p><p class="image"><img alt="Images" class="q-img" src="images/f0314-01.jpg" style="max-width: 100%; height: auto; margin: 10px 0; display: block;"/></p></div>', '["Submit analytical derivation in workspace."]'::jsonb, '<p class="ques"><strong><a href="ch19q.xhtml#ch19q_2" id="rch19q_2">2.</a></strong> (15 points)</p><p class="alphabrt">(a)</p><p class="hangp"><strong>1 pt</strong> for using a gravitational force expression in Newton’s second law</p><p class="hangp"><strong>1 pt</strong> for setting acceleration equal to $v^{2}$/$r$</p><p class="hangp"><strong>1 pt</strong> for the correct expression for $v$</p><p class="image"><img alt="Images" class="q-img" src="images/f0334-01.jpg" style="max-width: 100%; height: auto; margin: 10px 0; display: block;"/></p><p class="alphabrt">(b)</p><p class="hangp"><strong>1 pt</strong> for recognizing that the ratio can be calculated</p><p class="hangp"><strong>1 pt</strong> for using any appropriate method to get the correct answer, such as recognizing that the orbital radius is under a square root in the denominator of the speed expression and all other quantities are unchanging, so doubling the radius multiplies the speed by <img alt="Images" class="q-img" src="images/f0334-02.jpg" style="max-width: 100%; height: auto; margin: 10px 0; display: block;"/>.</p><p class="alphabrt">(c)</p><p class="hangp"><strong>1 pt</strong> for attempting to subtract the potential energies of satellite A at each orbital radius</p><p class="hangp"><strong>1 pt</strong> for the correct answer</p><p class="image"><img alt="Images" class="q-img" src="images/f0334-03.jpg" style="max-width: 100%; height: auto; margin: 10px 0; display: block;"/></p><p class="alphabrt">(d) Kepler’s third law says period is proportional to the 3/2 power of orbital radius. Since satellite B has twice the orbital radius, its period is 2<sup>3/2</sup> times larger. The ratio can be expressed as 2<sup>3/2</sup>, <img alt="Images" class="q-img" src="images/root8.jpg" style="max-width: 100%; height: auto; margin: 10px 0; display: block;"/>, <img alt="Images" class="q-img" src="images/2root2.jpg" style="max-width: 100%; height: auto; margin: 10px 0; display: block;"/>, 2.8, or any equivalent.</p><p class="hangp"><strong>1 pt</strong> for citing Kepler’s third law (or alternatively, expanding on the derivation in (a) by writing $v$ as orbital circumference divided by period)</p><p class="hangp"><strong>1 pt</strong> for the correct answer</p><p>(e)</p><p class="hangp"><strong>1 pt</strong> for summing kinetic and potential energies to get total mechanical energy (or using the fact that in an orbit, the total mechanical energy is half the potential energy)</p><p class="hangp"><strong>1 pt</strong> for an expression for gravitational potential energy, including the negative sign: $U$ = −<em>GMm</em>/$r$</p><p class="hangp"><strong>1 pt</strong> for the correct comparison—with a larger orbital radius, the mechanical energy for satellite B is less negative, and thus greater</p><p class="alphabrt">(f)</p><p class="hangp"><strong>1 pt</strong> for setting the total mechanical energy ($K +U$) to zero (because gravitational potential energy is defined as zero a long way away from the central planet, so the minimum mechanical energy a long way away will be zero)</p><p class="hangp"><strong>1 pt</strong> for the correct expressions for kinetic and potential energies at the current location.</p><p class="hangp"><strong>1 pt</strong> for the correct answer</p><p class="image"><img alt="Images" class="q-img" src="images/f0334-04.jpg" style="max-width: 100%; height: auto; margin: 10px 0; display: block;"/></p>'),
  ('ap-mock-physc-mech-3_q38', 'ap-mock-physc-mech-3', 38, true, 'FRQ_MANUAL_EVALUATION', 'AP Physics C: Mechanics Free Response', 'hard', '<div class=''frq-prompt''><h3>CM 3</h3><p class="ques"><strong><a href="ch19u.xhtml#rch19q_3" id="ch19q_3">3.</a></strong> In the laboratory, students roll a ball from rest without slipping down a surface inclined at an unknown angle $\theta$ to the horizontal. They measure the time $t$ for the ball to travel a distance $d$ measured along the incline. The ball has rotational inertia given by (2/3)<em>MR</em><sup>2</sup>, where $M$ and $R$ are the mass and radius of the ball, respectively.</p><p class="alphabrt">(A) Show that the speed $v$ of the ball’s center of mass after the distance $d$ is given by <img alt="Images" class="q-img" src="images/f0314-02.jpg" style="max-width: 100%; height: auto; margin: 10px 0; display: block;"/>.</p><p class="alphabr">(b) Show that $d$ is related to $t$ according to the equation <img alt="Images" class="q-img" src="images/f0314-03.jpg" style="max-width: 100%; height: auto; margin: 10px 0; display: block;"/>.</p><p class="alphabr">The students collect the data below.</p><p class="image"><img alt="Images" class="q-img" src="images/f0314-04.jpg" style="max-width: 100%; height: auto; margin: 10px 0; display: block;"/></p><p class="alphabr">(c) Indicate below which quantities could be graphed to yield a straight line whose slope could be used to calculate the angle $\theta$.</p><p class="alphabr-p">Vertical axis: _____</p><p class="alphabr-p">Horizontal axis: _____</p><p class="noindentt">Use the remaining rows in the table provided, as needed, to record any quantities you indicated that are not given in the table. Label each row you use and include units.</p><p class="alphabr">(d) Plot the data points for the quantities indicated in part (c) on the graph below. Clearly scale and label all axes, including units if appropriate. Draw a straight line that best represents the data.</p><p class="image"><img alt="Images" class="q-img" src="images/f0315-01.jpg" style="max-width: 100%; height: auto; margin: 10px 0; display: block;"/></p><p class="alphabr">(e) Using the straight line, calculate an experimental value for $\theta$.</p><p class="center1"><strong>STOP. End of Physics C—Mechanics Practice Exam 3—Free-Response Questions</strong></p></div>', '["Submit analytical derivation in workspace."]'::jsonb, '<p class="ques"><strong><a href="ch19q.xhtml#ch19q_3" id="rch19q_3">3.</a></strong> (15 points)</p><p class="alphabrt">(a)</p><p class="hangp"><strong>1 pt</strong> for an energy expression that includes rotational and linear kinetic energies</p><p class="hangp"><strong>1 pt</strong> for using correct expressions for gravitational, rotational kinetic, and linear kinetic energies</p><p class="hangp"><strong>1 pt</strong> for the relationship between $h$ and $d$, $h = d$ sin$\theta$. 1 for using the relation $\omega$ = <em>v/R</em> to cancel the $R$ term</p><p class="image"><img alt="Images" class="q-img" src="images/f0335-01.jpg" style="max-width: 100%; height: auto; margin: 10px 0; display: block;"/></p><p class="alphabrt">(b)</p><p class="hangp"><strong>1 pt</strong> for using a kinematic equation relating distance and time</p><p class="hangp"><strong>1 pt</strong> for using a kinematic equation to relate acceleration to the speed from part (a)</p><p class="hangpt">with zero initial speed, <em>$ν^{2}$</em> = 2<em>ad</em></p><p class="image"><img alt="Images" class="q-img" src="images/f0335-02.jpg" style="max-width: 100%; height: auto; margin: 10px 0; display: block;"/></p><p class="alphabrt">(c)</p><p class="hangp"><strong>1 pt</strong> For any combination that would create a straight line graph. I’ve used $d$ and <em>$t^{2}$</em>. It’s also legit to use $t$ and <img alt="Images" class="q-img" src="images/rootd.jpg" style="max-width: 100%; height: auto; margin: 10px 0; display: block;"/>. Or graphing $d$ and (3/10)<em>gt</em><sup>2</sup> would give a slope that straight-up represents sin $\theta$.</p><p class="alphabrt">(d) If the chosen axes don’t create a linear graph, only the first two of these points can be awarded.</p><p class="hangp"><strong>1 pt</strong> for data plotted correctly</p><p class="hangp"><strong>1 pt</strong> for linear scales on each axis that allow the data to take up about half of the grid area</p><p class="hangp"><strong>1 pt</strong> for correct units on both axes</p><p class="hangp"><strong>1 pt</strong> for a reasonable best-fit line</p><p class="image"><img alt="Images" class="q-img" src="images/f0336-01.jpg" style="max-width: 100%; height: auto; margin: 10px 0; display: block;"/></p><p class="alphabrt">(e)</p><p class="hangp"><strong>1 pt</strong> for correctly calculating the slope of the graph, using points on the line, not data points</p><p class="hangp"><strong>1 pt</strong> for relating the slope to its physical meaning—in the example, the slope is shown to be equal to (3/10)$g$ sin $\theta$</p><p class="hangp"><strong>1 pt</strong> for using the physical meaning of the slope in an attempt to determine $\theta$</p><p class="hangp"><strong>1 pt</strong> for a value of $\theta$ between 4 and 8 degrees</p><p class="image"><img alt="Images" class="q-img" src="images/f0336-02.jpg" style="max-width: 100%; height: auto; margin: 10px 0; display: block;"/></p><h4 class="h4c">AP Physics C—Mechanics</h4><p class="centera"><strong>Full Exam Scoring</strong></p><p class="noindentt1">Multiple-Choice: Number Correct______(35 max)</p><p class="noindentt1">Free-Response: Question 1______(15 max)</p><p class="noindentq">Question 2______(15 max)</p><p class="noindentq">Question 3______(15 max)</p><p class="noindentt1">Total Free-Response______(45 max)</p><p class="noindentt1">1.286 × Multiple-Choice + Free-Response = Raw Score______(90 max)</p><p class="image"><img alt="Images" class="q-img" src="images/f0337-01.jpg" style="max-width: 100%; height: auto; margin: 10px 0; display: block;"/></p>'),
  ('ap-mock-physc-mech-3_q39', 'ap-mock-physc-mech-3', 39, false, 'B', 'Practice Test 1 – Mechanics', 'medium', 'A constant horizontal force F applied to a block on an incline causes the block to move a distance d along the incline, as shown. If the coefficient of friction between the block and the incline is \muk, what is the work done by the applied force? (Do not assume that the block moves at constant velocity.)', '["W = Fd sin \\theta","W = Fd cos \\theta","W = mgd sin \\theta","W = mgd sin \\theta − \\muk mgd cos \\theta","W = mgd sin \\theta + \\mukd(mg cos \\theta + F sin \\theta)"]'::jsonb, '(B) Because the force is constant, the work is given by the dot product of the force and the displacement vectors: Choice (C) is the change in potential energy as the block is elevated. Choice (E) is the work that would have to be done by F to move the block up the incline at a constant speed; that is, the work done by F would be the negative of the work done by gravity and by friction (because the net work would have to be zero).'),
  ('ap-mock-physc-mech-3_q40', 'ap-mock-physc-mech-3', 40, false, 'E', 'Practice Test 1 – Mechanics', 'medium', 'The restoring force that acts on a mass is shown. For small amplitudes, the mass moves in simple harmonic motion with a period T. Which of the following is true of oscillations with larger amplitudes?', '["The motion is SHM with a period of T.","The motion is SHM with a period smaller than T.","The motion is SHM with a period larger than T.","The motion is no longer SHM, and its period is smaller than T.","The motion is no longer SHM, and its period is larger than T."]'::jsonb, '(E) See the figure. For large-amplitude oscillations, the restoring force is not linear, so the motion is not simple harmonic motion. Because the restoring force is smaller than its linear approximation about the origin (which would produce a period of T), it produces motion with a larger period. (Because the force is smaller, the particle oscillates more slowly and more time is required for each cycle.)'),
  ('ap-mock-physc-mech-3_q41', 'ap-mock-physc-mech-3', 41, false, 'C', 'Practice Test 1 – Mechanics', 'medium', 'A simple pendulum of length ℓ is moved to Mars (where g = 3.71 m/s2). Its period on Earth is 2.0 s. Its period on Mars is', '["0.02 s","2.0 s","3.25 s","600 s","6,000 s"]'::jsonb, '(C) The period of a simple pendulum is given by . If the quantities on Mars are denoted by primes, then the period on Mars is given by , where the unprimed values are those on Earth (note that the length is unchanged). Thus, s.'),
  ('ap-mock-physc-mech-3_q42', 'ap-mock-physc-mech-3', 42, false, 'B', 'Practice Test 1 – Mechanics', 'medium', 'At the instant that a mass in simple harmonic motion passes through its equilibrium position its acceleration is', '["maximum","zero","oppositely directed to its velocity","parallel to its velocity","cannot be determined without additional information"]'::jsonb, '(B) A simple harmonic oscillator is characterized by a = −\omega2x (found by plugging Hooke’s law into Newtons’ second law). At equilibrium, x = 0 and thus the acceleration is also zero.'),
  ('ap-mock-physc-mech-3_q43', 'ap-mock-physc-mech-3', 43, false, 'B', 'Practice Test 1 – Mechanics', 'medium', 'A solid sphere has a rotational inertia of when rotated about an axis tangent to its surface as shown. What is the rotational inertia of the sphere when it is rotated about a line passing through the center of the sphere?', '["Directly proportional to the parameter (linear scaling)","Inversely proportional to the square of the separation","Equal in magnitude with opposite orientation","Independent of the mass and charge distribution","Zero net change under static equilibrium conditions"]'::jsonb, '(B) According to the parallel axis theorem, the rotational inertia about an axis that does not pass through the center of mass (in this problem, the one that passes along the surface of the sphere) is equal to the rotational inertia about a parallel axis passing through the center of mass (in this problem, the axis passing through the sphere’s center) plus MD2, where D is the distance between the two axes (in this case, r): Solving this equation for the rotational inertia about the axis that passes through the center of mass yields choice (B).'),
  ('ap-mock-physc-mech-3_q44', 'ap-mock-physc-mech-3', 44, false, 'C', 'Practice Test 1 – Mechanics', 'medium', '<p>A simple pendulum of length L is released from rest with its string horizontal (Point I). It swings downward through a circular arc. At Point II (when the string makes a 45° angle with the vertical), what is the direction of the pendulum bob’s net acceleration vector?</p>', '["Purely radial toward the pivot center","Purely tangential in the direction of velocity","Inward and downward, angled between the radial and tangential directions","Directly downward toward the center of the Earth","Directly upward opposite gravity"]'::jsonb, '(C) Consider the figure. The pendulum is rotating in a circle, so it must experience a centripetal acceleration toward the center of the circle. The pendulum is also speeding up (because of conservation of energy, with gravitational potential energy being converted to kinetic energy), so it experiences a tangential acceleration parallel to its velocity. The sum of these accelerations produces a net acceleration that is neither purely radial nor purely tangential but rather points in the direction of choice (C).'),
  ('ap-mock-physc-mech-3_q45', 'ap-mock-physc-mech-3', 45, false, 'B', 'Practice Test 1 – Mechanics', 'medium', 'A mass m moving to the right with speed v collides and sticks to a mass of 3m moving upward with a speed 2v as shown. What is the final velocity of the two masses?', '["Directly proportional to the parameter (linear scaling)","Inversely proportional to the square of the separation","Equal in magnitude with opposite orientation","(E) none of the above","Zero net change under static equilibrium conditions"]'::jsonb, '(B) This is an inelastic collision such that momentum is conserved and the final velocity of both masses is the same (because they stick together and move with a common velocity). Conservation of momentum yields Solving these equations yields choice (B).'),
  ('ap-mock-physc-em-3_q1', 'ap-mock-physc-em-3', 1, false, 'A', 'AP Physics C: Electricity & Magnetism', 'medium', '<div class="passage-box" style="background: rgba(255,255,255,0.03); padding: 12px 16px; border-radius: 8px; margin-bottom: 14px; border-left: 3px solid #7c3aed;"><p class="image"><img alt="Images" class="q-img" src="images/t0317-01.jpg" style="max-width: 100%; height: auto; margin: 10px 0; display: block;"/></p><p class="noindent"><strong>Time:</strong> 45 minutes. You may refer to the constants sheet and the equation sheet, both of which are found in the appendix. You may use a calculator.</p><p class="image"><img alt="Images" class="q-img" src="images/f0319-01.jpg" style="max-width: 100%; height: auto; margin: 10px 0; display: block;"/></p><p class="noindent">Questions 1 and 2: The circuit above consists of three capacitors and a battery, which has been connected for a long time.</p></div><p class="ques"><strong><a href="ch19v.xhtml#rch19r_1" id="ch19r_1">1.</a></strong> What is the charge stored on the 6 nF capacitor?</p>', '["<p>96 nC</p>","<p>48 nC</p>","<p>432 nC</p>","<p>4 nC</p>","<p>144 nC</p>"]'::jsonb, '<p class="ques"><strong><a href="ch19r.xhtml#ch19r_1" id="rch19r_1">1.</a></strong> <strong>A—</strong>First, reduce the circuit to its simplest form. The parallel capacitors add algebraically to get an equivalent capacitance of 12 nF. Then the series capacitors add inversely by 1/$C_{eq}$ = 1/$C_{1}$ + 1/$C_{2}$. Add 1/12 and 1/6 to get 1/4 . . . that means the equivalent capacitance of the circuit is 4 nF. Now by $Q$ = <em>CV</em>, the whole circuit stores (4 nF) × (24 V) = 96 nC. Series capacitors each take the same charge as each other; that is ALSO equal to the total charge across the combination. So the 6 nF capacitor takes all 96 nC.</p>'),
  ('ap-mock-physc-em-3_q2', 'ap-mock-physc-em-3', 2, false, 'D', 'AP Physics C: Electricity & Magnetism', 'medium', '<div class="passage-box" style="background: rgba(255,255,255,0.03); padding: 12px 16px; border-radius: 8px; margin-bottom: 14px; border-left: 3px solid #7c3aed;"><p class="image"><img alt="Images" class="q-img" src="images/t0317-01.jpg" style="max-width: 100%; height: auto; margin: 10px 0; display: block;"/></p><p class="noindent"><strong>Time:</strong> 45 minutes. You may refer to the constants sheet and the equation sheet, both of which are found in the appendix. You may use a calculator.</p><p class="image"><img alt="Images" class="q-img" src="images/f0319-01.jpg" style="max-width: 100%; height: auto; margin: 10px 0; display: block;"/></p><p class="noindent">Questions 1 and 2: The circuit above consists of three capacitors and a battery, which has been connected for a long time.</p></div><p class="ques"><strong><a href="ch19v.xhtml#rch19r_2" id="ch19r_2">2.</a></strong> What is the voltage across the 8 nF capacitor?</p>', '["<p>2 V</p>","<p>12 V</p>","<p>16 V</p>","<p>8 V</p>","<p>3 V</p>"]'::jsonb, '<p class="ques"><strong><a href="ch19r.xhtml#ch19r_2" id="rch19r_2">2.</a></strong> <strong>D—</strong>The parallel capacitors take the same voltage as each other. Their equivalent capacitance is 12 nF. Both the voltage across this 12 nF equivalent capacitance and the voltage across the 6 nF capacitor must add to 24 V by Kirchhoff’s loop rule. And series capacitors take the same charge in $Q$ = <em>CV</em>. Solving for voltage, the voltage across each capacitor is <em>Q/C</em>, where $Q$ is the same for each. Even if you got the total charge wrong in question 1, you’ll still see that the bigger capacitor takes the smaller voltage, and by a factor of 2 here since 12 nF is twice 6 nF. The 12 nF equivalent capacitor takes 8 V across it (96 nC/12 nF). This means both of the parallel capacitors also take 8 V.</p>'),
  ('ap-mock-physc-em-3_q3', 'ap-mock-physc-em-3', 3, false, 'C', 'AP Physics C: Electricity & Magnetism', 'medium', '<div class="passage-box" style="background: rgba(255,255,255,0.03); padding: 12px 16px; border-radius: 8px; margin-bottom: 14px; border-left: 3px solid #7c3aed;"><p class="image"><img alt="Images" class="q-img" src="images/f0319-02.jpg" style="max-width: 100%; height: auto; margin: 10px 0; display: block;"/></p><p class="noindent">Questions 3 and 4: Two long, straight wires are separated by a distance $d$. The left-hand wire carries a current $I$ directed into the page; the right-hand wire carries a current $I$ directed out of the page. Points R and P are positions in space, located as shown in the diagram.</p></div><p class="ques"><strong><a href="ch19v.xhtml#rch19r_3" id="ch19r_3">3.</a></strong> What is the magnitude of the net magnetic field produced at point P?</p>', '["<p><img alt=\"Images\" class=\"q-img\" src=\"images/f0319-03.jpg\" style=\"max-width: 100%; height: auto; margin: 10px 0; display: block;\"/></p>","<p><img alt=\"Images\" class=\"q-img\" src=\"images/f0319-04.jpg\" style=\"max-width: 100%; height: auto; margin: 10px 0; display: block;\"/></p>","<p><img alt=\"Images\" class=\"q-img\" src=\"images/f0319-05.jpg\" style=\"max-width: 100%; height: auto; margin: 10px 0; display: block;\"/></p>","<p><img alt=\"Images\" class=\"q-img\" src=\"images/f0319-06.jpg\" style=\"max-width: 100%; height: auto; margin: 10px 0; display: block;\"/></p>","<p><img alt=\"Images\" class=\"q-img\" src=\"images/f0319-07.jpg\" style=\"max-width: 100%; height: auto; margin: 10px 0; display: block;\"/></p>"]'::jsonb, '<p class="ques"><strong><a href="ch19r.xhtml#ch19r_3" id="rch19r_3">3.</a></strong> <strong>C—</strong>The magnetic field due to a long straight wire is $μ_{0}$<em>I/</em>2<em>πr,</em> where $r$ is the distance from the wire to the point being considered. So for the right-hand wire, $r = d$, and for the left-hand wire, $r$ = 2$d$. The direction of the magnetic field is given by the second right-hand rule: point the thumb with the current, curl the fingers to show the field direction. That’s down the page due to the right-hand wire, up the page due to the left-hand wire. So the magnitudes of the two magnetic fields subtract to the total: $μ_{0}$<em>I/</em>2<em>πd</em> − $μ_{0}$<em>I/</em>2$\pi$(2$d$) gives choice C.</p>'),
  ('ap-mock-physc-em-3_q4', 'ap-mock-physc-em-3', 4, false, 'B', 'AP Physics C: Electricity & Magnetism', 'medium', '<div class="passage-box" style="background: rgba(255,255,255,0.03); padding: 12px 16px; border-radius: 8px; margin-bottom: 14px; border-left: 3px solid #7c3aed;"><p class="image"><img alt="Images" class="q-img" src="images/f0319-02.jpg" style="max-width: 100%; height: auto; margin: 10px 0; display: block;"/></p><p class="noindent">Questions 3 and 4: Two long, straight wires are separated by a distance $d$. The left-hand wire carries a current $I$ directed into the page; the right-hand wire carries a current $I$ directed out of the page. Points R and P are positions in space, located as shown in the diagram.</p></div><p class="ques"><strong><a href="ch19v.xhtml#rch19r_4" id="ch19r_4">4.</a></strong> What is the direction of the net magnetic field produced at point R?</p>', '["<p><img alt=\"Images\" class=\"q-img\" src=\"images/larrow.jpg\" style=\"max-width: 100%; height: auto; margin: 10px 0; display: block;\"/></p>","<p>Zero</p>","<p><img alt=\"Images\" class=\"q-img\" src=\"images/2arrow.jpg\" style=\"max-width: 100%; height: auto; margin: 10px 0; display: block;\"/></p>","<p><img alt=\"Images\" class=\"q-img\" src=\"images/rarrow.jpg\" style=\"max-width: 100%; height: auto; margin: 10px 0; display: block;\"/></p>","<p><img alt=\"Images\" class=\"q-img\" src=\"images/4arrow.jpg\" style=\"max-width: 100%; height: auto; margin: 10px 0; display: block;\"/></p>"]'::jsonb, '<p class="ques"><strong><a href="ch19r.xhtml#ch19r_4" id="rch19r_4">4.</a></strong> <strong>B—</strong>Use the second right-hand rule: point the thumb with the current, and the fingers show the magnetic field direction. The left-hand wire produces a field down and to the right. The right-hand wire produces a field up and to the left. These fields are of equal magnitude, because the currents are the same and the distances from the wires are the same. So the magnetic fields subtract to a net of zero.</p>')
ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.ap_exam_questions (id, test_id, question_order, is_frq, answer_key, topic, difficulty, question_text, options_json, explanation)
VALUES
  ('ap-mock-physc-em-3_q5', 'ap-mock-physc-em-3', 5, false, 'C', 'AP Physics C: Electricity & Magnetism', 'medium', '<div class="passage-box" style="background: rgba(255,255,255,0.03); padding: 12px 16px; border-radius: 8px; margin-bottom: 14px; border-left: 3px solid #7c3aed;"><p class="image"><img alt="Images" class="q-img" src="images/f0320-01.jpg" style="max-width: 100%; height: auto; margin: 10px 0; display: block;"/></p><p class="noindent">Questions 5 and 6: A proton initially moving right with speed $v$ enters a region in which the magnetic field is uniform. The proton is deflected along the path shown, exiting the magnetic field at position X. Next, an electron initially moving right with identical speed $v$ enters the magnetic field from the same position.</p></div><p class="ques"><strong><a href="ch19v.xhtml#rch19r_5" id="ch19r_5">5.</a></strong> Which of the labeled positions is closest to where the electron will exit the magnetic field?</p>', '["<p>A</p>","<p>B</p>","<p>C</p>","<p>D</p>","<p>X</p>"]'::jsonb, '<p class="ques"><strong><a href="ch19r.xhtml#ch19r_5" id="rch19r_5">5.</a></strong> <strong>C—</strong>Apply net$F$ = <em>ma</em> in circular motion. Here the net force on the object is <em>qvB</em>, and the acceleration in a circular path is $v^{2}$/$r$. Solve for $r$ to get $r$ = <em>mv/qB</em>. The electron and proton experience the same magnetic field $B$, the same speed $v$, and have the same amount of charge $q$. The difference is, the mass of a proton is something like 2000 times that of the electron. So the radius of the proton’s circular motion will be 2000 times bigger. The only position close to showing an orbital radius 2000 times smaller for the electron is C.</p>'),
  ('ap-mock-physc-em-3_q6', 'ap-mock-physc-em-3', 6, false, 'B', 'AP Physics C: Electricity & Magnetism', 'medium', '<div class="passage-box" style="background: rgba(255,255,255,0.03); padding: 12px 16px; border-radius: 8px; margin-bottom: 14px; border-left: 3px solid #7c3aed;"><p class="image"><img alt="Images" class="q-img" src="images/f0320-01.jpg" style="max-width: 100%; height: auto; margin: 10px 0; display: block;"/></p><p class="noindent">Questions 5 and 6: A proton initially moving right with speed $v$ enters a region in which the magnetic field is uniform. The proton is deflected along the path shown, exiting the magnetic field at position X. Next, an electron initially moving right with identical speed $v$ enters the magnetic field from the same position.</p></div><p class="ques"><strong><a href="ch19v.xhtml#rch19r_6" id="ch19r_6">6.</a></strong> The speed of the proton a very short time $t$ after entering the magnetic field is $v_{p}$; the speed of the electron the same time $t$ after entering the magnetic field is $v_{e}$. Which of the following correctly compares $v_{p}$ and $v_{e}$?</p>', '["<p>$v_{p}$ is smaller than $v_{e}$ by more than a factor of 10</p>","<p>$v_{p}$ is equal to $v_{e}$</p>","<p>$v_{p}$ is larger than $v_{e}$ by less than a factor of 10</p>","<p>$v_{p}$ is larger than $v_{e}$ by more than a factor of 10</p>","<p>$v_{p}$ is smaller than $v_{e}$ by less than a factor of 10</p>"]'::jsonb, '<p class="ques"><strong><a href="ch19r.xhtml#ch19r_6" id="rch19r_6">6.</a></strong> <strong>B—</strong>The direction of a magnetic force is always perpendicular to the velocity vector. This means that the magnetic force causes circular motion, but not a change in speed, for an object in the field. The magnetic field won’t change the speed of “any” particle.</p>'),
  ('ap-mock-physc-em-3_q7', 'ap-mock-physc-em-3', 7, false, 'B', 'AP Physics C: Electricity & Magnetism', 'medium', '<div class="passage-box" style="background: rgba(255,255,255,0.03); padding: 12px 16px; border-radius: 8px; margin-bottom: 14px; border-left: 3px solid #7c3aed;"><p class="image"><img alt="Images" class="q-img" src="images/f0320-02.jpg" style="max-width: 100%; height: auto; margin: 10px 0; display: block;"/></p><p class="noindent">Questions 7 and 8: A capacitor $C_{1}$ is initially connected to a 12 V battery for a long time, as in diagram 1. Next, in diagram 2 the battery is disconnected, and in diagram 3 $C_{1}$ is connected instead to capacitor $C_{2}$, which has an initial charge.</p></div><p class="ques"><strong><a href="ch19v.xhtml#rch19r_7" id="ch19r_7">7.</a></strong> Which of the following must be identical quantities in diagram 3?</p><p class="roman1t">I. The charge on each capacitor</p><p class="roman2">II. The voltage across each capacitor</p><p class="roman3">III. The capacitance of each capacitor</p>', '["<p>I only</p>","<p>II only</p>","<p>III only</p>","<p>I and II only</p>","<p>I, II, and III</p>"]'::jsonb, '<p class="ques"><strong><a href="ch19r.xhtml#ch19r_7" id="rch19r_7">7.</a></strong> <strong>B—</strong>Kirchoff’s loop rule indicates that since this a closed circuit loop, the voltage across each must be the same. Capacitance is a property of a capacitor, so the capacitances can be different regardless of what circuit they’re hooked up to. Then by $Q$ = <em>CV</em> the charge can be different if the capacitances are different, even with the same voltages.</p>'),
  ('ap-mock-physc-em-3_q8', 'ap-mock-physc-em-3', 8, false, 'C', 'AP Physics C: Electricity & Magnetism', 'medium', '<div class="passage-box" style="background: rgba(255,255,255,0.03); padding: 12px 16px; border-radius: 8px; margin-bottom: 14px; border-left: 3px solid #7c3aed;"><p class="image"><img alt="Images" class="q-img" src="images/f0320-02.jpg" style="max-width: 100%; height: auto; margin: 10px 0; display: block;"/></p><p class="noindent">Questions 7 and 8: A capacitor $C_{1}$ is initially connected to a 12 V battery for a long time, as in diagram 1. Next, in diagram 2 the battery is disconnected, and in diagram 3 $C_{1}$ is connected instead to capacitor $C_{2}$, which has an initial charge.</p></div><p class="ques"><strong><a href="ch19v.xhtml#rch19r_8" id="ch19r_8">8.</a></strong> Which of the following must be identical quantities for capacitor $C_{1}$ in circuits 1 and 3?</p><p class="roman1t">I. The voltage across $C_{1}$</p><p class="roman2">II. The charge on $C_{1}$</p><p class="roman3">III. The capacitance of $C_{1}$</p>', '["<p>I only</p>","<p>II only</p>","<p>III only</p>","<p>I and II only</p>","<p>I, II, and III</p>"]'::jsonb, '<p class="ques"><strong><a href="ch19r.xhtml#ch19r_8" id="rch19r_8">8.</a></strong> <strong>C—</strong>Capacitance is a property of a capacitor. It’s the same capacitor in both diagrams, so it has the same capacitance. Charge is free to flow between capacitors in diagram 3, so the charge on $C_{1}$ will change. And while the voltage across each capacitor in diagram 3 must be the same for each, that doesn’t have to be 12 V and probably will not be.</p>'),
  ('ap-mock-physc-em-3_q9', 'ap-mock-physc-em-3', 9, false, 'A', 'AP Physics C: Electricity & Magnetism', 'medium', '<div class="passage-box" style="background: rgba(255,255,255,0.03); padding: 12px 16px; border-radius: 8px; margin-bottom: 14px; border-left: 3px solid #7c3aed;"><p class="image"><img alt="Images" class="q-img" src="images/f0321-01.jpg" style="max-width: 100%; height: auto; margin: 10px 0; display: block;"/></p><p class="noindent">Questions 9 and 10 refer to the circuit above.</p></div><p class="ques"><strong><a href="ch19v.xhtml#rch19r_9" id="ch19r_9">9.</a></strong> What is the resistance of $R_{2}$?</p>', '["<p>33 Ω</p>","<p>3 Ω</p>","<p>150 Ω</p>","<p>100 Ω</p>","<p>20 Ω</p>"]'::jsonb, '<p class="ques"><strong><a href="ch19r.xhtml#ch19r_9" id="rch19r_9">9.</a></strong> <strong>A—</strong>The voltage across the middle branch of the circuit is <em>IR</em> = (2 A)(50 Ω) = 100 V. That’s the same as the voltage across $R_{2}$, because parallel resistors take the same voltage as each other. The current through $R_{2}$ is 3 A by Kirchoff’s junction rule (5 A enters the junction, 2 A goes down the middle, so the remaining 3 A goes through $R_{2}$). Set 100 V = (3 A)($R_{2}$) and solve for $R_{2}$ = 33 Ω.</p>'),
  ('ap-mock-physc-em-3_q10', 'ap-mock-physc-em-3', 10, false, 'A', 'AP Physics C: Electricity & Magnetism', 'medium', '<div class="passage-box" style="background: rgba(255,255,255,0.03); padding: 12px 16px; border-radius: 8px; margin-bottom: 14px; border-left: 3px solid #7c3aed;"><p class="image"><img alt="Images" class="q-img" src="images/f0321-01.jpg" style="max-width: 100%; height: auto; margin: 10px 0; display: block;"/></p><p class="noindent">Questions 9 and 10 refer to the circuit above.</p></div><p class="ques"><strong><a href="ch19v.xhtml#rch19r_10" id="ch19r_10">10.</a></strong> What is the voltage measured by the voltmeter?</p>', '["<p>200 V</p>","<p>20 V</p>","<p>60 V</p>","<p>100 V</p>","<p>250 V</p>"]'::jsonb, '<p class="ques"><strong><a href="ch19r.xhtml#ch19r_10" id="rch19r_10">10.</a></strong> <strong>A—</strong>Use Kirchoff’s loop rule. The battery provides 300 V. So the voltage drop across $R_{1}$ and the 50 Ω resistor must also be 300 V. The voltage across the 50 Ω resistor is (2 A)(50 Ω) = 100 V. So the remaining 200 V must be across $R_{1}$, which is what the voltmeter measures.</p>'),
  ('ap-mock-physc-em-3_q11', 'ap-mock-physc-em-3', 11, false, 'C', 'AP Physics C: Electricity & Magnetism', 'medium', '<p class="image"><img alt="Images" class="q-img" src="images/f0321-02.jpg" style="max-width: 100%; height: auto; margin: 10px 0; display: block;"/></p><p class="ques"><strong><a href="ch19v.xhtml#rch19r_11" id="ch19r_11">11.</a></strong> Four parallel-plate capacitors had different plate diameters $d$ and distances $x$ between the plates, as shown above. Which of the following ranks the capacitors according to their capacitance?</p>', '["<p>C = B &gt; A &gt; D</p>","<p>C &gt; A &gt; D &gt; B</p>","<p>C &gt; D &gt; A &gt; B</p>","<p>D &gt; C = B &gt; A</p>","<p>D &gt; C &gt; B &gt; A</p>"]'::jsonb, '<p class="ques"><strong><a href="ch19r.xhtml#ch19r_11" id="rch19r_11">11.</a></strong> <strong>C—</strong>The capacitance of a parallel plate capacitor is <em>ɛ</em><sub>0</sub>$A$/$d$, where $A$ is the plate area and $d$ is the distance between plates. Here that means the capacitance is proportional to $d^{2}$/$x$. Evaluating each capacitor according to $d^{2}$/$x$, capacitor C has largest capacitance of (2$d$ )<sup>2</sup><em>/x</em> = 4($d^{2}$/$x$). Capacitor D works out to 2($d^{2}$/$x$), capacitor A to 1($d^{2}$/$x$), and capacitor B to (1/2)($d^{2}$/$x$).</p>'),
  ('ap-mock-physc-em-3_q12', 'ap-mock-physc-em-3', 12, false, 'A', 'AP Physics C: Electricity & Magnetism', 'medium', '<p class="ques"><strong><a href="ch19v.xhtml#rch19r_12" id="ch19r_12">12.</a></strong> A parallel plate capacitor has capacitance $C$. Each plate of the capacitor holds a charge of magnitude $Q$. Which of the following expressions gives the energy stored in this capacitor?</p>', '["<p>½$Q^{2}$/$C$</p>","<p>$Q^{2}$/$C$</p>","<p><em>QC</em></p>","<p>½<em>QC</em><sup>2</sup></p>","<p>½<em>Q/C</em></p>"]'::jsonb, '<p class="ques"><strong><a href="ch19r.xhtml#ch19r_12" id="rch19r_12">12.</a></strong> <strong>A—</strong>The standard expression for energy stored on a capacitor is ½<em>CV</em><sup>2</sup>. But the fundamental equation for capacitors says that $Q$ = <em>CV</em>. So, substitute $V$ = <em>Q/C</em> into the standard expression. You’re left with ½$Q^{2}$/$C$.</p>'),
  ('ap-mock-physc-em-3_q13', 'ap-mock-physc-em-3', 13, false, 'A', 'AP Physics C: Electricity & Magnetism', 'medium', '<p class="image"><img alt="Images" class="q-img" src="images/f0322-01.jpg" style="max-width: 100%; height: auto; margin: 10px 0; display: block;"/></p><p class="ques"><strong><a href="ch19v.xhtml#rch19r_13" id="ch19r_13">13.</a></strong> In region 1, a uniform electric field of magnitude $E_{1}$ points right. In region 2, another uniform electric field of smaller magnitude $E_{2}$ points to the left, as shown above. A positive charge $+q$ is released from rest at the position indicated, at the far left of region 1. Which of the following graphs shows the speed $v$ of the charge as a function of time?</p>', '["<p><img alt=\"Images\" class=\"q-img\" src=\"images/f0322-02.jpg\" style=\"max-width: 100%; height: auto; margin: 10px 0; display: block;\"/></p>","<p><img alt=\"Images\" class=\"q-img\" src=\"images/f0322-03.jpg\" style=\"max-width: 100%; height: auto; margin: 10px 0; display: block;\"/></p>","<p><img alt=\"Images\" class=\"q-img\" src=\"images/f0322-04.jpg\" style=\"max-width: 100%; height: auto; margin: 10px 0; display: block;\"/></p>","<p><img alt=\"Images\" class=\"q-img\" src=\"images/f0322-05.jpg\" style=\"max-width: 100%; height: auto; margin: 10px 0; display: block;\"/></p>","<p><img alt=\"Images\" class=\"q-img\" src=\"images/f0322-06.jpg\" style=\"max-width: 100%; height: auto; margin: 10px 0; display: block;\"/></p>"]'::jsonb, '<p class="ques"><strong><a href="ch19r.xhtml#ch19r_13" id="rch19r_13">13.</a></strong> <strong>A—</strong>The force on the charge is <em>qE</em> and thus is uniform within each region. A uniform force produces a uniform acceleration, and thus a uniform slope on a velocity-time graph. The graph must be straight in each region. In region 1, $E$ is larger, so the force, the acceleration, and the slope of the velocity-time graph will be larger as well.</p>'),
  ('ap-mock-physc-em-3_q14', 'ap-mock-physc-em-3', 14, false, 'C', 'AP Physics C: Electricity & Magnetism', 'medium', '<p class="ques"><strong><a href="ch19v.xhtml#rch19r_14" id="ch19r_14">14.</a></strong> Consider a long, straight wire of circular cross section. The current through the wire has uniform current density across its cross-sectional area. Let the distance from the center of the wire along a cross-sectional slice be $r$. The magnetic field inside the wire is proportional to which of the following?</p>', '["<p>$r^{2}$</p>","<p>$r$<sup>1/2</sup></p>","<p>$r$</p>","<p>$r^{-}\\$2</p>","<p>$r^{-}\\$1</p>"]'::jsonb, '<p class="ques"><strong><a href="ch19r.xhtml#ch19r_14" id="rch19r_14">14.</a></strong> <strong>C—</strong>The symmetry is appropriate for Ampere’s law. Never evaluate the integral in Ampere’s law—it always simplifies to <em>BL</em> = $μ_{0} I_{enclosed}$. Here $L$ is the circumference of an amperian loop, 2<em>πr</em>. The current enclosed by this loop is a fraction of the total current. What fraction? A fraction equal to the area enclosed by the amperian loop over the total cross-sectional area of the wire. That’s <em>πr</em><sup>2</sup> over the total area of the wire. Point is, Ampere’s law looks like $B$(2<em>πr</em>) = $μ_{0}$(<em>πr</em><sup>2</sup>/total area)(total $I$ ). Solving for $B$, you’re left with an expression with $r$ by itself in the numerator. So the magnetic field is proportional to $r$. (You’ve memorized the equation $B = μ_{0} I$/2<em>πr</em>, but that’s for the magnetic field <em>outside</em> a long straight wire. This question is asking about $B$ inside the wire.)</p>'),
  ('ap-mock-physc-em-3_q15', 'ap-mock-physc-em-3', 15, false, 'B', 'AP Physics C: Electricity & Magnetism', 'medium', '<p class="image"><img alt="Images" class="q-img" src="images/f0322-07.jpg" style="max-width: 100%; height: auto; margin: 10px 0; display: block;"/></p><p class="ques"><strong><a href="ch19v.xhtml#rch19r_15" id="ch19r_15">15.</a></strong> The diagram above shows a cross section of a three-dimensional closed surface. While no electric charges are inside the surface, several point charges outside the surface produce the nonuniform electric field $E$ shown above. Consider the vector <em>dA</em> to have a magnitude equal to a small element of surface area, and direction pointed normal to the surface as shown. Which of the following is true about <img alt="Images" class="q-img" src="images/f0322-08.jpg" style="max-width: 100%; height: auto; margin: 10px 0; display: block;"/> evaluated for the entire surface?</p>', '["<p>It is positive, because the net charge of the objects producing this electric field must have been positive.</p>","<p>It is zero, because no charge is enclosed within the surface</p>","<p>It is negative, because the smaller electric field intersects more surface area than the larger electric field.</p>","<p>It is undefined, because the $E$ is not always parallel to <em>dA</em>.</p>","<p>It is positive, because the larger electric field intersects more surface area than the smaller electric field.</p>"]'::jsonb, '<p class="ques"><strong><a href="ch19r.xhtml#ch19r_15" id="rch19r_15">15.</a></strong> <strong>B—</strong>Gauss’s law says that for a closed surface, <img alt="Images" class="q-img" src="images/f0339-01.jpg" style="max-width: 100%; height: auto; margin: 10px 0; display: block;"/> is proportional to the charge enclosed by the surface. While the mathematics of Gauss’s law becomes difficult when the charge distribution and the surface are asymmetric, the law still holds. Since zero charge is enclosed, the integral becomes zero as well.</p>'),
  ('ap-mock-physc-em-3_q16', 'ap-mock-physc-em-3', 16, false, 'B', 'AP Physics C: Electricity & Magnetism', 'medium', '<p class="image"><img alt="Images" class="q-img" src="images/f0323-01.jpg" style="max-width: 100%; height: auto; margin: 10px 0; display: block;"/></p><p class="ques"><strong><a href="ch19v.xhtml#rch19r_16" id="ch19r_16">16.</a></strong> A uniform electric field of magnitude 400 N/C points to the right. Positions 1 and 2 are located in the electric field as shown. An object of charge +1.0 mC moves from position 1 to position 2. How much work is done on the object by the field?</p>', '["<p>−0.84 J</p>","<p>+0.84 J</p>","<p>+1.2 J</p>","<p>−1.2 J</p>","<p>0 J</p>"]'::jsonb, '<p class="ques"><strong><a href="ch19r.xhtml#ch19r_16" id="rch19r_16">16.</a></strong> <strong>B—</strong>The force on the object is uniform and equal to <em>qE</em> = (0.001 C)(400 N/C) = 0.40 N. The work done by a uniform force is equal to force times distance, where that distance must be parallel (or antiparallel) to the direction of the force. The electric force is to the right; the force on a positive charge is likewise to the right. So the necessary distance is horizontal only . . . that’s (3 m) (cos 45<sup>o</sup>) = 2.1 m. The work done is (0.40 N) × (2.1 m) = 0.84 J. That work is positive because the electric force is parallel, not antiparallel, to the component of displacement along the direction of force.</p>'),
  ('ap-mock-physc-em-3_q17', 'ap-mock-physc-em-3', 17, false, 'E', 'AP Physics C: Electricity & Magnetism', 'medium', '<p class="image"><img alt="Images" class="q-img" src="images/f0323-02.jpg" style="max-width: 100%; height: auto; margin: 10px 0; display: block;"/></p><p class="ques"><strong><a href="ch19v.xhtml#rch19r_17" id="ch19r_17">17.</a></strong> Two objects carry opposite charges of the same magnitude. Both objects move up the page with speed $v$, as shown above. Which of the following indicates the direction of the instantaneous electric and magnetic fields, if any, at point P directly between the objects?</p><p class="image"><img alt="Images" class="q-img" src="images/f0323-03.jpg" style="max-width: 100%; height: auto; margin: 10px 0; display: block;"/></p><p class="image"><img alt="Images" class="q-img" src="images/f0323-04.jpg" style="max-width: 100%; height: auto; margin: 10px 0; display: block;"/></p>', '["Electric Field: To the left; Magnetic Field: Out of the page","Electric Field: To the left; Magnetic Field: Into the page","Electric Field: Zero; Magnetic Field: Zero","Electric Field: To the right; Magnetic Field: Out of the page","Electric Field: To the right; Magnetic Field: Into the page"]'::jsonb, '<p class="ques"><strong><a href="ch19r.xhtml#ch19r_17" id="rch19r_17">17.</a></strong> <strong>E—</strong>For the magnetic field, think of the moving charges as current-carrying wires. Use the second right-hand rule. The left charge carries a current—a flow of positive charge—up the page. Point the thumb with the current and curl the fingers. The fingers point into the page at point P. Similarly, the right charge can be thought of as a flow of negative charge up the page, and so is equivalent to a flow of positive charge down the page. The magnetic field at point P due to the right charge is also into the page. So the net magnetic field is into the page. Then the electric field due to a charge points away from a positive, toward a negative charge. At point P that’s to the right due to the left-hand charge, and also to the right due to the right-hand charge, making the net electric field to the right.</p>'),
  ('ap-mock-physc-em-3_q18', 'ap-mock-physc-em-3', 18, false, 'E', 'AP Physics C: Electricity & Magnetism', 'medium', '<p class="ques"><strong><a href="ch19v.xhtml#rch19r_18" id="ch19r_18">18.</a></strong> Four isolated charge arrangements are shown above. Which of the following correctly ranks the magnitude of the electric force $F$ exerted by each charge on the other in its arrangement?</p>', '["<p>$F_{1}$ &gt; $F_{3}$ &gt; $F_{2}$ &gt; $F_{4}$</p>","<p>$F_{1}$ &gt; $F_{2}$ &gt; $F_{3}$ &gt; $F_{4}$</p>","<p>$F_{1} = F_{3}$ &gt; $F_{2} = F_{4}$</p>","<p>$F_{3}$ &gt; $F_{4}$ &gt; $F_{1} = F_{2}$</p>","<p>$F_{1} = F_{2}$ &gt; $F_{3} = F_{4}$</p>"]'::jsonb, '<p class="ques"><strong><a href="ch19r.xhtml#ch19r_18" id="rch19r_18">18.</a></strong> <strong>E—</strong>The magnitude of the electric force is given by $F$ = <em>kqq/d</em><sup>2</sup>. The magnitude of a force is just an amount—the + or − sign is not plugged into the equation. (The signs of the charges indicate directions of force—like charge signs repel, opposite charge signs repel.) So with the same amount of charge in each arrangement, the force will depend on the distance between charges—larger distance, smaller force.</p>'),
  ('ap-mock-physc-em-3_q19', 'ap-mock-physc-em-3', 19, false, 'B', 'AP Physics C: Electricity & Magnetism', 'medium', '<p class="ques"><strong><a href="ch19v.xhtml#rch19r_19" id="ch19r_19">19.</a></strong> A parallel plate capacitor is connected to a battery and charged fully. While still connected to the battery, a dielectric slab is inserted between the plates of the capacitor. Which of the following quantities will change when the slab is inserted?</p><p class="roman1t">I. The magnitude of the electric field between the plates</p><p class="roman2">II. The charge stored on the capacitor</p><p class="roman3">III. The potential difference between the plates</p>', '["<p>I only</p>","<p>II only</p>","<p>III only</p>","<p>I and III only</p>","<p>I, II, and III</p>"]'::jsonb, '<p class="ques"><strong><a href="ch19r.xhtml#ch19r_19" id="rch19r_19">19.</a></strong> <strong>B—</strong>Voltage—that is, potential difference—is provided by a battery. Since the battery remains connected, the potential difference across the plates does not change. Electric field between two parallel plates is equal to <em>V/d</em>, the potential difference between the plates divided by the distance between the plates. Neither $V$ nor $d$ changes, so electric field doesn’t change. A dielectric material changes the capacitance of the capacitor (the formula for the capacitance of a parallel-plate capacitor is $C$ = <em>kɛ</em><sub>0</sub><em>A/d</em>—the effect of the dielectric slab is to increase the $k$ term). By $Q$ = <em>CV</em>, a bigger $C$ with an unchanged $V$ gives larger charge $Q$.</p>'),
  ('ap-mock-physc-em-3_q20', 'ap-mock-physc-em-3', 20, false, 'D', 'AP Physics C: Electricity & Magnetism', 'medium', '<p class="image"><img alt="Images" class="q-img" src="images/f0324-01.jpg" style="max-width: 100%; height: auto; margin: 10px 0; display: block;"/></p><p class="ques"><strong><a href="ch19v.xhtml#rch19r_20" id="ch19r_20">20.</a></strong> A metal sphere is attached to an insulating stand. Conducting wire 1 is connected between the sphere and a very large metal desk in electrical contact with the ground. A negatively charged glass rod is brought very close to the sphere without touching it, as shown. Next, wire 1 is cut. Finally, the glass rod is moved far away from the sphere. Which of the pictures of the sphere models the sign and distribution of charge on the surface of the sphere at the end of this process?</p>', '["<p><img alt=\"Images\" class=\"q-img\" src=\"images/f0324-02.jpg\" style=\"max-width: 100%; height: auto; margin: 10px 0; display: block;\"/></p>","<p><img alt=\"Images\" class=\"q-img\" src=\"images/f0324-03.jpg\" style=\"max-width: 100%; height: auto; margin: 10px 0; display: block;\"/></p>","<p><img alt=\"Images\" class=\"q-img\" src=\"images/f0324-04.jpg\" style=\"max-width: 100%; height: auto; margin: 10px 0; display: block;\"/></p>","<p><img alt=\"Images\" class=\"q-img\" src=\"images/f0324-05.jpg\" style=\"max-width: 100%; height: auto; margin: 10px 0; display: block;\"/></p>","<p><img alt=\"Images\" class=\"q-img\" src=\"images/f0324-06.jpg\" style=\"max-width: 100%; height: auto; margin: 10px 0; display: block;\"/></p>"]'::jsonb, '<p class="ques"><strong><a href="ch19r.xhtml#ch19r_20" id="rch19r_20">20.</a></strong> <strong>D—</strong>Initially, the negative rod attracts positive charges in the conducting sphere and repels negative charges from the sphere into the ground. This makes the sphere have a net positive charge when the wire is cut. When the rod is moved far away, nothing is nearby to cause polarization. So the positive charges will distribute uniformly on the surface of the conductor, as in choice D.</p>'),
  ('ap-mock-physc-em-3_q21', 'ap-mock-physc-em-3', 21, false, 'B', 'AP Physics C: Electricity & Magnetism', 'medium', '<p class="ques"><strong><a href="ch19v.xhtml#rch19r_21" id="ch19r_21">21.</a></strong> A circular wire of radius $r$ is completely inside a magnetic field $B$, which increases with time $t$ according to the function $B$ = 3$t^{2}$. Which of the following is a correct expression for the EMF induced in the wire?</p>', '["<p>12<em>πr</em><sup>2</sup>$t$</p>","<p>6<em>πr</em><sup>2</sup>$t$</p>","<p>6<em>πrt</em><sup>2</sup></p>","<p>12<em>πrt</em></p>","<p>3<em>πr</em><sup>2</sup>$t^{2}$</p>"]'::jsonb, '<p class="ques"><strong><a href="ch19r.xhtml#ch19r_21" id="rch19r_21">21.</a></strong> <strong>B—</strong>The EMF is given by the time derivative of the magnetic flux, which is $B$ times the area of the loop. The area of the loop is <em>πr</em><sup>2</sup><sub>,</sub> so the expression for flux is 3<em>πr</em><sup>2</sup>$t^{2}$<sub>.</sub> The time derivative of this expression is 6<em>πr</em><sup>2</sup>$t$.</p>'),
  ('ap-mock-physc-em-3_q22', 'ap-mock-physc-em-3', 22, false, 'A', 'AP Physics C: Electricity & Magnetism', 'medium', '<p class="image"><img alt="Images" class="q-img" src="images/f0324-07.jpg" style="max-width: 100%; height: auto; margin: 10px 0; display: block;"/></p><p class="ques"><strong><a href="ch19v.xhtml#rch19r_22" id="ch19r_22">22.</a></strong> Two parallel plates—the positive plate on the right—produce a uniform electric field of 80 N/C. The plates are separated by 16 cm. Point A is next to the positive plate, while point P is directly between the plates. How much kinetic energy is gained by a proton that is released from rest at point A when it reaches point P ?</p>', '["<p>1.0 × 10<sup>−18</sup> J</p>","<p>1.4 × 10<sup>−6</sup> J</p>","<p>2.1 × 10<sup>−6</sup> J</p>","<p>0 J</p>","<p>2.0 × 10<sup>−18</sup> J</p>"]'::jsonb, '<p class="ques"><strong><a href="ch19r.xhtml#ch19r_22" id="rch19r_22">22.</a></strong> <strong>A—</strong>The kinetic energy gained by the proton is equal to the potential energy lost from A to B. The potential energy lost by the proton is $q$Δ$V$, where the change in electric potential Δ$V$ is <em>Ed</em> (derived from the equation for a uniform electric field, $E$ = Δ$V$/Δ$x$). The charge $q$ here is the charge of a proton; the distance $d$ is the distance from point A to point P, 0.08 m. Not the distance between plates, but the distance the proton moves! So the change in potential energy is (1.6 × 10<sup>-</sup>19 C)(80 N/C)(0.08 m) = 1.0 × 10<sup>-</sup>18 J.</p>'),
  ('ap-mock-physc-em-3_q23', 'ap-mock-physc-em-3', 23, false, 'D', 'AP Physics C: Electricity & Magnetism', 'medium', '<div class="passage-box" style="background: rgba(255,255,255,0.03); padding: 12px 16px; border-radius: 8px; margin-bottom: 14px; border-left: 3px solid #7c3aed;"><p class="image"><img alt="Images" class="q-img" src="images/f0324-08.jpg" style="max-width: 100%; height: auto; margin: 10px 0; display: block;"/></p><p class="noindent">Questions 23 and 24: Two metal spheres, labeled 1 and 2, have different radii, as shown above. The spheres initially each carried positive charge +<em>Q,</em> but have been in electrical contact by means of a long metal wire for a long time.</p></div><p class="ques"><strong><a href="ch19v.xhtml#rch19r_23" id="ch19r_23">23.</a></strong> A rectangular wire loop is located above a wire carrying a current $I$, which increases with time $t$ according to the equation $I$ = 3$t$. Which of the following is correct about the current induced in the wire loop?</p>', '["<p>It is counterclockwise and increasing.</p>","<p>It is clockwise and increasing.</p>","<p>It remains zero.</p>","<p>It is counterclockwise and constant.</p>","<p>It is clockwise and constant.</p>"]'::jsonb, '<p class="ques"><strong><a href="ch19r.xhtml#ch19r_23" id="rch19r_23">23.</a></strong> <strong>D—</strong>Induced current in the loop is, by Ohm’s law, the induced voltage (EMF) divided by the constant resistance of the loop. So whether induced current is constant depends on the EMF. Induced EMF is <em>dB/dt</em>. The magnetic field produced by a current-carrying wire depends linearly on $I$. The time derivative of $I$ is a constant value. Thus, the EMF is constant, and so is the induced current. Now, the direction of the magnetic field inside the wire is into the page by the second right-hand rule—point the thumb left with the current producing the field, the fingers curl into the page. This magnetic field is increasing because the current is increasing. So by Lenz’s law, increasing flux is into the page, making decreasing flux out of the page . . . and the induced current is found by pointing the right thumb with decreasing flux and curling the fingers. That’d be counterclockwise.</p>'),
  ('ap-mock-physc-em-3_q24', 'ap-mock-physc-em-3', 24, false, 'C', 'AP Physics C: Electricity & Magnetism', 'medium', '<div class="passage-box" style="background: rgba(255,255,255,0.03); padding: 12px 16px; border-radius: 8px; margin-bottom: 14px; border-left: 3px solid #7c3aed;"><p class="image"><img alt="Images" class="q-img" src="images/f0324-08.jpg" style="max-width: 100%; height: auto; margin: 10px 0; display: block;"/></p><p class="noindent">Questions 23 and 24: Two metal spheres, labeled 1 and 2, have different radii, as shown above. The spheres initially each carried positive charge +<em>Q,</em> but have been in electrical contact by means of a long metal wire for a long time.</p></div><p class="ques"><strong><a href="ch19v.xhtml#rch19r_24" id="ch19r_24">24.</a></strong> Which of the following statements is true about the electric potential at the center of each sphere?</p>', '["<p>It is greater for sphere 1.</p>","<p>It is zero for both spheres.</p>","<p>It is the same positive value for both spheres.</p>","<p>It is the same negative value for both spheres.</p>","<p>It is greater for sphere 2.</p>"]'::jsonb, '<p class="ques"><strong><a href="ch19r.xhtml#ch19r_24" id="rch19r_24">24.</a></strong> <strong>C—</strong>The electric field inside a conductor is zero everywhere—and these two spheres are a single conductor, so they have electric field zero everywhere. The equation $E$ = <em>−dV/dx</em> indicates that electric field tells how electric potential changes. Since electric field is zero, that means electric potential can’t change within the two spheres. But that potential isn’t zero! The electric potential is considered zero a long way away from any charges. Since these spheres have positive charge, they create positive potential outside of the spheres, and so they must have a uniform but positive potential everywhere inside.</p>'),
  ('ap-mock-physc-em-3_q25', 'ap-mock-physc-em-3', 25, false, 'D', 'AP Physics C: Electricity & Magnetism', 'medium', '<p class="image"><img alt="Images" class="q-img" src="images/f0324-09.jpg" style="max-width: 100%; height: auto; margin: 10px 0; display: block;"/></p><p class="ques"><strong><a href="ch19v.xhtml#rch19r_25" id="ch19r_25">25.</a></strong> Which of the following is true about the charge on each sphere after a long time?</p>', '["<p>Both spheres are positively charged, but sphere 1 holds a greater magnitude of charge.</p>","<p>Both spheres hold zero charge.</p>","<p>Sphere 2 holds positive charge, while sphere 1 holds negative charge.</p>","<p>Both spheres are positively charged, but sphere 2 holds a greater magnitude of charge.</p>","<p>Both spheres hold equal positive charge.</p>"]'::jsonb, '<p class="ques"><strong><a href="ch19r.xhtml#ch19r_25" id="rch19r_25">25.</a></strong> <strong>D—</strong>The positive charge initially on the conducting spheres must redistribute at the surface (because no charge can remain inside a conductor). We know the electric potential is the same everywhere in and on each sphere. And electric potential at the surface is given by <em>kQ/r</em>. The sphere with the bigger radius must likewise hold the bigger charge.</p>'),
  ('ap-mock-physc-em-3_q26', 'ap-mock-physc-em-3', 26, false, 'A', 'AP Physics C: Electricity & Magnetism', 'hard', '<p class="image"><img alt="Images" class="q-img" src="images/f0325-01.jpg" style="max-width: 100%; height: auto; margin: 10px 0; display: block;"/></p><p class="ques"><strong><a href="ch19v.xhtml#rch19r_26" id="ch19r_26">26.</a></strong> Circuit 1 above shows three identical resistors labeled X, Y, and Z. In circuit 2, resistor Z is removed. Which of the following correctly compares the current through and voltage across resistor Y in circuit 2 compared to circuit 1?</p><p class="image"><img alt="Images" class="q-img" src="images/f0325-02.jpg" style="max-width: 100%; height: auto; margin: 10px 0; display: block;"/></p><p class="image"><img alt="Images" class="q-img" src="images/f0325-03.jpg" style="max-width: 100%; height: auto; margin: 10px 0; display: block;"/></p>', '["Current through Y: Greater; Voltage across Y: Greater","Current through Y: Smaller; Voltage across Y: Smaller","Current through Y: Greater; Voltage across Y: Smaller","Current through Y: Smaller; Voltage across Y: Greater","Current through Y: Unchanged; Voltage across Y: Unchanged"]'::jsonb, '<p class="ques"><strong><a href="ch19r.xhtml#ch19r_26" id="rch19r_26">26.</a></strong> <strong>A—</strong>First, consider the whole circuit: the battery is the same, so the total voltage is unchanged. The equivalent resistance increases because we removed a parallel branch. By $V$ = <em>IR</em>, the total current must decrease. Now apply Ohm’s law to resistor X only: The reduced total current all runs through resistor X, but resistor X can’t change its own resistance. So by $V$ = <em>IR</em>, the voltage across bulb X is smaller than before. Kirchoff’s loop rule says that the voltage across X and Y must sum to V. . . so with smaller voltage across X, there must be greater voltage across Y. Now apply Ohm’s law to bulb Y: greater voltage with unchanged resistance means greater current, too.</p>'),
  ('ap-mock-physc-em-3_q27', 'ap-mock-physc-em-3', 27, false, 'C', 'AP Physics C: Electricity & Magnetism', 'hard', '<div class="passage-box" style="background: rgba(255,255,255,0.03); padding: 12px 16px; border-radius: 8px; margin-bottom: 14px; border-left: 3px solid #7c3aed;"><p class="noindent">Questions 27 and 28: The diagram above shows two concentric spherical conducting shells. The net charge on the inner sphere is −2$Q$. The net charge on the outer sphere is +3$Q$.</p></div><p class="ques"><strong><a href="ch19v.xhtml#rch19r_27" id="ch19r_27">27.</a></strong> What is the charge that resides on the outer surface of the outer shell?</p>', '["<p>+2$Q$</p>","<p>zero</p>","<p>$+Q$</p>","<p>+3$Q$</p>","<p>+5$Q$</p>"]'::jsonb, '<p class="ques"><strong><a href="ch19r.xhtml#ch19r_27" id="rch19r_27">27.</a></strong> <strong>C—</strong>The electric field inside a conductor is zero; charge can only sit on the surface of a conductor. Consider a Gaussian surface drawn inside the outer shell, going through point C. Since the shell is conducting, there must be zero electric field inside. Therefore, this Gaussian surface must enclose zero total charge. The inner shell contains charge −2$Q$, so the inner surface of the outer shell—the part inside this Gaussian surface—contains +2$Q$ to make the total charge inside the Gaussian surface zero. Then the remaining +1$Q$ of charge on the outer shell must be on the outer surface.</p>'),
  ('ap-mock-physc-em-3_q28', 'ap-mock-physc-em-3', 28, false, 'E', 'AP Physics C: Electricity & Magnetism', 'hard', '<div class="passage-box" style="background: rgba(255,255,255,0.03); padding: 12px 16px; border-radius: 8px; margin-bottom: 14px; border-left: 3px solid #7c3aed;"><p class="noindent">Questions 27 and 28: The diagram above shows two concentric spherical conducting shells. The net charge on the inner sphere is −2$Q$. The net charge on the outer sphere is +3$Q$.</p></div><p class="ques"><strong><a href="ch19v.xhtml#rch19r_28" id="ch19r_28">28.</a></strong> Four positions are labeled in the diagram. Which of the following correctly ranks those positions by the magnitude of the electric field at each position?</p>', '["<p>A &gt; B &gt; D &gt; C</p>","<p>D &gt; C &gt; B &gt; A</p>","<p>A &gt; B &gt; C &gt; D</p>","<p>D = B &gt; A = C</p>","<p>B &gt; D &gt; A = C</p>"]'::jsonb, '<p class="ques"><strong><a href="ch19r.xhtml#ch19r_28" id="rch19r_28">28.</a></strong> <strong>E—</strong>The electric field inside a conductor is zero, so C is the smallest. A Gaussian sphere through A encloses no charge, so the electric field at A is also zero. A Gaussian sphere at position B encloses −2$Q$; a Gaussian sphere at position D encloses $+Q$. So the sphere at D encloses a smaller magnitude of charge AND has a larger distance from the center, meaning D has a smaller electric field than B.</p>'),
  ('ap-mock-physc-em-3_q29', 'ap-mock-physc-em-3', 29, false, 'D', 'AP Physics C: Electricity & Magnetism', 'hard', '<p class="image"><img alt="Images" class="q-img" src="images/f0326-01.jpg" style="max-width: 100%; height: auto; margin: 10px 0; display: block;"/></p><p class="ques"><strong><a href="ch19v.xhtml#rch19r_29" id="ch19r_29">29.</a></strong> A uniform magnetic field of magnitude $B$ points at a 60<sup>o</sup> angle above the plane of a rectangular wire loop of dimensions $x$ by $y$ as shown above. What is the magnetic flux through the loop?</p>', '["<p><em>Bxy</em></p>","<p><em>Bx</em></p>","<p><img alt=\"Images\" class=\"q-img\" src=\"images/f0326-02.jpg\" style=\"max-width: 100%; height: auto; margin: 10px 0; display: block;\"/></p>","<p><img alt=\"Images\" class=\"q-img\" src=\"images/f0326-03.jpg\" style=\"max-width: 100%; height: auto; margin: 10px 0; display: block;\"/></p>","<p><em>By</em></p>"]'::jsonb, '<p class="ques"><strong><a href="ch19r.xhtml#ch19r_29" id="rch19r_29">29.</a></strong> <strong>D—</strong>Magnetic flux is <em>BA</em>, where $A$ is the area of the loop that is directly penetrated by the magnetic field. Here the magnetic field is penetrating the loop at an angle, so we need to look at the component of the magnetic field that penetrates directly, that is, at a 90<sup>o</sup> angle to the plane of the loop. That’s $B$ sin 60<sup>o</sup>, or <img alt="Images" class="q-img" src="images/f0341-01.jpg" style="max-width: 100%; height: auto; margin: 10px 0; display: block;"/>. (Not cosine of 60 degrees . . . if you’re not sure, note that the magnetic field is closer to straight-through the loop than to along the loop, so we want the bigger component, and sin 60<sup>o</sup> is bigger than cos 60<sup>o</sup>.) And the area of the loop is <em>xy</em>.</p>'),
  ('ap-mock-physc-em-3_q30', 'ap-mock-physc-em-3', 30, false, 'E', 'AP Physics C: Electricity & Magnetism', 'hard', '<p class="image"><img alt="Images" class="q-img" src="images/f0326-04.jpg" style="max-width: 100%; height: auto; margin: 10px 0; display: block;"/></p><p class="ques"><strong><a href="ch19v.xhtml#rch19r_30" id="ch19r_30">30.</a></strong> A 50 V battery is connected to a 70 W resistor and a 0.50 mF capacitor in the circuit shown above. The capacitor is uncharged when the switch is closed. Sometime later when the current through the resistor is 0.43 A, how much charge is stored on the capacitor?</p>', '["<p>0 μC</p>","<p>35 μC</p>","<p>25 μC</p>","<p>15 μC</p>","<p>10 μC</p>"]'::jsonb, '<p class="ques"><strong><a href="ch19r.xhtml#ch19r_30" id="rch19r_30">30.</a></strong> <strong>E—</strong>When the current is 0.43 A, the voltage across the resistor is (70 Ω)(0.43 A) = 30 V. That means the voltage across the capacitor is 20 V by Kirchoff’s loop rule, to add to the total 50 V in the battery. For a capacitor, the charge stored is given by $Q$ = <em>CV</em>. Here that’s (0.50 μF)(20 V) = 10 μC.</p>'),
  ('ap-mock-physc-em-3_q31', 'ap-mock-physc-em-3', 31, false, 'C', 'AP Physics C: Electricity & Magnetism', 'hard', '<div class="passage-box" style="background: rgba(255,255,255,0.03); padding: 12px 16px; border-radius: 8px; margin-bottom: 14px; border-left: 3px solid #7c3aed;"><p class="image"><img alt="Images" class="q-img" src="images/f0326-05.jpg" style="max-width: 100%; height: auto; margin: 10px 0; display: block;"/></p><p class="noindent">Questions 31 and 32: In the circuit above, the switch S has initially been closed for a very long time. Then the switch is opened.</p></div><p class="ques"><strong><a href="ch19v.xhtml#rch19r_31" id="ch19r_31">31.</a></strong> Immediately before the switch is opened, what is the current through $R_{1}$?</p>', '["<p>1.5 A</p>","<p>0.33 A</p>","<p>1.0 A</p>","<p>0.5 A</p>","<p>0 A</p>"]'::jsonb, '<p class="ques"><strong><a href="ch19r.xhtml#ch19r_31" id="rch19r_31">31.</a></strong> <strong>C—</strong>Inductors resist changes in current. Since the current has been flowing for a long time and thus is steady, the inductor has no effect on the circuit. It just acts as a bare wire. So the two resistors are both in parallel with the battery, taking the voltage of the battery. The current through $R_{1}$ is 12 V / 12 Ω = 1.0 A.</p>'),
  ('ap-mock-physc-em-3_q32', 'ap-mock-physc-em-3', 32, false, 'D', 'AP Physics C: Electricity & Magnetism', 'hard', '<div class="passage-box" style="background: rgba(255,255,255,0.03); padding: 12px 16px; border-radius: 8px; margin-bottom: 14px; border-left: 3px solid #7c3aed;"><p class="image"><img alt="Images" class="q-img" src="images/f0326-05.jpg" style="max-width: 100%; height: auto; margin: 10px 0; display: block;"/></p><p class="noindent">Questions 31 and 32: In the circuit above, the switch S has initially been closed for a very long time. Then the switch is opened.</p></div><p class="ques"><strong><a href="ch19v.xhtml#rch19r_32" id="ch19r_32">32.</a></strong> Immediately after the switch is opened, what is the current through $R_{1}$?</p>', '["<p>1.5 A</p>","<p>0.33 A</p>","<p>1.0 A</p>","<p>0.5 A</p>","<p>0 A</p>"]'::jsonb, '<p class="ques"><strong><a href="ch19r.xhtml#ch19r_32" id="rch19r_32">32.</a></strong> <strong>D—</strong>Inductors resist changes in current. So the current through the inductor now is the same as the current through the inductor before the switch was closed. The inductor was in series with the 24 Ω resistor, which carried 12 V/24 Ω = 0.5 A of current. With the battery removed, the resistor $R_{1}$ is in series with $R_{2}$, so it must carry the 0.5 A of current.</p>'),
  ('ap-mock-physc-em-3_q33', 'ap-mock-physc-em-3', 33, false, 'E', 'AP Physics C: Electricity & Magnetism', 'hard', '<p class="image"><img alt="Images" class="q-img" src="images/f0326-06.jpg" style="max-width: 100%; height: auto; margin: 10px 0; display: block;"/></p><p class="ques"><strong><a href="ch19v.xhtml#rch19r_33" id="ch19r_33">33.</a></strong> An electron is initially moving to the left with speed $v$ parallel to a wire carrying a constant rightward current $I$ as shown above. Which of the following indicates the direction of the magnetic force on the electron at the instant shown, and whether the long-term path of the electron is circular or not circular?</p><p class="image"><img alt="Images" class="q-img" src="images/f0326-07.jpg" style="max-width: 100%; height: auto; margin: 10px 0; display: block;"/></p>', '["Direction of Force: Upward (away from wire); Path: Circular","Direction of Force: Upward (away from wire); Path: Not circular","Direction of Force: Zero; Path: Straight line","Direction of Force: Downward (toward wire); Path: Circular","Direction of Force: Downward (toward wire); Path: Not circular"]'::jsonb, '<p class="ques"><strong><a href="ch19r.xhtml#ch19r_33" id="rch19r_33">33.</a></strong> <strong>E—</strong>The magnetic field produced by a current-carrying wire is given by the second right-hand rule: Point the thumb to the right and curl. The fingers point out of the page above the wire, so that’s the direction of the magnetic field. Now the force on the electron is given by the first right-hand rule: Point with the velocity, curl the fingers out of the page with the magnetic field, and the thumb gives the upward force on a positive charge. An electron has a negative charge, though, so the force is downward. Because the magnetic force is perpendicular to the velocity, the path is initially circular. But because the magnetic field isn’t uniform (it drops off the farther you get from the current-carrying wire), the magnetic force isn’t always of the same magnitude, and so the path becomes not circular.</p>'),
  ('ap-mock-physc-em-3_q34', 'ap-mock-physc-em-3', 34, false, 'E', 'AP Physics C: Electricity & Magnetism', 'hard', '<div class="passage-box" style="background: rgba(255,255,255,0.03); padding: 12px 16px; border-radius: 8px; margin-bottom: 14px; border-left: 3px solid #7c3aed;"><p class="noindent">Questions 34 and 35: Two small objects, each carrying negative electric charge of magnitude 2.0 μC, are placed on the $x$-axis at positions $x_{a}$ = +4.0 cm and $x_{b}$ = -4.0 cm.</p></div><p class="ques"><strong><a href="ch19v.xhtml#rch19r_34" id="ch19r_34">34.</a></strong> What is the electric potential at position $x$ = 0?</p>', '["<p>+900,000 V</p>","<p>+450,000 V</p>","<p>0 V</p>","<p>-450,000 V</p>","<p>-900,000 V</p>"]'::jsonb, '<p class="ques"><strong><a href="ch19r.xhtml#ch19r_34" id="rch19r_34">34.</a></strong> <strong>E—</strong>Each charge contributes a potential of <em>kQ/d</em> to the total potential at the origin. That’s (9 × 10<sup>9</sup> N/$m^{2}$/$C^{2}$)(−2 × 10<sup>−6</sup> C)/(0.04 m) = −450,000 V per charge. Both charges are negative and so produce negative electric potentials, and electric potentials add as scalars, making the total potential −900,000 V.</p>'),
  ('ap-mock-physc-em-3_q35', 'ap-mock-physc-em-3', 35, false, 'D', 'AP Physics C: Electricity & Magnetism', 'hard', '<div class="passage-box" style="background: rgba(255,255,255,0.03); padding: 12px 16px; border-radius: 8px; margin-bottom: 14px; border-left: 3px solid #7c3aed;"><p class="noindent">Questions 34 and 35: Two small objects, each carrying negative electric charge of magnitude 2.0 μC, are placed on the $x$-axis at positions $x_{a}$ = +4.0 cm and $x_{b}$ = -4.0 cm.</p></div><p class="ques"><strong><a href="ch19v.xhtml#rch19r_35" id="ch19r_35">35.</a></strong> What is the magnitude of the electric field at position $x$ = 0?</p>', '["<p>44 N/C</p>","<p>2.2 million N/C</p>","<p>1.1 million N/C</p>","<p>0 N/C</p>","<p>22 N/C</p>"]'::jsonb, '<p class="ques"><strong><a href="ch19r.xhtml#ch19r_35" id="rch19r_35">35.</a></strong> <strong>D—</strong>By $E$ = <em>kQ/$d^{2}$</em>, each charge creates an electric field of the same magnitude (because both are of the same amount of charge, located the same distance from the origin). A negative charge produces an electric field toward that charge. So the fields produced by each charge are pointed in opposite directions. Electric fields add as vectors; equal magnitude vectors pointing in opposite directions subtract to zero.</p>'),
  ('ap-mock-physc-em-3_q36', 'ap-mock-physc-em-3', 36, true, 'FRQ_MANUAL_EVALUATION', 'AP Physics C: Electricity & Magnetism Free Response', 'hard', '<div class=''frq-prompt''><h3>E&M 1</h3><p class="ques"><strong><a href="ch19w.xhtml#rch19s_1" id="ch19s_1">1.</a></strong> A rod of length $L$ carrying positive charge has uniform linear charge density <em>ℓ</em>. Points A and B are located distances $d$ and 2$d$, respectively, from the center of the line.</p><p class="alphabrt">(a) Consider the situation when $L$ is much, much greater than $d$.</p><p class="roman4r">i. Derive an expression for the magnitude of the electric field at point A. Express your answer in terms of given variables and fundamental constants.</p><p class="roman4r">ii. On the dot below, draw the direction of the electric field at point A.</p><p class="image"><img alt="Images" class="q-img" src="images/f0328-02.jpg" style="max-width: 100%; height: auto; margin: 10px 0; display: block;"/></p><p class="roman4r">iii. Let the magnitude of the electric field at point A be $E_{A1}$. Is the magnitude of the electric field at point B greater than, less than, or equal to ½$E_{A1}$? Justify your answer.</p><p class="roman4r">iv. Derive an expression for the electric potential difference between points A and B. Express your answer in terms of given variables and fundamental constants.</p><p class="alphabr">(B) Now consider the situation when $L$ is NOT much greater than $d$.</p><p class="roman4r">i. In terms of given variables and fundamental constants, derive an integral with limits that could be evaluated to determine the magnitude of the electric field at point A.</p><p class="roman4r">ii. On the dot below, draw the direction of the electric field at point A.</p><p class="image"><img alt="Images" class="q-img" src="images/f0328-03.jpg" style="max-width: 100%; height: auto; margin: 10px 0; display: block;"/></p><p class="roman4r">iii. Let the magnitude of the electric field in this case at point A be $E_{A2}$. Is $E_{A2}$ greater than, less than, or equal to $E_{A1}$? Justify your answer.</p><p class="image"><img alt="Images" class="q-img" src="images/f0328-04.jpg" style="max-width: 100%; height: auto; margin: 10px 0; display: block;"/></p></div>', '["Submit analytical derivation in workspace."]'::jsonb, '<p class="ques"><strong><a href="ch19s.xhtml#ch19s_1" id="rch19s_1">1.</a></strong> (15 points)</p><p class="alphabrt">(a)</p><p class="roman4r"> i. Use Gauss’s law with a cylindrical Gaussian surface. Because the length of the rod is big now compared to $d$, the electric field will always point straight away from the rod—so straight through the surface. Then Gauss’s law says <em>EA</em> = $Q$/<em>ɛ</em><sub>0</sub>. Here the area A of the cylindrical Gaussian surface $A$ is 2<em>πrL</em>; we’re looking for the electric field a distance $d$ from the rod, so replace $r$ with $d$. The charge $Q$ enclosed by the surface is <em>λL</em>. Solve for $E$ to get $E$ = <em>λ/</em>(2<em>πdɛ</em><sub>0</sub>).</p><p class="hangp"><strong>1 pt</strong> for attempting to use Gauss’s law reasonably</p><p class="hangp"><strong>1 pt</strong> for the correct area term</p><p class="hangp"><strong>1 pt</strong> for the correct expression for the charge enclosed</p><p class="hangp"><strong>1 pt</strong> for the correct answer</p><p class="roman4rtb"> ii.</p><p class="hangp"><strong>1 pt</strong> for an arrow straight up the page</p><p class="image"><img alt="Images" class="q-img" src="images/f0342-01.jpg" style="max-width: 100%; height: auto; margin: 10px 0; display: block;"/></p><p class="roman4r">iii. The electric field expression is inversely related to the first power of $r$, the distance from the rod. That means doubling $r$ cuts the electric field in half. (If you stated an incorrect expression in part (i) that has a clear $r$ dependence, then award credit here for an answer and justification consistent with that incorrect equation.)</p><p class="hangp"><strong>1 pt</strong> for the answer</p><p class="hangp"><strong>1 pt</strong> for a correct justification</p><p class="roman4rtb">iv.</p><p class="hangp"><strong>1 pt</strong> for substituting an electric field expression from part (i) into <img alt="Images" class="q-img" src="images/f0342-02.jpg" style="max-width: 100%; height: auto; margin: 10px 0; display: block;"/>.</p><p class="hangp"><strong>1 pt</strong> for correct limits and an attempt to evaluate the integral with respect to $r$</p><p class="hangp"><strong>1 pt</strong> for the correct answer in any form that has correctly evaluated the correct integral</p><p class="image"><img alt="Images" class="q-img" src="images/f0342-03.jpg" style="max-width: 100%; height: auto; margin: 10px 0; display: block;"/></p><p class="alphabrt">(b)</p><p class="roman4r"> i.</p><p class="hangp"><strong>1 pt</strong> for using (not just writing) the expression <img alt="Images" class="q-img" src="images/f0343-01.jpg" style="max-width: 100%; height: auto; margin: 10px 0; display: block;"/></p><p class="hangp"><strong>1 pt</strong> for substituting <em>dq</em> = <em>λdx</em></p><p class="hangp"><strong>1 pt</strong> for using geometry to write $r$, the distance from a position on the rod to point A, in terms of $x$ and $d$ as shown in the diagram below</p><p class="hangp"><strong>1 pt</strong> for correct limits</p><p class="image"><img alt="Images" class="q-img" src="images/f0343-02.jpg" style="max-width: 100%; height: auto; margin: 10px 0; display: block;"/></p><p class="roman4rtb"> ii.</p><p class="hangp"><strong>1 pt</strong> for an upward pointing arrow</p><p class="image"><img alt="Images" class="q-img" src="images/f0343-03.jpg" style="max-width: 100%; height: auto; margin: 10px 0; display: block;"/></p>'),
  ('ap-mock-physc-em-3_q37', 'ap-mock-physc-em-3', 37, true, 'FRQ_MANUAL_EVALUATION', 'AP Physics C: Electricity & Magnetism Free Response', 'hard', '<div class=''frq-prompt''><h3>E&M 2</h3><p class="ques"><strong><a href="ch19w.xhtml#rch19s_2" id="ch19s_2">2.</a></strong> A single rectangular copper coil of dimensions $s$ by <em>ℓ</em> is moving to the right with speed $v$. Let position $x$ = 0 be where the right-hand edge of the coil enters a uniform magnetic field of magnitude $B$ pointing into the page. Let position $x = D$ be where the left-hand edge of the coil exits the field.</p><p class="alphar1">(a) i. On the axes below, sketch the shape of a graph of the current induced in the coil as a function of position, from $x$ = 0 to $x = D$ + <em>ℓ</em>. Represent clockwise current as positive.</p><p class="image"><img alt="Images" class="q-img" src="images/f0329-01.jpg" style="max-width: 100%; height: auto; margin: 10px 0; display: block;"/></p><p class="roman4r">ii. Justify the shape of your graph for the region $x$ = 0 to $x$ = <em>ℓ</em>.</p><p class="roman4r">iii. In terms of given variables and fundamental constants, write an expression for the maximum induced EMF in the coil.</p><p class="alphabr">(b) Describe any changes to your graph if instead a copper coil of the same rectangular dimensions but consisting of two loops of wire moved with speed $v$ through the magnetic field. Justify your response.</p><p class="alphabr">(c) Now the original single coil enters the field as before, but the coil speeds up with acceleration $a$ starting from rest at position $x$ = 0.</p><p class="roman4r">i. Consider only the time during which the coil is entering the magnetic field. Derive an expression for the induced EMF in the coil as a function of position $x$. Express your answer in terms of given variables and fundamental constants.</p><p class="roman4r">ii. Sketch the shape of a graph of the current induced in this coil as a function of position, from $x$ = 0 to $x = D$ + <em>ℓ</em>.</p><p class="image"><img alt="Images" class="q-img" src="images/f0329-02.jpg" style="max-width: 100%; height: auto; margin: 10px 0; display: block;"/></p></div>', '["Submit analytical derivation in workspace."]'::jsonb, '<p class="ques"><strong><a href="ch19s.xhtml#ch19s_2" id="rch19s_2">2.</a></strong> (15 points)</p><p class="alphabrt">(a)</p><p class="roman4r"> i.</p><p class="hangp"><strong>1 pt</strong> for zero current between <em>ℓ</em> and $D$</p><p class="hangp"><strong>1 pt</strong> for a horizontal line between 0 and <em>ℓ</em></p><p class="hangp"><strong>1 pt</strong> for a horizontal line on the opposite side of the axis between $D$ and $D$ + <em>ℓ</em></p><p class="hangp"><strong>1 pt</strong> for a graph entirely below the horizontal axis between 0 and <em>ℓ</em></p><p class="roman4rtb"> ii.</p><p class="hangp"><strong>1 pt</strong> For explaining that in general equation <em>ɛ</em> = <em>Bℓv</em>, here each term is unchanging, so the EMF and hence the current isn’t changing either</p><p class="image"><img alt="Images" class="q-img" src="images/f0343-04.jpg" style="max-width: 100%; height: auto; margin: 10px 0; display: block;"/></p><p class="roman4rtb"> ii.</p><p class="hangp"><strong>1 pt</strong> for the correct answer, <em>Bsv.</em> (Not <em>Bℓv</em>, because $s$ is the side of the rectangle that is not changing how much is inside the field.)</p><p class="alphabrc">(b)</p><p class="hangp"><strong>1 pt</strong> for recognizing that the magnetic flux or the induced EMF is doubled</p><p class="hangp"><strong>1 pt</strong> for recognizing that the coil’s resistance is also doubled</p><p class="hangp"><strong>1 pt</strong> for using Ohm’s law ($I$ = EMF/resistance) to determine that the induced current is unchanged</p><p class="alphabrt">(c)</p><p class="roman4r"> i. Answer is <img alt="Images" class="q-img" src="images/f0344-01.jpg" style="max-width: 100%; height: auto; margin: 10px 0; display: block;"/>.</p><p class="hangp"><strong>1 pt</strong> for using the definition of induced EMF, <em>ɛ</em> = $d$Φ<em>/dt</em> or <em>ɛ</em> = <em>Bsv</em> (full credit whether or not negative sign is included)</p><p class="hangp"><strong>1 pt</strong> for substituting <img alt="Images" class="q-img" src="images/f0344-02.jpg" style="max-width: 100%; height: auto; margin: 10px 0; display: block;"/></p><p class="roman4rtb"> ii.</p><p class="hangp"><strong>1 pt</strong> for the first segment starting at zero and concave toward the horizontal axis (This point is awarded if the shape is correct, even if drawn above the axis.)</p><p class="hangp"><strong>1 pt</strong> for a curved segment between $D$ and $D$ + <em>ℓ</em></p><p class="hangp"><strong>1 pt</strong> for this last segment starting at a greater vertical value than the first segment ended</p><p class="hangp"><strong>1 pt</strong> for this last segment being concave toward the horizontal axis (Based on the answer to part (i), the square root function indicates the concave-down shape of the graph. The faster the coil moves, the more induced EMF/current by <em>ɛ</em> = <em>Bsv</em>. And the coil moves faster farther along the track because of the acceleration.)</p><p class="image"><img alt="Images" class="q-img" src="images/f0344-03.jpg" style="max-width: 100%; height: auto; margin: 10px 0; display: block;"/></p>'),
  ('ap-mock-physc-em-3_q38', 'ap-mock-physc-em-3', 38, true, 'FRQ_MANUAL_EVALUATION', 'AP Physics C: Electricity & Magnetism Free Response', 'hard', '<div class=''frq-prompt''><h3>E&M 3</h3><p class="ques"><strong><a href="ch19w.xhtml#rch19s_3" id="ch19s_3">3.</a></strong> In the laboratory, you are given a wire coil of unknown inductance. You have available digital ammeters, digital voltmeters, and a variety of known resistors and capacitors.</p><p class="alphabrt">(a) Using the available equipment, describe an experimental procedure that could determine the inductance of the wire coil. Give enough detail that another student could reproduce your method.</p><p class="alphabr">(b) Draw a labeled diagram showing how the inductor is connected to the other equipment in your procedure.</p><p class="alphabr">(c) Explain how the inductance of the coil will be determined from the measurements made.</p><p class="image"><img alt="Images" class="q-img" src="images/f0329-03.jpg" style="max-width: 100%; height: auto; margin: 10px 0; display: block;"/></p><p class="alphabr">(d) The coil is determined to have an inductance of 25 mH. It is connected to a 15 V battery, a 100 W resistor, and a 50 W resistor in the circuit shown. The circuit has been connected for a long time with the switch in position A. Next, the switch is thrown to position B.</p><p class="roman4r">i. Determine the current through the inductor immediately before the switch is thrown. Briefly justify your answer.</p><p class="roman4r">ii. Determine the current through the inductor immediately after the switch is thrown. Briefly justify your answer.</p><p class="roman4r">iii. Determine the current through the inductor after the switch has been in position B for a long time. Briefly justify your answer.</p><p class="center1"><strong>STOP. End of Physics C—Electricity and Magnetism Practice Exam 3—Free-Response Questions</strong></p></div>', '["Submit analytical derivation in workspace."]'::jsonb, '<p class="ques"><strong><a href="ch19s.xhtml#ch19s_3" id="rch19s_3">3.</a></strong> (15 points)</p><p class="quesna">Parts (a), (b), and (c) are graded all together. As long as the elements that earn points are there somewhere, award credit.</p><p class="alphabrt">(a) Connect the inductor to a charged capacitor. Use a digital voltmeter to measure the voltage across the inductor as a function of time. Determine the period of the oscillating voltage from the voltmeter’s output.</p><p class="quesna">This example uses a circuit with a capacitor and inductor; something similar can be done with a resistor-inductor circuit and its time constant, or with other circuits.</p><p class="hangp"><strong>1 pt</strong> for a conceptually plausible plan to collect data that could lead to a calculation of $L$</p><p class="hangp"><strong>1 pt</strong> for a well-specified procedure that indicates correct use of appropriate equipment to measure the required quantities</p><p class="hangp"><strong>1 pt</strong> for using more than a single measurement, that is, a graph or multiple trials to reduce error</p><p class="alphabrt">(b) An example consistent with the method above:</p><p class="image"><img alt="Images" class="q-img" src="images/f0345-01.jpg" style="max-width: 100%; height: auto; margin: 10px 0; display: block;"/></p><p class="hangp"><strong>1 pt</strong> for drawing a diagram of a circuit that would function, that is, a complete circuit</p><p class="hangp"><strong>1 pt</strong> for correctly placing an ammeter or voltmeter (an ammeter must be connected in series, a voltmeter in parallel)</p><p class="hangp"><strong>1 pt</strong> for correct placement of all other equipment specified in part (a)</p><p class="quesna">(c) An example continued from above:</p><p class="quesna">The reading on the voltmeter will oscillate with a period given by <img alt="Images" class="q-img" src="images/f0345-02.jpg" style="max-width: 100%; height: auto; margin: 10px 0; display: block;"/>. On the voltmeter output, look at the time from one maximum voltage to the next—that’s the period. Plug in that period and the capacitance $C$, and you can solve for the inductance $L$.</p><p class="hangp"><strong>1 pt</strong> for recognizing the existence of a period for an <em>LC</em> circuit, or a time constant for an <em>LR</em> circuit</p><p class="hangp"><strong>1 pt</strong> for correctly relating the period/time constant to other circuit properties</p><p class="hangp"><strong>1 pt</strong> for explaining how to find the period or time constant from the collected data</p><p class="roman4rtb"> i. The battery has been connected for a long time, so the inductor acts as a bare wire. The resistance of the active branch is 100 Ω, the battery is 15 V, so the current is $I$ = <em>V/R</em> = 15 V/100 Ω = 0.15 A.</p><p class="hangp"><strong>1 pt</strong> for the correct answer</p><p class="hangp"><strong>1 pt</strong> for a correct verbal justification</p><p class="roman4rtb"> ii. The inductor resists changes in current. Since the current was 0.15 A right before the switch was closed, the initial current after the switch is closed is also 0.15 A.</p><p class="hangp"><strong>1 pt</strong> for the correct answer</p><p class="hangp"><strong>1 pt</strong> for a correct verbal justification</p><p class="roman4rtb">iii. After a long time, the current again reaches a steady state with the inductor acting as a bare wire. The total resistance this time is 150 Ω, so the current is 15 V/150 Ω = 0.10 A.</p><p class="hangp"><strong>1 pt</strong> for the correct answer</p><p class="hangp"><strong>1 pt</strong> for a correct verbal justification</p><h4 class="h4c">AP Physics C—Electricity and Magnetism</h4><p class="centera"><strong>Full Exam Scoring</strong></p><p class="noindentt1">Multiple-Choice: Number Correct______(35 max)</p><p class="noindentt1">Free-Response: Question 1______(15 max)</p><p class="noindentq">Question 2______(15 max)</p><p class="noindentq">Question 3______(15 max)</p><p class="noindentt1">Total Free-Response______(45 max)</p><p class="noindentt1">1.286 × Multiple-Choice + Free-Response = Raw Score______(90 max)</p><p class="image"><img alt="Images" class="q-img" src="images/f0346-01.jpg" style="max-width: 100%; height: auto; margin: 10px 0; display: block;"/></p>'),
  ('ap-mock-physc-em-3_q39', 'ap-mock-physc-em-3', 39, false, 'E', 'Practice Test 1 – E&M', 'medium', 'Consider the circuit shown, which contains two identical lightbulbs connected to a battery. The top bulb’s intensity will', '["decrease slightly if either of the two switches is closed.","decrease slightly if S1 is closed but not if S2 is closed.","decrease slightly if S2 is closed but not if S1 is closed.","remain unchanged if S2 is closed but go out completely if S1 is closed.","remain unchanged if S1 is closed but go out completely if S2 is closed."]'::jsonb, '(E) If S1 is closed, a parallel circuit with an equivalent resistance of R/2 will be created. Thus the current drawn from the battery will double and half that current will go through the top bulb, yielding the same current that flowed before the switch S1 was closed. If S2 is closed, creating a path of zero resistance, no current will pass through either bulb.'),
  ('ap-mock-physc-em-3_q40', 'ap-mock-physc-em-3', 40, false, 'D', 'Practice Test 1 – E&M', 'medium', 'What is the voltage V in the circuit shown in the figure?', '["1 V","2 V","4 V","6 V","8 V"]'::jsonb, '(D) A Kirchhoff’s loop equation for the right loop [clockwise: 8 V − (1 A)(4 Ω) − (I)(2 Ω) = 0] reveals that the current i moving from point A to point B must be 2.0 A (upward). A node equation at point A then shows the current through the left 2 Ω resistor must be 1.0 A flowing to the right. Finally, a Kirchhoff’s loop equation for the left loop [clockwise: V − (1 A)(2 Ω) − (2 A)(2 Ω) = 0] allows us to calculate the voltage of the battery as 6 V.'),
  ('ap-mock-physc-em-3_q41', 'ap-mock-physc-em-3', 41, false, 'D', 'Practice Test 1 – E&M', 'medium', 'A battery of voltage V0 is connected in series with a resistor of resistance R and an uncharged capacitor C at t = 0. What is the current in the circuit at the instant t = R C?', '["(V0 / R) * (1 - 1/e)","Zero","(V0 / R) * (1 - 1/e^2)","(V0 / R) * (1/e)","V0 / R"]'::jsonb, 'The current in an RC series charging circuit is I(t) = (V0 / R) e^{-t / (RC)}. At t = RC, the current is I(RC) = (V0 / R) e^{-1} = (V0 / R) * (1/e).'),
  ('ap-mock-physc-em-3_q42', 'ap-mock-physc-em-3', 42, false, 'A', 'Practice Test 1 – E&M', 'medium', 'A sphere of radius R contains a negative charge with a charge density given by the function \rho = −cr4, where r equals the distance from the center of the sphere. Where does the electric field have the smallest magnitude?', '["at the center of the sphere","at some point between the center of the sphere and the surface of the sphere","at the surface of the sphere","outside the sphere","The electric field is constant in space."]'::jsonb, '(A) At the center of the sphere, as a result of symmetry, the electric field is zero. At every other point in space, the electric field is nonzero.'),
  ('ap-mock-physc-em-3_q43', 'ap-mock-physc-em-3', 43, false, 'E', 'Practice Test 1 – E&M', 'medium', 'Referring to the charge distribution described in question 4, where is the potential smallest in magnitude?', '["at the center of the sphere","at some point between the center of the sphere and the surface of the sphere","at the surface of the sphere","outside the sphere","at infinity"]'::jsonb, '(E) The electric field at all points in space is directed toward the center of the sphere because the sphere is negatively charged. Electric fields always point in the direction of decreasing potential. With the conventional choice of zero potential at infinity, the potential will be negative everywhere else and thus smallest in magnitude at infinity.'),
  ('ap-mock-physc-em-3_q44', 'ap-mock-physc-em-3', 44, false, 'A', 'Practice Test 1 – E&M', 'medium', 'A loop is moved out of a magnetic field at constant velocity as shown. Which of the following statements about the induced current in the loop and the applied force on the loop is correct? (The magnetic field points into the page.)', '["The current is clockwise, and the applied force is to the right.","The current is clockwise, and the applied force is to the left.","The current is counterclockwise, and the applied force is to the right.","The current is counterclockwise, and the applied force is to the left.","The current is counterclockwise, and the applied force is into the page."]'::jsonb, '(A) To oppose the decrease in flux going into the page, the current flows clockwise. What magnetic force does this produce on the loop? The magnetic forces on the top and bottom of the loop, which point up and down, respectively, cancel each other out. The force on the left side of the loop points to the left, and there is zero force on the right side of the loop (because the magnetic field is zero in this region), so the net magnetic force points to the left. Because the applied force must cancel out this magnetic force in order for the loop to move at constant velocity, it then points to the right.'),
  ('ap-mock-physc-em-3_q45', 'ap-mock-physc-em-3', 45, false, 'C', 'Practice Test 1 – E&M', 'medium', 'Which of the following statements about electric field lines and equipotential curves is false?', '["Equipotential lines are always perpendicular to electric field lines.","The electric field magnitude is greater in regions where the density of electric field lines is greater.","Electric field lines begin and end only at the locations of negative and positive charges, respectively.","The work required to move a charge along an equipotential line is zero.","Electric field lines never penetrate metals."]'::jsonb, '(C) Electric field lines begin and end at locations of positive and negative charges, respectively (not the other way around).'),
  ('ap-topic-physc-tension-drills_q1', 'ap-topic-physc-tension-drills', 1, true, 'FRQ_MANUAL_EVALUATION', 'Tension Dynamics', 'medium', '<p><strong>Tension Drill #1:</strong> Assuming all pulleys and ropes are massless, find the tension in each rope and the acceleration of the set of masses shown:</p><p class="ques"><strong>1.</strong> Frictionless</p><p class="image"><img alt="Images" class="q-img" src="images/f0064-02.jpg" style="max-width: 100%; height: auto; margin: 10px 0; display: block;"/></p>', '["Submit analytical / algebraic result in workspace."]'::jsonb, '<p class="ques"><strong>1.</strong> <img alt="Images" class="q-img" src="images/e0065-01.jpg" style="max-width: 100%; height: auto; margin: 10px 0; display: block;"/></p>'),
  ('ap-topic-physc-tension-drills_q2', 'ap-topic-physc-tension-drills', 2, true, 'FRQ_MANUAL_EVALUATION', 'Tension Dynamics', 'medium', '<p><strong>Tension Drill #2:</strong> Assuming all pulleys and ropes are massless, find the tension in each rope and the acceleration of the set of masses shown:</p><p class="ques"><strong>2.</strong> Frictionless</p><p class="image"><img alt="Images" class="q-img" src="images/f0064-03.jpg" style="max-width: 100%; height: auto; margin: 10px 0; display: block;"/></p>', '["Submit analytical / algebraic result in workspace."]'::jsonb, '<p class="ques"><strong>2.</strong> <img alt="Images" class="q-img" src="images/e0065-02.jpg" style="max-width: 100%; height: auto; margin: 10px 0; display: block;"/></p>'),
  ('ap-topic-physc-tension-drills_q3', 'ap-topic-physc-tension-drills', 3, true, 'FRQ_MANUAL_EVALUATION', 'Tension Dynamics', 'medium', '<p><strong>Tension Drill #3:</strong> Assuming all pulleys and ropes are massless, find the tension in each rope and the acceleration of the set of masses shown:</p><p class="ques"><strong>3.</strong> Frictionless</p><p class="image"><img alt="Images" class="q-img" src="images/f0064-04.jpg" style="max-width: 100%; height: auto; margin: 10px 0; display: block;"/></p>', '["Submit analytical / algebraic result in workspace."]'::jsonb, '<p class="ques"><strong>3.</strong> <img alt="Images" class="q-img" src="images/e0065-03.jpg" style="max-width: 100%; height: auto; margin: 10px 0; display: block;"/></p>'),
  ('ap-topic-physc-tension-drills_q4', 'ap-topic-physc-tension-drills', 4, true, 'FRQ_MANUAL_EVALUATION', 'Tension Dynamics', 'medium', '<p><strong>Tension Drill #4:</strong> Assuming all pulleys and ropes are massless, find the tension in each rope and the acceleration of the set of masses shown:</p><p class="ques"><strong>4.</strong> Coefficient of Friction $\mu$</p><p class="image"><img alt="Images" class="q-img" src="images/f0064-05.jpg" style="max-width: 100%; height: auto; margin: 10px 0; display: block;"/></p>', '["Submit analytical / algebraic result in workspace."]'::jsonb, '<p class="ques"><strong>4.</strong> <img alt="Images" class="q-img" src="images/e0065-04.jpg" style="max-width: 100%; height: auto; margin: 10px 0; display: block;"/></p>'),
  ('ap-topic-physc-tension-drills_q5', 'ap-topic-physc-tension-drills', 5, true, 'FRQ_MANUAL_EVALUATION', 'Tension Dynamics', 'medium', '<p><strong>Tension Drill #5:</strong> Assuming all pulleys and ropes are massless, find the tension in each rope and the acceleration of the set of masses shown:</p><p class="ques"><strong>5.</strong></p><p class="image"><img alt="Images" class="q-img" src="images/f0064-06.jpg" style="max-width: 100%; height: auto; margin: 10px 0; display: block;"/></p>', '["Submit analytical / algebraic result in workspace."]'::jsonb, '<p class="ques"><strong>5.</strong> <img alt="Images" class="q-img" src="images/e0065-05.jpg" style="max-width: 100%; height: auto; margin: 10px 0; display: block;"/></p>'),
  ('ap-topic-physc-tension-drills_q6', 'ap-topic-physc-tension-drills', 6, true, 'FRQ_MANUAL_EVALUATION', 'Tension Dynamics', 'medium', '<p><strong>Tension Drill #6:</strong> Assuming all pulleys and ropes are massless, find the tension in each rope and the acceleration of the set of masses shown:</p><p class="ques"><strong>6.</strong></p><p class="image"><img alt="Images" class="q-img" src="images/f0064-07.jpg" style="max-width: 100%; height: auto; margin: 10px 0; display: block;"/></p>', '["Submit analytical / algebraic result in workspace."]'::jsonb, '<p class="ques"><strong>6.</strong> <img alt="Images" class="q-img" src="images/e0065-06.jpg" style="max-width: 100%; height: auto; margin: 10px 0; display: block;"/></p>'),
  ('ap-topic-physc-tension-drills_q7', 'ap-topic-physc-tension-drills', 7, true, 'FRQ_MANUAL_EVALUATION', 'Tension Dynamics', 'medium', '<p><strong>Tension Drill #7:</strong> Assuming all pulleys and ropes are massless, find the tension in each rope and the acceleration of the set of masses shown:</p><p class="ques"><strong>7.</strong> Frictionless</p><p class="image"><img alt="Images" class="q-img" src="images/f0064-08.jpg" style="max-width: 100%; height: auto; margin: 10px 0; display: block;"/></p>', '["Submit analytical / algebraic result in workspace."]'::jsonb, '<p class="ques"><strong>7.</strong> <img alt="Images" class="q-img" src="images/e0065-07.jpg" style="max-width: 100%; height: auto; margin: 10px 0; display: block;"/></p>'),
  ('ap-topic-physc-tension-drills_q8', 'ap-topic-physc-tension-drills', 8, true, 'FRQ_MANUAL_EVALUATION', 'Tension Dynamics', 'medium', '<p><strong>Tension Drill #8:</strong> Assuming all pulleys and ropes are massless, find the tension in each rope and the acceleration of the set of masses shown:</p><p class="ques"><strong>8.</strong> Frictionless</p><p class="image"><img alt="Images" class="q-img" src="images/f0064-09.jpg" style="max-width: 100%; height: auto; margin: 10px 0; display: block;"/></p>', '["Submit analytical / algebraic result in workspace."]'::jsonb, '<p class="ques"><strong>8.</strong> <img alt="Images" class="q-img" src="images/e0065-08.jpg" style="max-width: 100%; height: auto; margin: 10px 0; display: block;"/></p>'),
  ('ap-topic-physc-tension-drills_q9', 'ap-topic-physc-tension-drills', 9, true, 'FRQ_MANUAL_EVALUATION', 'Tension Dynamics', 'medium', '<p><strong>Tension Drill #9:</strong> Assuming all pulleys and ropes are massless, find the tension in each rope and the acceleration of the set of masses shown:</p><p class="ques"><strong>9.</strong> Frictionless</p><p class="image"><img alt="Images" class="q-img" src="images/f0065-01.jpg" style="max-width: 100%; height: auto; margin: 10px 0; display: block;"/></p>', '["Submit analytical / algebraic result in workspace."]'::jsonb, '<p class="ques"><strong>9.</strong> <img alt="Images" class="q-img" src="images/e0065-09.jpg" style="max-width: 100%; height: auto; margin: 10px 0; display: block;"/></p>')
